window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 2, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 66 year old woman on multiple QT prolonging medications becomes lightheaded on telemetry, and the alarm records the rhythm strip shown during her symptoms. She still has a pulse and a measurable blood pressure when you arrive. Which immediate intravenous therapy is most appropriate for the arrhythmia captured on this recording?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous magnesium sulfate given as a bolus to suppress the arrhythmia", "B": "Intravenous procainamide loaded slowly to stabilize the ventricular myocardium overall", "C": "Intravenous amiodarone infused to prolong refractoriness across the ventricular tissue", "D": "Intravenous adenosine pushed rapidly to interrupt atrioventricular nodal impulse conduction", "E": "Intravenous metoprolol titrated to reduce sympathetic drive and heart rate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous magnesium sulfate given as a bolus to suppress the arrhythmia): The strip shows a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose QRS complexes twist around the baseline in a patient loaded with QT prolonging drugs, which identifies torsades de pointes, and magnesium is the first line suppressive therapy along with stopping offending agents. Procainamide and amiodarone both prolong the QT interval further and can perpetuate the arrhythmia. Adenosine targets the AV node, which plays no role here, and beta blockade alone does not address the acute repolarization abnormality driving the rhythm.", "image": "question_images/q_2.png"}, {"id": 3, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 26 year old man has chest pain, an elevated troponin, and a normal coronary CT angiogram two weeks after a febrile viral illness. His clinician suspects acute myocarditis and wants noninvasive confirmation before considering any biopsy. Which imaging study best establishes this diagnosis, and by identifying what tissue findings?", "options": {"A": "Transthoracic echocardiography, which detects the pathognomonic speckled myocardial texture of inflammation", "B": "Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging, identifying myocardial edema and late gadolinium enhancement patterns", "C": "Exercise nuclear perfusion imaging, which localizes inflammation to the affected coronary territories", "D": "Coronary artery calcium scoring, which excludes myocarditis when the score is zero", "E": "Chest computed tomography without contrast, measuring myocardial attenuation as an inflammation index"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging, identifying myocardial edema and late gadolinium enhancement patterns): CMR applies the Lake Louise criteria, combining T2 based evidence of edema with T1 based abnormalities including late gadolinium enhancement in a nonischemic, typically subepicardial or midwall distribution, making it the noninvasive test of choice. Echocardiography assesses function but has no texture finding specific to myocarditis. Perfusion imaging and calcium scoring evaluate coronary disease rather than inflammation, and noncontrast chest CT cannot characterize myocardial tissue this way."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "normal_cardiovascular_anatomy_and_physiology", "question": "While teaching conduction system anatomy, you explain why sinus node dysfunction can complicate proximal right coronary artery occlusion. In the majority of individuals, which vessel gives rise to the sinoatrial nodal artery, and what is the approximate frequency of that anatomic arrangement in the general population?", "options": {"A": "The left anterior descending artery, in roughly 80 percent of people", "B": "The circumflex artery, in approximately 90 percent of the general population", "C": "The ramus intermedius branch, in about half of the studied individuals", "D": "The right coronary artery, in roughly 60 percent of individuals studied", "E": "The left main artery directly, in nearly three quarters of people"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The right coronary artery, in roughly 60 percent of individuals studied): the sinoatrial nodal artery arises from the proximal right coronary artery in about 60 percent of people, with the circumflex supplying it in most of the remainder, which is why proximal right coronary occlusion can produce sinus bradycardia and arrest. The circumflex figure of 90 percent inverts the true proportions. The left anterior descending, a ramus branch, and the left main directly are not typical origins of this artery."}, {"id": 8, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A medical assistant asks you to review the office protocol for routine blood pressure measurement so that staff obtain accurate readings for treatment decisions. The clinic intends to follow the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline recommendations on patient preparation, positioning, and cuff use. Which element of measurement technique is performed correctly according to that guideline?", "options": {"A": "Taking the reading as soon as the patient sits down on the table", "B": "Placing the cuff over a thin shirt sleeve to save preparation time", "C": "Letting the patient describe symptoms aloud while the device records the pressure", "D": "Having the patient sit on the examination table with legs swinging freely", "E": "Supporting the bare arm at heart level after five minutes of quiet rest"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Supporting the bare arm at heart level after five minutes of quiet rest): Accurate technique requires about five minutes of quiet rest, then a bare arm supported at heart level with the back supported and feet flat on the floor. An unsupported or dependent arm, a cuff over clothing, talking during the reading, or dangling legs each raise the recorded value by several mm Hg. Measuring the moment the patient sits captures activity related elevation rather than resting pressure."}, {"id": 11, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 70 year old man with HFrEF taking spironolactone 25 mg daily returns for scheduled follow up, and the laboratory results shown are obtained. He feels well and reports no new complaints. Based on these values and standard mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist safety practice, which next step is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Continue the current dose and repeat chemistries at the next routine annual visit", "B": "Increase spironolactone to 50 mg daily to reach the guideline target dose", "C": "Add oral potassium supplementation to protect against diuretic related electrolyte losses", "D": "Hold the spironolactone and recheck potassium and renal function within several days", "E": "Switch to eplerenone at an equivalent dose while keeping the same monitoring schedule"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold the spironolactone and recheck potassium and renal function within several days): his potassium of 5.8 mmol/L with worsened renal function crosses the threshold at which mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist therapy should be interrupted, since continuing risks dangerous hyperkalemia. Uptitrating the dose or adding potassium supplements would push the potassium higher still. Eplerenone carries the same hyperkalemia liability as spironolactone, and waiting a full year to recheck chemistry ignores the abnormality entirely.", "image": "question_images/q_11.png"}, {"id": 13, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 29 year old woman presents with abrupt palpitations and lightheadedness that began while climbing stairs. Her blood pressure is stable and her lungs are clear. You review the rhythm strip shown, obtained at presentation before any medication was given. Which diagnosis best explains the tachycardia captured on this recording?", "options": {"A": "Sinus tachycardia with first degree atrioventricular block and low amplitude P waves", "B": "Atrial flutter conducting to the ventricles in a fixed two to one ratio", "C": "Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia with retrograde atrial activation hidden in the QRS", "D": "Multifocal atrial tachycardia arising from several competing atrial foci with varying P wave morphologies and intervals", "E": "Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia arising from the right ventricular outflow tract"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia with retrograde atrial activation hidden in the QRS): The strip shows a regular narrow complex tachycardia with no discernible P waves, the classic appearance of typical AVNRT, in which near simultaneous atrial and ventricular activation buries the retrograde P wave. Sinus tachycardia and multifocal atrial tachycardia would both show visible P waves, and multifocal atrial tachycardia would also be irregular. Flutter at a two to one ratio usually reveals sawtooth waves between complexes, and ventricular tachycardia produces wide rather than narrow QRS complexes.", "image": "question_images/q_13.png"}, {"id": 14, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 41 year old man has right atrial and right ventricular enlargement on echocardiography, but transthoracic imaging shows an intact fossa ovalis. Transesophageal imaging reveals a superior sinus venosus atrial septal defect. Which additional anatomic abnormality should be specifically sought before planning repair in this patient?", "options": {"A": "A cleft in the anterior mitral leaflet producing regurgitation into the left atrium", "B": "A bicuspid aortic valve with associated dilation of the ascending aorta", "C": "A persistent left superior vena cava draining into the coronary sinus", "D": "An apically displaced septal tricuspid leaflet with atrialization of the right ventricle", "E": "Anomalous drainage of the right upper pulmonary vein into the superior vena cava"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Anomalous drainage of the right upper pulmonary vein into the superior vena cava): Superior sinus venosus defects are frequently accompanied by partial anomalous pulmonary venous return, most often involving the right upper vein. Identifying this changes the operation, since the vein must be baffled to the left atrium during repair. A cleft mitral leaflet belongs with primum defects, and apical tricuspid displacement defines Ebstein anomaly."}, {"id": 15, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 27 year old woman has an incidental finding on an overnight Holter recorded during sleep: intermittent grouped beating with occasional dropped QRS complexes. She is asymptomatic and takes no medications. Which electrocardiographic behavior identifies the benign, typically vagally mediated form of second degree atrioventricular block seen here?", "options": {"A": "Progressive PR prolongation across successive beats until a P wave fails to conduct", "B": "Fixed PR intervals with sudden conduction failure of an isolated P wave", "C": "A constant two to one ratio with a widened QRS in conducted beats", "D": "Atrial and ventricular rates that appear fully independent of each other", "E": "PR intervals that shorten progressively before each nonconducted atrial impulse arrives"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Progressive PR prolongation across successive beats until a P wave fails to conduct): Mobitz type I, or Wenckebach, block shows lengthening PR intervals that culminate in a dropped beat, reflects decremental conduction within the AV node, and is common and benign during sleep in young people with high vagal tone. Fixed PR intervals with sudden failure describe Mobitz type II, an infranodal problem with a different prognosis. Two to one conduction with a wide QRS also suggests infranodal disease, independent atrial and ventricular activity defines complete block, and progressively shortening PR intervals describe none of the block patterns."}, {"id": 16, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 62 year old woman with a history of heparin induced thrombocytopenia requires anticoagulation during primary percutaneous coronary intervention for an inferior infarction. The interventional team chooses an agent that inhibits its target enzyme directly without a plasma cofactor. Which anticoagulant best fits this clinical requirement?", "options": {"A": "Bivalirudin, a direct thrombin inhibitor that does not cross react with heparin antibodies", "B": "Enoxaparin, a low molecular weight heparin with predominant activity against factor Xa", "C": "Warfarin, a vitamin K antagonist titrated to the international normalized ratio", "D": "Unfractionated heparin, an indirect inhibitor acting through the cofactor antithrombin", "E": "Fondaparinux, a synthetic pentasaccharide anticoagulant that potentiates circulating antithrombin selectively against activated factor Xa"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bivalirudin, a direct thrombin inhibitor that does not cross react with heparin antibodies): bivalirudin inhibits thrombin directly without a cofactor and is a standard procedural choice after heparin induced thrombocytopenia. Unfractionated heparin is unsafe here, and enoxaparin can cross react with the pathogenic antibodies. Fondaparinux still works through antithrombin rather than directly, and warfarin is unsuitable for procedural anticoagulation and can worsen this condition acutely."}, {"id": 18, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 64 year old man with chronic stable angina walks two blocks before predictable chest tightness begins. His cardiologist starts metoprolol succinate as first line antianginal therapy and titrates to a resting heart rate near 60 beats per minute. How does this medication reduce his anginal symptoms?", "options": {"A": "It lowers heart rate and contractility, reducing myocardial oxygen demand during exertion", "B": "It inhibits the late inward sodium current within myocytes, directly easing diastolic ventricular wall tension", "C": "It dilates epicardial coronary arteries through release of endothelial nitric oxide", "D": "It increases collateral vessel growth by stimulating vascular endothelial growth factor", "E": "It raises hemoglobin oxygen affinity, improving delivery to the ischemic myocardium"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It lowers heart rate and contractility, reducing myocardial oxygen demand during exertion): beta blockade blunts sympathetic stimulation of rate and contractility, and slower rates also lengthen diastole, when coronary perfusion occurs. Late sodium current inhibition is the mechanism of ranolazine, and nitric oxide mediated dilation describes nitrates. Beta blockers neither stimulate collateral growth through growth factors nor change hemoglobin oxygen affinity."}, {"id": 19, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 62 year old woman with a flail mitral leaflet segment is examined before surgical referral, and you document her murmur carefully for the operative record. Which set of auscultatory features is most typical of chronic severe mitral regurgitation from leaflet prolapse or flail?", "options": {"A": "A blowing apical systolic murmur radiating toward the axilla, often with an S3", "B": "A harsh basal systolic murmur radiating to the neck with a delayed carotid upstroke", "C": "A rumbling diastolic murmur at the apex introduced by an early diastolic snap", "D": "A soft early diastolic murmur along the sternal border with bounding peripheral pulses", "E": "A systolic murmur at the lower sternal border that intensifies during held inspiration"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A blowing apical systolic murmur radiating toward the axilla, often with an S3): the mitral regurgitant jet is directed into the left atrium, so the murmur is loudest at the apex and follows the jet toward the axilla, and in severe disease the large diastolic inflow volume generates a third heart sound. A harsh basal murmur with carotid radiation and a delayed upstroke describes aortic stenosis. An apical diastolic rumble after a snap indicates mitral stenosis, and an early diastolic sternal border murmur with bounding pulses indicates aortic regurgitation. Inspiratory intensification at the lower sternal border points to tricuspid regurgitation."}, {"id": 21, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 40 year old man in a regular narrow complex tachycardia does not respond to vagal maneuvers. You prepare intravenous adenosine and warn him about transient chest discomfort and flushing. Which statement best describes the primary electrophysiologic action that makes adenosine effective for terminating atrioventricular node dependent reentrant tachycardias?", "options": {"A": "It blocks sodium channels in atrial myocardium and slows intraatrial conduction velocity", "B": "It prolongs ventricular repolarization by inhibiting the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current", "C": "It transiently blocks conduction through the atrioventricular node, interrupting the reentrant circuit", "D": "It stimulates beta receptors, shortening nodal refractoriness and accelerating anterograde conduction", "E": "It blocks calcium channels selectively in ventricular myocardium, reducing myocardial contractile force"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It transiently blocks conduction through the atrioventricular node, interrupting the reentrant circuit): Adenosine activates A1 receptors, increasing potassium conductance and suppressing calcium mediated conduction in the AV node, which briefly interrupts any circuit that requires the node, such as AVNRT or orthodromic AVRT. Sodium channel blockade in atrial tissue describes class I agents, and inhibition of the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current describes class III drugs. Beta receptor stimulation would speed rather than block nodal conduction, and adenosine does not act by selective ventricular calcium channel blockade."}, {"id": 31, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 54 year old woman with a bileaflet mechanical valve in the mitral position takes warfarin, and her anticoagulation clinic asks you to confirm the intended intensity of therapy. According to guideline recommendations, which target international normalized ratio is appropriate for a mechanical mitral prosthesis?", "options": {"A": "A target of 1.5 with aspirin reserved for patients who develop emboli", "B": "A target of 2.0 kept deliberately low to reduce bleeding in older adults", "C": "A target of 3.0, generally maintained within a range of 2.5 to 3.5", "D": "A target of 4.0 reflecting the higher thrombotic risk of the mitral position", "E": "A target of 2.5 for the first three months, then aspirin taken alone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A target of 3.0, generally maintained within a range of 2.5 to 3.5): mechanical valves in the mitral position carry higher thrombotic risk than aortic prostheses because of lower flow velocities, so the recommended warfarin intensity is an international normalized ratio of 3.0. A target of 2.5 applies to bileaflet aortic prostheses without additional risk factors, not to mitral valves. Values near 1.5 to 2.0 are subtherapeutic for mechanical prostheses, and a routine target of 4.0 adds bleeding risk without established benefit. Direct oral anticoagulants and aspirin substitution are not acceptable replacements for warfarin with mechanical valves."}, {"id": 33, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 68 year old woman recovering from a non ST elevation infarction hesitates to enroll in cardiac rehabilitation because of the driving distance involved. Her nurse practitioner reviews the documented benefits of participation with her. Which outcome benefit is supported by evidence for cardiac rehabilitation after infarction?", "options": {"A": "Regression of established coronary stenoses to angiographically normal vessel caliber", "B": "Reduced cardiovascular mortality and fewer hospital readmissions among program participants", "C": "Elimination of the need for continued statin and antiplatelet medication therapy", "D": "Restoration of previously infarcted myocardial tissue through exercise stimulated regeneration of functioning cardiomyocytes", "E": "A documented rise in ejection fraction of 15 points within 6 weeks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced cardiovascular mortality and fewer hospital readmissions among program participants): participation after infarction is associated with lower cardiovascular mortality, fewer readmissions, and better functional capacity and quality of life. Exercise training does not shrink stenoses to normal caliber or regenerate infarcted myocardium. Rehabilitation complements rather than replaces statin and antiplatelet therapy, and no fixed 15 point ejection fraction gain is an expected outcome."}, {"id": 36, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 59 year old woman with heart failure reports that she feels comfortable sitting and doing light housework, but climbing two flights of stairs or walking briskly on level ground reliably produces dyspnea that resolves promptly with rest. Which NYHA functional class corresponds most closely to this reported symptom pattern?", "options": {"A": "Class I, since she remains able to complete all household activities without assistance", "B": "Class III, since stair climbing represents less than ordinary physical activity for adults", "C": "Class IV, since her symptoms occur predictably during her usual daily routine", "D": "Class II to III transitional status, which the classification records as a combined grade", "E": "Class II, slight limitation with symptoms on ordinary exertion and comfort at rest"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Class II, slight limitation with symptoms on ordinary exertion and comfort at rest): brisk walking and stair climbing are ordinary physical activities, and symptoms limited to that level of exertion with comfort at rest define class II. Class III requires symptoms with less than ordinary activity, such as walking across a room, and class IV requires symptoms at rest. The classification uses four discrete classes and does not record combined transitional grades."}, {"id": 37, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "An emergency clinician asks you to summarize the electrocardiographic features that separate acute pericarditis from ST elevation myocardial infarction before activating the catheterization laboratory for a 41 year old man with chest pain and ST elevation. Which description most accurately characterizes the ST segment pattern of acute pericarditis?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse concave upward elevation lacking reciprocal depression except in lead aVR", "B": "Convex elevation confined to anatomically contiguous leads with reciprocal inferior depression", "C": "Elevation that evolves into pathologic Q waves within twenty four hours", "D": "Elevation limited to the precordial leads with hyperacute T waves preceding it", "E": "Alternating elevation and depression that shifts territories on serial tracings hourly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Diffuse concave upward elevation lacking reciprocal depression except in lead aVR): pericardial inflammation involves the whole subepicardium, producing widespread concave elevation with PR depression, and only aVR and sometimes V1 show reciprocal type changes. Convex elevation in contiguous leads with true reciprocal depression indicates regional infarction. Evolution into Q waves and hyperacute T waves are infarction features, and a shifting territorial pattern describes neither condition accurately."}, {"id": 38, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "An 81 year old woman with a heavily calcified aortic valve is being staged before possible valve replacement, and her echocardiographer reports the continuity equation valve area. Under the 2020 ACC/AHA guideline, which calculated aortic valve area is consistent with severe aortic stenosis?", "options": {"A": "An orifice measuring 2.0 cm² with preserved leaflet excursion on imaging", "B": "An orifice measuring 1.8 cm² accompanied by mild concentric ventricular thickening", "C": "An orifice measuring 1.5 cm² when the mean gradient is 25 mmHg", "D": "An orifice measuring 1.0 cm² or smaller on quantitative Doppler assessment", "E": "An orifice measuring 2.5 cm² with heavy annular calcium but normal gradients"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An orifice measuring 1.0 cm² or smaller on quantitative Doppler assessment): severe aortic stenosis is defined by a valve area of 1.0 cm² or less, together with a jet velocity of at least 4 m/s and a mean gradient of at least 40 mmHg in the high gradient form. Areas between about 1.0 and 1.5 cm² represent moderate stenosis, and values near 2.0 cm² or larger indicate mild narrowing of a valve whose normal area is 3.0 to 4.0 cm². Annular calcium without a reduced orifice or elevated gradient does not itself establish severity."}, {"id": 42, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 28 year old woman with pleuritic chest pain has an ECG obtained within hours of symptom onset, and you are asked which pattern corresponds to the earliest electrocardiographic stage of acute pericarditis. Which constellation of findings defines stage one of the classic four stage evolution in this disease?", "options": {"A": "Widespread T wave inversion accompanying gradual resolution of the earlier ST segment elevation changes across leads", "B": "Normalization of the ST segments and PR segments across the recorded leads", "C": "Isolated PR segment elevation in lead aVR without accompanying ST segment abnormality", "D": "Diffuse concave ST segment elevation accompanied by PR segment depression in multiple leads", "E": "Territorial convex ST elevation with reciprocal depression in the opposing leads"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diffuse concave ST segment elevation accompanied by PR segment depression in multiple leads): stage one appears within hours to days and reflects diffuse subepicardial inflammation. Territorial convex elevation with reciprocal depression describes myocardial infarction rather than pericarditis. Normalization of segments and widespread T wave inversion belong to the later second and third stages, while PR elevation confined to aVR is only one component of stage one rather than its definition."}, {"id": 43, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 58 year old man with borderline 10 year atherosclerotic risk undergoes coronary artery calcium scoring to refine a statin decision. The result is zero, and he does not smoke, does not have diabetes, and has no family history of premature coronary disease. What does this calcium score of zero most strongly indicate?", "options": {"A": "Freedom from noncalcified soft plaque in addition to calcified plaque on this study", "B": "A need for repeat scoring within six months to confirm the finding", "C": "Low near term event risk that supports deferring statin therapy with reassessment later", "D": "An imaging artifact, because borderline risk patients rarely have truly negative scans", "E": "Enough reassurance to discontinue further blood pressure and lifestyle risk factor counseling"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Low near term event risk that supports deferring statin therapy with reassessment later): a score of zero in a borderline risk adult without diabetes or current smoking predicts very low near term event rates and supports deferring statin therapy with reassessment in five to ten years. It cannot establish freedom from soft plaque, because noncalcified disease is invisible to calcium scanning, and no six month confirmatory scan is needed. Blood pressure and lifestyle counseling must continue, and a negative scan in this setting is a valid result rather than an artifact."}, {"id": 45, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 29 year old woman at 36 weeks of gestation is diagnosed with peripartum cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 30 percent. The inpatient team assembles a medication plan that must remain safe for the fetus until delivery occurs. Which heart failure drug class must be withheld before delivery, and why?", "options": {"A": "Beta blockers, because beta 1 selective agents interfere with fetal cardiac development", "B": "Loop diuretics, because decongestion lowers placental perfusion below acceptable viable thresholds", "C": "Hydralazine, because direct vasodilators cross the placenta and produce fetal hypotension", "D": "Digoxin, because it concentrates in amniotic fluid and produces fetal bradycardia", "E": "ACE inhibitors, because they cause fetal renal injury and are withheld until after delivery"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (ACE inhibitors, because they cause fetal renal injury and are withheld until after delivery): ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNI, and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists are fetotoxic, with renin angiotensin blockers causing fetal renal dysgenesis, oligohydramnios, and other injury, so they are introduced only postpartum. Beta 1 selective beta blockers such as metoprolol are generally continued during pregnancy. Loop diuretics can be used cautiously for congestion, hydralazine is actually a preferred vasodilator in pregnancy, and digoxin is considered compatible with gestation."}, {"id": 51, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "During a heart failure lecture for physician assistants, an instructor reviews the ejection fraction categories from the 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA guideline. A patient has exertional dyspnea, elevated natriuretic peptides, and a documented left ventricular ejection fraction of 45 percent. Which classification category does this patient's ejection fraction measurement place them in?", "options": {"A": "Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, requiring only diuretic therapy for symptom control", "B": "Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, the category with the strongest device evidence", "C": "Heart failure with improved ejection fraction, reflecting recovery from a previously lower value", "D": "Heart failure with mildly reduced ejection fraction, defined by values of 41 to 49 percent", "E": "Advanced heart failure, because the ejection fraction has fallen beneath the normal range"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Heart failure with mildly reduced ejection fraction, defined by values of 41 to 49 percent): an ejection fraction of 45 percent with symptoms falls within the HFmrEF band. Preserved ejection fraction requires a value of at least 50 percent, and reduced ejection fraction requires 40 percent or lower. Improved ejection fraction describes a rise above 40 percent from a previously reduced baseline, and advanced heart failure is a clinical stage rather than an ejection fraction band."}, {"id": 54, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 68 year old man with an acute inferior ST elevation infarction becomes profoundly hypotensive after a single sublingual nitroglycerin tablet. Right sided leads show 1.5 mm ST elevation in V4R. Which statement best explains the recommended avoidance of nitrates when this complication is present?", "options": {"A": "The infarcted right ventricle depends on preload, which venodilation from nitrates sharply reduces", "B": "Nitrates accelerate atrioventricular conduction and provoke rapid ventricular rates during inferior infarction", "C": "Nitrates bind circulating heparin and neutralize anticoagulation during the acute infarct period", "D": "Nitrates constrict the pulmonary circulation and precipitate acute right sided pressure overload", "E": "Nitrates trigger coronary steal that diverts flow away from the occluded artery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The infarcted right ventricle depends on preload, which venodilation from nitrates sharply reduces): ST elevation in V4R identifies right ventricular infarction, in which stroke volume depends on venous return, so venodilating agents can cause profound hypotension. Nitrates do not accelerate atrioventricular conduction, neutralize heparin, or constrict the pulmonary vasculature. Coronary steal is a concern with arteriolar dilators such as dipyridamole rather than with nitrate venodilation."}, {"id": 56, "domain": "normal_cardiovascular_anatomy_and_physiology", "question": "A new graduate reviewing ECG fundamentals asks you to state the normal adult limits for atrioventricular conduction and ventricular depolarization so she can screen tracings systematically. Which combination correctly states the normal PR interval range and the upper limit of normal QRS duration in adults?", "options": {"A": "PR interval 80 to 100 ms with QRS duration under 60 ms", "B": "PR interval 200 to 300 ms with QRS duration under 160 ms", "C": "PR interval 120 to 200 ms with QRS duration under 120 ms", "D": "PR interval 300 to 400 ms with QRS duration under 200 ms", "E": "PR interval 40 to 120 ms with QRS duration under 80 ms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (PR interval 120 to 200 ms with QRS duration under 120 ms): normal atrioventricular conduction takes 120 to 200 ms from atrial onset to ventricular onset, and normal ventricular depolarization completes in less than 120 ms, with 120 ms or more defining bundle branch level delay. Ranges starting at 80 ms or below describe preexcitation territory rather than normal conduction. A PR of 200 to 300 ms already meets first degree atrioventricular block criteria, and the widest listed ranges are clearly pathologic."}, {"id": 57, "domain": "normal_cardiovascular_anatomy_and_physiology", "question": "During right heart catheterization teaching, a fellow asks you to state the accepted normal range for the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure so she can recognize left sided filling abnormalities. Which range represents a normal mean pulmonary capillary wedge pressure in a healthy resting adult undergoing catheterization?", "options": {"A": "A mean value of approximately 4 to 12 mmHg at rest", "B": "A mean value of approximately 18 to 25 mmHg at rest", "C": "A mean value of approximately 0 to 2 mmHg at rest", "D": "A mean value of approximately 15 to 30 mmHg at rest", "E": "A mean value of approximately 25 to 40 mmHg at rest"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A mean value of approximately 4 to 12 mmHg at rest): the wedge pressure estimates left atrial pressure, which normally runs 4 to 12 mmHg, and values above roughly 15 mmHg suggest left sided congestion. The 15 to 30 mmHg range describes normal pulmonary artery systolic pressure rather than wedge pressure. A near zero mean is below physiologic left atrial pressure, and the two highest ranges represent significant pulmonary venous hypertension."}, {"id": 63, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 39 year old woman is diagnosed with a first episode of idiopathic acute pericarditis after presenting with positional chest pain, a friction rub, and diffuse ST elevation. She has normal renal function and no bleeding history. Which regimen represents guideline supported first line medical therapy for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Prednisone at a moderate dose tapered over approximately four to six weeks", "B": "Colchicine used alone at standard dosing for a total of one month", "C": "An NSAID or aspirin combined with colchicine continued for approximately three months", "D": "A proton pump inhibitor with acetaminophen and strict bed rest for two weeks", "E": "Anakinra given daily by subcutaneous injection until inflammatory markers fully normalize"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An NSAID or aspirin combined with colchicine continued for approximately three months): the antiinflammatory drug controls symptoms while adjunctive colchicine roughly halves the recurrence rate, and three months is the recommended colchicine duration for a first episode. Corticosteroids are reserved for contraindications or specific indications because they promote recurrence. Colchicine monotherapy for one month is too brief and omits the antiinflammatory backbone, interleukin 1 blockade is reserved for refractory recurrent disease, and acetaminophen with rest lacks antiinflammatory efficacy."}, {"id": 69, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 64 year old woman started lisinopril six weeks ago for stage 2 hypertension and now reports a persistent dry tickling cough without fever, wheeze, dyspnea, or reflux symptoms. Her examination and chest radiograph are unremarkable. Which mechanism best explains this well recognized adverse effect of her new antihypertensive medication?", "options": {"A": "Accumulation of bradykinin and substance P that sensitizes airway cough receptors", "B": "Histamine release from mast cells producing bronchospasm and upper airway irritation", "C": "Eosinophilic airway inflammation resembling cough variant asthma on bronchial biopsy", "D": "Acid reflux provoked by lower esophageal sphincter relaxation from the drug", "E": "Direct stimulation of pulmonary stretch receptors by rising angiotensin II levels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Accumulation of bradykinin and substance P that sensitizes airway cough receptors): Angiotensin converting enzyme normally degrades bradykinin and substance P, so inhibiting it allows these peptides to accumulate and sensitize airway cough fibers, producing the classic dry cough. The cough is a class effect and resolves within days to weeks of stopping the drug. Histamine release, eosinophilic inflammation, and reflux do not explain the reaction, and angiotensin II levels fall rather than rise on therapy."}, {"id": 70, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 63 year old man dies of heart failure during treatment for suspected endocarditis, and autopsy examination of the mitral valve is performed to establish the diagnosis definitively. Under the Duke framework, which pathologic finding establishes a definite diagnosis of infective endocarditis?", "options": {"A": "Sterile fibrin platelet deposits along the leaflet closure line without inflammatory cells", "B": "Myxomatous degeneration with leaflet redundancy and elongated chordae on gross inspection", "C": "Caseating granulomas within the myocardium adjacent to the valve annulus", "D": "Bland thrombus adherent to a central venous catheter tip cultured after removal", "E": "Microorganisms demonstrated within a vegetation by culture or histologic examination"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Microorganisms demonstrated within a vegetation by culture or histologic examination): the Duke pathologic criteria establish definite endocarditis when organisms are shown by culture or histology in a vegetation, in an embolized vegetation, or in an intracardiac abscess, or when active endocarditis is confirmed histologically in such a lesion. Sterile fibrin platelet deposits describe nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis, which lacks organisms and active inflammation. Myxomatous degeneration is a structural leaflet disorder without infection, and caseating granulomas suggest tuberculosis rather than typical infective endocarditis. Thrombus on a catheter tip, even when culture positive, does not demonstrate endocardial infection."}, {"id": 71, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 19 year old basketball player with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has a harsh systolic murmur at the lower left sternal border, and you ask him to squat from a standing position during auscultation. Which change in his outflow tract murmur do you anticipate with squatting?", "options": {"A": "It intensifies because squatting empties the ventricle and increases septal mitral contact", "B": "It converts to a diastolic murmur as ventricular compliance changes with position", "C": "It stays unchanged since positional maneuvers mainly influence right sided heart murmurs", "D": "It becomes continuous as squatting raises pressures in both systole and diastole", "E": "It softens because greater venous return and afterload enlarge the ventricular cavity"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (It softens because greater venous return and afterload enlarge the ventricular cavity): squatting simultaneously raises preload and afterload, distending the left ventricle and pushing the septum away from the mitral apparatus, which lessens the dynamic gradient and quiets the murmur. Standing and Valsalva strain have the opposite effect, shrinking the ventricle and intensifying the murmur, which is the signature behavior distinguishing this lesion from fixed aortic stenosis. Squatting fills rather than empties the ventricle, so intensification is backwards physiology. The murmur remains systolic, and positional maneuvers strongly affect left sided dynamic obstruction."}, {"id": 75, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 58 year old man with fevers has two blood cultures growing viridans group streptococci drawn 12 hours apart, and his echocardiogram shows an oscillating mass on the mitral valve. When applying the Duke criteria to his case, which finding constitutes a major criterion?", "options": {"A": "Fever above 38 degrees Celsius documented on two consecutive hospital days", "B": "Painless macules on the palms and soles noted on admission examination", "C": "Persistently positive blood cultures with an organism typical for infective endocarditis", "D": "Glomerulonephritis with red blood cell casts discovered on urine microscopy during this hospital admission", "E": "A history of injection drug use disclosed during the social history"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Persistently positive blood cultures with an organism typical for infective endocarditis): the two Duke major criteria are blood cultures positive with typical organisms in a persistent pattern and evidence of endocardial involvement, such as a vegetation, abscess, new prosthetic dehiscence, or new valvular regurgitation. Viridans streptococci in two separate cultures drawn more than 12 hours apart satisfy the microbiologic major criterion. Fever, injection drug use, and immunologic phenomena such as glomerulonephritis are minor criteria. Painless palm and sole macules are Janeway lesions, a vascular phenomenon that also counts only as a minor criterion."}, {"id": 77, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 24 year old woman experiences syncope when her alarm clock sounds and again in the early weeks after childbirth. Her ECG shows QT prolongation with low amplitude notched T waves, and a KCNH2 mutation is identified. Which subtype does this represent, and which supportive measure is specifically emphasized?", "options": {"A": "Long QT type 1, in which mexiletine is the preferred initial therapy", "B": "Long QT type 3, in which beta blockers are typically the least useful", "C": "Brugada syndrome, in which quinidine can reduce recurrent arrhythmic event frequency", "D": "Long QT type 2, in which maintaining normal serum potassium is emphasized", "E": "Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, in which flecainide supplements beta blockade"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Long QT type 2, in which maintaining normal serum potassium is emphasized): KCNH2 mutations impair the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current, producing long QT type 2, whose events are classically provoked by sudden arousal and by the postpartum period, and keeping potassium replete supports the residual repolarizing current alongside beta blockade. Type 1 relates to KCNQ1 with exertional events, and type 3 relates to SCN5A with events at rest. Brugada syndrome and catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia arise from different genes and do not produce this notched T wave phenotype."}, {"id": 82, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 63 year old man with HFrEF is transitioned from lisinopril to sacubitril valsartan and successfully titrated to the target dose. Three months later his clinician wants to follow a natriuretic peptide to assess his trajectory. Which biomarker should be trended in this situation, and for what pharmacologic reason?", "options": {"A": "BNP, because neprilysin inhibition makes it the more responsive marker of decongestion", "B": "NT proBNP, because neprilysin inhibition raises BNP while NT proBNP is not a substrate", "C": "High sensitivity troponin, which replaces natriuretic peptides once an ARNI is prescribed", "D": "Soluble ST2, the guideline preferred biomarker for patients receiving combined neprilysin blockade", "E": "Galectin 3, since fibrosis markers track remodeling more closely than stretch markers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (NT proBNP, because neprilysin inhibition raises BNP while NT proBNP is not a substrate): sacubitril blocks neprilysin, the enzyme that degrades BNP, so BNP levels rise on therapy and no longer reflect filling pressures reliably. NT proBNP is not degraded by neprilysin, so it remains interpretable and typically falls as the patient improves. Troponin, soluble ST2, and galectin 3 provide prognostic information but none replaces natriuretic peptide monitoring in this setting."}, {"id": 86, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 76 year old woman with heart failure and an ejection fraction of 58 percent asks whether any medication class has been shown to help patients like her. Her nurse practitioner reviews the 2022 guideline recommendations for preserved ejection fraction. Which drug class earned a class 2a recommendation in this population?", "options": {"A": "Digoxin, which improved exercise capacity and quality of life in preserved ejection fraction", "B": "Long acting nitrates, which raise daily activity tolerance by lowering nocturnal ventricular filling pressures during sleep", "C": "SGLT2 inhibitors, which reduced heart failure hospitalizations in dedicated preserved ejection fraction trials", "D": "High dose beta blockade, which extends survival by suppressing chronotropic incompetence mechanisms", "E": "Calcium channel blockers, which reverse the diastolic stiffness underlying the whole syndrome"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (SGLT2 inhibitors, which reduced heart failure hospitalizations in dedicated preserved ejection fraction trials): EMPEROR Preserved demonstrated a reduction in the composite of cardiovascular death and heart failure hospitalization, making SGLT2 inhibition the best supported drug therapy in HFpEF and earning the 2a grade. Nitrates failed to improve activity in this population, and digoxin has no established role here. Neither beta blockers nor calcium channel blockers carry outcome evidence for preserved ejection fraction."}, {"id": 88, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "During evaluation of a 27 year old man with newly diagnosed aortic coarctation, the consulting cardiologist plans a full echocardiographic survey for associated congenital lesions. Which coexisting cardiac abnormality is found most frequently in patients with coarctation and should be specifically assessed on his imaging?", "options": {"A": "Ebstein malformation with apical displacement of the septal tricuspid leaflet", "B": "Congenitally corrected transposition with a systemic morphologic right ventricle", "C": "A secundum atrial septal defect with right ventricular volume overload", "D": "Anomalous origin of the left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery", "E": "A bicuspid aortic valve, present in roughly half of coarctation patients"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A bicuspid aortic valve, present in roughly half of coarctation patients): Bicuspid valves accompany coarctation in about fifty to seventy percent of cases and carry lifelong risks of stenosis, regurgitation, and aortopathy. This association mandates careful imaging of the valve and the ascending aorta. The other listed lesions occur independently and are not characteristic companions of coarctation."}, {"id": 89, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 77 year old man has heart failure symptoms, left ventricular wall thickness of 17 mm, bilateral carpal tunnel release five years ago, and lumbar spinal stenosis. His clinician suspects cardiac amyloidosis based on this cluster. Which electrocardiographic and echocardiographic combination is the classic red flag supporting this suspicion?", "options": {"A": "Low limb lead voltage on ECG despite increased ventricular wall thickness on echocardiography", "B": "Tall precordial voltage on the electrocardiogram matching the degree of ventricular wall thickening seen on imaging", "C": "Diffuse ST segment elevation on ECG with a small pericardial effusion on imaging", "D": "Delta waves on ECG with hyperdynamic systolic function and cavity obliteration", "E": "Peaked T waves on ECG with dilated thin walled ventricles on imaging"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Low limb lead voltage on ECG despite increased ventricular wall thickness on echocardiography): amyloid infiltration thickens the walls without adding electrical mass, so the discordance between an echocardiogram suggesting hypertrophy and an ECG showing low or normal voltage is the hallmark clue, especially alongside bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome and spinal stenosis. True hypertrophy from pressure overload usually produces tall voltage that matches the imaging. Diffuse ST elevation suggests pericarditis, delta waves indicate preexcitation, and dilated thin walled ventricles describe a different cardiomyopathy altogether."}, {"id": 90, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 49 year old man with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation asks about a pill in the pocket strategy using flecainide. Before prescribing a class Ic agent you review its major safety exclusion, which was established by a landmark arrhythmia suppression trial. In which patients are class Ic antiarrhythmic drugs avoided?", "options": {"A": "Patients with well controlled hypertension and no other cardiac history documented", "B": "Patients with isolated mitral valve prolapse and trivial regurgitation on echocardiography", "C": "Patients with prior myocardial infarction or other significant structural heart disease", "D": "Patients with lone atrial fibrillation occurring in a structurally normal heart", "E": "Patients taking a concomitant atrioventricular nodal blocking agent for rate control"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Patients with prior myocardial infarction or other significant structural heart disease): The CAST trial showed increased mortality when class Ic sodium channel blockers were given to patients after myocardial infarction, and this proarrhythmic risk extends to significant structural heart disease more broadly, so flecainide and propafenone are reserved for structurally normal hearts. Lone atrial fibrillation in a normal heart is precisely the setting where these agents are appropriate. Controlled hypertension without hypertrophy and trivial mitral prolapse are not exclusions, and pairing a class Ic drug with a nodal blocking agent is actually advised to prevent rapid conduction if flutter emerges."}, {"id": 92, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 22 year old woman with lifelong palpitations has echocardiography showing marked apical displacement of the septal tricuspid leaflet with a large atrialized portion of the right ventricle and severe tricuspid regurgitation. Beyond the valve disease itself, which electrophysiologic abnormality is classically associated with this congenital malformation?", "options": {"A": "Progressive infranodal block requiring pacemaker implantation early in adult life", "B": "Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia characteristically triggered by physical exertion or sudden emotional stress", "C": "Accessory atrioventricular pathways producing Wolff Parkinson White pattern preexcitation and tachycardia", "D": "A congenitally prolonged QT interval with pause dependent torsades de pointes", "E": "Sinus node dysfunction from surgical scarring within the atrial baffle pathways"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Accessory atrioventricular pathways producing Wolff Parkinson White pattern preexcitation and tachycardia): Ebstein anomaly is the congenital lesion most strongly linked to accessory pathways, which are often right sided and sometimes multiple. Reentrant tachycardia through these pathways explains her palpitations and should prompt electrophysiologic evaluation. Progressive block characterizes congenitally corrected transposition, and baffle related sinus node dysfunction follows atrial switch surgery."}, {"id": 94, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "During a hospital quality conference, a nurse practitioner is asked to state the biomarker criterion within the fourth universal definition of acute myocardial infarction. The definition pairs myocardial injury with clinical evidence of ischemia. Which biomarker pattern is required before acute myocardial infarction can be diagnosed?", "options": {"A": "A single troponin value above the assay limit of detection on arrival", "B": "Any measurable troponin elevation persisting unchanged across several days of serial testing", "C": "Creatine kinase MB elevation exceeding twice the total creatine kinase activity measured", "D": "A brain natriuretic peptide level exceeding the age adjusted diagnostic threshold value", "E": "A rise or fall of troponin with at least one value above the 99th percentile"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A rise or fall of troponin with at least one value above the 99th percentile): the universal definition requires a dynamic troponin change with at least one value above the 99th percentile upper reference limit plus evidence of ischemia. A single detectable value or a flat persistent elevation indicates injury without proving an acute event. Creatine kinase MB and natriuretic peptides are not the required biomarkers in the current definition."}, {"id": 95, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 71 year old woman with end stage kidney disease on hemodialysis reports two hours of nonexertional chest wall soreness. Her electrocardiogram is unchanged from prior tracings. Serial high sensitivity troponin measurements appear in the laboratory results shown. Which interpretation of these troponin values is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "An acute myocardial infarction proven by two values above the reference limit", "B": "Assay interference that mandates repeating both troponin measurements promptly on a different analytical platform", "C": "Unstable angina, because the electrocardiogram shows no new ischemic changes", "D": "Chronic myocardial injury, shown by elevated values without a significant serial change", "E": "A laboratory error, because kidney disease should not affect troponin concentrations"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chronic myocardial injury, shown by elevated values without a significant serial change): patients on dialysis commonly have stable troponin elevation, and the flat pattern between measurements indicates chronic injury rather than an acute event. Acute infarction requires a significant rise or fall, not simply two elevated values. Unstable angina requires troponin below the reference limit, and kidney disease raises troponin through reduced clearance and chronic injury rather than assay error.", "image": "question_images/q_95.png"}, {"id": 97, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 61 year old woman has a left ventricular ejection fraction of 38 percent discovered on an echocardiogram ordered to evaluate an incidental murmur. She reports no dyspnea, fatigue, or edema either now or at any previous time. Which ACC/AHA stage matches this presentation, based on its formal guideline definition?", "options": {"A": "Stage C, because a reduced ejection fraction by itself establishes clinically symptomatic heart failure disease", "B": "Stage A, because she remains asymptomatic no matter what the imaging shows", "C": "Stage B, defined by structural heart disease without current or prior heart failure symptoms", "D": "Stage D, because an ejection fraction below 40 percent indicates advanced disease", "E": "Stage C, because subclinical congestion is assumed once the ejection fraction falls"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stage B, defined by structural heart disease without current or prior heart failure symptoms): a reduced ejection fraction in a patient who has never had symptoms is the classic pre heart failure, stage B, presentation. Stage C requires current or prior symptoms, which she has never experienced, and stage D describes refractory symptoms despite maximal therapy. Stage A would require the absence of any structural abnormality, which her imaging rules out."}, {"id": 98, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "An asymptomatic 72 year old man has severe aortic stenosis with a jet velocity of 4.2 m/s and preserved ventricular function, and he asks how often his valve should be reimaged. Per guideline surveillance recommendations, which echocardiographic follow up interval is appropriate for asymptomatic severe aortic stenosis?", "options": {"A": "Every 3 to 5 years as long as he remains free of symptoms", "B": "Every 6 to 12 months, with earlier imaging for any change in symptoms", "C": "Every 5 years alternating between echocardiography and cardiac magnetic resonance imaging", "D": "Once more at one year, then no imaging until symptoms clearly develop", "E": "Every 2 to 3 years combined with annual exercise treadmill testing for screening"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Every 6 to 12 months, with earlier imaging for any change in symptoms): severe asymptomatic aortic stenosis progresses unpredictably, so guidelines recommend echocardiography every 6 to 12 months to track velocity, gradient, and ventricular function, and any new symptom prompts immediate reassessment. Intervals of 3 to 5 years are reserved for mild disease, and 1 to 2 years for moderate disease. Stopping surveillance until symptoms declare themselves risks missing ventricular decompensation, which is itself an indication for intervention in severe stenosis."}, {"id": 100, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A colleague asks you to review the safety counseling given to patients starting angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors, focusing specifically on angioedema. You want the teaching points to reflect the epidemiology and clinical behavior of this reaction as described in current cardiovascular pharmacology references. Which statement about this adverse effect is accurate?", "options": {"A": "Histamine driven mast cell degranulation is the principal mediator of the swelling", "B": "Antihistamines with corticosteroids provide definitive treatment once airway swelling has begun", "C": "The reaction can first appear months or years after starting the medication", "D": "White patients experience this complication at substantially higher rates than Black patients", "E": "Changing to a different agent within the same class prevents recurrent episodes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The reaction can first appear months or years after starting the medication): Angioedema from this class is bradykinin mediated and can surface at any point in therapy, including long after an uneventful start, which is why counseling cannot stop at the first refill. Because histamine is not the driver, antihistamines and corticosteroids work poorly, and management centers on stopping the drug and protecting the airway. Black patients have higher rates than White patients, and the reaction is a class effect, so switching within the class is unsafe."}, {"id": 101, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A pharmacy resident is organizing antiarrhythmic drugs by the Vaughan Williams classification and asks where amiodarone, sotalol, and dofetilide primarily belong, and what that drug class does at the cellular level. Which statement correctly identifies the class and its principal electrophysiologic action on cardiac tissue?", "options": {"A": "Class I, drugs that block sodium channels and slow phase zero depolarization", "B": "Class II, drugs that antagonize beta receptors and slow sinus node discharge", "C": "Class IV, drugs that block calcium channels and thereby slow conduction through the atrioventricular node", "D": "Class III, drugs that block potassium channels and prolong repolarization and refractoriness", "E": "Class V, a grouping for agents with mixed or otherwise unclassified actions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Class III, drugs that block potassium channels and prolong repolarization and refractoriness): Amiodarone, sotalol, dofetilide, and ibutilide are classified as class III agents because their dominant shared action is potassium channel blockade, which lengthens the action potential duration, the refractory period, and the QT interval. Sodium channel blockade with slowed phase zero defines class I, beta receptor antagonism defines class II, and calcium channel blockade defines class IV. Amiodarone and sotalol each carry additional properties from other classes, but their primary classification rests on repolarization prolongation."}, {"id": 102, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "An echocardiography report describes a circumferential pericardial effusion in a 52 year old woman, and you must classify its size to guide the intensity of further workup and follow up imaging. By echocardiographic measurement of the diastolic echo free space, which finding defines a large pericardial effusion?", "options": {"A": "A circumferential effusion visible in both systole and diastole on subcostal imaging", "B": "An echo free space measuring between 5 and 10 millimeters in diastole", "C": "An effusion that produces visible swinging motion of the heart within the sac", "D": "An echo free space of 10 to 20 millimeters measured during diastole", "E": "An echo free diastolic space measuring greater than 20 millimeters in width"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (An echo free diastolic space measuring greater than 20 millimeters in width): standard grading calls an effusion mild below 10 millimeters, moderate from 10 to 20 millimeters, and large above 20 millimeters, measured as the diastolic echo free space. The 5 to 10 and 10 to 20 millimeter ranges therefore describe mild and moderate effusions. Mere circumferential visibility does not size an effusion, and a swinging heart suggests hemodynamic significance rather than defining the size category."}, {"id": 104, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 65 year old man with nonischemic cardiomyopathy remains symptomatic in NYHA class II despite optimal medical therapy. His ejection fraction is 30 percent and his ECG shows sinus rhythm with a markedly widened QRS complex. Which electrocardiographic combination provides the strongest indication for cardiac resynchronization therapy?", "options": {"A": "QRS ≥120 milliseconds with a right bundle branch block morphology on ECG", "B": "QRS ≥150 milliseconds with a left bundle branch block morphology on ECG", "C": "QRS ≥110 milliseconds with a nonspecific intraventricular conduction delay pattern", "D": "Any QRS width once the ejection fraction has fallen below 35 percent", "E": "PR prolongation ≥240 milliseconds accompanied by an otherwise narrow QRS complex"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (QRS ≥150 milliseconds with a left bundle branch block morphology on ECG): The class I indication for resynchronization requires an ejection fraction of 35 percent or less, persistent symptoms on optimal therapy, sinus rhythm, and a left bundle branch block with a QRS of at least 150 milliseconds, the combination in which trials showed the clearest benefit. Right bundle branch block and nonspecific conduction delay patterns derive less benefit and carry weaker recommendations. Ejection fraction alone without QRS prolongation is not an indication, and isolated PR prolongation with a narrow QRS does not create dyssynchrony that resynchronization corrects."}, {"id": 105, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A cardiology physician assistant is reviewing injectable diabetes therapies for a 62 year old woman with type 2 diabetes and a prior stroke. She asks which glucagon like peptide 1 receptor agonists carry proven atherosclerotic benefit. Which set of agents demonstrated reduced major adverse cardiovascular events in their outcome trials?", "options": {"A": "Liraglutide, subcutaneous semaglutide, and dulaglutide, each tested in large randomized trials", "B": "Exenatide twice daily combined with basal insulin in observational registry cohorts", "C": "Lixisenatide, which was evaluated primarily in patients after acute coronary syndrome", "D": "Any agent belonging to the broader incretin family, including the oral dipeptidyl peptidase 4 inhibitors", "E": "Short acting exenatide, based on pooled analyses of small glycemic studies"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Liraglutide, subcutaneous semaglutide, and dulaglutide, each tested in large randomized trials): LEADER, SUSTAIN-6, and REWIND respectively showed these three agents lower major adverse cardiovascular events, driving guideline preference in atherosclerotic disease. Lixisenatide and short acting exenatide were neutral in their trials, so they carry no comparable indication. Dipeptidyl peptidase 4 inhibitors have not reduced cardiovascular events, and registry data on exenatide with insulin cannot establish outcome benefit."}, {"id": 108, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 47 year old woman has echocardiographic findings suggesting pulmonary hypertension, and her clinician is arranging the next diagnostic step before any therapy is chosen. What role does right heart catheterization play in the contemporary evaluation of suspected pulmonary arterial hypertension?", "options": {"A": "It is the required confirmatory study, since echocardiography estimates rather than measures pulmonary hemodynamics", "B": "It is reserved for patients who have already failed two oral therapy classes", "C": "It is interchangeable with cardiac magnetic resonance for establishing the diagnosis", "D": "It is chiefly a research oriented procedure carrying negligible practical bearing on routine contemporary treatment decisions", "E": "It is needed solely to place a monitoring catheter before initiating diuretics"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is the required confirmatory study, since echocardiography estimates rather than measures pulmonary hemodynamics): guidelines mandate right heart catheterization before diagnosing pulmonary arterial hypertension because direct measurement of mean pressure, wedge pressure, resistance, and output defines the hemodynamic subtype and anchors therapy, while echocardiographic pressure estimates can err in either direction. Delaying the study until oral therapies fail reverses the correct order, since treatment should never start on an unconfirmed diagnosis. Magnetic resonance characterizes the right ventricle but cannot replace pressure measurement, and catheterization has nothing to do with diuretic initiation."}, {"id": 109, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 21 year old man referred for a systolic murmur undergoes dynamic auscultation and echocardiography, with the key findings summarized in the exhibit shown. Integrating the maneuver responses with the imaging data, which diagnosis best explains the murmur described in this exhibit?", "options": {"A": "Calcific valvular obstruction with a fixed gradient and delayed carotid upstroke", "B": "Myxomatous mitral prolapse producing a click and a late systolic murmur", "C": "Dynamic subvalvular outflow obstruction caused by asymmetric septal thickening of the ventricle", "D": "A membranous subaortic ridge producing a fixed outflow gradient beneath an anatomically normal aortic valve", "E": "Supravalvular narrowing of the ascending aorta with unequal arm blood pressures"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dynamic subvalvular outflow obstruction caused by asymmetric septal thickening of the ventricle): a murmur that intensifies with standing and Valsalva strain yet softens with squatting and handgrip identifies obstruction that worsens when the ventricle is small, and the thickened septum with systolic anterior motion and a labile gradient completes the picture of hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy. Fixed valvular, subvalvular membrane, and supravalvular obstructions all soften when preload falls because flow decreases across a constant orifice. Prolapse produces a click murmur complex at the apex rather than a harsh sternal border ejection murmur, and no click is described here.", "image": "question_images/q_109.png"}, {"id": 115, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 79 year old woman describes lightheadedness when rising from bed each morning. You plan orthostatic vital signs and want to apply the standard consensus definition of orthostatic hypotension when interpreting the numbers your nurse records at the bedside. Which blood pressure change after standing meets that accepted diagnostic definition?", "options": {"A": "Any symptomatic fall in systolic pressure within ten minutes of arising", "B": "A fall of ≥20 mm Hg systolic or ≥10 diastolic within three minutes", "C": "A fall of ≥30 mm Hg systolic sustained for at least five minutes", "D": "A heart rate rise above 30 beats per minute upon standing upright", "E": "A systolic value below 100 mm Hg at any point after standing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A fall of ≥20 mm Hg systolic or ≥10 diastolic within three minutes): The consensus definition requires a sustained drop of at least 20 mm Hg systolic or 10 mm Hg diastolic within three minutes of standing, regardless of symptoms. Symptoms alone without the numeric fall, or arbitrary absolute values such as 100 mm Hg, do not define the condition. A heart rate rise above 30 beats per minute without hypotension defines postural tachycardia syndrome instead."}, {"id": 118, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 34 year old woman with menorrhagia and a hemoglobin of 6.2 g/dL presents with exertional dyspnea, bounding pulses, and a flow murmur. Her clinician explains that severe anemia can produce a distinctive form of heart failure. What hemodynamic pattern defines high output heart failure in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Reduced cardiac output with elevated systemic vascular resistance and cold extremities", "B": "Normal cardiac output with impaired relaxation and elevated left atrial pressure", "C": "Reduced cardiac output caused by primary myocardial injury from iron excess", "D": "Elevated cardiac output with low systemic vascular resistance and congestive symptoms", "E": "Fixed low stroke volume with compensatory tachycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Elevated cardiac output with low systemic vascular resistance and congestive symptoms): severe anemia lowers blood viscosity and tissue oxygen delivery, so vasodilation and reflex increases in stroke volume and heart rate raise output while congestion develops despite a hyperdynamic circulation. Cold extremities with high resistance and low output describe cardiogenic shock physiology, and impaired relaxation with normal output describes diastolic dysfunction. Iron excess injuring the myocardium is a feature of hemochromatosis, not of anemia driven high output states."}, {"id": 120, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 63 year old woman reports 30 minutes of rest angina that resolved before arrival. Her electrocardiogram shows 1 mm ST depression in leads V4 through V6 without ST elevation. Serial high sensitivity troponin values are obtained as displayed in the laboratory results shown. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Non ST elevation myocardial infarction with a dynamic rise in troponin values", "B": "Unstable angina with rest symptoms and biomarkers remaining below the decision threshold", "C": "ST elevation myocardial infarction requiring immediate activation of the catheterization laboratory", "D": "Stable angina from a fixed obstructive plaque producing predictable exertional symptoms", "E": "Acute pericarditis producing diffuse inflammation without any myocardial cellular injury"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Non ST elevation myocardial infarction with a dynamic rise in troponin values): the rising troponin pattern with ischemic ST depression and no ST elevation defines this diagnosis. Unstable angina requires troponin values that stay below the 99th percentile, which these results exclude. ST elevation infarction is excluded by the electrocardiogram, while stable angina and pericarditis do not produce this dynamic biomarker rise.", "image": "question_images/q_120.png"}, {"id": 123, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 66 year old man reports dizziness and blurred vision that appear reproducibly while he uses his left arm to wash windows overhead. His left brachial systolic pressure measures 35 points lower than the right. Which mechanism most likely produces his neurologic symptoms during this kind of arm activity?", "options": {"A": "Embolization of plaque debris from a left subclavian lesion into the posterior cerebral circulation", "B": "Compression of the vertebral artery by cervical osteophytes during neck rotation", "C": "Orthostatic hypotension unmasked by exertion in the setting of antihypertensive therapy", "D": "Retrograde vertebral artery flow diverting blood from the brainstem to the exercising arm", "E": "Carotid sinus hypersensitivity triggered by repetitive overhead positioning of the arm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Retrograde vertebral artery flow diverting blood from the brainstem to the exercising arm): a proximal subclavian stenosis upstream of the vertebral origin causes the exercising arm to recruit blood retrograde down the ipsilateral vertebral artery, stealing flow from the posterior circulation and producing vertebrobasilar symptoms. The interarm pressure gradient localizes the obstruction, and symptoms tied specifically to arm work distinguish steal from embolization, which occurs unpredictably. Osteophyte compression relates to neck position rather than arm use, and neither orthostasis nor carotid sinus sensitivity explains the pressure differential."}, {"id": 124, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 72 year old woman with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction has a mean pulmonary arterial pressure of 33 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 21 mmHg. Her son asks why she is not receiving the pulmonary arterial hypertension drugs he read about. What is the guiding treatment principle for her condition?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary vasodilators are first line once the mean pressure exceeds thirty", "B": "Therapy targets the underlying left heart disease, because pulmonary arterial hypertension drugs can worsen congestion", "C": "Endothelin antagonists are preferred over diuretics for postcapillary physiology", "D": "Combination oral pulmonary vasodilator therapy should begin promptly before loop diuretic doses are meticulously titrated upward", "E": "Prostacyclin infusion offers the best survival benefit in this hemodynamic pattern"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Therapy targets the underlying left heart disease, because pulmonary arterial hypertension drugs can worsen congestion): her wedge pressure above 15 mmHg marks group 2 postcapillary disease, where trials of endothelin antagonists, phosphodiesterase inhibitors, and prostanoids have shown no benefit and sometimes harm, since dilating the pulmonary bed increases flow into a congested left heart. Management therefore centers on decongestion, blood pressure control, and treating the preserved ejection fraction syndrome itself. Pressure magnitude alone does not trigger vasodilator use, and none of the vasodilator strategies listed is appropriate here."}, {"id": 127, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 69 year old man with a 4.8 centimeter abdominal aortic aneurysm returns for scheduled duplex surveillance imaging. Comparison with the study performed six months earlier shows clear interval enlargement of the sac. What magnitude of growth over a six month period is generally accepted as an indication for elective repair?", "options": {"A": "Expansion greater than 0.5 centimeters within six months, even below the diameter threshold", "B": "Any measurable sac enlargement confirmed on two consecutive surveillance imaging studies performed three months apart", "C": "Growth exceeding one full centimeter accumulated across five years of surveillance", "D": "Enlargement greater than 0.3 centimeters within one year of continued observation", "E": "Growth sufficient to reach eighty percent of the diameter threshold for men"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Expansion greater than 0.5 centimeters within six months, even below the diameter threshold): expansion exceeding 0.5 centimeters over six months marks a biologically unstable aneurysm with elevated rupture risk, so rapid growth is an accepted repair indication independent of reaching 5.5 centimeters. Small measurement differences between studies often reflect technique variability, so minor enlargement or slow growth over years does not justify surgery. A fixed fraction of the diameter threshold has no role in timing repair decisions."}, {"id": 128, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 48 year old man is newly diagnosed with nonischemic dilated cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 25 percent. His cardiology team starts all four pillar medications and discusses possible device therapy at a future visit. Before offering a primary prevention ICD, which step does the guideline direct in this scenario?", "options": {"A": "Implant promptly, since nonischemic disease carries the highest early arrhythmic risk", "B": "Reassess ejection fraction after at least 3 months of guideline directed medical therapy", "C": "Obtain genetic testing first, since results determine defibrillator eligibility in nonischemic disease", "D": "Perform an electrophysiology study to document inducible ventricular tachycardia before implantation", "E": "Order a myocardial biopsy to exclude sarcoidosis before any device decision is made"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reassess ejection fraction after at least 3 months of guideline directed medical therapy): newly diagnosed nonischemic cardiomyopathy often improves substantially on the four pillars, so the guideline requires a trial of optimized therapy, generally at least 3 months, with repeat imaging before committing to a primary prevention device. Immediate implantation would expose many patients whose ejection fraction recovers to unnecessary hardware. Electrophysiology studies do not reliably risk stratify nonischemic disease, and neither genetic testing nor biopsy is a mandatory gate, although selected findings such as lamin variants can influence decisions."}, {"id": 130, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 45 year old man with a body mass index of 44 presents with orthopnea, elevated jugular venous pressure, and an audible S3 gallop, yet his BNP measures only 62 pg/mL. The certification exam emphasizes natriuretic peptide confounders. Which statement best explains this apparently discordant natriuretic peptide result?", "options": {"A": "Obesity lowers measured natriuretic peptide levels, so true heart failure may be underrecognized", "B": "A BNP below 100 pg/mL makes heart failure an unlikely explanation for his symptoms", "C": "Constrictive physiology suppresses peptide release, suggesting pericardial disease rather than myocardial disease", "D": "Laboratory interference from lipemia falsely lowered the reported natriuretic peptide concentration measured", "E": "Young age suppresses ventricular peptide synthesis until roughly the sixth decade of life"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Obesity lowers measured natriuretic peptide levels, so true heart failure may be underrecognized): obese patients have lower circulating natriuretic peptide concentrations, likely through reduced production and increased clearance, so a value below the usual rule out threshold cannot exclude heart failure when the examination shows clear congestion. His bedside findings make heart failure likely despite the number. Pericardial constriction is not suggested by an S3 with orthopnea in a patient with severe obesity, lipemia interference is not a recognized cause of this pattern, and age does not suppress peptide synthesis."}, {"id": 131, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 45 year old man with a decrescendo early diastolic murmur at the left sternal border has a blood pressure of 160/50 mmHg. Which hemodynamic mechanism best explains the markedly widened pulse pressure observed in patients with chronic severe aortic regurgitation?", "options": {"A": "Elevated systolic pressure caused by reflex sinus tachycardia together with increased circulating catecholamine concentrations", "B": "Reduced diastolic pressure from concurrent systemic vasodilation caused by chronic medication effects", "C": "Increased systolic pressure from aortic wall stiffening with preserved diastolic aortic recoil", "D": "Low diastolic pressure produced by rapid ventricular relaxation and impaired atrial emptying", "E": "Large stroke volume raising systolic pressure while diastolic runoff into the ventricle lowers diastolic pressure"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Large stroke volume raising systolic pressure while diastolic runoff into the ventricle lowers diastolic pressure): the volume loaded ventricle ejects an augmented stroke volume, elevating systolic pressure, and regurgitation back through the incompetent valve drains the aorta during diastole. This combination produces the wide pulse pressure and the bounding peripheral pulses that underlie the classic named signs. Catecholamine excess and medication related vasodilation are not the primary valve mediated mechanisms. Isolated aortic stiffening raises systolic pressure in elderly hypertension but does not create the profound diastolic runoff seen here."}, {"id": 133, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 66 year old man presents with chest pressure, bradycardia, and hypotension. His electrocardiogram demonstrates 2 mm ST segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with reciprocal depression in lead aVL. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded as the culprit vessel in this infarction?", "options": {"A": "The right coronary artery, which supplies the inferior wall in most patients", "B": "The left anterior descending artery, which supplies the anterior wall and septum", "C": "The left main coronary artery, which supplies most of the left ventricle", "D": "The ramus intermedius branch, which supplies a variable anterolateral myocardial territory", "E": "The obtuse marginal branch, which supplies the mid portion of the lateral wall"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The right coronary artery, which supplies the inferior wall in most patients): elevation in leads II, III, and aVF localizes injury to the inferior wall, which the right coronary artery perfuses in roughly 85 percent of people. The accompanying bradycardia fits this vessel because it usually supplies the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes. Left anterior descending and left main occlusions produce anterior or widespread precordial changes, while obtuse marginal and ramus occlusions produce lateral patterns."}, {"id": 134, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 31 year old woman with mitral valve prolapse performs the strain phase of the Valsalva maneuver while you auscultate her midsystolic click and late systolic murmur at the apex. During the strain phase, which change in the timing of her click do you expect?", "options": {"A": "The click moves later in systole because the ventricle fills more completely", "B": "The click is replaced by a diastolic opening sound as pressures change", "C": "The click arrives earlier in systole as the smaller ventricle prolapses sooner", "D": "The click merges with the second heart sound and becomes difficult to distinguish", "E": "The click doubles as both leaflets prolapse at separate moments during the strain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The click arrives earlier in systole as the smaller ventricle prolapses sooner): Valsalva strain lowers venous return, and in a smaller ventricle the chordae and redundant leaflet reach their tension point earlier in ejection, moving the click toward S1 and lengthening the murmur that follows it. A later click reflects maneuvers that enlarge the ventricle, such as squatting or passive leg raising. The click is a systolic event and does not convert to an opening sound, which belongs to mitral stenosis. Movement is toward the first heart sound rather than into S2, and a doubled click is not the expected maneuver response."}, {"id": 135, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "During examination of a patient with chronic severe aortic regurgitation, you palpate the radial artery while raising the arm and feel a forceful rapid upstroke that collapses abruptly. Which term is traditionally used for this characteristic peripheral pulse of hemodynamically significant aortic regurgitation?", "options": {"A": "The water hammer pulse, also called the Corrigan pulse in classic descriptions", "B": "Pulsus parvus et tardus, a small volume pulse with a delayed peak", "C": "Pulsus paradoxus, an inspiratory fall in systolic pressure exceeding 10 mmHg", "D": "Pulsus alternans, in which strong and weak beats alternate with regular rhythm", "E": "The dicrotic pulse, with a palpable second wave occurring during early diastole"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The water hammer pulse, also called the Corrigan pulse in classic descriptions): the augmented stroke volume produces a slapping rapid upstroke, and diastolic runoff through the incompetent valve causes the abrupt collapse, a combination accentuated by elevating the arm. Pulsus parvus et tardus is the weak delayed pulse of severe aortic stenosis, the hemodynamic opposite. Pulsus paradoxus points toward tamponade or severe airway obstruction, and pulsus alternans indicates advanced ventricular systolic failure. A dicrotic pulse with a palpable diastolic wave occurs in low output states with compliant vessels rather than regurgitant runoff."}, {"id": 136, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 52 year old woman reports a fluttering sensation and intermittent skipped beats at rest, worse after coffee. Her examination and echocardiogram are unremarkable. You review the rhythm strip shown, obtained while she was experiencing her typical symptoms. Which rhythm diagnosis best describes the repeating pattern on this tracing?", "options": {"A": "Atrial bigeminy with premature P waves and narrow early QRS complexes", "B": "Second degree atrioventricular block producing regularly grouped conducted sinus beats", "C": "Intermittent bundle branch aberrancy alternating with normally conducted sinus beats", "D": "Ventricular bigeminy with a premature wide complex following each sinus beat", "E": "Atrial fibrillation with an irregular response and occasional aberrantly conducted beats"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ventricular bigeminy with a premature wide complex following each sinus beat): The strip shows an alternating pattern in which every conducted sinus beat is followed by an early, wide, bizarre complex without a preceding premature P wave, the definition of ventricular bigeminy. Atrial bigeminy also alternates but produces narrow early complexes preceded by premature P waves. Grouped beating from second degree block features dropped beats rather than added early ones, aberrancy would follow premature atrial activity, and atrial fibrillation would show an irregularly irregular rhythm without a repeating paired pattern.", "image": "question_images/q_136.png"}, {"id": 137, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 74 year old man with an ejection fraction of 28 percent has an aortic valve area of 0.9 cm² but a mean gradient of only 24 mmHg. Which purpose does low dose dobutamine stress echocardiography serve in evaluating this pattern of suspected aortic stenosis?", "options": {"A": "It measures coronary flow reserve to determine whether concurrent bypass surgery is also clinically warranted", "B": "It provokes coronary vasospasm to exclude an ischemic cause of ventricular dysfunction", "C": "It distinguishes truly severe stenosis from pseudosevere disease by augmenting forward flow", "D": "It quantifies mitral regurgitation severity under stress before valve surgery is planned", "E": "It assesses exercise capacity in patients unable to perform a standard treadmill protocol"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It distinguishes truly severe stenosis from pseudosevere disease by augmenting forward flow): in classical low flow, low gradient stenosis with reduced ejection fraction, dobutamine increases stroke volume, and a truly severe valve shows a rising gradient with a persistently small orifice while a pseudosevere valve opens further as flow improves. The test also identifies contractile reserve, which informs operative risk discussion. It is not designed to measure coronary flow reserve or provoke vasospasm, which involve different pharmacologic protocols. Stress evaluation of mitral regurgitation and functional capacity testing are separate indications that do not address this diagnostic question."}, {"id": 140, "domain": "normal_cardiovascular_anatomy_and_physiology", "question": "A pharmacology student wants to understand why beta blockers relieve angina, and you begin by reviewing what drives myocardial oxygen consumption in the intact heart. Which set of factors constitutes the principal determinants of myocardial oxygen demand in a beating human heart under physiologic conditions?", "options": {"A": "Hemoglobin concentration, arterial oxygen saturation, and the coronary perfusion pressure", "B": "Serum catecholamine clearance, thyroid hormone level, and resting membrane potential", "C": "Diastolic filling time, coronary collateral density, and capillary oxygen extraction", "D": "Heart rate, contractility, and ventricular wall tension are the principal determinants", "E": "Blood viscosity, mean airway pressure, and pulmonary capillary transit time"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Heart rate, contractility, and ventricular wall tension are the principal determinants): oxygen demand rises with faster rates, stronger inotropy, and greater wall stress, which itself scales with chamber pressure and radius by the Laplace relationship, and beta blockade lowers all three. Hemoglobin, saturation, and perfusion pressure govern oxygen supply rather than demand. Diastolic filling time and collaterals also belong to the supply side, catecholamine clearance and thyroid state act only through the primary determinants, and the viscosity based option lists no cardiac demand variable."}, {"id": 141, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 44 year old woman treated with mediastinal radiation for Hodgkin lymphoma at age 24 presents with exertional angina. Her only conventional risk factor is treated hypertension. Which pattern of coronary involvement is most characteristic of radiation associated coronary artery disease in long term cancer survivors like this patient?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse distal small vessel disease characteristically sparing the proximal segments and the coronary ostia", "B": "Isolated right coronary artery spasm without fixed stenosis on provocative testing", "C": "Aneurysmal dilation of all three epicardial vessels with slow antegrade flow", "D": "Ostial and proximal stenoses, often involving the left main, appearing years after exposure", "E": "Myocardial bridging acquired from radiation induced fibrosis of the overlying epicardium"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ostial and proximal stenoses, often involving the left main, appearing years after exposure): radiation injures vessels within the treated field, producing ostial and proximal stenoses, frequent left main involvement, and a latency of a decade or more after exposure. The disease is proximal rather than distal small vessel, is fibrotic and fixed rather than spastic, and does not present as triple vessel ectasia or acquired bridging. Young survivors can therefore develop surgical anatomy despite few conventional risk factors."}, {"id": 146, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 64 year old man with lifestyle limiting claudication asks about medication options after starting a structured walking program and receiving guideline based vascular risk factor treatment. His cardiologist considers adding cilostazol to improve his pain free walking distance. Which comorbid condition would make prescribing cilostazol inappropriate for this patient under current labeling?", "options": {"A": "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease treated with a long acting inhaled bronchodilator regimen", "B": "Stage three chronic kidney disease with an estimated glomerular filtration rate near forty milliliters per minute", "C": "Well controlled type two diabetes mellitus managed with metformin and lifestyle measures", "D": "Prior ischemic stroke with residual mild weakness on single antiplatelet therapy", "E": "Heart failure of any severity, given increased mortality seen with related phosphodiesterase inhibitors"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Heart failure of any severity, given increased mortality seen with related phosphodiesterase inhibitors): cilostazol is a phosphodiesterase three inhibitor, and oral agents of this class increased mortality in patients with heart failure, so the drug carries a boxed contraindication in heart failure of any severity. Pulmonary disease, moderate kidney disease, diabetes, and prior stroke do not preclude its use, although dose adjustment or caution may apply in some situations. Screening for a heart failure history is therefore a required step before this medication is prescribed."}, {"id": 149, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "During vasoreactivity testing with inhaled nitric oxide, a 36 year old woman with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension shows a hemodynamic change the team must interpret. Which result defines an acute positive vasoreactivity response under accepted criteria and identifies a potential calcium channel blocker candidate?", "options": {"A": "Any visible fall in pulmonary pressure accompanied by subjective improvement in dyspnea", "B": "A 20 percent fall in pulmonary vascular resistance with heart rate unchanged", "C": "Normalization of the wedge pressure while the mean pulmonary pressure stays constant", "D": "A rise in cardiac output exceeding one liter per minute at constant pressure", "E": "A mean pressure fall of ≥10 mmHg to below 40 mmHg with preserved cardiac output"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A mean pressure fall of ≥10 mmHg to below 40 mmHg with preserved cardiac output): the accepted positive response requires the mean pulmonary arterial pressure to drop by at least 10 mmHg, reach an absolute value under 40 mmHg, and do so with unchanged or improved output, identifying the small group who may respond durably to calcium channel blockade. Symptom change and modest resistance falls do not meet the definition. Wedge pressure behavior is not part of the criteria, and an isolated output rise without the required pressure fall does not qualify."}, {"id": 151, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 78 year old woman with a non ST elevation acute coronary syndrome has a calculated creatinine clearance of 22 mL/min. The admitting team plans to use enoxaparin rather than unfractionated heparin. Which dosing adjustment does severe renal impairment require for therapeutic enoxaparin in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Increase the dose to 1.5 mg/kg twice daily to overcome impaired absorption", "B": "No adjustment, because low molecular weight heparins undergo exclusively hepatic clearance", "C": "Reduce dosing to 1 mg/kg once daily when creatinine clearance falls below 30 mL/min", "D": "Switch to fondaparinux, which is preferred over heparins in severe renal failure", "E": "Add routine anti factor Xa monitoring while continuing the standard twice daily dose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce dosing to 1 mg/kg once daily when creatinine clearance falls below 30 mL/min): enoxaparin is renally cleared and accumulates in severe impairment, so the therapeutic dose drops from twice daily to once daily below this clearance threshold. Increasing the dose or continuing the standard regimen with monitoring alone invites bleeding from accumulation. Fondaparinux is itself contraindicated at this level of renal dysfunction, so switching to it would not be safe."}, {"id": 152, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 47 year old untreated man has office blood pressure averaging 122/78 mm Hg, yet seven days of duplicate morning and evening home readings average 137/88 mm Hg with a validated upper arm device. He smokes and has a strong family history of stroke. Which term best describes this blood pressure pattern?", "options": {"A": "Masked hypertension, normal office readings with elevated out of office averages", "B": "White coat hypertension, raised office readings with reassuring home measurement averages", "C": "Pseudohypertension, calcified arteries producing cuff values higher than intra arterial pressure", "D": "Elevated blood pressure, a category defined by the office measurements alone", "E": "Nocturnal nondipping, loss of the expected overnight decline in blood pressure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Masked hypertension, normal office readings with elevated out of office averages): His office values look acceptable while a properly conducted week of home monitoring shows sustained elevation, which defines masked hypertension. This pattern carries cardiovascular risk similar to sustained hypertension and is easy to miss when decisions rest on office numbers alone. White coat hypertension is the mirror image, pseudohypertension involves rigid calcified vessels, and nondipping describes nocturnal ambulatory behavior rather than this office versus home discrepancy."}, {"id": 154, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 34 year old man is referred after a systolic murmur is heard at a screening physical, and echocardiography shows asymmetric septal thickening without another identifiable cardiac or systemic cause. His clinician recalls the diagnostic threshold for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy in adults. Which wall thickness finding establishes this diagnosis in an adult patient?", "options": {"A": "Unexplained wall thickness of 11 mm at any single ventricular site in adults", "B": "Unexplained left ventricular wall thickness of 15 mm or more in an adult", "C": "Wall thickness of 20 mm or more, since lesser values reflect athletic remodeling", "D": "Concentric thickening of 13 mm accompanied by longstanding treated systemic hypertension", "E": "Right ventricular free wall thickness above 5 mm with preserved septal dimensions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Unexplained left ventricular wall thickness of 15 mm or more in an adult): hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is diagnosed when maximal left ventricular wall thickness reaches 15 mm without another cause such as hypertension or aortic stenosis, and a lower threshold of 13 to 14 mm applies when a family history or a pathogenic sarcomere variant is present. A measurement of 11 mm sits within normal limits. Thickening explained by hypertension does not qualify, 20 mm is not the diagnostic floor, and right ventricular measurements are not the defining criterion."}, {"id": 157, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "You are reviewing medication lists to identify patients at greatest risk of hyperkalemia from antihypertensive therapy. Several regimens include agents acting on the renin angiotensin aldosterone system, sometimes combined with other drugs or with chronic kidney disease. Which clinical combination carries the highest risk of a dangerous potassium rise?", "options": {"A": "Amlodipine with chlorthalidone in a patient who has normal renal function", "B": "Losartan alone in a young patient with an estimated filtration rate above 90", "C": "Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate in a patient with reduced ejection fraction", "D": "Lisinopril plus spironolactone with regular ibuprofen use in stage 3 kidney disease", "E": "Metoprolol with hydrochlorothiazide in a patient taking a daily proton pump inhibitor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lisinopril plus spironolactone with regular ibuprofen use in stage 3 kidney disease): This regimen stacks four independent potassium raising influences, angiotensin blockade, aldosterone antagonism, prostaglandin inhibition that suppresses renin and reduces renal perfusion, and impaired baseline excretion from kidney disease. Diuretic containing combinations tend to lower potassium instead. Losartan alone with excellent filtration, vasodilator combinations, and beta blockade with a thiazide each pose far smaller risks of a dangerous rise."}, {"id": 158, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 52 year old man with chronic severe aortic regurgitation demonstrates rhythmic flushing and blanching at the nail beds when gentle pressure is applied to the nail tip. When documenting this classic peripheral manifestation of the lesion, which eponymous physical sign are you describing?", "options": {"A": "Quincke sign, which reflects transmission of the exaggerated pulse into the capillary circulation", "B": "de Musset sign, rhythmic head bobbing reflecting transmission of the hyperdynamic arterial pulse to the head", "C": "Duroziez sign, a to and fro sound heard over a partially compressed femoral artery", "D": "Traube sign, a booming pistol shot sound auscultated over the femoral vessels", "E": "Corrigan sign, an abrupt distention and quick collapse felt in the carotid pulse"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Quincke sign, which reflects transmission of the exaggerated pulse into the capillary circulation): visible nail bed capillary pulsation with gentle nail tip compression is the Quincke sign of chronic aortic regurgitation. Head bobbing with each systole is the de Musset sign, and the femoral findings are Duroziez sign, a double sound with partial compression, and Traube sign, a pistol shot sound without compression. The abrupt rise and collapse of the carotid pulse is the Corrigan sign, a large vessel rather than capillary finding."}, {"id": 159, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 39 year old man reports months of episodic pounding headaches, drenching sweats, and palpitations, during which a home cuff has recorded values near 210/110 mm Hg that later settle spontaneously. Between the spells he feels completely normal. Which initial biochemical study best evaluates the diagnosis you should suspect in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Aldosterone and renin measured while seated during a quiet mid morning visit", "B": "Serum cortisol drawn at bedtime alongside a paired plasma glucose level", "C": "Plasma free metanephrines or urinary fractionated metanephrines collected over 24 hours", "D": "Thyroid stimulating hormone with free thyroxine to assess for possible thyrotoxicosis", "E": "Random plasma catecholamine concentrations drawn during a routine clinic visit between symptomatic spells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Plasma free metanephrines or urinary fractionated metanephrines collected over 24 hours): The triad of episodic headache, sweating, and palpitations with paroxysmal severe hypertension suggests pheochromocytoma, and metanephrines are the recommended screen because tumors methylate catecholamines continuously, keeping these metabolites elevated even between spells. Random catecholamines drawn during a quiet period are frequently normal and insensitive. Aldosterone testing targets sustained hypokalemic hypertension, and cortisol and thyroid studies address different syndromes."}, {"id": 160, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "You are grading chronic aortic regurgitation on transthoracic echocardiography for a 57 year old man and want to cite quantitative and supportive Doppler markers of severe disease. Which finding supports classification of aortic regurgitation as severe under current guideline criteria?", "options": {"A": "A vena contracta width of 0.2 cm with a narrow central jet", "B": "A regurgitant fraction of 20 percent accompanied by brief early diastolic descending aortic flow reversal", "C": "A regurgitant volume of 25 mL per beat with normal ventricular dimensions", "D": "An effective orifice area of 0.05 cm² with trivial color flow signal", "E": "Holodiastolic flow reversal in the proximal descending aorta with vena contracta above 0.6 cm"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Holodiastolic flow reversal in the proximal descending aorta with vena contracta above 0.6 cm): severe aortic regurgitation is supported by a vena contracta wider than 0.6 cm, a regurgitant volume of 60 mL or more, a regurgitant fraction of 50 percent or more, an effective orifice of 0.3 cm² or more, and flow reversal throughout diastole in the descending aorta. A 0.2 cm vena contracta, a 20 percent fraction, and a 25 mL volume all sit in the mild range. Brief early diastolic reversal is a normal or mild finding, while the holodiastolic pattern reflects substantial runoff back into the ventricle."}, {"id": 161, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 21 year old woman describes episodic white then blue color change of several fingers with cold exposure, followed by redness and tingling on rewarming. Her attacks are symmetric, her thumbs are spared, her examination is entirely normal, and she has no ulcers. Which feature set most supports a primary rather than secondary process?", "options": {"A": "Young age, symmetric attacks, spared thumbs, and absence of any tissue injury", "B": "Onset after age 40 with asymmetric attacks confined to one hand", "C": "Digital pitting scars accompanied by abnormal nailfold capillary loops on examination", "D": "Positive antinuclear antibodies at high titer with sclerodactyly of both hands", "E": "Attacks triggered by cold that progress to fingertip ulceration each winter"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Young age, symmetric attacks, spared thumbs, and absence of any tissue injury): primary Raynaud phenomenon typically begins in young women, produces symmetric attacks that spare the thumbs, and never causes tissue damage, with normal nailfold capillaries and negative serologies. Older onset, asymmetry, digital pitting, ulceration, abnormal capillaroscopy, and high titer autoantibodies all point toward a secondary cause such as systemic sclerosis. Recognizing the benign pattern spares a young patient unnecessary invasive evaluation while identifying who genuinely needs serologic workup."}, {"id": 163, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 54 year old woman has progressive dyspnea, normal left ventricular size and ejection fraction, severe diastolic dysfunction, and no pericardial abnormality on any imaging. Restrictive cardiomyopathy is suspected by her care team. Which echocardiographic constellation is the classic supporting picture for this diagnosis on a standard transthoracic study?", "options": {"A": "Dilated thin walled ventricles with globally reduced systolic contraction and functional regurgitation", "B": "Systolic anterior mitral motion with dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction", "C": "Marked biatrial enlargement with reduced tissue Doppler velocities and nondilated ventricles", "D": "Isolated right ventricular dilation with regional wall aneurysms and tricuspid tethering", "E": "Hyperdynamic small ventricles with cavity obliteration during systole and normal atria"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Marked biatrial enlargement with reduced tissue Doppler velocities and nondilated ventricles): stiff, noncompliant ventricles in restrictive cardiomyopathy transmit chronically high filling pressures backward, dilating both atria while the ventricles themselves stay normal in size with preserved ejection fraction, and intrinsic myocardial disease depresses tissue Doppler velocities. Dilated thin walled ventricles describe dilated cardiomyopathy. Systolic anterior motion belongs to hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and isolated right ventricular dilation with regional aneurysms suggests arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy."}, {"id": 164, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 70 year old man with a fusiform infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm attends scheduled surveillance imaging. He is asymptomatic, is at average operative risk, and his aneurysm has grown slowly over several years of follow up. At which maximum aneurysm diameter do current guidelines recommend elective repair for men like him?", "options": {"A": "4.5 centimeters, because rupture risk rises sharply beyond this measurement in men", "B": "6.5 centimeters, provided that interval growth remains under one centimeter during each year of surveillance", "C": "5.0 centimeters, matching the threshold applied to patients of either sex", "D": "5.5 centimeters, the diameter at which repair benefit outweighs procedural risk in men", "E": "6.0 centimeters, unless the patient develops tenderness on abdominal palpation earlier"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (5.5 centimeters, the diameter at which repair benefit outweighs procedural risk in men): randomized trials of early surgery versus surveillance showed no survival advantage for repairing smaller aneurysms in men, so 5.5 centimeters is the accepted elective threshold at average risk. A 4.5 or 5.0 centimeter trigger would expose men to operative risk without proven benefit, and 5.0 centimeters is the threshold used for women rather than for both sexes. Waiting until 6.0 or 6.5 centimeters accepts a substantially higher annual rupture risk than modern repair carries."}, {"id": 168, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 58 year old man with 2 hours of chest pain and an anterior ST elevation infarction presents to a remote critical access hospital. Anticipated transfer time makes timely primary intervention impossible, so fibrinolytic therapy is chosen. Within how many minutes of hospital arrival should the fibrinolytic be administered?", "options": {"A": "Within 90 minutes, mirroring the device goal at intervention capable hospitals", "B": "Within 120 minutes, matching the accepted window for interfacility transfer strategies", "C": "Within 10 minutes, the same target used for obtaining the first electrocardiogram", "D": "Within 30 minutes of arrival, the guideline door to needle performance goal", "E": "Within 60 minutes, provided serial troponin results have confirmed myocardial necrosis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Within 30 minutes of arrival, the guideline door to needle performance goal): when fibrinolysis is the chosen reperfusion strategy, the drug should be given within 30 minutes of hospital arrival. The 90 and 120 minute intervals are device goals for direct presentation and transfer, and the 10 minute target applies to acquiring the first electrocardiogram. Waiting for serial troponin results delays reperfusion and is never required once the electrocardiogram is diagnostic."}, {"id": 169, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 55 year old woman with a body mass index of 41 kg/m2 reports exertional dyspnea, and her echocardiogram shows a preserved ejection fraction with diastolic abnormalities. Her physician assistant reviews how obesity itself promotes heart failure with preserved ejection fraction. Which mechanism best explains this link?", "options": {"A": "Recurrent coronary plaque rupture producing clinically silent infarctions with cumulative impairment of systolic ventricular function", "B": "Expanded plasma volume, epicardial fat inflammation, and increased ventricular stiffness with microvascular dysfunction", "C": "Progressive valvular thickening from lipid deposition producing mixed stenotic and regurgitant lesions", "D": "Autonomic denervation of the sinus node producing chronotropic excess during ordinary activity", "E": "Fatty replacement of the right ventricular free wall producing arrhythmogenic remodeling"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Expanded plasma volume, epicardial fat inflammation, and increased ventricular stiffness with microvascular dysfunction): obesity raises circulating volume, and inflamed visceral and epicardial adipose tissue promotes myocardial fibrosis, coronary microvascular disease, and the stiff ventricle that defines the obese phenotype of heart failure with preserved ejection fraction. Silent infarction from plaque rupture would produce a reduced ejection fraction picture instead. Valvular lipid deposition, sinus node denervation, and fatty right ventricular replacement are not the mechanisms tying obesity to this syndrome."}, {"id": 170, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A nurse practitioner is teaching students about cardiorenal syndrome classification using a patient admitted with acute decompensated heart failure whose creatinine rose from 1.1 to 1.9 mg/dL during the admission. Which statement accurately describes type 1 cardiorenal syndrome as illustrated by this case?", "options": {"A": "Chronic kidney disease gradually produces left ventricular hypertrophy and accelerated coronary calcification", "B": "A systemic disorder such as sepsis injures the heart and kidneys simultaneously", "C": "Chronic heart failure causes progressive nephron loss over many years of congestion", "D": "Acute cardiac dysfunction produces acute kidney injury through congestion and reduced renal perfusion", "E": "Acute kidney injury provokes cardiac decompensation through volume overload and electrolyte shifts"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute cardiac dysfunction produces acute kidney injury through congestion and reduced renal perfusion): type 1 cardiorenal syndrome describes worsening renal function driven by acute heart failure, where elevated venous pressure and low forward flow injure the kidney. Acute kidney injury provoking cardiac decompensation describes type 3, chronic heart failure causing gradual nephron loss describes type 2, and a systemic insult injuring both organs describes type 5. Chronic kidney disease driving hypertrophy and vascular calcification is the type 4 pattern."}, {"id": 175, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A pharmacist reviewing a heart failure order set notes that not every beta blocker carries evidence in HFrEF, and the certification exam tests this distinction directly. Which beta blockers demonstrated mortality reduction in randomized HFrEF trials and are therefore the guideline endorsed choices for this specific indication?", "options": {"A": "Atenolol, propranolol, and nadolol, the agents with the longest clinical track record", "B": "Metoprolol tartrate in divided doses, which matches the succinate formulation for survival", "C": "Labetalol and atenolol, chosen for their combined alpha and beta receptor effects", "D": "Sotalol and carvedilol, combining ventricular rate control with protective antiarrhythmic blockade of potassium channels", "E": "Bisoprolol, carvedilol, and sustained release metoprolol succinate, the agents with proven mortality benefit"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Bisoprolol, carvedilol, and sustained release metoprolol succinate, the agents with proven mortality benefit): these three reduced death in CIBIS II, COPERNICUS and related trials, and MERIT HF respectively, so the guideline names them specifically rather than endorsing the class. Metoprolol tartrate has not shown equivalent survival benefit and is not an accepted substitute. Atenolol, propranolol, nadolol, labetalol, and sotalol lack HFrEF mortality evidence, and sotalol adds proarrhythmic risk."}, {"id": 176, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 58 year old man with HFrEF tolerates lisinopril without cough or hyperkalemia but cannot afford an ARNI at current pricing. His clinician plans to titrate ACE inhibition toward the doses used in the landmark survival trials. Which regimen represents an appropriate guideline target dose for ACE inhibitor therapy?", "options": {"A": "Enalapril 10 to 20 mg twice daily, the dosing range proven in mortality trials", "B": "Lisinopril 5 mg once daily, since low doses match high doses for survival", "C": "Captopril 6.25 mg three times daily continued indefinitely as the maintenance target", "D": "Ramipril 1.25 mg nightly, minimizing hypotension while preserving the full mortality benefit", "E": "Enalapril 2.5 mg twice daily, the highest dose studied in chronic heart failure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Enalapril 10 to 20 mg twice daily, the dosing range proven in mortality trials): the target doses in the guideline reflect what the survival trials actually used, such as enalapril 10 to 20 mg twice daily and lisinopril 20 to 40 mg daily. The 2.5 mg and 6.25 mg regimens listed are starting doses, not maintenance targets. Higher doses of ACE inhibitors outperformed very low doses for clinical outcomes, so deliberately staying at the lowest dose sacrifices benefit."}, {"id": 177, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 34 year old woman is found to have an atrial septal defect during evaluation of a murmur. Echocardiography localizes the defect to the region of the fossa ovalis in the mid portion of the atrial septum. Which type of atrial septal defect does this location indicate?", "options": {"A": "An ostium primum defect involving the atrioventricular septum adjacent to the valve plane", "B": "A sinus venosus defect positioned near the superior vena cava entry site", "C": "An ostium secundum defect, the most common form seen in adult patients", "D": "A coronary sinus defect with unroofing along the left atrial wall", "E": "A muscular defect within the trabeculated inferior portion of the septum"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An ostium secundum defect, the most common form seen in adult patients): Secundum defects arise at the fossa ovalis and account for roughly seventy percent of atrial septal defects. Primum defects lie low in the septum next to the atrioventricular valves, often with a cleft mitral valve. Sinus venosus and coronary sinus defects sit outside the fossa ovalis region, near the caval veins or the coronary sinus."}, {"id": 179, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 47 year old man with atypical chest discomfort is evaluated using the European Society of Cardiology 0 and 1 hour high sensitivity troponin algorithm, which current chest pain guidance endorses. What combination of baseline value and 1 hour change allows rapid rule out of myocardial infarction?", "options": {"A": "A baseline above the 99th percentile paired with a falling repeat value", "B": "A very low baseline concentration combined with an absent or minimal 1 hour change", "C": "Any baseline value provided the patient describes complete resolution of chest discomfort", "D": "A normal electrocardiogram alone without measurement of any repeat troponin concentration", "E": "A baseline below the limit of detection accompanied by a large upward delta"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A very low baseline concentration combined with an absent or minimal 1 hour change): rapid rule out requires a very low starting concentration together with a minimal delta on the 1 hour repeat. A baseline above the 99th percentile or a large upward delta moves the patient toward rule in rather than rule out. Symptom resolution and a normal electrocardiogram do not replace the serial biomarker measurements the algorithm requires."}, {"id": 182, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 35 year old woman with a normally functioning bicuspid aortic valve has an ascending aortic diameter of 4.2 cm on her initial transthoracic echocardiogram. In planning her long term care, which surveillance approach for the aortopathy associated with bicuspid valves is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "No further aortic imaging is needed while the valve itself functions normally", "B": "Repeat imaging is deferred until the diameter reaches the surgical threshold of 5.5 cm", "C": "Serial imaging of the aortic root and ascending aorta, with interval guided by size", "D": "Annual coronary angiography to track aortic dimensions and exclude silent coronary disease", "E": "Surveillance with chest radiographs, reserving tomographic imaging for new chest pain episodes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Serial imaging of the aortic root and ascending aorta, with interval guided by size): bicuspid valves carry an intrinsic aortopathy, and a diameter of 4.2 cm warrants ongoing surveillance with echocardiography, or with computed tomography or magnetic resonance when the aorta is not well seen, at intervals determined by dimension and growth rate. Waiting until the surgical threshold would miss the growth trajectory that guides timing and family counseling. Normal valve function does not remove the aortic risk, since dilation progresses independently of stenosis or regurgitation. Invasive angiography and plain radiographs are not appropriate tools for measuring aortic size."}, {"id": 184, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 74 year old man with severe reactive airway disease and recent dipyridamole intolerance cannot exercise because of claudication, yet needs functional evaluation for probable angina. Which pharmacologic stress approach is most appropriate for perfusion or wall motion imaging in this patient with bronchospastic disease?", "options": {"A": "A standard regadenoson bolus protocol identical to that used for other patients", "B": "High dose adenosine infusion with continuous bronchodilator nebulization running simultaneously", "C": "Exercise treadmill testing with the workload limited to the first protocol stage", "D": "Dobutamine infusion titrated upward, with atropine added if heart rate response is inadequate", "E": "Cold pressor testing using immersion of the hand in ice water"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dobutamine infusion titrated upward, with atropine added if heart rate response is inadequate): dobutamine raises heart rate and contractility through beta stimulation, providing diagnostic stress without adenosine receptor activation, and atropine supplements an insufficient chronotropic response. Adenosine and a routine regadenoson protocol both carry bronchospasm risk in severe reactive airway disease with prior vasodilator intolerance. A single treadmill stage gives inadequate workload in a patient limited by claudication, and cold pressor testing is not a diagnostic stress modality."}, {"id": 188, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A patient with suspected vasospastic angina has a normal coronary angiogram and nondiagnostic ambulatory monitoring. The interventional team considers pharmacologic provocation in the catheterization laboratory to confirm the diagnosis before committing the patient to long term therapy. Which agent is the standard intracoronary provocative drug used for this purpose?", "options": {"A": "Adenosine, which unmasks spasm by producing maximal hyperemia in the microcirculation", "B": "Dobutamine, which raises myocardial oxygen demand until coronary spasm is triggered", "C": "Nitroglycerin, given incrementally until paradoxical vasoconstriction appears on repeat angiography", "D": "Acetylcholine, which provokes focal or diffuse spasm in susceptible epicardial segments", "E": "Regadenoson, chosen because its selective receptor activity reproduces the nocturnal episodes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acetylcholine, which provokes focal or diffuse spasm in susceptible epicardial segments): intracoronary acetylcholine is the standard provocative agent, producing spasm in susceptible segments through muscarinic stimulation of dysfunctional endothelium and smooth muscle. Adenosine and regadenoson are hyperemic agents used for flow reserve and perfusion imaging rather than spasm provocation. Dobutamine raises demand rather than testing vasomotor tone, and nitroglycerin relieves spasm rather than provoking it."}, {"id": 191, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "You order 24 hour ambulatory monitoring for a 49 year old woman whose office readings average 132/82 mm Hg. You want to know whether her out of office values confirm hypertension using thresholds the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline considers equivalent to an office value of 130/80 mm Hg. Which ambulatory result confirms the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "A 24 hour mean of 130/80 mm Hg or higher on the report", "B": "A daytime mean of 140/90 mm Hg or higher during waking hours", "C": "A 24 hour mean of 125/75 mm Hg or higher on the report", "D": "A nighttime mean of 120/80 mm Hg or higher while the patient sleeps", "E": "Any single daytime reading above 135/85 mm Hg recorded during normal activity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A 24 hour mean of 125/75 mm Hg or higher on the report): The guideline treats an office value of 130/80 as corresponding to a 24 hour ambulatory mean of 125/75, a daytime mean of 130/80, and a nighttime mean of 110/65. A full day cutoff of 130/80 is too high because lower nocturnal pressures are averaged into the 24 hour figure. Diagnosis rests on averaged values, so a single daytime reading proves nothing on its own."}, {"id": 194, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 25 year old athlete has ST elevation on a preparticipation ECG, and you must distinguish acute pericarditis from benign early repolarization using the tracing itself. In lead V6, which measurement finding supports a diagnosis of acute pericarditis rather than early repolarization in this clinical situation?", "options": {"A": "A J point notch at the terminal QRS with tall peaked T waves", "B": "An ST segment that returns to baseline during a brief exercise test", "C": "A T wave amplitude exceeding ten millimeters with a normal ST segment", "D": "An isoelectric ST segment accompanied by prominent U waves in V6", "E": "A ratio of ST elevation to T wave amplitude of 0.25 or greater"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A ratio of ST elevation to T wave amplitude of 0.25 or greater): in V6, dividing the height of the ST segment by the height of the T wave yields a ratio that favors pericarditis when it reaches 0.25, because early repolarization produces proportionally taller T waves. A J point notch with tall T waves is the typical early repolarization appearance. Normalization with exercise also suggests early repolarization, and an isoelectric ST segment or a tall T wave with normal ST does not indicate pericarditis."}, {"id": 198, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 79 year old man is confirmed to have wild type transthyretin cardiac amyloidosis with NYHA class II symptoms. His family asks whether any medication changes the disease course itself rather than only managing congestion. Which disease modifying therapy has demonstrated a mortality benefit in this specific condition?", "options": {"A": "Colchicine, which interrupts amyloid fibril deposition through microtubule inhibition in cardiomyocytes", "B": "High dose melphalan with autologous stem cell transplantation aimed at suppressing hepatic transthyretin production", "C": "Sacubitril valsartan, which stabilizes transthyretin tetramers while also blocking neprilysin degradation", "D": "Tafamidis, a transthyretin stabilizer that reduced mortality and cardiovascular hospitalizations in trials", "E": "Prednisone tapers, which shrink existing myocardial amyloid deposits over several months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tafamidis, a transthyretin stabilizer that reduced mortality and cardiovascular hospitalizations in trials): tafamidis binds and stabilizes the transthyretin tetramer, preventing its dissociation into amyloidogenic monomers, and the ATTR ACT trial showed lower all cause mortality and fewer cardiovascular hospitalizations, earning it a guideline recommendation in NYHA class I to III disease. Melphalan and transplantation treat light chain amyloidosis, a different disease. Colchicine, corticosteroids, and sacubitril valsartan neither stabilize transthyretin nor remove existing deposits."}, {"id": 199, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 71 year old man hospitalized for decompensated heart failure has minimal urine output despite escalating doses of intravenous furosemide infusion. His team plans sequential nephron blockade to overcome compensatory distal sodium reabsorption. Which medication strategy correctly applies this principle in the management of loop diuretic resistance?", "options": {"A": "Adding intravenous mannitol to raise tubular osmolality upstream of the loop segment", "B": "Rotating between different loop diuretics daily to prevent receptor downregulation from developing", "C": "Restricting dietary sodium to less than 500 mg daily instead of adding medications", "D": "Adding metolazone to block distal tubule sodium reabsorption that limits loop diuretic response", "E": "Holding all diuretics for 48 hours so that tubular sensitivity can reset"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Adding metolazone to block distal tubule sodium reabsorption that limits loop diuretic response): chronic loop diuretic exposure causes hypertrophy of distal tubular cells that reclaim the sodium escaping the loop, and adding a thiazide type agent such as metolazone blocks that downstream segment. This combination requires close potassium and sodium monitoring because the diuresis can be profound. Rotating loop agents does not address the distal adaptation, mannitol is not a decongestion strategy in heart failure, and stopping diuretics in a congested patient invites worsening decompensation."}, {"id": 201, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 60 year old man attends two clinic visits four weeks apart. His seated blood pressure, measured after adequate rest with a correctly sized cuff on a bare arm, averages 146/94 mm Hg across both encounters. He feels well and takes no regular medications. How does the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline classify this average blood pressure?", "options": {"A": "Stage 2 hypertension, because systolic ≥140 mm Hg or diastolic ≥90 mm Hg", "B": "Stage 1 hypertension, pending confirmation by a third office visit next month", "C": "Hypertensive urgency, since sustained diastolic elevation above 90 mm Hg threatens organs", "D": "Elevated blood pressure, given his lack of symptoms or documented organ damage", "E": "Masked hypertension, which requires a period of home readings before drug therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stage 2 hypertension, because systolic ≥140 mm Hg or diastolic ≥90 mm Hg): Both his systolic of 146 and his diastolic of 94 sit at or above the stage 2 cutoffs, and two properly performed visits already satisfy the confirmation requirement. Stage 1 covers systolic 130 to 139 or diastolic 80 to 89, which his readings exceed. Urgency describes severe elevation above 180/120, and masked hypertension means normal office values with high out of office values, the opposite pattern."}, {"id": 202, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A physician assistant evaluates a 74 year old woman with exertional dyspnea, elevated natriuretic peptides, and echocardiographic evidence of diastolic dysfunction. Her left ventricular systolic function appears grossly normal on imaging. Under the 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA classification, which ejection fraction finding is required to categorize her condition as heart failure with preserved ejection fraction?", "options": {"A": "A left ventricular ejection fraction of 50 percent or higher with other heart failure evidence", "B": "A left ventricular ejection fraction of 45 percent or higher measured on stress echocardiography", "C": "A left ventricular ejection fraction between 41 and 49 percent with documented diastolic dysfunction", "D": "A normal ejection fraction confirmed on two studies performed at least six months apart", "E": "An ejection fraction exceeding 60 percent together with left atrial enlargement on imaging"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A left ventricular ejection fraction of 50 percent or higher with other heart failure evidence): HFpEF requires symptoms, an ejection fraction of at least 50 percent, and objective evidence such as elevated natriuretic peptides or raised filling pressures. Values between 41 and 49 percent belong to the mildly reduced category, not the preserved one. Neither a 60 percent floor, a stress study, nor confirmation on two studies months apart appears anywhere in the definition."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 55 year old woman scheduled for a dental extraction reviews her cardiac history with you and asks whether she should receive antibiotic prophylaxis against infective endocarditis beforehand. For which of the following cardiac conditions is routine endocarditis prophylaxis not recommended under current guidance?", "options": {"A": "A history of infective endocarditis treated successfully with intravenous antibiotics last year", "B": "A transcatheter bioprosthetic aortic valve implanted eighteen months ago without complications", "C": "Unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease awaiting staged surgical palliation next month", "D": "Mitral valve prolapse with regurgitation in a patient with no prior endocarditis", "E": "A heart transplant recipient who developed structural regurgitation of a cardiac valve"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mitral valve prolapse with regurgitation in a patient with no prior endocarditis): prolapse, even with a murmur or regurgitation, was removed from prophylaxis recommendations because the lifetime risk of endocarditis is low and procedure related bacteremia is a minor contributor compared with daily activities. Prior endocarditis, prosthetic valves including transcatheter devices, unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease, and transplant recipients with valvulopathy all remain high risk conditions for which prophylaxis before qualifying dental procedures is still advised. The distinction rests on the risk of a severe or fatal outcome from endocarditis, which is concentrated in those four groups."}, {"id": 206, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A routine chest radiograph in an asymptomatic 44 year old woman shows a smooth round opacity at the right heart border, and chest CT demonstrates a thin walled homogeneous fluid density lesion abutting the pericardium. Which statement best characterizes the typical pericardial cyst suggested by these findings?", "options": {"A": "A malignant lesion of mesothelial origin that mandates prompt surgical resection", "B": "A communication between pericardium and pleura formed after unrecognized chest trauma", "C": "A loculated purulent collection that requires drainage and prolonged antibiotic therapy", "D": "A solid fibrous tumor arising from the pericardium with compressive physiology", "E": "A benign fluid filled structure most often located at the right cardiophrenic angle"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A benign fluid filled structure most often located at the right cardiophrenic angle): pericardial cysts are congenital, thin walled, fluid filled lesions, roughly 70 percent of which sit at the right cardiophrenic angle, and asymptomatic cysts are simply followed. They are not malignant mesothelial tumors and are not solid fibrous masses, so resection is reserved for symptoms or diagnostic uncertainty. A purulent collection would show clinical infection with complex fluid, and a traumatic pleuropericardial communication is a different, acquired entity."}, {"id": 207, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 58 year old man presents with 40 minutes of crushing substernal chest pressure, diaphoresis, and nausea. The emergency physician obtains a 12 lead electrocardiogram within 10 minutes of arrival. Which electrocardiographic finding establishes the diagnosis of ST segment elevation myocardial infarction in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Isolated ST segment depression of 1 mm confined to leads V5 and V6", "B": "T wave inversion deeper than 3 mm in two anatomically contiguous limb leads", "C": "New ST elevation of ≥1 mm at the J point in two contiguous leads", "D": "A single premature ventricular complex followed by a fully compensatory pause on rhythm strip", "E": "PR segment depression with diffuse concave ST elevation across nearly all recorded leads"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (New ST elevation of ≥1 mm at the J point in two contiguous leads): the universal definition requires new ST elevation in two contiguous leads, with higher thresholds applying only in leads V2 and V3. Isolated lateral ST depression and deep T wave inversion indicate ischemia but do not meet elevation criteria for infarction. Diffuse concave elevation with PR depression suggests pericarditis, and an isolated premature ventricular complex has no diagnostic weight for infarction."}, {"id": 210, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A pharmacology review question asks a certification candidate to identify the site and mechanism of furosemide's diuretic effect, since this knowledge underpins dosing decisions and the drug's dependence on kidney function during congestion. Which mechanism correctly describes how loop diuretics produce natriuresis in patients with heart failure?", "options": {"A": "Blockade of the sodium potassium chloride cotransporter in the thick ascending limb", "B": "Inhibition of the sodium chloride cotransporter located within the distal convoluted tubule", "C": "Antagonism of aldosterone receptors in the principal cells of the collecting duct", "D": "Osmotic retention of filtrate water throughout the proximal tubule and descending limb", "E": "Inhibition of carbonic anhydrase reducing bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Blockade of the sodium potassium chloride cotransporter in the thick ascending limb): loop diuretics inhibit the NKCC2 transporter, preventing reabsorption of a large fraction of filtered sodium and producing the most powerful diuresis of any class. Sodium chloride cotransporter inhibition in the distal tubule describes thiazides, and aldosterone receptor antagonism in the collecting duct describes mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists. Osmotic agents and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors act in the proximal nephron and are not loop mechanisms."}, {"id": 212, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 66 year old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease develops peripheral edema, a loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound, and jugular distension. His nurse practitioner explains the concept of cor pulmonale to a student. Which definition best captures this diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Left ventricular systolic pump failure that happens to coexist incidentally with longstanding chronic pulmonary disease", "B": "Any right sided volume overload, most commonly caused by tricuspid valve endocarditis", "C": "Right ventricular structural and functional alteration caused by lung disease and its pulmonary hypertension", "D": "Biventricular dilation from systemic viral infection involving both cardiac chambers", "E": "Pericardial constriction producing systemic venous congestion with clear lung fields"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right ventricular structural and functional alteration caused by lung disease and its pulmonary hypertension): cor pulmonale describes right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, or failure arising from disorders of the lungs, their vasculature, or ventilation, with hypoxic vasoconstriction and vascular remodeling raising the afterload the right ventricle must face. Left ventricular failure coexisting with lung disease is a separate entity and is specifically excluded from the definition. Valvular endocarditis, viral biventricular dilation, and pericardial constriction each produce venous congestion through mechanisms unrelated to pulmonary disease."}, {"id": 214, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 76 year old man reports several near syncopal episodes this week. He takes no atrioventricular nodal blocking medications. You review the rhythm strip shown, captured on telemetry during one symptomatic episode this morning. Which conduction abnormality best describes the pattern that has been recorded on this tracing?", "options": {"A": "First degree atrioventricular block with a uniformly prolonged but stable PR interval", "B": "Wenckebach conduction with progressive PR lengthening before each dropped ventricular beat", "C": "Complete heart block with fully independent atrial and ventricular activity throughout", "D": "Mobitz type II block with abruptly nonconducted P waves and fixed PR intervals", "E": "Blocked premature atrial complexes mimicking an intermittent atrioventricular conduction disturbance"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mobitz type II block with abruptly nonconducted P waves and fixed PR intervals): The strip shows on time sinus P waves that intermittently fail to conduct while the PR interval of every conducted beat remains constant, the defining feature of Mobitz type II and a marker of infranodal disease with risk of progression to complete block. Wenckebach conduction would show PR intervals stretching progressively before the dropped beat. Complete block would show no relationship at all between P waves and QRS complexes, first degree block drops nothing, and blocked premature atrial complexes arrive early rather than on time.", "image": "question_images/q_214.png"}, {"id": 215, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 58 year old woman with persistent dyspnea six months after an acute pulmonary embolism is being evaluated for chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension. Her physician assistant selects the screening imaging study. Why is ventilation perfusion scanning preferred over computed tomography angiography for excluding this diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "It delivers substantially higher spatial resolution of the segmental pulmonary arteries than computed tomography angiography provides", "B": "It doubles as an assessment of left ventricular systolic function during the acquisition", "C": "A normal perfusion pattern effectively excludes chronic thromboembolic disease, which computed tomography can miss", "D": "It quantifies pulmonary vascular resistance directly without requiring any catheterization afterward", "E": "It avoids radiation entirely, making it safer for serial repetition in young patients"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A normal perfusion pattern effectively excludes chronic thromboembolic disease, which computed tomography can miss): ventilation perfusion scintigraphy is the recommended screening test because its sensitivity for chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension approaches 96 to 97 percent, while organized, webbed, and distal disease can escape detection on computed tomography angiography read by nonexpert eyes. The scan does not image arteries at higher resolution, and it provides no ventricular or resistance measurement. It also involves radiation, so the safety claim misstates its advantage."}, {"id": 221, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "An 82 year old woman on digoxin for rate control presents with nausea, visual disturbance, and a new arrhythmia on telemetry. Her digoxin level returns markedly elevated. Which arrhythmia is considered classic for digoxin toxicity because it combines increased automaticity with impaired atrioventricular conduction at the same time?", "options": {"A": "Atrial flutter with one to one atrioventricular conduction during physical exertion", "B": "Torsades de pointes triggered during the night by pause dependent early afterdepolarizations on continuous telemetry monitoring", "C": "Sinus tachycardia with intermittent right bundle branch block aberrancy on exertion", "D": "Preexcited atrial fibrillation conducting rapidly over an accessory bypass tract", "E": "Paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with block, often accompanied by accelerated junctional rhythms"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with block, often accompanied by accelerated junctional rhythms): Digoxin increases automaticity through calcium loading and delayed afterdepolarizations while simultaneously enhancing vagal tone at the AV node, so atrial tachycardia with atrioventricular block is the classic toxic rhythm, and accelerated junctional rhythms and bidirectional ventricular tachycardia are also characteristic. Torsades de pointes reflects QT prolonging drugs rather than digoxin, which actually shortens the QT interval. One to one flutter, preexcited atrial fibrillation, and sinus tachycardia with aberrancy have no specific association with cardiac glycoside excess."}, {"id": 224, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 49 year old woman with a strong family history of premature coronary disease is evaluated for residual atherosclerotic risk. Her clinician orders an advanced lipid assessment, and the laboratory results shown accompany this question. Based on these values, which conclusion about her lipoprotein(a) result is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "The measured level reflects recent dietary indiscretion and should be rechecked after an overnight fasting period", "B": "The elevation is largely genetically determined and identifies independent atherosclerotic risk", "C": "The value is a laboratory artifact because her LDL cholesterol is acceptable", "D": "The result will normalize once moderate intensity statin therapy is started", "E": "The elevation becomes clinically meaningful mainly when triglycerides exceed 500 mg/dL"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The elevation is largely genetically determined and identifies independent atherosclerotic risk): a level of 128 nmol/L exceeds the roughly 125 nmol/L risk threshold, is about 80 to 90 percent genetically determined, and marks independent risk beyond her acceptable LDL cholesterol. Diet has little effect on the level, statins do not lower it, and a clearly elevated value alongside normal conventional lipids does not suggest an artifact. Its prognostic significance does not depend on the triglyceride concentration.", "image": "question_images/q_224.png"}, {"id": 225, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 72 year old woman undergoes surveillance for an asymptomatic infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm first detected three years ago. Her surgeon explains that the recommended repair threshold differs between men and women because rupture behavior differs by sex. At which aneurysm diameter is elective repair generally recommended for women at average risk?", "options": {"A": "5.5 centimeters, identical to the threshold that is used for male patients", "B": "5.0 centimeters, reflecting rupture at smaller aortic diameters among women", "C": "4.0 centimeters, once any measurable interval growth has been documented", "D": "6.0 centimeters, provided semiannual imaging shows a stable sac diameter", "E": "4.5 centimeters, when combined with at least moderate chronic obstructive disease"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (5.0 centimeters, reflecting rupture at smaller aortic diameters among women): women have smaller native aortas and rupture at smaller absolute diameters, so elective repair is recommended at 5.0 centimeters rather than the 5.5 centimeter threshold used for men. A 4.0 or 4.5 centimeter trigger would operate on aneurysms whose rupture risk remains lower than procedural risk, and pulmonary comorbidity does not lower the diameter threshold. Deferring repair to 6.0 centimeters would leave a woman exposed to a high annual rupture hazard."}, {"id": 227, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 71 year old man with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension starts midodrine three times daily. You counsel him carefully about dose timing because the drug's pressor effect can create a specific hazard whenever he lies flat. Which instruction correctly reflects the standard timing rule for this particular medication?", "options": {"A": "Take each dose with food at breakfast, dinner, and again at bedtime", "B": "Take the medication on waking and repeat whenever dizziness occurs during the day", "C": "Hold the morning dose on days when he plans to remain seated", "D": "Double the afternoon dose before physically demanding activities such as yard work", "E": "Take the final dose at least four hours before lying down for the night"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Take the final dose at least four hours before lying down for the night): Midodrine is an alpha agonist prodrug whose pressor effect lasts several hours, so a dose taken too close to bedtime produces supine hypertension while the patient sleeps. Standard counseling schedules doses during upright daytime hours, with the last dose no later than four hours before lying down. Bedtime dosing, symptom triggered redosing, and unsupervised dose doubling each raise the supine pressure hazard."}, {"id": 232, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 67 year old man with a non ST elevation infarction was treated with fondaparinux on admission and is now being taken for coronary angiography with likely intervention. What additional anticoagulation step is recommended in the catheterization laboratory for patients who arrive on this agent?", "options": {"A": "Administer intravenous protamine before arterial access to reverse the circulating pentasaccharide", "B": "Double the fondaparinux dose immediately before crossing the culprit coronary lesion", "C": "Give supplemental unfractionated heparin during the procedure to prevent catheter thrombosis", "D": "Withhold all further anticoagulation because the subcutaneous dose covers the full procedure", "E": "Substitute a fibrinolytic bolus for procedural anticoagulation once arterial access is obtained"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give supplemental unfractionated heparin during the procedure to prevent catheter thrombosis): fondaparinux alone provides inadequate procedural anticoagulation and has been associated with catheter thrombosis, so an agent with antithrombin activity such as unfractionated heparin is added during intervention. Protamine does not reverse fondaparinux, and doubling its dose does not solve the catheter thrombosis problem. Relying on the subcutaneous dose alone or substituting a fibrinolytic bolus would leave the procedure unprotected."}, {"id": 234, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 49 year old woman with intermediate risk stable chest pain and no known coronary disease is being scheduled for noninvasive evaluation. The team considers coronary computed tomography angiography as the first test. Which property makes this modality particularly useful in such patients according to current guidance?", "options": {"A": "It quantifies fractional flow reserve invasively during the same imaging session", "B": "It measures myocardial oxygen consumption directly within each perfusion territory imaged", "C": "It provokes ischemia pharmacologically while simultaneously demonstrating regional wall motion", "D": "Its high negative predictive value reliably excludes obstructive coronary artery disease", "E": "It requires no ionizing radiation or iodinated contrast to visualize the arteries"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Its high negative predictive value reliably excludes obstructive coronary artery disease): a normal coronary computed tomography angiogram makes obstructive disease very unlikely, which is why the test is favored for intermediate risk patients without known disease. The study is anatomic rather than functional, so it neither provokes ischemia nor measures oxygen consumption, and invasive fractional flow reserve requires catheterization. The examination does use ionizing radiation and iodinated contrast."}, {"id": 235, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A physician assistant reviewing a right heart catheterization report asks how pulmonary hypertension is now defined hemodynamically. The 2022 European guidelines updated the threshold from earlier definitions. Which mean pulmonary arterial pressure value at rest establishes the diagnosis of pulmonary hypertension under the current definition?", "options": {"A": "A mean pressure above 25 mmHg, unchanged from the historical threshold", "B": "A systolic pressure above 35 mmHg estimated during the catheterization run", "C": "A mean pressure above 30 mmHg measured during symptom limited exercise", "D": "A wedge pressure above 15 mmHg regardless of the pulmonary arterial mean", "E": "A mean pulmonary arterial pressure above 20 mmHg measured at rest"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A mean pulmonary arterial pressure above 20 mmHg measured at rest): the 2022 European Society of Cardiology and European Respiratory Society guidelines lowered the defining threshold from the historical 25 mmHg to above 20 mmHg, reflecting evidence that mortality risk rises above the normal mean of about 14 mmHg. The older 25 mmHg cutoff no longer defines the disease. Systolic estimates do not define the condition, exercise values are not part of the resting definition, and wedge pressure serves to classify rather than establish pulmonary hypertension."}, {"id": 237, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A previously healthy 38 year old man deteriorates over two weeks with new heart failure, sustained ventricular tachycardia, and progressive high grade atrioventricular block despite escalating support. His team pursues urgent endomyocardial biopsy because one myocarditis subtype demands rapid diagnosis and immunosuppression. Which subtype fits this fulminant arrhythmic presentation?", "options": {"A": "Lymphocytic myocarditis, which routinely requires combination immunosuppression at its first presentation", "B": "Giant cell myocarditis, which causes rapid deterioration with ventricular arrhythmias and heart block", "C": "Loeffler endocarditis, defined by eosinophilic thrombus formation along the ventricular apex", "D": "Chagas myocarditis, the leading fulminant myocarditis in patients without travel exposure", "E": "Radiation induced myocarditis, presenting decades after mediastinal exposure with conduction disease"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Giant cell myocarditis, which causes rapid deterioration with ventricular arrhythmias and heart block): this rare subtype strikes younger adults with rapidly progressive heart failure, sustained ventricular tachycardia, and conduction block, and survival depends on early biopsy diagnosis followed by combined immunosuppression, with many patients needing mechanical support or transplant. Ordinary lymphocytic myocarditis is usually self limited and does not routinely warrant immunosuppression. Loeffler disease is an eosinophilic endomyocardial process, Chagas disease requires exposure in endemic regions, and radiation injury develops slowly over years."}, {"id": 243, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 66 year old man with hypertension and a 40 pack year smoking history reports cramping calf pain that appears with walking and resolves within minutes of rest. His physician orders resting ankle brachial index testing to evaluate for peripheral artery disease. Which resting ankle brachial index value establishes the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "A value of 1.10 or lower, reflecting reduced distal perfusion pressure in the limb", "B": "A value between 0.91 and 1.00, indicating borderline arterial narrowing requiring confirmation", "C": "A value of 0.90 or below, indicating hemodynamically significant lower extremity arterial obstruction", "D": "A resting value above 1.40, which confirms severe calcified obstruction affecting both distal tibial arterial segments", "E": "A value of 1.00 or lower measured after a standardized treadmill exercise protocol"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A value of 0.90 or below, indicating hemodynamically significant lower extremity arterial obstruction): a resting ankle brachial index of 0.90 or below is diagnostic of peripheral artery disease. Values between 0.91 and 0.99 are borderline rather than diagnostic, and results from 1.00 to 1.40 fall in the normal range. A result above 1.40 reflects noncompressible calcified vessels and is uninterpretable rather than confirmatory, while exercise testing is an adjunct for symptomatic patients with normal resting values, not the primary diagnostic criterion."}, {"id": 244, "domain": "normal_cardiovascular_anatomy_and_physiology", "question": "A physician assistant reviewing heart sounds asks you to connect each component of the cardiac cycle to its audible landmark before auscultating patients in clinic. Which valvular events produce the first and second heart sounds, respectively, during a single normal cardiac cycle at rest?", "options": {"A": "Aortic and pulmonic opening at S1, mitral and tricuspid opening at S2", "B": "Mitral and tricuspid closure at S1, aortic and pulmonic closure at S2", "C": "Mitral closure alone at S1, tricuspid closure alone producing S2", "D": "Aortic closure at S1, followed by pulmonic closure generating S2", "E": "Atrial contraction at S1, with ventricular relaxation itself producing S2"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mitral and tricuspid closure at S1, aortic and pulmonic closure at S2): S1 marks closure of the atrioventricular valves as ventricular systole begins, and S2 marks closure of the semilunar valves as systole ends and isovolumic relaxation starts. Valve opening is normally silent, so options built on opening sounds are wrong. S1 is not produced by mitral closure alone with tricuspid closure making S2, aortic closure belongs to S2 rather than S1, and atrial contraction relates to S4 when audible."}, {"id": 246, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 62 year old woman with chronic kidney disease and a urine albumin to creatinine ratio of 450 mg/g has blood pressure averaging 148/88 mm Hg despite committed lifestyle efforts. Guidelines identify one drug class as the preferred foundation of therapy in this specific setting. Which starting agent best fits that recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Amlodipine, because dihydropyridine calcium channel blockade lowers systemic pressure and protects the kidneys effectively", "B": "Chlorthalidone, because volume control is the dominant mechanism in kidney disease", "C": "Metoprolol succinate, because sympathetic overactivity drives most albuminuric kidney disease", "D": "Hydralazine, because direct arteriolar dilation preserves glomerular filtration during treatment", "E": "Lisinopril, because blocking angiotensin lowers intraglomerular pressure and slows albuminuric progression"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Lisinopril, because blocking angiotensin lowers intraglomerular pressure and slows albuminuric progression): In chronic kidney disease with significant albuminuria, an angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker is the recommended foundation because efferent arteriolar relaxation reduces intraglomerular pressure and proteinuria. Calcium channel blockers and thiazides lower systemic pressure but lack the same antiproteinuric kidney outcome data. Beta blockers and hydralazine have no comparable role in slowing albuminuric nephropathy."}, {"id": 248, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 30 year old woman with Down syndrome has a low lying atrial septal defect immediately adjacent to the atrioventricular valves, with left axis deviation on the electrocardiogram. Which valvular abnormality is most characteristically associated with this ostium primum type of defect on echocardiography?", "options": {"A": "Rheumatic style commissural fusion of the mitral leaflets with diastolic doming", "B": "A cleft in the anterior mitral leaflet causing variable degrees of regurgitation", "C": "Myxomatous prolapse of both mitral leaflets with chordal elongation and redundancy", "D": "Congenital pulmonic valve stenosis with systolic doming and post stenotic dilation", "E": "A quadricuspid aortic valve with central coaptation failure and diastolic leak"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A cleft in the anterior mitral leaflet causing variable degrees of regurgitation): Primum defects are part of the atrioventricular septal defect spectrum, in which the left atrioventricular valve is trileaflet with a cleft. The cleft produces regurgitation that often dictates surgical timing and technique. Commissural fusion suggests rheumatic disease, and pulmonic stenosis is not a defining feature of this lesion."}, {"id": 249, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 38 year old woman presents 2 weeks postpartum with an acute coronary syndrome, and angiography demonstrates a long smooth narrowing of the mid left anterior descending artery consistent with an intramural hematoma. Which statement accurately characterizes spontaneous coronary artery dissection, the most likely diagnosis here?", "options": {"A": "It results from rupture of a lipid rich atherosclerotic plaque with overlying thrombus", "B": "It occurs predominantly in older men with longstanding diabetes and heavy smoking", "C": "It requires immediate multivessel stenting to prevent extension of the dissection plane", "D": "It is best treated with fibrinolytic therapy to dissolve the intramural hematoma", "E": "It mostly affects younger women, and stable cases are often managed conservatively"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (It mostly affects younger women, and stable cases are often managed conservatively): spontaneous coronary artery dissection predominantly affects younger women, including peripartum patients and those with fibromuscular dysplasia, and stable cases frequently heal with conservative management because stenting risks extending the dissection. The mechanism is an intramural hematoma or intimal tear rather than atherosclerotic plaque rupture. Fibrinolysis is discouraged because it can enlarge the hematoma, and routine multivessel stenting is not the preferred approach."}, {"id": 251, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 70 year old man with a dilated ischemic cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 25 percent has moderate to severe mitral regurgitation despite structurally normal appearing leaflets. Which statement best captures the defining mechanism of secondary, or functional, mitral regurgitation in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Myxomatous leaflet redundancy allows systolic billowing of excess leaflet tissue superiorly beyond the annular plane", "B": "Ventricular remodeling tethers the leaflets and dilates the annulus, preventing effective coaptation", "C": "Rheumatic scarring retracts the leaflets so they fail to close during systole", "D": "Infective vegetations perforate a leaflet and create an eccentric regurgitant jet", "E": "Chordal rupture from fibroelastic deficiency causes a flail segment with severe leakage"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ventricular remodeling tethers the leaflets and dilates the annulus, preventing effective coaptation): in secondary mitral regurgitation the leaflets and chordae are structurally normal, but displacement of the papillary muscles and annular dilation from ventricular remodeling hold the leaflets apart. Treatment therefore centers on guideline directed therapy for the failing ventricle, with valve intervention considered in selected persistent cases. Myxomatous billowing, chordal rupture, rheumatic retraction, and endocarditis perforation all represent intrinsic valve disease and define primary regurgitation instead."}, {"id": 258, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 55 year old man has vasospastic angina confirmed by acetylcholine provocation showing focal spasm with chest pain and ST elevation. He has stopped smoking, and his blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg. Which class of medication is considered first line therapy to prevent his recurrent episodes of coronary spasm?", "options": {"A": "Calcium channel blockers, which relax coronary smooth muscle and prevent recurrent spasm", "B": "Cardioselective beta blockers, which lower heart rate and reduce myocardial oxygen demand", "C": "Loop diuretics, which reduce preload and lessen nocturnal ventricular wall stress", "D": "Antiplatelet monotherapy, which stabilizes endothelium and stops spasm at plaque sites", "E": "Statins alone, which restore endothelial nitric oxide sufficiently to abolish episodes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Calcium channel blockers, which relax coronary smooth muscle and prevent recurrent spasm): calcium channel blockers are first line because they directly relax coronary vascular smooth muscle and reliably reduce spasm frequency. Beta blockade does not prevent spasm and nonselective agents can worsen it, while diuretics address preload rather than vasomotor tone. Antiplatelet agents and statins are reasonable adjuncts for coexisting atherosclerosis but do not dependably suppress spastic episodes."}, {"id": 259, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A laboratory director is validating a new high sensitivity cardiac troponin assay for the emergency department of a community hospital. The cardiology committee asks how the upper reference limit that defines myocardial injury should be established for reporting. Which approach do current recommendations support for this threshold?", "options": {"A": "A single shared cutoff applied identically to adult men and adult women", "B": "A threshold set at ten times the assay limit of blank for all patients", "C": "A cutoff derived from a population of hospitalized patients with known coronary artery disease", "D": "Sex specific 99th percentile limits derived from a healthy reference population", "E": "A value chosen to match the older conventional assay cutoff already in use"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sex specific 99th percentile limits derived from a healthy reference population): high sensitivity assays should report sex specific 99th percentile upper reference limits because healthy men run higher concentrations than healthy women. A single shared cutoff can miss injury in women and overcall it in men. Thresholds anchored to diseased inpatients, the limit of blank, or a legacy assay do not define the reference limit correctly."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 71 year old woman with longstanding diabetes mellitus and chronic kidney disease undergoes ankle brachial index testing to evaluate exertional leg discomfort. The measured value on her symptomatic side is 1.52. What does this result indicate, and which additional noninvasive study is recommended to clarify whether arterial disease is present?", "options": {"A": "Noncompressible calcified arteries, which should prompt measurement of a toe brachial index", "B": "Normal arterial compliance, which supports evaluating alternative musculoskeletal causes of her symptoms", "C": "Severe proximal aortoiliac obstruction, which should prompt urgent catheter based angiography for confirmation", "D": "Borderline peripheral artery disease, which should prompt repeat testing after treadmill exercise", "E": "Elevated systemic blood pressure, which should prompt ambulatory monitoring before further vascular studies"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Noncompressible calcified arteries, which should prompt measurement of a toe brachial index): an ankle brachial index above 1.40 means medial calcification, common in diabetes and kidney disease, prevents cuff compression of the tibial arteries, so the number cannot be trusted. The digital arteries are usually spared from calcification, so a toe brachial index gives a reliable perfusion measurement in this setting. The result does not indicate normal compliance or borderline disease, and invasive angiography is not the appropriate next step for an uninterpretable screening value."}, {"id": 261, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 59 year old man with a non ST elevation infarction is scheduled for percutaneous coronary intervention, and the team considers prasugrel for potent platelet inhibition. His records are reviewed for safety before loading. Which element of his history makes prasugrel contraindicated rather than simply cautioned?", "options": {"A": "Age of 76 years, which requires consideration of a reduced maintenance dose", "B": "Body weight of 58 kilograms recorded at the most recent clinic visit", "C": "Chronic kidney disease with an estimated glomerular filtration rate near 40", "D": "A transient ischemic attack that occurred 2 years before this admission", "E": "Concurrent proton pump inhibitor therapy prescribed for erosive esophagitis last year"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A transient ischemic attack that occurred 2 years before this admission): any history of stroke or transient ischemic attack contraindicates prasugrel because of excess intracranial bleeding observed in trials. Age of 75 years or more and weight under 60 kilograms call for caution or a reduced dose rather than prohibition. Moderate kidney disease and proton pump inhibitor use matter more for dosing and for clopidogrel interactions than for prasugrel eligibility."}, {"id": 262, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 78 year old woman is choosing between prosthesis types before elective aortic valve replacement and wants to understand the main long term tradeoff of a bioprosthetic valve compared with a mechanical valve. Which statement most accurately characterizes bioprosthetic valves in this decision?", "options": {"A": "They avoid lifelong warfarin but undergo structural deterioration, particularly in younger recipients", "B": "They require the same lifelong anticoagulation intensity that is currently used for mechanical mitral valve prostheses", "C": "They last longer than mechanical valves when implanted in patients under 50", "D": "They carry a lower endocarditis risk because biologic tissue resists bacterial adhesion", "E": "They produce loud metallic closure sounds that patients frequently notice at night"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They avoid lifelong warfarin but undergo structural deterioration, particularly in younger recipients): tissue valves do not require indefinite anticoagulation in patients without another indication, but leaflet calcification and degeneration accelerate in younger patients, who may face reoperation or valve in valve procedures. Mechanical valves are the durable option yet commit the patient to lifelong warfarin, which is why age and bleeding risk drive the shared decision. Bioprostheses carry a broadly similar endocarditis risk to mechanical valves, and both prosthesis types qualify for endocarditis prophylaxis. Metallic closure sounds are a feature of mechanical, not tissue, valves."}, {"id": 263, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 40 year old woman with Eisenmenger syndrome from an unrepaired ventricular septal defect has a hemoglobin of 19.8 g/dL, no hyperviscosity symptoms, and normal iron studies. Her new physician proposes scheduled therapeutic phlebotomy. Which principle should guide the response to secondary erythrocytosis in this population?", "options": {"A": "Routine phlebotomy is avoided because it causes iron deficiency and worsens outcomes", "B": "Phlebotomy should proceed on a fixed schedule to keep hematocrit below fifty", "C": "Erythrocytosis here reflects a myeloproliferative process requiring bone marrow evaluation first", "D": "Anticoagulation replaces phlebotomy as the standard treatment for elevated hemoglobin levels", "E": "Hemodilution with intravenous fluids is preferred once hemoglobin rises above eighteen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Routine phlebotomy is avoided because it causes iron deficiency and worsens outcomes): Erythrocytosis in cyanotic disease is a compensatory response to hypoxemia, and repeated venesection produces iron deficient microcytes that raise viscosity and stroke risk. Phlebotomy is reserved for true hyperviscosity symptoms after dehydration is excluded, with volume replacement given. Fixed hematocrit targets and routine hemodilution lack supporting evidence in this population."}, {"id": 264, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 29 year old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has an echocardiogram showing small verrucous masses on the mitral valve consistent with Libman-Sacks endocarditis. Her physician assistant reviews the character of these lesions before counseling her. Which description accurately characterizes Libman-Sacks vegetations?", "options": {"A": "Rapidly enlarging infected masses that require prolonged intravenous antibiotics and early surgery", "B": "Calcified degenerative nodules confined to the aortic valve of older adults", "C": "Pedunculated benign tumors arising from the atrial septum near the fossa ovalis", "D": "Transient fibrin strands that appear during flares and resolve within days", "E": "Sterile platelet and fibrin vegetations, usually mitral, that can embolize or scar the valve"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Sterile platelet and fibrin vegetations, usually mitral, that can embolize or scar the valve): Libman-Sacks lesions are noninfective deposits favoring the mitral leaflets, associated with lupus and antiphospholipid antibodies, and their importance lies in embolic stroke risk and progressive valvular regurgitation from healing and fibrosis. Infected enlarging masses describe infective endocarditis, which requires antibiotics these sterile lesions do not need. Calcified aortic nodules, septal tumors such as myxomas, and transient fibrin strands describe unrelated entities."}, {"id": 266, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "Three months after a large untreated anterior infarction, a 65 year old man has progressive heart failure. His electrocardiogram shows persistent ST segment elevation in the anterior leads, and echocardiography reveals a thin walled dyskinetic apical segment with a wide neck. Which complication do these findings indicate?", "options": {"A": "A ventricular pseudoaneurysm, a contained rupture communicating through a narrow neck", "B": "Recurrent ST elevation infarction within the territory of the original artery", "C": "Constrictive pericarditis developing after an unrecognized episode of hemorrhagic pericarditis", "D": "Chronic pulmonary embolism producing right ventricular dilation and septal flattening", "E": "A true left ventricular aneurysm formed by thinned scarred myocardium after infarction"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A true left ventricular aneurysm formed by thinned scarred myocardium after infarction): persistent ST elevation months after infarction with a thin dyskinetic segment and a wide neck is the classic picture of a true aneurysm, which contains all layers of scarred wall. A pseudoaneurysm is a contained rupture with a narrow neck, the opposite geometry. Recurrent infarction causes new symptoms and dynamic changes, while constrictive pericarditis and chronic embolism produce right sided rather than these left ventricular findings."}, {"id": 271, "domain": "normal_cardiovascular_anatomy_and_physiology", "question": "An angiography report describes a patient as right dominant, and a student asks what anatomic feature the interventionalist actually used to assign coronary dominance. Which definition correctly describes how dominance is determined when reviewing a diagnostic coronary angiogram in routine clinical practice?", "options": {"A": "The vessel with the largest luminal diameter at its ostium defines dominance", "B": "The artery perfusing the greatest total myocardial mass defines the dominant system", "C": "The artery giving rise to the posterior descending artery defines dominance", "D": "The vessel supplying the sinoatrial node determines the dominant circulation", "E": "The side with more angiographically visible branches is designated dominant"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The artery giving rise to the posterior descending artery defines dominance): dominance is assigned by which vessel supplies the posterior descending artery and the posterolateral branches, with the right coronary doing so in roughly 85 percent of people, the circumflex in about 8 percent, and codominance in the remainder. Ostial caliber and branch counts are not the defining criteria. Total perfused mass actually favors the left system in most hearts despite right dominance, and sinoatrial nodal supply is a separate anatomic question."}, {"id": 272, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "An emergency clinician calculates a Thrombolysis in Myocardial Infarction risk score for a 66 year old woman with a suspected non ST elevation acute coronary syndrome to estimate her short term event risk. Which variable is one of the seven components of this score?", "options": {"A": "Left ventricular ejection fraction measured by transthoracic echocardiography during the admission", "B": "Aspirin use within the preceding 7 days before the current presentation", "C": "Body mass index calculated from the measured height and weight on arrival", "D": "Serum creatinine concentration measured at the time of emergency department arrival", "E": "Systolic blood pressure recorded during the initial set of vital signs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aspirin use within the preceding 7 days before the current presentation): the seven components are age of 65 years or more, three or more coronary risk factors, known stenosis of ≥50 percent, ST deviation, two or more anginal episodes in 24 hours, recent aspirin use, and elevated cardiac markers. Creatinine and systolic blood pressure belong to the GRACE model rather than this score. Ejection fraction and body mass index appear in neither instrument."}, {"id": 274, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 70 year old man with HFrEF weighing 92 kg has been maintained on carvedilol 6.25 mg twice daily for a year without any titration. His resting heart rate is 78 and blood pressure is 128/76. What is the guideline target dose of carvedilol that uptitration should aim to reach?", "options": {"A": "Carvedilol 12.5 mg twice daily, the ceiling dose studied in randomized trials", "B": "Carvedilol 25 mg twice daily, with 50 mg twice daily for patients over 85 kg", "C": "Carvedilol 3.125 mg twice daily, since low dose therapy provides equivalent protection", "D": "Carvedilol 50 mg three times daily when resting heart rate stays above 70", "E": "Carvedilol 25 mg once daily, converting to the extended release formulation at goal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Carvedilol 25 mg twice daily, with 50 mg twice daily for patients over 85 kg): the trial proven target for immediate release carvedilol is 25 mg twice daily, and heavier patients such as this 92 kg man may be titrated to 50 mg twice daily. His heart rate and blood pressure leave clear room for uptitration from the current low dose. Neither 12.5 mg twice daily nor 3.125 mg twice daily is a maintenance target, and three times daily dosing is not how carvedilol is prescribed."}, {"id": 275, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 34 year old man with known Wolff Parkinson White syndrome presents with a rapid, irregular, wide complex tachycardia consistent with preexcited atrial fibrillation. He remains hemodynamically stable while treatment is planned. Which pharmacologic approach is considered appropriate for this rhythm, given the danger of accelerating conduction over the pathway?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous procainamide, which slows conduction over the accessory pathway itself", "B": "Intravenous verapamil, which controls rate by blocking the atrioventricular node", "C": "Intravenous digoxin, which enhances vagal tone and slows atrioventricular nodal conduction", "D": "Intravenous adenosine, which briefly interrupts transmission through the atrioventricular node", "E": "Intravenous diltiazem, which decreases nodal conduction and lowers the ventricular rate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous procainamide, which slows conduction over the accessory pathway itself): In preexcited atrial fibrillation, agents that act on the accessory pathway, such as procainamide or ibutilide, slow the dangerous anterograde conduction, and electrical cardioversion is used if instability develops. Verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin, and adenosine all act preferentially on the AV node, which can shift even more atrial impulses onto the accessory pathway and precipitate ventricular fibrillation. Selective nodal blockade is therefore the wrong target in this specific rhythm despite being routine in ordinary atrial fibrillation."}, {"id": 276, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A cardiology fellow reviews serial echocardiograms of an enlarging pericardial effusion and asks in what order the classic chamber compression signs of tamponade tend to appear as intrapericardial pressure rises. Which sequence correctly describes the earliest echocardiographic collapse finding relative to the later one in evolving tamponade?", "options": {"A": "Right atrial collapse during systole, appearing before right ventricular diastolic collapse", "B": "Left atrial collapse in diastole, appearing before either right sided chamber sign", "C": "Right ventricular diastolic collapse, appearing before detectable right atrial compression occurs", "D": "Left ventricular systolic collapse, appearing before the other chamber compression findings", "E": "Simultaneous four chamber collapse, appearing once pulseless electrical activity has developed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Right atrial collapse during systole, appearing before right ventricular diastolic collapse): the right atrium has the lowest pressure and thinnest wall, so it buckles first when intrapericardial pressure exceeds its pressure during ventricular systole, and right ventricular diastolic collapse follows as pressures climb, being more specific for tamponade. Right ventricular collapse preceding atrial collapse reverses the true order. Left sided chamber collapse is uncommon and late, and four chamber collapse at arrest describes a terminal event rather than the diagnostic sequence."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 58 year old man collapses in the emergency department waiting room. The monitor shows a chaotic, rapidly undulating rhythm without identifiable QRS complexes, and he has no palpable pulse. A code is called and the team looks to you for direction. Which intervention takes priority for this rhythm?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous amiodarone administered before any electrical therapy has been attempted", "B": "Synchronized cardioversion delivered at low energy after brief procedural sedation has been administered", "C": "Transcutaneous pacing initiated promptly at the maximal available output setting", "D": "Intravenous epinephrine given first, followed by a scheduled repeat rhythm check", "E": "Immediate defibrillation with an unsynchronized high energy shock alongside ongoing CPR"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Immediate defibrillation with an unsynchronized high energy shock alongside ongoing CPR): Pulseless chaotic activity without organized QRS complexes is ventricular fibrillation, and survival depends chiefly on minimizing the time to defibrillation while high quality compressions continue. Synchronized cardioversion is impossible because there is no QRS on which to synchronize, and pacing has no role in a fibrillating ventricle. Epinephrine and amiodarone are adjuncts given during ongoing resuscitation, but neither should delay the shock that actually terminates the rhythm."}, {"id": 283, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 61 year old man develops recurrent runs of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia during hospitalization for chest pain. Between runs his ECG shows a normal corrected QT interval. Which underlying cause should be considered first when polymorphic ventricular tachycardia occurs in the presence of a normal baseline QT interval?", "options": {"A": "Congenital long QT syndrome unmasked by recent adrenergic or emotional stress", "B": "Drug induced repolarization delay from a recently added antiarrhythmic medication", "C": "Acute myocardial ischemia, which commonly drives polymorphic tachycardia without QT prolongation", "D": "Severe hypomagnesemia producing early afterdepolarizations and pause dependent arrhythmia initiation", "E": "Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia from a ryanodine receptor mutation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acute myocardial ischemia, which commonly drives polymorphic tachycardia without QT prolongation): Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with a normal QT interval, particularly in a patient with active chest pain, is most often ischemic, and urgent evaluation for revascularization is the priority. Congenital long QT syndrome, drug induced repolarization delay, and hypomagnesemia all characteristically prolong the QT interval and produce torsades de pointes. Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is an inherited condition of younger patients triggered by exertion, an unlikely first consideration in an older man with chest pain."}, {"id": 284, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 19 year old college athlete is referred after an irregular screening examination. He denies syncope or sustained palpitations. You review the rhythm strip shown, recorded at rest in sinus rhythm during his evaluation visit today. Which finding best characterizes the abnormality that is present on this baseline tracing?", "options": {"A": "Progressive PR prolongation ending in a nonconducted P wave each cycle", "B": "Ventricular preexcitation with a short PR interval and a delta wave", "C": "A prolonged corrected QT interval with broad based late peaking T waves", "D": "Complete bundle branch block producing a widened QRS without altered PR timing", "E": "Second degree atrioventricular block with abrupt intermittently dropped ventricular beats"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ventricular preexcitation with a short PR interval and a delta wave): The strip shows sinus P waves followed almost immediately by QRS complexes that begin with a slurred upstroke, indicating that an accessory pathway is activating ventricular muscle before the normal conduction system. Bundle branch block also widens the QRS but preserves a normal PR interval and lacks the slurred initial deflection. Progressive PR prolongation and abruptly dropped beats describe the two forms of second degree block, and QT prolongation affects repolarization rather than the PR segment and QRS onset.", "image": "question_images/q_284.png"}, {"id": 288, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A heart team meeting debates revascularization for a 66 year old man with three vessel coronary disease. The interventional cardiologist presents an angiographic score before any discussion of surgical risk or comorbidity. What does the SYNTAX score primarily quantify when guiding the choice between bypass surgery and percutaneous intervention?", "options": {"A": "The patient's operative mortality risk based on age, renal function, and comorbidities", "B": "The anatomic complexity and extent of coronary disease on the diagnostic angiogram", "C": "The probability that a stented lesion will develop restenosis within one year", "D": "The total burden of ischemia measured during vasodilator stress perfusion imaging", "E": "The completeness of revascularization achieved after the chosen procedure has finished"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The anatomic complexity and extent of coronary disease on the diagnostic angiogram): the SYNTAX score grades the anatomic complexity, extent, and location of coronary disease from the angiogram to inform the surgery versus stenting decision. Operative mortality is estimated by surgical risk models rather than by SYNTAX, and the score does not measure restenosis probability, ischemic burden on perfusion imaging, or completeness of revascularization afterward. Higher scores favor surgery while lower scores make stenting more reasonable."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 74 year old woman with atrial fibrillation and a mechanical mitral valve prosthesis asks whether she can switch from warfarin to a direct oral anticoagulant for convenience. Which statement best reflects current guideline direction on anticoagulant selection for patients who have her specific type of valve prosthesis?", "options": {"A": "Apixaban is preferred over warfarin because of its lower intracranial bleeding rates", "B": "Dabigatran is an acceptable substitute provided her renal function remains within the normal laboratory limits", "C": "Rivaroxaban may replace warfarin once the prosthesis has been in place a year", "D": "Warfarin remains the recommended anticoagulant, since direct agents failed trials in mechanical valves", "E": "Low dose aspirin combined with clopidogrel is a reasonable alternative to warfarin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Warfarin remains the recommended anticoagulant, since direct agents failed trials in mechanical valves): Patients with mechanical heart valves, and those with moderate to severe rheumatic mitral stenosis, should receive a vitamin K antagonist rather than a direct oral anticoagulant. Dabigatran was studied against warfarin in mechanical valve patients and produced more thromboembolic and bleeding events, and the factor Xa inhibitors have no supporting trial data in this population. Antiplatelet combinations do not provide adequate protection against valve thrombosis or cardioembolic stroke in this setting."}, {"id": 293, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 69 year old man with recurrent congestion despite adherent oral furosemide use is switched to torsemide by his heart failure clinician, who cites a pharmacokinetic advantage relevant to bowel wall edema. Which property distinguishes torsemide from furosemide and motivates this substitution in patients with persistent volume overload?", "options": {"A": "Torsemide acts primarily in the distal tubule, a segment spared by bowel wall edema", "B": "Torsemide requires no renal excretion, keeping its potency stable in kidney disease", "C": "Torsemide has more predictable oral absorption, with bioavailability near 80 to 100 percent", "D": "Torsemide binds albumin poorly, allowing greater free drug delivery to the nephron", "E": "Torsemide carries potassium sparing properties that reduce requirements for oral supplementation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Torsemide has more predictable oral absorption, with bioavailability near 80 to 100 percent): oral furosemide absorption ranges roughly from 10 to 100 percent and worsens with gut edema, whereas torsemide is absorbed reliably and also lasts longer. Both drugs remain loop agents acting at the thick ascending limb, not the distal tubule. Torsemide is still highly protein bound and renally active like other loops, and it spares no potassium, so supplementation needs are unchanged."}, {"id": 296, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "During a quality review, a fellow asks you to restate the standard definitions used for ventricular tachycardia on rhythm recordings, including what separates nonsustained from sustained episodes. Which statement gives the accepted rate and duration criteria commonly used for these two categories of ventricular tachycardia on monitoring?", "options": {"A": "Three or more beats above 60 per minute; sustained means beyond ten seconds", "B": "Five or more beats above 120 per minute; sustained means beyond two minutes", "C": "Three or more beats above 100 per minute; sustained lasts at least 30 seconds", "D": "Ten or more beats above 100 per minute; sustained means recurrence within one day", "E": "Any wide QRS run above 150 per minute persisting for over five seconds"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Three or more beats above 100 per minute; sustained lasts at least 30 seconds): Ventricular tachycardia is conventionally defined as three or more consecutive ventricular beats at a rate above 100 per minute, and an episode is called sustained when it lasts at least 30 seconds or requires earlier termination for hemodynamic compromise. Definitions built on 60 or 120 per minute, five or ten beat minimums, or two minute and one day markers do not match the accepted convention. A rate of 150 per minute for five seconds describes neither the standard rate floor nor the sustained threshold."}, {"id": 309, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 70 year old man with refractory angina is considered for ranolazine. His medication list includes several other agents, and the pharmacist screens for combinations that would preclude starting the drug. Which coadministered medication is a contraindication to ranolazine because of its effect on ranolazine metabolism?", "options": {"A": "Metoprolol succinate, which competes directly with ranolazine for shared renal tubular secretion pathways", "B": "Clarithromycin, a strong CYP3A inhibitor that markedly raises ranolazine plasma concentrations", "C": "Atorvastatin, which saturates the intestinal absorption transporters shared with ranolazine", "D": "Sublingual nitroglycerin, which potentiates the systemic vasodilation produced by ranolazine", "E": "Losartan, which displaces ranolazine from albumin and doubles free drug levels"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clarithromycin, a strong CYP3A inhibitor that markedly raises ranolazine plasma concentrations): strong CYP3A inhibitors such as clarithromycin and ketoconazole markedly raise ranolazine exposure and are contraindicated because high concentrations prolong the QT interval. Metoprolol, atorvastatin, nitroglycerin, and losartan do not block ranolazine metabolism in a prohibited way, although ranolazine itself can raise simvastatin and digoxin levels. Moderate inhibitors such as diltiazem require a lower ranolazine maximum rather than complete avoidance."}, {"id": 312, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "During bedside teaching, you demonstrate dynamic auscultation in a patient with mitral valve prolapse by having her squat promptly from a standing position while you auscultate the apex. Which effect does squatting, which raises both venous return and afterload, have on the click and murmur?", "options": {"A": "The click arrives earlier and the murmur lengthens as the ventricle gets smaller", "B": "The murmur becomes continuous, spilling through the second heart sound into diastole", "C": "The click moves later in systole and the murmur becomes shorter in duration", "D": "The click splits into two components as both leaflets prolapse at different times", "E": "The murmur grows markedly louder while the click becomes muffled and difficult to hear"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The click moves later in systole and the murmur becomes shorter in duration): squatting increases venous return and afterload, enlarging the left ventricle so the leaflet does not prolapse until later in ejection. The delayed click leaves less systole for regurgitation, so the murmur shortens. An earlier click with a longer murmur is the standing and Valsalva response, when the ventricle is smaller. The sounds remain confined to systole, and a split click or selective muffling is not the expected maneuver response."}, {"id": 313, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "The 77 year old man with suspected cardiac amyloidosis undergoes technetium pyrophosphate scintigraphy, which shows grade 2 myocardial uptake. His nurse practitioner recalls that this scan can confirm transthyretin amyloidosis only under one essential condition. Which additional step is required before accepting this result as diagnostic of ATTR disease?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the scan after 6 months to demonstrate stable or progressive myocardial uptake", "B": "Perform coronary angiography to exclude ischemic uptake mimicking infiltrative myocardial disease", "C": "Exclude a plasma cell disorder using serum free light chains and immunofixation studies", "D": "Obtain a fat pad biopsy, which rules out cardiac involvement when negative", "E": "Document a family history of amyloidosis in at least one first degree relative"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Exclude a plasma cell disorder using serum free light chains and immunofixation studies): light chain amyloidosis can occasionally produce positive pyrophosphate scans, and missing it delays chemotherapy for a rapidly fatal disease, so serum free light chains plus serum and urine immunofixation must be normal before grade 2 or 3 uptake is accepted as diagnostic of transthyretin disease. When a monoclonal protein is found, tissue biopsy with typing is required instead. Repeat imaging, coronary angiography, family history, and fat pad sampling do not resolve this specific diagnostic fork."}, {"id": 315, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 59 year old man with insulin treated diabetes has angina and angiography showing severe three vessel coronary disease that is anatomically suitable for either revascularization strategy. His surgical risk is acceptable and his ejection fraction is 50 percent. Which statement reflects the best supported revascularization approach for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Percutaneous intervention is preferred because it avoids the wound healing risks of diabetes", "B": "Either strategy produces equivalent survival, so patient preference alone should decide here", "C": "Bypass surgery is favored, with lower mortality and fewer infarctions in trials", "D": "Medical therapy without revascularization is superior when the diabetes is insulin treated", "E": "Hybrid revascularization is the documented first choice for diabetic multivessel disease"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Bypass surgery is favored, with lower mortality and fewer infarctions in trials): randomized evidence in diabetic multivessel disease shows bypass surgery lowers mortality and myocardial infarction compared with stenting, at the cost of more early strokes. Survival is therefore not equivalent between strategies, medical therapy alone is inferior for symptomatic three vessel disease, and hybrid procedures lack comparable outcome evidence. Wound healing concerns influence surgical technique but do not overturn the survival advantage."}, {"id": 322, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 47 year old smoker describes recurrent chest pain that wakes her between midnight and early morning, resolves within minutes of sublingual nitroglycerin, and almost never occurs during exertion. Ambulatory monitoring during one episode shows transient ST segment elevation that resolves with the pain. Which diagnosis do these features most strongly suggest?", "options": {"A": "Unstable angina from a ruptured plaque in the proximal left anterior descending", "B": "Gastroesophageal reflux triggered by recumbency and relieved coincidentally by the nitroglycerin", "C": "Microvascular angina with elevated microcirculatory resistance and preserved epicardial vessel caliber", "D": "Vasospastic angina with transient occlusive spasm of an epicardial coronary artery", "E": "Takotsubo syndrome precipitated by recurrent nocturnal emotional stress and catecholamine surge"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Vasospastic angina with transient occlusive spasm of an epicardial coronary artery): rest pain clustering between midnight and morning, prompt nitrate response, and transient ST elevation during episodes are the classic signature of epicardial coronary spasm. Plaque rupture typically produces crescendo pain not confined to night, reflux does not cause ST elevation, and microvascular angina is usually exertional without ST elevation. Takotsubo produces a persistent wall motion abnormality rather than brief self terminating episodes."}, {"id": 325, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A cardiology fellow asks a nurse practitioner to summarize the five clinical groups of pulmonary hypertension with a representative example of each. The fellow is preparing a teaching conference on the World Health Organization classification for the coming week. Which pairing correctly matches a classification group with an appropriate example condition from that group?", "options": {"A": "Group 1 includes pulmonary hypertension arising from chronic hypoxic lung disease such as longstanding severe emphysema", "B": "Group 3 comprises pulmonary hypertension from interstitial lung disease and chronic obstructive disease", "C": "Group 2 covers chronic thromboembolic obstruction of the proximal pulmonary arteries", "D": "Group 4 describes left heart failure transmitting pressure into the pulmonary veins", "E": "Group 5 is limited to connective tissue disease associated pulmonary arterial hypertension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Group 3 comprises pulmonary hypertension from interstitial lung disease and chronic obstructive disease): the classification assigns pulmonary arterial hypertension including idiopathic and connective tissue forms to group 1, left heart disease to group 2, lung disease and hypoxia to group 3, chronic thromboembolic disease to group 4, and multifactorial conditions such as sarcoidosis to group 5. Emphysema therefore belongs in group 3 rather than group 1. Chronic thromboembolic obstruction defines group 4, left heart transmission defines group 2, and connective tissue disease sits within group 1 rather than group 5."}, {"id": 327, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 70 year old man has asymptomatic Mobitz type II second degree atrioventricular block found on preoperative evaluation, with no reversible cause identified on careful review. A colleague asks why this rhythm is managed differently from Wenckebach block. Which statement best captures the standard teaching about this conduction lesion?", "options": {"A": "It usually reflects block occurring within the atrioventricular node and resolves with careful observation", "B": "It is driven mainly by vagal tone and tends to improve with exercise", "C": "It carries a benign prognosis provided the QRS complexes remain narrow", "D": "It responds durably to atropine, which restores stable one to one conduction", "E": "It usually reflects infranodal disease and warrants permanent pacing even without symptoms"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (It usually reflects infranodal disease and warrants permanent pacing even without symptoms): Mobitz type II block typically arises below the AV node in the His Purkinje system, carries a substantial risk of abrupt progression to complete heart block with an unreliable escape, and is a pacing indication even in asymptomatic patients once reversible causes are excluded. Block within the node driven by vagal tone that improves with exercise describes Wenckebach physiology. Atropine often fails or worsens infranodal block, and a narrow QRS does not confer a benign prognosis for this lesion."}, {"id": 329, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A physician assistant is writing the prescription for dapagliflozin in a 61 year old woman with HFrEF, an ejection fraction of 32 percent, and an eGFR of 48. She wants to use the same regimen studied in the pivotal heart failure outcome trial. Which dosing regimen should be prescribed for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Dapagliflozin 5 mg once daily, titrated monthly toward a 20 mg maximum", "B": "Dapagliflozin 10 mg once daily, the fixed dose studied in the outcome trial", "C": "Dapagliflozin 10 mg twice daily, matching the schedule of the diabetes program", "D": "Dapagliflozin 2.5 mg daily, reduced because her eGFR falls below 60", "E": "Dapagliflozin 25 mg every other day, adjusted for her measured renal clearance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dapagliflozin 10 mg once daily, the fixed dose studied in the outcome trial): DAPA HF used a single fixed dose of 10 mg daily with no titration scheme, and the heart failure indication carries that same dose. Her eGFR of 48 requires no dose reduction, since the trial enrolled patients down to an eGFR of 30. Twice daily dosing, alternate day dosing, and monthly uptitration are not part of any approved heart failure regimen for this drug."}, {"id": 331, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 57 year old man describes exertional chest pressure that began occurring at rest over the past 48 hours, lasting up to 15 minutes per episode. Serial high sensitivity troponin values remain below the 99th percentile and his electrocardiogram shows transient ST depression. Which diagnosis applies?", "options": {"A": "Unstable angina, new rest angina in an acute coronary syndrome without myocyte necrosis", "B": "Non ST elevation myocardial infarction with a diagnostic rise in cardiac troponin", "C": "Stable angina, predictable exertional symptoms unchanged in pattern for several months", "D": "Vasospastic angina, transient ST elevation occurring at night with preserved exercise capacity", "E": "Gastroesophageal reflux, burning discomfort provoked by meals and relieved by antacids"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Unstable angina, new rest angina in an acute coronary syndrome without myocyte necrosis): ischemic symptoms occurring at rest with dynamic ST depression but troponin staying under the 99th percentile define unstable angina. A diagnostic troponin rise would reclassify the event as non ST elevation infarction, and a stable unchanged exertional pattern describes stable angina. Vasospastic angina produces transient ST elevation rather than depression, and reflux does not cause ischemic electrocardiographic changes."}, {"id": 332, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 63 year old man with a bileaflet mechanical aortic valve implanted two years ago develops persistent atrial fibrillation. His warfarin has been managed to an INR target of 2.5 since the surgery. Which of the following is the most appropriate adjustment to his anticoagulation?", "options": {"A": "Switch him to apixaban now that atrial fibrillation is the dominant indication", "B": "Continue the current INR target because the aortic prosthesis governs intensity", "C": "Add clopidogrel to warfarin while keeping the existing INR target unchanged", "D": "Raise the warfarin target INR to 3.0 given the added risk factor", "E": "Add full dose aspirin and recheck the INR at the usual interval"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Raise the warfarin target INR to 3.0 given the added risk factor): atrial fibrillation is a thromboembolic risk factor that raises the recommended INR target to 3.0 for a mechanical aortic valve. Direct oral anticoagulants remain contraindicated with any mechanical prosthesis. Adding antiplatelet agents increases bleeding without substituting for the higher anticoagulation intensity the combined risk requires."}, {"id": 336, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 46 year old woman with human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 positive breast cancer is starting a planned year of trastuzumab after completing anthracycline therapy. Her cardio-oncology physician assistant designs cardiac monitoring. Which surveillance plan is most appropriate during trastuzumab treatment?", "options": {"A": "A single baseline echocardiogram, with repeat imaging reserved solely for new cardiac symptoms", "B": "Monthly cardiac magnetic resonance imaging throughout the entire treatment year", "C": "Troponin measurement before each dose in place of any echocardiographic imaging", "D": "Echocardiography at baseline and roughly every three months while therapy continues", "E": "Annual imaging beginning five years after the final trastuzumab infusion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Echocardiography at baseline and roughly every three months while therapy continues): trastuzumab produces a largely reversible decline in ejection fraction that develops during treatment, so serial imaging about every three months allows dysfunction to be caught, the drug held, and heart failure therapy started before irreversible injury. Baseline imaging alone misses the interval declines that are typically asymptomatic at first. Monthly magnetic resonance is impractical and unnecessary, troponin cannot replace functional imaging for this agent, and starting surveillance years after completion inverts the risk window."}, {"id": 337, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 65 year old woman with symptomatic paroxysmal atrial fibrillation and normal ventricular function is to begin sotalol for rhythm control. Her creatinine clearance is 65 mL/min and baseline corrected QT interval is 430 ms. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to initiating this drug?", "options": {"A": "Begin at home with a follow up electrocardiogram at the three month visit", "B": "Initiate with electrocardiographic monitoring and QT measurement after each dose titration", "C": "Begin therapy only after a cardioverter defibrillator is implanted to protect against drug induced proarrhythmia", "D": "Load intravenously in clinic and transition to oral dosing the same day", "E": "Start together with low dose amiodarone to improve overall rhythm control"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Initiate with electrocardiographic monitoring and QT measurement after each dose titration): sotalol prolongs the QT interval in dose dependent fashion, so initiation is performed with serial electrocardiograms, typically during inpatient monitoring, with dose adjustment guided by the measured interval and renal function. Unmonitored home initiation with a distant follow up tracing misses early torsades risk. A prophylactic defibrillator, a same day intravenous load, and combination with amiodarone are each inappropriate ways to begin therapy."}, {"id": 341, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 66 year old man undergoes transcarotid artery revascularization with stent placement for symptomatic internal carotid stenosis. The procedure is uncomplicated, and he is ready for discharge on the following day. Which antiplatelet strategy is recommended after carotid stenting to protect against early stent related thrombotic events?", "options": {"A": "Aspirin alone at low dose, continued indefinitely without an additional agent", "B": "Warfarin bridged with heparin until the international normalized ratio stabilizes", "C": "Clopidogrel alone for two weeks, followed by lifelong low dose aspirin", "D": "Ticagrelor with rivaroxaban for one month, then single antiplatelet therapy", "E": "Aspirin plus clopidogrel for at least one month, then long term single therapy"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Aspirin plus clopidogrel for at least one month, then long term single therapy): dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and clopidogrel is standard after carotid stenting, continued for at least 30 days while the stent endothelializes, after which a single agent is maintained indefinitely. Aspirin monotherapy from the start leaves the fresh stent underprotected against platelet mediated thrombosis. Warfarin and anticoagulant containing combinations add bleeding risk without evidence in this setting, and clopidogrel alone for two weeks is shorter and narrower than the studied regimens."}, {"id": 342, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 66 year old man with an acute inferior infarction becomes hypotensive with a heart rate near forty and sinus rhythm on the monitor. He is diaphoretic and lightheaded while reperfusion therapy is being arranged. Intravenous access is in place. Which of the following is the most appropriate first line therapy?", "options": {"A": "Immediate transvenous pacemaker placement before any pharmacologic therapy is tried", "B": "Intravenous atropine in incremental doses while monitoring rate and perfusion", "C": "Intravenous epinephrine infusion started at a moderate dose without other measures", "D": "Isoproterenol infusion titrated upward until the systolic pressure normalizes", "E": "A fluid bolus alone with reassessment of the heart rate afterward"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous atropine in incremental doses while monitoring rate and perfusion): symptomatic sinus bradycardia in inferior infarction usually reflects heightened vagal tone, which responds to atropine as the recommended first drug. Transvenous pacing is reserved for atropine refractory bradycardia or high grade block. Epinephrine and isoproterenol raise myocardial oxygen demand during active ischemia and follow atropine failure, and a fluid bolus does not correct the chronotropic problem."}, {"id": 344, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 68 year old woman with hypertension controlled on two medications is found to have 70 percent atherosclerotic stenosis of one renal artery during imaging performed for another indication. Her creatinine is stable and her blood pressure remains reasonably controlled. What is the recommended initial management of this incidental lesion?", "options": {"A": "Renal artery stenting performed now to prevent progression toward eventual ischemic nephropathy over time", "B": "Surgical bypass of the stenosis before any trial of additional medication", "C": "Discontinuing renin angiotensin blockade to protect perfusion of the affected kidney", "D": "Optimized medical therapy including a renin angiotensin inhibitor, statin, and antiplatelet agent", "E": "Repeat invasive angiography with pressure gradient measurement every six months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Optimized medical therapy including a renin angiotensin inhibitor, statin, and antiplatelet agent): randomized trials including CORAL showed that routine stenting adds no benefit over medical therapy for atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis, so treatment starts with a renin angiotensin inhibitor, statin, and antiplatelet therapy with monitoring of kidney function. Prophylactic stenting and surgical bypass are reserved for specific failures of medical management, such as refractory hypertension or recurrent flash pulmonary edema. Renin angiotensin blockade is beneficial rather than harmful in unilateral disease, and serial invasive angiography exposes her to risk without guiding therapy."}, {"id": 346, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 78 year old man describes angina and dyspnea when climbing one flight of stairs. Echocardiography demonstrates a heavily calcified aortic valve, peak velocity 4.5 m/s, mean gradient 47 mmHg, and valve area 0.7 cm², with an ejection fraction of 60 percent. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Repeat transthoracic echocardiography in six months to reassess gradients and symptoms", "B": "Begin an angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor and reassess symptoms in three months", "C": "Refer for aortic valve replacement after heart team evaluation of intervention options", "D": "Order an exercise treadmill test to quantify his functional capacity objectively", "E": "Start high intensity statin therapy to slow progression of valve calcification"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer for aortic valve replacement after heart team evaluation of intervention options): symptomatic severe high gradient aortic stenosis carries a class 1 indication for valve replacement, and exertional angina with dyspnea qualifies. Watchful waiting, vasodilator therapy, and statins do not change outcomes once symptoms appear in severe stenosis. Exercise testing is contraindicated when severe stenosis is already clearly symptomatic."}, {"id": 347, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "Three weeks after drug-eluting stent placement for NSTEMI, a 61 year old man reports disabling dyspnea on ticagrelor with normal cardiac and pulmonary evaluations. The team decides to switch him to clopidogrel. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to make this switch?", "options": {"A": "Start clopidogrel 75 mg daily beginning with the next scheduled ticagrelor dose", "B": "Stop ticagrelor for five days, then begin clopidogrel 75 mg daily", "C": "Give clopidogrel 600 mg 24 hours after the last ticagrelor dose, then 75 mg daily", "D": "Stop all P2Y12 therapy and continue aspirin alone for the remaining year", "E": "Add clopidogrel to ticagrelor for one full overlapping week before finally stopping the ticagrelor"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give clopidogrel 600 mg 24 hours after the last ticagrelor dose, then 75 mg daily): ticagrelor's effect fades quickly, so a de-escalation switch in the first month requires a full clopidogrel load timed 24 hours after the final ticagrelor dose to avoid a gap in platelet inhibition. Starting maintenance dosing without a load, or interposing a five day washout, leaves a fresh stent underprotected. Dropping to aspirin alone this early risks stent thrombosis, and overlapping two P2Y12 inhibitors adds bleeding without benefit."}, {"id": 351, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "An active 69 year old woman with severe aortic stenosis and an ejection fraction of 62 percent undergoes supervised treadmill testing. At four minutes she develops dyspnea, and her systolic blood pressure falls from 138 to 118 mmHg despite increasing workload. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Continue annual echocardiographic surveillance since she remains asymptomatic in daily life", "B": "Refer for aortic valve replacement based on the abnormal exercise response", "C": "Repeat the treadmill test in six months to confirm the abnormal finding", "D": "Begin a beta blocker and reassess her exercise tolerance in clinic", "E": "Order dobutamine stress echocardiography to clarify the hemodynamic significance further"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for aortic valve replacement based on the abnormal exercise response): exercise induced symptoms or a fall in systolic blood pressure converts asymptomatic severe stenosis into an indication for valve replacement. Continued surveillance ignores a demonstrated abnormal response that predicts adverse events, and repeating the test simply delays definitive care. Dobutamine testing is reserved for low flow low gradient stenosis with reduced ejection fraction, which she does not have."}, {"id": 353, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 60 year old man suffered an anterior myocardial infarction three weeks ago, underwent primary percutaneous revascularization, and now has an ejection fraction of 28 percent on guideline directed therapy. He is asymptomatic at rest and walking daily. His cardiologist considers defibrillator therapy for primary prevention. Which of the following is the most appropriate plan?", "options": {"A": "Implant a defibrillator this week while the infarct scar is still recent", "B": "Repeat the ejection fraction at least 40 days after the infarction", "C": "Order an exercise stress test to determine whether a device is needed", "D": "Schedule device implantation to coincide with his next annual examination", "E": "Start amiodarone as a substitute for any future defibrillator consideration"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the ejection fraction at least 40 days after the infarction): primary prevention defibrillator implantation is deferred until at least 40 days after myocardial infarction because early implantation trials showed no survival benefit and ventricular function often recovers with revascularization and medical therapy. Implanting during the first weeks therefore commits some patients to devices they will not need. A stress test does not determine device candidacy, waiting for an annual visit imposes an arbitrary delay beyond the reassessment window, and amiodarone does not substitute for defibrillator protection."}, {"id": 355, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 69 year old man with atrial fibrillation, a CHA2DS2-VASc score of 3, and normal renal function takes apixaban. He is scheduled for elective spinal surgery with high bleeding risk. He has no mechanical valve or recent stroke. Which of the following is the most appropriate perioperative anticoagulation plan?", "options": {"A": "Continue apixaban through surgery given the higher priority of stroke prevention", "B": "Stop apixaban seven days before surgery and restart one month afterward", "C": "Stop apixaban and bridge with therapeutic enoxaparin until the night before", "D": "Switch to warfarin two weeks before surgery for easier perioperative reversal", "E": "Hold apixaban about 48 hours preoperatively and resume when hemostasis allows"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Hold apixaban about 48 hours preoperatively and resume when hemostasis allows): direct oral anticoagulants have short half lives, so a roughly 48 hour hold before a high bleeding risk procedure with normal renal function provides adequate washout, and heparin bridging increases bleeding without reducing stroke in atrial fibrillation. Continuing through spinal surgery risks catastrophic bleeding into a closed space. A seven day hold with a one month gap is excessive, and a warfarin switch complicates rather than simplifies management."}, {"id": 358, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 66 year old man undergoes on pump bypass surgery with three grafts and returns from the operating room with minimal chest tube drainage and stable hemodynamics. The intensive care team plans antiplatelet therapy to protect early graft patency. When should aspirin be given after this operation?", "options": {"A": "Within six hours of surgery once significant ongoing bleeding has been excluded", "B": "After the chest tubes are removed, typically on the second postoperative day", "C": "At the two week wound check, once sternal healing has been confirmed", "D": "Only after platelet function testing demonstrates full recovery from cardiopulmonary bypass", "E": "Deferred to one month, because early aspirin adds bleeding without patency benefit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Within six hours of surgery once significant ongoing bleeding has been excluded): aspirin within six hours of surgery, once bleeding is excluded, improves early graft patency and reduces ischemic events. Waiting for chest tube removal, the two week wound check, or platelet function recovery sacrifices the interval when grafts are most vulnerable to thrombosis. Deferring a month on bleeding grounds misstates the evidence, which shows early aspirin is safe in the absence of active hemorrhage."}, {"id": 361, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 69 year old man with HFrEF began spironolactone five weeks ago alongside ARNI, beta blocker, and SGLT2 inhibitor therapy. He is asymptomatic with stable creatinine of 1.4 mg/dL and blood pressure of 116/74 mm Hg. His serial potassium values appear in the trend shown. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Hold spironolactone now, counsel on dietary potassium, and recheck the level soon", "B": "Continue spironolactone unchanged at the current dose because he remains free of any symptoms", "C": "Stop the SGLT2 inhibitor since it is the most recently marketed agent", "D": "Increase the loop diuretic dose while continuing spironolactone at 25 mg", "E": "Repeat the potassium in three months while keeping every dose the same"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hold spironolactone now, counsel on dietary potassium, and recheck the level soon): the trend shown crosses above 5.5 mEq/L, the threshold at which a mineralocorticoid antagonist should be held rather than merely reduced. Continuing the drug unchanged or delaying the recheck for months risks dangerous hyperkalemia on a clearly rising trajectory. The SGLT2 inhibitor does not raise potassium and may even lower hyperkalemia risk, and escalating the loop diuretic in a euvolemic patient is not an appropriate potassium management strategy.", "image": "question_images/q_361.png"}, {"id": 364, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 49 year old accountant had an uncomplicated NSTEMI treated with a single drug-eluting stent. Before discharge, his ejection fraction is normal, he walks the ward without symptoms, and he asks when he can return to his sedentary desk job. Which of the following is the most appropriate counseling?", "options": {"A": "Remain off work for three months to allow complete myocardial scar maturation", "B": "Return only after a normal maximal exercise test formally clears him", "C": "Return the day after discharge, since desk work carries no cardiac demand", "D": "Return within about two weeks if he remains asymptomatic during ordinary activity", "E": "Retire from accounting, since workplace stress independently causes recurrent infarction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Return within about two weeks if he remains asymptomatic during ordinary activity): after an uncomplicated infarction with preserved ventricular function, sedentary work can typically resume within roughly two weeks, alongside cardiac rehabilitation. A three month absence or retirement medicalizes recovery far beyond the evidence, and maximal exercise testing is not required to clear a low risk patient for desk work. Returning the day after discharge shortchanges early recovery, medication titration, and symptom surveillance."}, {"id": 365, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 24 year old man with genetically confirmed Marfan syndrome has an aortic root measuring 4.1 centimeters, well below his surgical threshold. He asks what ongoing care is needed to reduce his future risk of aortic dissection. Which surveillance and medical strategy is recommended for him at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Annual aortic imaging plus beta blocker or angiotensin receptor blocker therapy", "B": "Imaging repeated every five years, with medication started once growth appears", "C": "Immediate prophylactic root replacement, since dissection risk is independent of diameter", "D": "Exercise restriction alone, since medications lack evidence for slowing aortic growth", "E": "Serial d dimer measurement, with imaging reserved for rising laboratory values"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Annual aortic imaging plus beta blocker or angiotensin receptor blocker therapy): patients with Marfan syndrome need annual imaging of the aortic root and prophylactic medical therapy, and both beta blockers and angiotensin receptor blockers slow aortic root growth in trials. A five year imaging interval could miss the growth that changes surgical timing, while prophylactic replacement at 4.1 centimeters would expose him to operative risk years early, since dissection risk clearly rises with diameter. Activity guidance complements rather than replaces medication, and d dimer has no surveillance role in aneurysm care."}, {"id": 367, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A general cardiologist newly inherits several adults with congenital heart disease and wants to triage who needs care coordinated through a specialized adult congenital heart disease center. According to guideline recommendations on care delivery, which of the following patients most clearly requires such specialized center involvement?", "options": {"A": "A woman with a Fontan circulation presenting for routine ongoing cardiac care", "B": "A man with a repaired secundum defect, normal chambers, and no symptoms", "C": "A woman with a tiny muscular ventricular septal defect and normal pressures", "D": "A man with an isolated bicuspid aortic valve and a normal aorta", "E": "A woman with mitral valve prolapse discovered incidentally on preoperative echocardiography"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A woman with a Fontan circulation presenting for routine ongoing cardiac care): Single ventricle palliation represents anatomic and physiologic complexity of the highest category, and guidelines advise that such patients receive ongoing management with adult congenital specialists. Repaired simple defects without residua and small restrictive shunts can reasonably be followed in general cardiology. Bicuspid valves with normal aortas and mitral prolapse are managed routinely outside specialized centers."}, {"id": 368, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 42 year old man with subacute fever and a large pericardial effusion undergoes diagnostic pericardiocentesis, and you review the laboratory results shown from the pericardial fluid analysis. He recently immigrated from a high tuberculosis burden country. Which diagnosis do these fluid results most strongly support in this clinical context?", "options": {"A": "Uremic pericarditis producing a transudative effusion from retained nitrogenous solutes in the circulation", "B": "Viral pericarditis with a bland effusion expected to resolve without therapy", "C": "Tuberculous pericarditis, supported by a lymphocytic exudate with elevated adenosine deaminase", "D": "Simple parapneumonic irritation with reactive mesothelial cells and normal chemistry", "E": "Hypothyroid effusion accumulating slowly from myxedema related capillary permeability"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tuberculous pericarditis, supported by a lymphocytic exudate with elevated adenosine deaminase): the laboratory results shown demonstrate an adenosine deaminase of 68 U/L, a protein rich lymphocyte predominant exudate, and low fluid glucose, a pattern highly suggestive of tuberculosis in a patient from an endemic region. Uremic and hypothyroid effusions are typically transudative without adenosine deaminase elevation. A bland viral effusion would not produce these chemistry abnormalities, and a simple reactive process shows normal chemistry.", "image": "question_images/q_368.png"}, {"id": 369, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 61 year old man with severe primary mitral regurgitation from degenerative posterior leaflet prolapse reports exertional dyspnea for three months. Echocardiography shows an ejection fraction of 56 percent and left ventricular end systolic diameter 44 mm. His surgical risk is low. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Transcatheter edge to edge repair as first line therapy for degenerative disease", "B": "Guideline directed heart failure medical therapy with echocardiographic reassessment planned in six months", "C": "Mitral valve replacement with a bioprosthesis as the preferred initial operation", "D": "Surgical mitral repair at a center experienced in degenerative valve reconstruction", "E": "Serial natriuretic peptide monitoring with surgery when the levels begin rising"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Surgical mitral repair at a center experienced in degenerative valve reconstruction): symptomatic severe primary mitral regurgitation is a class 1 indication for intervention, and surgical repair is preferred over replacement for degenerative disease because it preserves the subvalvular apparatus and avoids prosthetic complications. Transcatheter repair is reserved for patients at high or prohibitive surgical risk. Medical therapy or biomarker surveillance merely defers definitive correction."}, {"id": 370, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 31 year old woman with a mechanical mitral valve is six weeks pregnant. Her INR has been stable on warfarin 4 mg daily. After counseling about fetal and maternal risks she asks which strategy protects her valve best. Which of the following is the most appropriate first trimester plan?", "options": {"A": "Switch to dose adjusted low molecular weight heparin for the first trimester", "B": "Switch to an unfractionated heparin infusion in hospital until the second trimester", "C": "Continue warfarin at her current dose with close INR monitoring", "D": "Switch to apixaban since it crosses the placenta in small amounts", "E": "Stop anticoagulation until twelve weeks and restart warfarin in the second trimester"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue warfarin at her current dose with close INR monitoring): when the warfarin dose is 5 mg daily or less, continuing warfarin through the first trimester is reasonable because embryopathy risk is low at that dose and warfarin protects the valve more reliably than heparin regimens. Heparin strategies are preferred when the dose exceeds 5 mg. Apixaban is contraindicated with mechanical valves, and stopping anticoagulation invites valve thrombosis."}, {"id": 371, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 42 year old woman with mitral valve prolapse and moderate mitral regurgitation, no prosthetic material, and no history of endocarditis is scheduled for routine dental cleaning with probing of the gingival margins. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to endocarditis prophylaxis?", "options": {"A": "Amoxicillin 2 g orally before the appointment given her regurgitant lesion", "B": "Clindamycin 600 mg orally because streptococci colonize prolapsed valve tissue", "C": "No antibiotic prophylaxis because her lesion is not a high risk condition", "D": "Azithromycin 500 mg the night before and the morning of cleaning", "E": "Amoxicillin before this visit and before any future gastrointestinal endoscopy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No antibiotic prophylaxis because her lesion is not a high risk condition): current recommendations restrict prophylaxis to the highest risk conditions, such as prosthetic valves or prosthetic material, prior endocarditis, certain congenital lesions, and transplant valvulopathy, and native valve prolapse with regurgitation is not among them. An antibiotic here exposes her to adverse effects without expected benefit. Prophylaxis for gastrointestinal endoscopy was abandoned even for high risk patients."}, {"id": 372, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 79 year old man with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction presents with two plus lower extremity edema, orthopnea, and a five pound weight gain over one week. Blood pressure is 148/84 mm Hg and oxygen saturation is 93 percent. He takes an SGLT2 inhibitor and losartan. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Increase the losartan dose to address the volume overload through afterload reduction", "B": "Add a beta blocker to lengthen diastole and improve ventricular filling", "C": "Start amlodipine to lower the blood pressure and relieve the orthopnea", "D": "Order cardiopulmonary exercise testing before adjusting any of his medications", "E": "Begin a loop diuretic to relieve congestion and titrate toward euvolemia"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Begin a loop diuretic to relieve congestion and titrate toward euvolemia): symptomatic volume overload in HFpEF is treated with diuretics regardless of the underlying regimen, since congestion drives his orthopnea, edema, and hypoxemia. Raising losartan or adding amlodipine addresses blood pressure without removing the excess sodium and water. A beta blocker does not decongest and can blunt the chronotropic response many HFpEF patients rely on, and exercise testing has no role while he is actively congested."}, {"id": 374, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 71 year old man with a bioprosthetic mitral valve implanted three years ago develops mild exertional dyspnea. Echocardiography shows a rising transmitral gradient and thickened leaflets with restricted motion, suggesting bioprosthetic valve thrombosis. He is hemodynamically stable. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?", "options": {"A": "A trial of vitamin K antagonist therapy with echocardiographic reassessment of gradients", "B": "Urgent redo mitral valve replacement before leaflet destruction becomes irreversible", "C": "Systemic fibrinolysis now using a slow infusion low dose alteplase protocol", "D": "Dual antiplatelet therapy given for three months with repeat echocardiographic imaging afterward", "E": "Transcatheter valve in valve implantation to restore the effective orifice area"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A trial of vitamin K antagonist therapy with echocardiographic reassessment of gradients): suspected bioprosthetic valve thrombosis in a hemodynamically stable patient is treated first with a vitamin K antagonist, which commonly restores leaflet motion and lowers gradients on follow up imaging. Reoperation and valve in valve implantation are reserved for anticoagulation failure or instability. Fibrinolysis is directed at obstructive mechanical prosthesis thrombosis, and antiplatelet therapy is insufficient for established thrombus."}, {"id": 380, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "During surveillance of a 38 year old man with repaired tetralogy of Fallot, the electrocardiogram shows QRS duration increasing to 185 milliseconds alongside progressive right ventricular dilation. In risk stratification for this population, which adverse outcome is this degree of QRS prolongation most strongly associated with?", "options": {"A": "Sustained ventricular tachycardia and sudden cardiac death from ventricular arrhythmia", "B": "High grade atrioventricular block eventually requiring urgent permanent pacemaker implantation for symptom control", "C": "Thromboembolic stroke arising from stagnant flow within the dilated right ventricle", "D": "Rapid progression of aortic root dilation toward accepted dissection thresholds", "E": "Refractory atrial flutter circuits developing around the old ventriculotomy scar"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sustained ventricular tachycardia and sudden cardiac death from ventricular arrhythmia): QRS duration ≥ 180 milliseconds in repaired tetralogy reflects right ventricular dilation and slowed conduction across scar, and it is a validated marker for ventricular tachycardia and sudden death. This finding strengthens the case for timely pulmonary valve intervention and arrhythmia evaluation. Conduction block, stroke, and aortic events are not the outcomes this marker predicts."}, {"id": 381, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 76 year old man received a surgical aortic bioprosthesis two years ago. He now develops persistent atrial fibrillation with a CHA2DS2-VASc score of 4 and normal renal function. Which of the following is the most appropriate long term anticoagulation choice for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Warfarin managed with an INR target of 3.0 reflecting the combined thromboembolic indications", "B": "Aspirin plus clopidogrel since the bioprosthesis lowers his embolic risk", "C": "A direct oral anticoagulant such as apixaban at standard stroke prevention dosing", "D": "Warfarin for three months followed by a switch to aspirin monotherapy", "E": "Left atrial appendage occlusion as the preferred first line stroke prevention"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A direct oral anticoagulant such as apixaban at standard stroke prevention dosing): a bioprosthetic valve beyond the early postoperative period does not preclude direct oral anticoagulants, which are appropriate for atrial fibrillation with this risk score. Vitamin K antagonists are required only for mechanical valves or significant rheumatic mitral stenosis, and an INR of 3.0 would add bleeding risk without benefit. Antiplatelet combinations underprotect at this score, and appendage occlusion is reserved for patients unable to take anticoagulants."}, {"id": 382, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 44 year old woman presents with two hours of atypical chest discomfort, now resolved. Her ECG is normal, she has no risk factors, and her HEART score is 2. Serial high sensitivity troponins over six hours are 4, 5, and 4 ng/L, all below the reference limit. Which of the following is the most appropriate disposition?", "options": {"A": "Admit for observation with repeat troponin measurements every six hours overnight", "B": "Urgent invasive angiography before discharge to exclude high risk anatomy definitively", "C": "Admit and begin therapeutic anticoagulation for a presumed missed coronary event", "D": "Discharge with instructions to return only if pain lasts over an hour", "E": "Discharge home with primary care follow up and no routine further cardiac testing"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Discharge home with primary care follow up and no routine further cardiac testing): a HEART score of 2 with normal, unchanging high sensitivity troponins identifies a low risk patient in whom discharge without urgent additional testing is explicitly endorsed. Overnight observation, anticoagulation, and angiography all treat a diagnosis her flat biomarker series has effectively excluded. Discharge advice keyed only to pain lasting an hour gives her the wrong return precautions rather than structured follow up."}, {"id": 383, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 59 year old man with recurrent myocardial infarctions takes atorvastatin 80 mg and ezetimibe 10 mg daily with confirmed adherence. LDL cholesterol remains 105 mg/dL. He has no familial hypercholesterolemia diagnosis but clearly established very high risk disease. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Add a PCSK9 monoclonal antibody such as evolocumab to current therapy", "B": "Stop ezetimibe and rely on lifestyle intensification with dietary counseling alone", "C": "Add fenofibrate to address residual risk through triglyceride reduction primarily", "D": "Repeat the lipid panel in one year before changing any therapy", "E": "Add colesevelam as the preferred third agent for LDL lowering here"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Add a PCSK9 monoclonal antibody such as evolocumab to current therapy): a very high risk patient whose LDL remains at or above 70 mg/dL despite maximal statin plus ezetimibe meets the indication for PCSK9 inhibition, which produces roughly 60 percent further reduction and lowers events. Removing ezetimibe or waiting a year moves him away from goal. Fenofibrate targets triglycerides without proven event reduction here, and bile acid sequestrants are weaker, harder to tolerate, and not the preferred next step."}, {"id": 384, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 69 year old man received a drug-eluting stent in the right coronary artery two months ago for stable angina. His surgeon proposes elective inguinal hernia repair next month and asks about timing and antiplatelet management. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Postpone the elective operation until at least six months after stent placement", "B": "Proceed next month, stopping both antiplatelet agents five days before surgery", "C": "Proceed next month with bridging low molecular weight heparin replacing antiplatelets", "D": "Proceed next month while continuing dual antiplatelet therapy through the operation", "E": "Cancel surgery permanently, since stented patients should avoid elective procedures altogether"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Postpone the elective operation until at least six months after stent placement): elective noncardiac surgery is optimally delayed six months after drug-eluting stenting so dual antiplatelet therapy is not interrupted during the highest risk window for stent thrombosis. Stopping both agents at three months, with or without heparin, invites thrombosis because heparin does not substitute for platelet inhibition. Operating through dual therapy is reserved for surgery that cannot wait, and a truly elective hernia repair can wait, though not forever."}, {"id": 385, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 71 year old man with dyspnea has an aortic valve area of 0.9 cm², mean gradient 26 mmHg, peak velocity 3.3 m/s, and ejection fraction 30 percent. The valve is heavily calcified. Which of the following is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?", "options": {"A": "Proceed directly to valve replacement because the calcification suggests severe stenosis", "B": "Right heart catheterization to measure pulmonary pressures before any valve decision", "C": "Exercise treadmill testing to determine whether his dyspnea is truly cardiac", "D": "Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging to quantify myocardial fibrosis and guide the timing of replacement", "E": "Low dose dobutamine stress echocardiography to separate true severe from pseudosevere stenosis"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Low dose dobutamine stress echocardiography to separate true severe from pseudosevere stenosis): low flow low gradient stenosis with reduced ejection fraction requires low dose dobutamine testing to distinguish true severe stenosis from pseudosevere disease and to assess contractile reserve. Calcification supports severity but does not establish it by itself. Exercise testing is unsafe if the stenosis proves severe, and pulmonary pressures or fibrosis imaging do not answer the central severity question."}, {"id": 387, "domain": "normal_cardiovascular_anatomy_and_physiology", "question": "Before starting a QT prolonging antiarrhythmic in clinic, you calculate a corrected QT interval for a 55 year old man and separately for a 60 year old woman. Which thresholds should you apply to label each corrected QT interval as prolonged when screening these adult patients?", "options": {"A": "Above 380 ms in men and above 390 ms in women", "B": "Above 520 ms in men and above 540 ms in women", "C": "Above 400 ms in both sexes when heart rate is normal", "D": "Above 450 ms in men and above 460 ms in women", "E": "Above 600 ms in either sex, with shorter values acceptable"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Above 450 ms in men and above 460 ms in women): conventional screening labels a corrected QT prolonged beyond 450 ms in adult men and 460 ms in adult women, with values of 500 ms or more marking substantially increased torsades risk. Thresholds near 380 to 400 ms sit inside the normal range and would flag healthy patients. Waiting for 520, 540, or 600 ms before acting would ignore clinically important prolongation well below those extremes."}, {"id": 388, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 62 year old man presents to a rural emergency department 60 minutes after onset of crushing chest pain. The ECG shows anterior ST-segment elevation. The nearest PCI-capable hospital can achieve a first medical contact to device time of 90 minutes. Which of the following is the most appropriate reperfusion strategy?", "options": {"A": "Administer full dose fibrinolysis at the local hospital before arranging any transfer", "B": "Give half dose fibrinolysis and transfer immediately for planned rescue angioplasty", "C": "Transfer immediately for primary PCI without administering fibrinolytic therapy beforehand", "D": "Start an intravenous heparin infusion and observe for spontaneous reperfusion locally", "E": "Obtain emergency echocardiography to confirm wall motion abnormality before deciding on transfer"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transfer immediately for primary PCI without administering fibrinolytic therapy beforehand): a first medical contact to device time of 120 minutes or less favors primary PCI, and 90 minutes is well within that window. Fibrinolysis, whether full or half dose, adds bleeding risk without benefit when timely PCI is available. Observing on heparin or delaying transfer for echocardiography withholds definitive reperfusion from an evolving anterior infarction."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 63 year old woman has classic exertional angina and a perfusion study with a moderate anterior defect, yet angiography shows smooth coronary arteries without stenosis. Her symptoms persist despite reassurance. The team wants a definitive physiologic diagnosis during a planned repeat catheterization. Which testing strategy is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Invasive coronary function testing with adenosine derived flow reserve and acetylcholine provocation", "B": "Left ventriculography to identify apical ballooning as the source of her symptoms", "C": "Intravascular ultrasound imaging of all three vessels to detect angiographically hidden atherosclerotic stenosis", "D": "Right heart catheterization with exercise to measure pulmonary pressures during symptoms", "E": "Repeat perfusion imaging with a different tracer to confirm the anterior defect"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Invasive coronary function testing with adenosine derived flow reserve and acetylcholine provocation): invasive coronary function testing measures adenosine based flow reserve and microcirculatory resistance and adds acetylcholine provocation, distinguishing microvascular dysfunction from epicardial or microvascular spasm. Intravascular ultrasound looks for hidden plaque rather than physiology, ventriculography addresses takotsubo rather than her chronic exertional pattern, and exercise hemodynamic study targets pulmonary causes. Repeating perfusion imaging would not explain the mechanism already demonstrated."}, {"id": 391, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "An 84 year old man with symptomatic severe aortic stenosis has an STS score of 4 percent, favorable iliofemoral anatomy on computed tomography, and a trileaflet valve suitable for a transcatheter prosthesis. He wishes to avoid a prolonged recovery. Which of the following is the most appropriate intervention strategy?", "options": {"A": "Surgical aortic valve replacement with a mechanical prosthesis and lifelong warfarin", "B": "Surgical aortic valve replacement with a bioprosthesis given his acceptable calculated operative risk score", "C": "Balloon aortic valvuloplasty as definitive therapy to avoid any prosthetic implant", "D": "Transfemoral transcatheter aortic valve implantation after heart team confirmation of candidacy", "E": "Medical therapy with diuretics and follow up imaging every six months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Transfemoral transcatheter aortic valve implantation after heart team confirmation of candidacy): for symptomatic patients older than 80 years, the 2020 guideline recommends transfemoral transcatheter replacement when anatomy is suitable. Surgery offers no advantage at this age with favorable access, and a mechanical prosthesis is a particularly poor fit for an octogenarian. Balloon valvuloplasty and medical therapy alone both leave severe stenosis effectively untreated."}, {"id": 394, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 63 year old man with chronic stable angina walks two blocks before chest tightness despite metoprolol succinate 200 mg daily. Resting heart rate is 55 beats per minute and blood pressure is 132/80 mmHg. He uses sublingual nitroglycerin twice weekly. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Increase metoprolol further until his resting heart rate falls below 50", "B": "Add amlodipine while continuing the current beta blocker dose unchanged", "C": "Add verapamil to deepen rate control on top of the beta blocker", "D": "Replace metoprolol with ranolazine as the primary antianginal going forward", "E": "Schedule PCI without additional medication changes or further symptom assessment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Add amlodipine while continuing the current beta blocker dose unchanged): his heart rate is already at goal, so the next move for persistent angina is adding a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker or a long acting nitrate rather than pushing the beta blocker. Further beta blockade risks symptomatic bradycardia with little antianginal gain. Verapamil combined with a beta blocker invites conduction disease and bradycardia, dropping the beta blocker for ranolazine removes proven first line therapy, and revascularization comes after optimized medical treatment, not before it."}, {"id": 397, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 24 year old woman presents with 30 minutes of sudden palpitations. Blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg and she is alert without chest pain or dyspnea. The monitor shows a regular narrow complex tachycardia. Intravenous access has not yet been obtained. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step?", "options": {"A": "Perform immediate synchronized cardioversion after brief procedural sedation is administered", "B": "Attempt a modified Valsalva maneuver with passive leg raise at the bedside", "C": "Give oral metoprolol and observe the patient on telemetry for one hour", "D": "Administer intravenous amiodarone as a loading infusion over the first ten minutes", "E": "Apply transcutaneous pacing pads and begin overdrive pacing at a rapid rate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Attempt a modified Valsalva maneuver with passive leg raise at the bedside): vagal maneuvers are the recommended first intervention for hemodynamically stable narrow complex tachycardia, and the modified technique terminates more episodes than the standard method. Immediate cardioversion is reserved for unstable patients or refractory episodes. Oral beta blockade acts too slowly, amiodarone is not first line for stable supraventricular tachycardia, and overdrive pacing is not an initial bedside therapy."}, {"id": 399, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 77 year old woman stable on warfarin and digoxin for atrial fibrillation is started on amiodarone for recurrent symptomatic episodes after cardioversion. One week later she calls asking whether her other medications need any change. She feels well. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "No changes are needed while she remains free of new symptoms", "B": "Stop warfarin temporarily until the amiodarone reaches a steady state level", "C": "Double the digoxin dose to offset the effect of the new drug", "D": "Check only a thyroid panel now and adjust nothing else currently", "E": "Reduce the digoxin and warfarin doses with closer laboratory monitoring"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Reduce the digoxin and warfarin doses with closer laboratory monitoring): amiodarone inhibits P glycoprotein and cytochrome enzymes, raising digoxin levels and potentiating warfarin, so both drugs are typically reduced with closer checks of the international normalized ratio and the digoxin level. Waiting for symptoms invites toxicity that can itself present as arrhythmia. Stopping warfarin creates a stroke risk gap, doubling digoxin compounds the interaction, and thyroid testing alone ignores the immediate pharmacokinetic problem."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 53 year old woman with exertional dyspnea has an echocardiogram showing a peak tricuspid regurgitant velocity of 3.7 m/s with a dilated right ventricle and flattened septum. Her physician assistant applies guideline echo probability assessment. How should this study be categorized and acted upon?", "options": {"A": "Low probability of pulmonary hypertension, so reassurance and routine follow up suffice", "B": "Indeterminate study that should simply be repeated after six months of observation", "C": "High echocardiographic probability, which warrants referral for right heart catheterization", "D": "Diagnostic of pulmonary arterial hypertension, allowing therapy to start without catheterization", "E": "Artifactual velocity, because septal flattening invalidates the tricuspid regurgitant signal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (High echocardiographic probability, which warrants referral for right heart catheterization): a tricuspid regurgitant velocity above 3.4 m/s places the study in the high probability category, and additional right heart signs such as ventricular dilation and septal flattening strengthen that assignment, so the guideline pathway moves to invasive hemodynamic confirmation. Echocardiography assigns probability but never establishes pulmonary arterial hypertension by itself, so starting therapy now would be premature. Neither reassurance nor a six month repeat fits a high probability result, and septal flattening supports rather than invalidates the findings."}, {"id": 402, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 66 year old man with symptomatic peripheral artery disease and a prior myocardial infarction remains at high ischemic risk despite high intensity statin therapy and daily aspirin. His bleeding risk is judged to be low. Based on randomized trial evidence, which antithrombotic intensification strategy reduces major adverse cardiovascular and limb events?", "options": {"A": "Rivaroxaban 2.5 milligrams twice daily added to continued low dose aspirin", "B": "Full dose apixaban replacing aspirin to provide comprehensive anticoagulant protection", "C": "Ticagrelor added to aspirin indefinitely at the dose used after stenting", "D": "Warfarin titrated to an international normalized ratio between two and three", "E": "Clopidogrel substituted for aspirin along with a renewed supervised walking program"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rivaroxaban 2.5 milligrams twice daily added to continued low dose aspirin): the COMPASS trial showed that vascular dose rivaroxaban at 2.5 milligrams twice daily plus aspirin reduced cardiovascular death, stroke, myocardial infarction, and major adverse limb events in stable atherosclerotic disease compared with aspirin alone. Full dose anticoagulation with apixaban or warfarin has no supporting trial evidence in this setting and increases bleeding. Ticagrelor added to aspirin and clopidogrel substitution each showed no comparable reduction in limb outcomes for stable peripheral disease."}, {"id": 403, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 60 year old man with prior MI stopped atorvastatin 80 mg because of bilateral thigh aching that resolved within two weeks of discontinuation. Creatine kinase was normal. His LDL cholesterol is now 148 mg/dL, and he is reluctant but willing to try again. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Declare statin intolerance permanent and start a PCSK9 monoclonal antibody now", "B": "Resume atorvastatin 80 mg together with coenzyme Q10 supplementation to prevent recurrent aching", "C": "Start ezetimibe monotherapy and avoid all statin exposure from this point", "D": "Check thyroid function and vitamin D, deferring all lipid therapy meanwhile", "E": "Rechallenge with a different statin at low dose, such as rosuvastatin twice weekly"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Rechallenge with a different statin at low dose, such as rosuvastatin twice weekly): most patients with statin associated muscle symptoms tolerate rechallenge with an alternative agent, lower dose, or intermittent dosing, and statins remain the foundation after infarction. Labeling him permanently intolerant after one agent, or moving to ezetimibe or PCSK9 therapy first, abandons the most proven drug class prematurely. Repeating the identical high dose invites the same symptoms, coenzyme Q10 lacks convincing benefit, and deferring all therapy leaves a very high risk patient untreated."}, {"id": 404, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 63 year old woman who smokes reports six months of periumbilical pain beginning thirty minutes after meals, a growing fear of eating, and a fifteen pound weight loss. Upper endoscopy and abdominal ultrasound are unremarkable. Which diagnostic study should be obtained next to evaluate her suspected underlying diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Computed tomography angiography of the mesenteric vessels to assess for ostial stenoses", "B": "Gastric emptying scintigraphy to evaluate for postprandial symptoms from gastroparesis", "C": "Colonoscopy with random biopsies to exclude microscopic inflammatory mucosal disease", "D": "Small bowel capsule endoscopy to identify occult mucosal ulceration or masses", "E": "Hepatobiliary iminodiacetic acid scanning to assess for chronic acalculous cholecystitis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Computed tomography angiography of the mesenteric vessels to assess for ostial stenoses): postprandial pain, food fear, and weight loss in a smoker form the classic triad of chronic mesenteric ischemia, and computed tomography angiography is the preferred test because it directly visualizes the ostial atherosclerotic stenoses of the celiac and mesenteric arteries. Gastroparesis causes nausea and fullness rather than timed ischemic pain, and microscopic colitis causes diarrhea. Capsule endoscopy and biliary scintigraphy evaluate mucosal and gallbladder disease, neither of which produces this vascular pattern."}, {"id": 409, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "Four patients with patent foramen ovale are discussed at a neurology cardiology case conference to select candidates for percutaneous closure. Each had a recent cerebral event and completed vascular imaging, prolonged rhythm monitoring, and hypercoagulability review. For which patient does device closure carry the strongest evidence of benefit?", "options": {"A": "A 68 year old whose monitoring captured paroxysmal atrial fibrillation episodes", "B": "A 52 year old whose event was a transient global amnesia episode", "C": "A 39 year old with an infarct explained by vertebral artery dissection", "D": "A 47 year old with an embolic infarct and no alternative cause found", "E": "A 58 year old with pure sensory lacunar syndrome and longstanding diabetes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A 47 year old with an embolic infarct and no alternative cause found): Closure trials showed reduced stroke recurrence in patients aged 18 to 60 whose embolic events remained cryptogenic after thorough evaluation. Atrial fibrillation, arterial dissection, and small vessel lacunar disease each supply a competing mechanism better treated directly. Transient global amnesia is not an ischemic indication for closure."}, {"id": 410, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 66 year old woman with persistent angina is prescribed ranolazine after her cardiologist confirms she takes no interacting medications and has normal renal and hepatic function. The clinic nurse asks exactly how the prescription should be written. Which regimen reflects the recommended initiation and titration of ranolazine?", "options": {"A": "1000 mg twice daily from the outset to reach effective levels quickly", "B": "500 mg three times daily, increased weekly according to the symptom response", "C": "250 mg once daily at bedtime, doubled every three days as tolerated", "D": "2000 mg once daily using the extended release formulation to simplify adherence", "E": "500 mg twice daily initially, increased to a maximum of 1000 mg twice daily"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (500 mg twice daily initially, increased to a maximum of 1000 mg twice daily): the labeled regimen begins at 500 mg twice daily and titrates to a maximum of 1000 mg twice daily based on symptoms. Starting at the maximum dose, three times daily schedules, once daily bedtime dosing, and a single 2000 mg dose all fall outside the approved extended release regimen. Doses above the maximum increase QT related risk without additional antianginal benefit."}, {"id": 412, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 47 year old woman develops new asymmetric color changes of the fingers with cold exposure, along with a painful fingertip ulcer on one index finger. Her clinician suspects a secondary cause rather than primary Raynaud phenomenon. Which initial evaluation is most appropriate to investigate for an underlying systemic disorder?", "options": {"A": "Cold immersion provocation testing to document the timing of triphasic color change", "B": "Genetic testing for hereditary connective tissue disease before any serologic studies", "C": "Upper extremity catheter angiography to map digital artery occlusions bilaterally", "D": "Antinuclear antibody testing combined with examination of the nailfold capillaries", "E": "Empiric calcium channel blockade with reassessment of symptoms in six months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Antinuclear antibody testing combined with examination of the nailfold capillaries): late onset, asymmetry, and digital ulceration are red flags for secondary Raynaud phenomenon, and the highest yield initial evaluation pairs antinuclear antibody testing with nailfold capillaroscopy, since abnormal capillary loops and positive serologies strongly predict an underlying connective tissue disease such as systemic sclerosis. Provocation testing only reproduces the vasospasm without identifying a cause. Invasive angiography is not an initial study, genetic panels do not screen for acquired autoimmune disease, and empiric treatment without evaluation delays the diagnosis driving her tissue injury."}, {"id": 414, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 57 year old woman with a body mass index of 39 kg/m2 and heart failure with preserved ejection fraction remains limited by dyspnea and poor exercise tolerance despite diuretics and a sodium glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor. Her physician assistant considers therapy targeting the obesity itself. Which addition is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Sibutramine, an appetite suppressant with sympathomimetic properties considered suited to selected cardiac patients", "B": "Semaglutide, which reduced symptoms and body weight in dedicated preserved ejection fraction trials", "C": "Short term very low calorie fasting cycles supervised through weekly telephone contact", "D": "Orlistat, favored because fat malabsorption also lowers circulating natriuretic peptides", "E": "Phentermine monotherapy, dosed at bedtime to limit daytime sympathetic stimulation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Semaglutide, which reduced symptoms and body weight in dedicated preserved ejection fraction trials): the STEP-HFpEF program showed weekly semaglutide produced large improvements in symptom scores, six minute walk distance, and weight in patients with obesity related preserved ejection fraction heart failure. Sibutramine was withdrawn for cardiovascular harm, and phentermine's sympathomimetic load makes it a poor fit for this population. Orlistat has no meaningful heart failure evidence, and unsupervised severe caloric restriction risks lean mass loss without trial support."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "During invasive testing for angina with nonobstructive arteries, a 58 year old woman has a coronary flow reserve of 1.7 and an index of microcirculatory resistance of 38, with no epicardial spasm after acetylcholine. Which treatment approach is most appropriate for the disorder these findings establish?", "options": {"A": "Long acting nitrates as the first line therapy, titrated to symptom relief", "B": "Immediate percutaneous intervention on the vessel with the lowest flow reserve", "C": "High dose dihydropyridine therapy directed specifically at abolishing epicardial vasospasm", "D": "A beta blocker with a statin and ACE inhibitor, targeting microvascular angina", "E": "Anticoagulation with a direct oral agent to improve capillary bed perfusion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A beta blocker with a statin and ACE inhibitor, targeting microvascular angina): low flow reserve with high microcirculatory resistance and no inducible spasm establishes structural microvascular dysfunction, for which beta blockade with a statin and ACE inhibition is the usual initial strategy. Nitrates are frequently ineffective in microvascular angina, dihydropyridines target the epicardial spasm she does not have, and neither stenting nor anticoagulation treats microcirculatory disease. Symptom guided titration with risk factor control is the mainstay."}, {"id": 417, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 65 year old man three months out from drug-eluting stenting for STEMI needs extraction of a single molar. His dentist requests that aspirin and ticagrelor both be held for a week beforehand. Which of the following is the most appropriate response from the cardiology team?", "options": {"A": "Agree to hold both agents, since dental bleeding is difficult to control", "B": "Hold ticagrelor for one week but continue aspirin through the extraction", "C": "Advise proceeding on both agents, using local hemostatic measures for the extraction", "D": "Postpone the extraction until dual therapy is completed at twelve months", "E": "Convert ticagrelor to a continuous heparin infusion for the week surrounding the dental procedure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise proceeding on both agents, using local hemostatic measures for the extraction): minor dental procedures carry low bleeding risk that local measures such as sutures and tranexamic rinses control, so interrupting dual therapy three months after STEMI stenting is unnecessary risk. Holding both agents, or even the P2Y12 inhibitor for a full week, exposes a recent stent for the sake of manageable oozing. Deferring simple dental care for nine months invites infection, and heparin does not provide antiplatelet protection."}, {"id": 419, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 31 year old man with resistant hypertension is evaluated for possible aortic coarctation. His examination and noninvasive measurements are summarized in the exhibit shown. Applying accepted hemodynamic thresholds for intervention, which conclusion about the need for coarctation repair is best supported by these findings?", "options": {"A": "Repair is unnecessary because thigh pressures remain within the normal adult range", "B": "Repair should wait until ambulatory monitoring confirms nocturnal blood pressure elevation", "C": "Intervention is warranted, since hypertension accompanies an arm leg gradient ≥ 20 mmHg", "D": "Balloon testing during catheterization must document a gradient above sixty first", "E": "Antihypertensive titration alone is preferred given the absence of heart failure symptoms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intervention is warranted, since hypertension accompanies an arm leg gradient ≥ 20 mmHg): The exhibit shows upper extremity pressures 40 mmHg above the thigh measurement with delayed femoral pulses and collateral murmurs, meeting the accepted threshold of hypertension plus a gradient ≥ 20 mmHg. Anatomic narrowing with diastolic runoff corroborates significant obstruction. Medication titration without relieving the mechanical lesion leaves the gradient and its consequences untreated.", "image": "question_images/q_419.png"}, {"id": 420, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 55 year old woman is found to have an asymptomatic moderate pericardial effusion measuring 15 millimeters on echocardiography, with normal blood pressure and no chamber collapse. She takes no relevant medications. What is the most appropriate initial diagnostic approach to this newly discovered effusion in your clinic?", "options": {"A": "Immediate diagnostic pericardiocentesis in the procedure suite to obtain fluid before serum laboratory testing", "B": "Empiric antituberculous therapy while awaiting results of interferon gamma testing", "C": "Surgical pericardial window with biopsy as the initial diagnostic maneuver", "D": "Assess inflammatory markers and screen for systemic, thyroid, renal, and neoplastic causes", "E": "Repeat echocardiography in one year without additional laboratory evaluation now"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Assess inflammatory markers and screen for systemic, thyroid, renal, and neoplastic causes): workup of a moderate effusion starts with clinical context, CRP, thyroid and renal function, and evaluation for malignancy or autoimmune disease, since elevated markers point toward treating as pericarditis while a negative screen supports surveillance. Pericardiocentesis and surgical drainage with biopsy are reserved for tamponade, suspected purulent or neoplastic fluid, or persistent unexplained large effusions. Empiric tuberculosis therapy is premature, and deferring all evaluation for a year ignores treatable causes."}, {"id": 421, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 49 year old Black man has stage 2 hypertension without heart failure, chronic kidney disease, diabetes, or prior cardiovascular events. You are selecting the drug classes most likely to lower his blood pressure effectively as initial therapy, based on guideline recommendations for this population. Which regimen is the best starting choice?", "options": {"A": "Oral lisinopril together with metoprolol, pairing renin angiotensin blockade with added central sympathetic control", "B": "Losartan alone, titrated upward every two weeks until goal is reached", "C": "Amlodipine with chlorthalidone, combining a calcium channel blocker and thiazide diuretic", "D": "Metoprolol with hydralazine, combining rate control and direct arteriolar vasodilation", "E": "Doxazosin with clonidine, addressing vascular tone and central sympathetic outflow"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Amlodipine with chlorthalidone, combining a calcium channel blocker and thiazide diuretic): In Black adults without heart failure or chronic kidney disease, thiazide type diuretics and calcium channel blockers lower blood pressure more effectively as initial therapy than renin angiotensin blockade, and his stage 2 values above goal warrant starting two agents together. Losartan monotherapy is both less effective in this population and insufficient for his severity. Beta blockers, alpha blockers, and central agonists are not first line choices without compelling indications."}, {"id": 424, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 68 year old man with HFrEF and chronic right sided congestion takes oral furosemide 80 mg each morning but continues to gain weight, and bowel wall edema is suspected of limiting drug absorption. Renal function is stable and adherence is confirmed. Which of the following is the most appropriate outpatient adjustment to his diuretic regimen?", "options": {"A": "Split furosemide into 40 mg twice daily while keeping the same total dose", "B": "Switch to oral torsemide 40 mg daily given its more reliable absorption", "C": "Add hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg while keeping furosemide 80 mg unchanged", "D": "Replace furosemide with spironolactone 50 mg daily for its potassium effect", "E": "Continue the current regimen and restrict fluid to one liter per day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Switch to oral torsemide 40 mg daily given its more reliable absorption): torsemide has high and consistent oral bioavailability even with gut edema, and 40 mg approximates the potency of 80 mg of oral furosemide. Splitting the same poorly absorbed furosemide dose does not solve the absorption problem driving his weight gain. A low dose thiazide addresses distal sodium reabsorption rather than absorption failure, spironolactone at this dose is not an effective decongestant substitute, and fluid restriction alone will not reverse ongoing congestion."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 66 year old man with diabetes and typical exertional angina undergoes exercise nuclear imaging that shows ST depression at 4 METs, a large anterior perfusion defect with transient ischemic dilation, and a drop in blood pressure during exercise. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Intensify antianginal therapy and repeat perfusion imaging in three to six months", "B": "Order coronary CT angiography to confirm the perfusion findings noninvasively first", "C": "Refer for invasive coronary angiography with intent to revascularize as indicated", "D": "Begin cardiac rehabilitation and reassess symptoms after twelve supervised exercise sessions", "E": "Obtain stress echocardiography as a second functional test to resolve discordance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer for invasive coronary angiography with intent to revascularize as indicated): ischemia at low workload, a large defect, transient ischemic dilation, and exertional hypotension are high risk findings that predict extensive disease, often left main or multivessel, and warrant invasive definition. Medical intensification alone with delayed reimaging accepts months of avoidable risk. Adding CT angiography or a second functional test duplicates information the abnormal study already provides, and rehabilitation is an adjunct rather than the response to a markedly abnormal result."}, {"id": 426, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 70 year old man with a mechanical mitral valve on warfarin undergoes drug-eluting stenting for NSTEMI. The admitting team suggests converting warfarin to apixaban to simplify his combined antithrombotic regimen after PCI. Which of the following is the most appropriate anticoagulation decision?", "options": {"A": "Convert to apixaban, since direct agents bleed less in combined therapy", "B": "Convert to dabigatran, which was studied specifically for mechanical valve patients", "C": "Stop anticoagulation for one month while dual antiplatelet therapy protects him", "D": "Continue warfarin with clopidogrel, minimizing the duration of added aspirin", "E": "Continue warfarin but target a lower INR of 1.5 to 2 during dual therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue warfarin with clopidogrel, minimizing the duration of added aspirin): mechanical valves require warfarin because direct oral anticoagulants are contraindicated, and dabigatran specifically caused more thromboembolism and bleeding in the trial that tested it for mechanical valves. The combined regimen is managed by keeping warfarin, using clopidogrel as the P2Y12 inhibitor, and shortening aspirin exposure. Stopping anticoagulation invites valve thrombosis, and lowering the INR below the valve's therapeutic target trades stroke protection for a modest bleeding gain."}, {"id": 429, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 64 year old man on pembrolizumab presents with fatigue, chest discomfort, and dyspnea. The exhibit shown summarizes his presentation. He is admitted with suspected immune checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis and the oncology team asks cardiology for immediate management. Which initial treatment should be started without waiting for biopsy confirmation?", "options": {"A": "High dose intravenous methylprednisolone, one gram daily, with checkpoint inhibitor therapy held", "B": "Oral colchicine and ibuprofen, following the standard pathway for acute pericarditis", "C": "Urgent coronary angiography and empiric dual antiplatelet therapy for presumed infarction", "D": "Observation on continuous telemetry monitoring alone until an endomyocardial biopsy establishes the diagnosis firmly", "E": "Intravenous immunoglobulin as single therapy while the checkpoint inhibitor is continued"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (High dose intravenous methylprednisolone, one gram daily, with checkpoint inhibitor therapy held): checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis carries mortality approaching half of fulminant cases, so pulse dose corticosteroids must begin immediately on clinical suspicion, with the offending immunotherapy stopped, rather than after histologic proof. Waiting on telemetry for biopsy results loses the treatment window in a disease that can deteriorate within hours. A pericarditis regimen does not address lymphocytic myocardial infiltration, angiography with antiplatelets treats a diagnosis the rising creatine kinase and new heart block argue against, and immunoglobulin alone while continuing the drug leaves the trigger in place.", "image": "question_images/q_429.png"}, {"id": 430, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 44 year old woman has confirmed stage 1 hypertension with home averages of 133/84 mm Hg. She has no diabetes, kidney disease, or cardiovascular history, and her calculated 10 year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk is 6 percent. According to the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline, what is the recommended initial management for her?", "options": {"A": "Begin a single first line agent and reassess her within one month", "B": "Begin two complementary antihypertensive agents together to reach goal quickly and prevent hypertensive progression", "C": "Refer for renal artery imaging before choosing any treatment strategy", "D": "Lifestyle modification alone with repeat blood pressure evaluation in three to six months", "E": "Begin low dose aspirin with lifestyle counseling and repeat labs annually"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lifestyle modification alone with repeat blood pressure evaluation in three to six months): For stage 1 hypertension without clinical cardiovascular disease, diabetes, or chronic kidney disease and a 10 year atherosclerotic risk below 10 percent, the guideline recommends lifestyle change first with reassessment in three to six months. Drug therapy at this stage is reserved for higher risk patients, and dual therapy targets stage 2 values. Aspirin adds bleeding risk without addressing her pressure, and imaging has no role absent secondary cause features."}, {"id": 431, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 59 year old man with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation asks about the pill in the pocket approach with flecainide, which worked well for a friend. His history includes a myocardial infarction two years ago with a preserved ejection fraction. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Flecainide is avoided after infarction, so a different strategy should be chosen", "B": "Flecainide is acceptable if each dose is taken with a beta blocker", "C": "Flecainide can be used once a stress test excludes any residual ischemia", "D": "Flecainide is reasonable at half the usual dose given his cardiac history", "E": "Flecainide is appropriate because his preserved ejection fraction removes prior concerns"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Flecainide is avoided after infarction, so a different strategy should be chosen): class IC agents increased mortality in patients with prior myocardial infarction in the CAST trial, and coronary scar remains a contraindication even with preserved ventricular function. Pairing the drug with a beta blocker addresses organized flutter with rapid conduction, not the proarrhythmic scar substrate. Neither a negative stress test, a reduced dose, nor a normal ejection fraction removes the infarction related risk."}, {"id": 433, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "During telemetry monitoring, a 64 year old man suddenly becomes unresponsive and pulseless. The monitor shows the rhythm on the strip shown. Chest compressions are started and a defibrillator arrives within one minute. Pads are placed. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Deliver an unsynchronized defibrillation shock and resume compressions immediately afterward", "B": "Give intravenous epinephrine first and deliver a defibrillation shock only after two full minutes of compressions", "C": "Give intravenous amiodarone before delivering the first defibrillation attempt", "D": "Perform synchronized cardioversion at low energy to avoid myocardial injury", "E": "Intubate the patient before any electrical therapy is delivered to him"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Deliver an unsynchronized defibrillation shock and resume compressions immediately afterward): the strip shows ventricular fibrillation, for which immediate defibrillation is the single most effective intervention, followed at once by compressions. Epinephrine and amiodarone come later in the algorithm, after at least one shock. Synchronization is impossible in fibrillation because there is no organized QRS to track, and airway placement must not delay the shock.", "image": "question_images/q_433.png"}, {"id": 435, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 78 year old man with symptomatic severe aortic stenosis had coronary bypass surgery nine years ago with a patent internal mammary graft crossing the midline. His STS score is 8.5 percent, and computed tomography confirms suitable femoral access. Which of the following is the most appropriate intervention?", "options": {"A": "Redo sternotomy with surgical valve replacement despite the elevated operative risk", "B": "Medical therapy alone because reoperative valve surgery carries substantial surgical hazard", "C": "Transfemoral transcatheter valve implantation after heart team review of his anatomy", "D": "Balloon valvuloplasty repeated as needed to palliate symptoms without prosthesis implantation", "E": "Apical transcatheter access to avoid manipulating the previously grafted coronary vessels"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transfemoral transcatheter valve implantation after heart team review of his anatomy): high surgical risk from a prior sternotomy with a patent graft crossing the midline, combined with suitable femoral anatomy, strongly favors transfemoral transcatheter replacement. Redo surgery exposes the graft to injury at chest reentry, and balloon valvuloplasty provides only transient relief. Alternative access routes are reserved for patients without adequate femoral vessels."}, {"id": 439, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 57 year old man develops chest pain at home and arrives by ambulance at a PCI-capable hospital 40 minutes after symptom onset. The ECG transmitted en route shows inferior ST elevation, and the catheterization team has been activated. Which of the following is the most appropriate reperfusion approach?", "options": {"A": "Fibrinolysis in the emergency department, since it is faster than laboratory activation", "B": "Primary PCI with a first medical contact to device goal of 90 minutes", "C": "Facilitated PCI, giving a fibrinolytic now and proceeding to angioplasty afterward", "D": "Delayed angiography tomorrow if overnight medical therapy fully relieves his pain", "E": "Bedside echocardiography before activation to confirm an inferior wall motion abnormality"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Primary PCI with a first medical contact to device goal of 90 minutes): at a PCI-capable hospital, primary PCI is the preferred reperfusion strategy, with a 90 minute first medical contact to device target for direct presenters. Fibrinolysis, alone or as a facilitated bridge to angioplasty, adds bleeding without improving outcomes when the laboratory is immediately available. Waiting overnight or pausing for confirmatory echocardiography in a patient with diagnostic ST elevation only lengthens ischemic time."}, {"id": 441, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 72 year old woman with HFrEF started sacubitril valsartan two weeks ago. Her creatinine has risen from 1.1 to 1.35 mg/dL, an increase of roughly 22 percent. She feels well, blood pressure is 114/72 mm Hg, potassium is 4.6 mEq/L, and she has no congestion. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Stop sacubitril valsartan and refer to nephrology for evaluation of renal injury", "B": "Continue current therapy and repeat renal function testing in one to two weeks", "C": "Halve the sacubitril valsartan dose and add a loop diuretic at the same time", "D": "Order renal artery imaging before deciding whether the drug can be continued", "E": "Switch to hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate to protect the remaining renal function"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue current therapy and repeat renal function testing in one to two weeks): a creatinine rise of up to about 30 percent after starting a renin angiotensin system inhibitor reflects an expected hemodynamic effect and does not require stopping the drug when the patient is stable. Discontinuation or substitution with hydralazine and nitrates sacrifices survival benefit for a laboratory change within the accepted range. Dose reduction with an added diuretic could worsen renal perfusion in a euvolemic patient, and imaging is reserved for larger or progressive rises suggesting renovascular disease."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 59 year old man with HFrEF has been hospitalized twice for heart failure in the past twelve months, now requires furosemide 120 mg twice daily, and recently needed his sacubitril valsartan dose lowered because of symptomatic hypotension. His systolic blood pressure runs near 92 mm Hg. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in his care?", "options": {"A": "Increase the diuretic further and continue routine follow up every six months", "B": "Add digoxin and defer any specialty consultation until another hospitalization occurs", "C": "Enroll him in cardiac rehabilitation as the definitive response to these events", "D": "Refer now for advanced heart failure evaluation at a specialized center", "E": "Order a repeat echocardiogram annually and reassess the trajectory afterward"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer now for advanced heart failure evaluation at a specialized center): repeated hospitalizations, escalating diuretic requirements, hypotension limiting guideline directed therapy, and intolerance of neurohormonal dosing are core referral triggers captured by the I NEED HELP mnemonic, and referral should occur before end organ injury forecloses options such as transplant or durable support. Escalating diuretics with routine follow up, adding digoxin while waiting for the next admission, or annual imaging each accept continued decline. Rehabilitation is a useful adjunct but does not address an advanced disease trajectory."}, {"id": 447, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 35 year old man with Eisenmenger syndrome develops mild systemic hypertension, and his primary care clinician considers adding a potent short acting systemic arterial vasodilator. From the standpoint of shunt physiology, what is the principal hemodynamic danger of lowering systemic vascular resistance in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Coronary steal develops as blood is diverted away from the hypertrophied ventricle", "B": "Systemic vasodilation raises pulmonary venous pressure and provokes flash pulmonary edema", "C": "Reflex tachycardia shortens diastole and abruptly reduces subendocardial perfusion time", "D": "Venous pooling elevates hematocrit acutely and precipitates a hyperviscosity crisis", "E": "Right to left shunting increases, deepening hypoxemia and risking circulatory collapse"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Right to left shunting increases, deepening hypoxemia and risking circulatory collapse): Shunt direction in Eisenmenger physiology follows the balance between pulmonary and systemic resistance, and the pulmonary side is fixed by vascular disease. Dropping systemic resistance therefore drives more desaturated blood across the defect, worsening cyanosis and potentially causing syncope or death. The other mechanisms are not the dominant hazard of afterload reduction here."}, {"id": 448, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 49 year old woman presents with palpitations, weight loss, and atrial fibrillation at 142 beats per minute with the laboratory results shown. Blood pressure is 128/74 mmHg and she has no heart failure signs. Alongside starting a thionamide, which agent is most appropriate for immediate rate control?", "options": {"A": "Digoxin loading, since glycosides work especially well in hyperthyroid atrial fibrillation", "B": "Intravenous amiodarone infusion, chosen here for its combined rate control and rhythm control properties", "C": "Immediate electrical cardioversion followed by flecainide for maintenance of sinus rhythm", "D": "Propranolol, which slows the ventricular rate and blunts peripheral thyroid hormone effects", "E": "Verapamil monotherapy, titrated upward until the resting rate falls below eighty"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Propranolol, which slows the ventricular rate and blunts peripheral thyroid hormone effects): beta blockade is first line for thyrotoxic atrial fibrillation because it controls rate, relieves adrenergic symptoms, and at higher doses propranolol modestly reduces peripheral conversion of thyroxine to triiodothyronine. Digoxin performs poorly in hyperthyroidism because increased clearance and sympathetic tone blunt its effect. Amiodarone would add a large iodine load to a thyrotoxic gland, and cardioversion is premature because sinus rhythm rarely holds until the hormone excess is corrected.", "image": "question_images/q_448.png"}, {"id": 451, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 58 year old man with chronic HFrEF is admitted with worsening dyspnea and fatigue. His findings on arrival are summarized in the exhibit shown. He has already received one dose of intravenous furosemide with modest urine output. Which of the following is the most appropriate addition to his management at this point?", "options": {"A": "Add an intravenous nitroglycerin infusion to reduce afterload and speed the ongoing decongestion", "B": "Begin a dobutamine infusion to augment cardiac output while continuing diuresis", "C": "Give a one liter crystalloid bolus to improve the low blood pressure", "D": "Restart the home carvedilol dose to control the elevated heart rate", "E": "Add oral metolazone alone and continue observation without other changes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Begin a dobutamine infusion to augment cardiac output while continuing diuresis): the exhibit shown describes a cold and wet profile, with hypotension, cool extremities, rising lactate and creatinine, and congestion, which indicates low output requiring inotropic support to enable effective decongestion. A vasodilator would drop an already low pressure, and a fluid bolus adds volume to a congested patient. Restarting a beta blocker during low output decompensation suppresses the compensatory contractility he depends on, and a thiazide alone does not address hypoperfusion.", "image": "question_images/q_451.png"}, {"id": 452, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 63 year old asymptomatic man with severe primary mitral regurgitation has serial echocardiograms showing an ejection fraction of 63 percent while his left ventricular end systolic diameter has grown from 36 to 42 mm over eighteen months. His operative risk is low. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Continue six month interval imaging because the ejection fraction remains normal", "B": "Refer for mitral valve surgery based on the end systolic diameter", "C": "Begin a beta blocker to limit remodeling and repeat imaging in one year", "D": "Obtain transesophageal echocardiography and defer any decision until symptoms emerge", "E": "Start sacubitril valsartan and reassess the ventricular dimensions in six months"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for mitral valve surgery based on the end systolic diameter): a left ventricular end systolic diameter of 40 mm or more in severe primary regurgitation indicates intervention even with preserved ejection fraction and no symptoms, and the documented growth to 42 mm meets that criterion. Deferring until symptoms appear or the fraction declines risks irreversible dysfunction. Medical therapy does not alter the course of primary degenerative valve disease."}, {"id": 454, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 45 year old marathon runner has an incidental finding on overnight ambulatory monitoring of progressive PR prolongation followed by single dropped beats during sleep, resolving with daytime activity. He has no syncope, presyncope, or fatigue, and his daytime conduction is normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Permanent dual chamber pacemaker implantation to prevent progression of the block", "B": "Reassurance without therapy, since this pattern reflects vagal tone in athletes", "C": "Electrophysiology study to localize the level of block within the conduction system", "D": "Restriction from endurance exercise until the nocturnal conduction findings resolve", "E": "An implantable loop recorder to capture additional nocturnal conduction data"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reassurance without therapy, since this pattern reflects vagal tone in athletes): nocturnal Wenckebach block in a trained endurance athlete is a benign consequence of high vagal tone, and asymptomatic type I block requires no pacing. A pacemaker is indicated only for symptomatic or infranodal disease. Invasive study and prolonged monitoring add nothing when the pattern is classic and asymptomatic, and exercise restriction is unwarranted for a physiologic finding."}, {"id": 455, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 70 year old man on sotalol 120 mg twice daily for atrial fibrillation develops an acute kidney injury during a diarrheal illness. A routine electrocardiogram now shows a corrected QT interval of 540 ms without arrhythmia. He feels weak but has no syncope. Which of the following is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Continue sotalol at the current dose with a repeat tracing next week", "B": "Halve the current sotalol dose and recheck the corrected interval at the next outpatient visit", "C": "Add oral magnesium supplements while continuing the current sotalol regimen", "D": "Hold sotalol, admit for monitoring, and correct electrolytes and volume status", "E": "Switch to dofetilide at a standard dose while kidney function recovers"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold sotalol, admit for monitoring, and correct electrolytes and volume status): sotalol is renally cleared, so acute kidney injury raises drug levels while diarrhea depletes potassium and magnesium, and a corrected QT of 540 ms signals imminent torsades risk requiring drug cessation and monitored correction. Continuing or merely halving the dose leaves a markedly prolonged interval unprotected. Oral magnesium does not address drug accumulation, and dofetilide shares both renal clearance and QT liability."}, {"id": 459, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 17 year old asymptomatic soccer player has preexcitation discovered on a preparticipation electrocardiogram. Exercise testing shows persistence of the delta wave at peak heart rates. His family asks how to determine whether the pathway is dangerous before he continues competitive sports. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Invasive electrophysiology study to measure the accessory pathway refractory period", "B": "Permanent disqualification from all competitive athletics without further cardiac testing", "C": "Empiric daily flecainide to slow pathway conduction during athletic activity", "D": "Annual resting electrocardiograms with clearance to play if unchanged", "E": "Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging to define the pathway location before decisions"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Invasive electrophysiology study to measure the accessory pathway refractory period): persistent preexcitation at high heart rates fails to identify a low risk pathway, so invasive testing of pathway refractoriness and inducibility is the appropriate way to risk stratify a young athlete, with ablation available in the same session. Disqualification is unnecessary when the pathway can be characterized or cured. Empiric flecainide, surveillance tracings, and magnetic resonance imaging do not measure conduction risk."}, {"id": 460, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 66 year old man has confirmed stage 1 hypertension averaging 134/82 mm Hg on home monitoring. He has hyperlipidemia, continues to smoke, and his calculated 10 year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk is 14 percent. Following the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline, what is the most appropriate initial management strategy for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Lifestyle modification alone for now with structured clinical reassessment in three to six months", "B": "One first line antihypertensive combined with comprehensive lifestyle modification now", "C": "Two antihypertensives from complementary classes started together at half doses", "D": "Statin therapy first, deferring blood pressure treatment until risk is retested", "E": "Ambulatory monitoring for one more month before any therapeutic decision"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (One first line antihypertensive combined with comprehensive lifestyle modification now): With stage 1 hypertension and a 10 year atherosclerotic risk of 10 percent or higher, the guideline recommends starting drug therapy alongside lifestyle change rather than lifestyle measures alone. Initial two drug therapy is reserved for stage 2 values or readings far above goal. His elevation is already confirmed by home monitoring, so further observation delays indicated treatment, and statin therapy addresses lipids without substituting for blood pressure control."}, {"id": 463, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "An 80 year old woman with prior bypass surgery presents with recurrent angina. Angiography shows a chronically occluded, diffusely degenerated vein graft to the right coronary artery, while the native right coronary artery has a discrete severe stenosis that appears technically approachable. Which revascularization approach is generally preferred here?", "options": {"A": "Recanalize the occluded vein graft, since it was the surgeon's intended conduit", "B": "Redo coronary bypass surgery through resternotomy to replace the failed vein graft with a new vein", "C": "Treat both the graft and the native vessel during the same procedure", "D": "Medical therapy alone, because neither potential target can be approached safely", "E": "Percutaneous intervention of the native right coronary artery rather than the graft"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Percutaneous intervention of the native right coronary artery rather than the graft): intervention on the native right coronary artery is generally preferred over attempting a chronically occluded degenerated graft, because native vessel results are more durable with less embolic hazard. Recanalizing an occluded old vein graft has poor long term patency, redo surgery carries elevated risk for a single territory, and treating both targets adds hazard without added benefit. Medical therapy alone concedes symptoms that a feasible native vessel intervention could relieve."}, {"id": 464, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 46 year old man arrives late for his appointment, having jogged from the parking garage after smoking a cigarette and finishing a large coffee on the way. The nurse plans to check his blood pressure immediately so the visit stays on schedule. What should you advise to obtain a valid reading?", "options": {"A": "Measure now but subtract 10 mm Hg to correct for recent exertion", "B": "Measure blood pressure in both arms immediately and record whichever of the two values is lower", "C": "Delay measurement about 30 minutes after caffeine, exercise, and smoking, then rest him", "D": "Have him lie supine for two minutes and measure at the wrist", "E": "Skip today's reading and rely on his home device numbers from last year"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delay measurement about 30 minutes after caffeine, exercise, and smoking, then rest him): Guideline technique asks patients to avoid caffeine, exercise, and smoking for at least 30 minutes before measurement, followed by about five minutes of seated rest, because each exposure transiently raises pressure. No validated arithmetic correction exists, so subtracting a fixed amount invents a number. Choosing the lower of two immediate arm readings, wrist measurement while supine, and year old home data all substitute unreliable information for a properly obtained value."}, {"id": 466, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 66 year old man with severe secondary mitral regurgitation has an ejection fraction of 18 percent, left ventricular end systolic diameter 74 mm, and recurrent hospitalizations despite optimal medical therapy, recently requiring intravenous inotropes. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in his care?", "options": {"A": "Transcatheter edge to edge repair to interrupt the cycle of decompensation", "B": "Surgical mitral annuloplasty using an undersized complete rigid ring", "C": "Escalation of diuretic therapy with a continuous home inotrope infusion administered with palliative intent", "D": "Evaluation for advanced heart failure therapies including transplantation or mechanical support", "E": "Percutaneous mitral valve replacement through a transseptal approach at a trial center"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Evaluation for advanced heart failure therapies including transplantation or mechanical support): an ejection fraction below 20 percent with an end systolic diameter above 70 mm and inotrope dependence falls outside the population in which transcatheter repair improved outcomes and identifies end stage ventricular disease. Such patients belong in an advanced heart failure pathway for transplantation or mechanical support. Neither valve surgery nor palliative inotropes addresses the failing ventricle definitively."}, {"id": 468, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 34 year old competitive cyclist has highly symptomatic paroxysmal atrial fibrillation several times per month. He has no structural heart disease, no comorbidities, and strongly prefers to avoid long term daily medication after reading about treatment options. Which of the following is the most appropriate counseling about first line catheter ablation?", "options": {"A": "Ablation is offered only after two different antiarrhythmic drugs have failed", "B": "Ablation is a guideline supported first line option for a patient like him", "C": "Ablation is deferred until episodes become persistent rather than paroxysmal", "D": "Ablation is reserved for patients who cannot tolerate any nodal blocking agent", "E": "Ablation is discouraged in endurance athletes until they stop competitive training"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ablation is a guideline supported first line option for a patient like him): current guidance gives first line catheter ablation a class 1 recommendation for symptomatic paroxysmal atrial fibrillation in younger patients with few comorbidities, where it outperforms drugs for maintaining sinus rhythm. No rule requires failure of one or two drugs first in this group. Waiting for persistence reduces success rates, and neither drug intolerance nor retirement from sport is a prerequisite."}, {"id": 469, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 45 year old man with chronic severe aortic regurgitation from a bicuspid valve reports new dyspnea walking uphill. Echocardiography shows an ejection fraction of 58 percent, left ventricular end systolic diameter 48 mm, and holodiastolic flow reversal in the descending aorta. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Refer for aortic valve surgery because symptoms have developed with severe regurgitation", "B": "Begin extended release nifedipine and reassess his exercise tolerance in three months", "C": "Repeat echocardiography in six months to confirm progression of ventricular size", "D": "Order cardiopulmonary exercise testing before committing to an operative treatment pathway", "E": "Start a loop diuretic and follow his symptoms at routine clinic visits"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for aortic valve surgery because symptoms have developed with severe regurgitation): symptomatic severe aortic regurgitation is a class 1 indication for surgery regardless of ejection fraction. Vasodilators and diuretics palliate but do not change the natural history once symptoms begin, and surveillance defers definitive treatment past the point the guideline sets. Additional functional testing is unnecessary when the symptom itself already establishes the indication."}, {"id": 471, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 42 year old man with paroxysmal hypertension, headaches, and drenching sweats undergoes biochemical evaluation; interfering medications were stopped beforehand and sampling was performed supine through an indwelling catheter after 30 minutes of rest. Given the laboratory results shown, what is the most appropriate next step in evaluating his suspected secondary hypertension?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the metanephrine panel seated in clinic to confirm reproducibility first", "B": "Proceed directly to adrenal venous sampling to lateralize the hormone secretion", "C": "Begin phenoxybenzamine and schedule adrenalectomy without any localizing imaging studies", "D": "Obtain computed tomography of the abdomen and pelvis to localize the tumor", "E": "Order a clonidine suppression test before pursuing cross sectional imaging"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Obtain computed tomography of the abdomen and pelvis to localize the tumor): The laboratory results shown reveal plasma metanephrine and normetanephrine values roughly five times the upper reference limits under ideal sampling conditions, which is biochemically diagnostic of pheochromocytoma or paraganglioma rather than a borderline result needing confirmation. The next step is cross sectional imaging of the abdomen and pelvis, where most of these tumors arise. Clonidine suppression and repeat sampling suit equivocal mild elevations, venous sampling belongs to aldosteronism workups, and surgery cannot be planned before the tumor is found.", "image": "question_images/q_471.png"}, {"id": 475, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 60 year old woman is discharged five days after an uncomplicated STEMI with an ejection fraction of 52 percent. She previously walked daily and gardened, and asks what physical activity is safe during her first weeks at home before rehabilitation begins. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Resume daily walking at a comfortable pace, deferring heavy lifting for now", "B": "Rest in bed except for bathroom trips until her rehabilitation intake visit", "C": "Resume all prior activity immediately, including moving soil bags and digging", "D": "Limit activity to seated tasks indoors until a six week echocardiogram", "E": "Start high intensity interval training at home to accelerate her conditioning"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Resume daily walking at a comfortable pace, deferring heavy lifting for now): early progressive ambulation is encouraged after an uncomplicated infarction, while strenuous or heavy isometric work waits until supervised rehabilitation guides progression. Bed rest and seated confinement promote deconditioning and thrombosis without protecting the heart. Jumping straight back to heavy gardening or unsupervised high intensity intervals skips the graded increase in workload that early recovery calls for."}, {"id": 476, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 54 year old man with confirmed vasospastic angina continues to have weekly nocturnal episodes despite amlodipine 10 mg daily and complete smoking cessation. His blood pressure is 122/74 mm Hg and he has no heart failure. Which addition to his regimen is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "A nonselective beta blocker added to blunt the catecholamine trigger of his coronary spasm", "B": "A long acting nitrate, or a second calcium channel blocker from another class", "C": "Low dose aspirin at bedtime to interrupt platelet initiated coronary spasm", "D": "Ivabradine to slow his nocturnal heart rate below the spasm threshold", "E": "An implantable loop recorder before any change in medication is made"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A long acting nitrate, or a second calcium channel blocker from another class): when spasm persists despite a maximized single calcium channel blocker, adding a long acting nitrate or combining dihydropyridine and nondihydropyridine classes is the accepted escalation. Nonselective beta blockade can worsen spasm, aspirin does not suppress vasomotor episodes, and ivabradine has no role in spasm prevention. A loop recorder would document rather than treat episodes already captured with diagnostic changes."}, {"id": 477, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 65 year old man with HFrEF takes oral furosemide 40 mg daily for maintenance volume control. Because of a persistent supply problem at his pharmacy, his clinician plans to convert him to bumetanide at an equivalent oral dose. Which of the following is the most appropriate replacement prescription for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe bumetanide 1 mg daily as the approximate equipotent oral dose", "B": "Prescribe bumetanide 5 mg daily to match the furosemide milligram strength", "C": "Prescribe bumetanide 0.25 mg daily to reduce the chance of overdiuresis", "D": "Prescribe bumetanide 40 mg daily since oral loop doses convert one to one", "E": "Prescribe bumetanide 10 mg daily and halve it if his weight begins falling"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Prescribe bumetanide 1 mg daily as the approximate equipotent oral dose): the standard oral equivalence is furosemide 40 mg to bumetanide 1 mg to torsemide 20 mg. Doses of 5, 10, or 40 mg of bumetanide represent five to forty times the intended potency and would cause profound volume and electrolyte depletion. A dose of 0.25 mg supplies only a quarter of his established requirement and would allow congestion to recur."}, {"id": 479, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 68 year old man on amiodarone for ventricular arrhythmia develops thyrotoxicosis. Ultrasound shows a normal sized gland without nodules, and color Doppler demonstrates absent vascularity, consistent with the destructive form. His defibrillator makes stopping amiodarone negotiable. What is the most appropriate initial pharmacologic treatment for this patient?", "options": {"A": "High dose methimazole to shut down new thyroid hormone synthesis rapidly", "B": "Radioactive iodine ablation performed as soon as scheduling allows this month", "C": "Potassium perchlorate alone to discharge the intrathyroidal iodine stores gradually", "D": "Urgent total thyroidectomy before any medical stabilization has been attempted", "E": "Oral prednisone in moderate to high dose, with taper guided by thyroid function"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Oral prednisone in moderate to high dose, with taper guided by thyroid function): type 2 amiodarone induced thyrotoxicosis is a destructive thyroiditis releasing preformed hormone, so glucocorticoids that quiet the inflammatory process are the established therapy and most patients respond within weeks. Methimazole targets new hormone synthesis, which is the mechanism of type 1 rather than this destructive form. Radioiodine fails because the iodine loaded gland has negligible uptake, and thyroidectomy is reserved for refractory or unstable cases after medical therapy."}, {"id": 480, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 64 year old woman with a dual chamber pacemaker for sinus node dysfunction, with a reliable underlying rhythm, is scheduled for knee arthroplasty using monopolar electrocautery confined to the leg. The anesthesiologist asks whether the device requires reprogramming. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Reprogram the pacemaker to an asynchronous mode before the patient enters the room", "B": "Cancel surgery until a full device interrogation is completed at the implanting center", "C": "Require the surgeon to switch entirely to bipolar electrocautery before proceeding", "D": "No reprogramming is needed because cautery far below the device rarely interferes", "E": "Suspend all pacing output during surgery and monitor the underlying rhythm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No reprogramming is needed because cautery far below the device rarely interferes): with surgery below the umbilicus, electromagnetic interference reaching a pectoral generator is minimal, and a patient who is not pacemaker dependent tolerates rare inhibited beats without harm. Routine asynchronous programming and mandatory bipolar cautery are unnecessary for distal procedures. Canceling surgery for interrogation is disproportionate, and suspending pacing output removes a safety feature for no benefit."}, {"id": 483, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 54 year old man's blood pressure remains 158/96 mmHg despite adherence to three antihypertensive agents including a diuretic at optimal doses. He is obese, snores loudly, and his wife describes pauses in his breathing at night. What is the most appropriate next diagnostic step for his resistant hypertension?", "options": {"A": "Renal artery duplex ultrasound before any other secondary cause is considered", "B": "A trial of a fourth antihypertensive class without further evaluation of causes", "C": "Plasma metanephrines to exclude pheochromocytoma as the leading candidate diagnosis", "D": "Sleep testing to evaluate for obstructive sleep apnea given his symptom profile", "E": "Twenty four hour urinary sodium collection to document dietary indiscretion first"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sleep testing to evaluate for obstructive sleep apnea given his symptom profile): sleep apnea is among the most common secondary contributors to resistant hypertension, and this man's obesity, loud snoring, and witnessed apneas make it the leading consideration, so polysomnography or home sleep testing should come next. Renal artery imaging and metanephrines target causes his history does not suggest. Adding a fourth drug without seeking the driver leaves the underlying disorder untreated, and a urine sodium collection addresses adherence rather than his clear apnea signal."}, {"id": 489, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 66 year old woman with prior NSTEMI and peripheral artery disease takes rosuvastatin 40 mg daily with good adherence. Her LDL cholesterol is 82 mg/dL on repeat measurement. She has no medication complaints and her other risk factors are controlled. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Continue current therapy alone, since any value below 100 mg/dL suffices", "B": "Add ezetimibe 10 mg daily to the maximally tolerated statin regimen", "C": "Start a PCSK9 monoclonal antibody before trying any oral additions", "D": "Switch rosuvastatin to atorvastatin 80 mg to obtain deeper LDL lowering", "E": "Add extended release niacin to raise HDL and lower LDL together"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Add ezetimibe 10 mg daily to the maximally tolerated statin regimen): she is a very high risk patient whose LDL remains at or above the 70 mg/dL threshold on maximal statin therapy, which is the trigger for adding ezetimibe first. A PCSK9 inhibitor is reserved for those still above threshold after ezetimibe, both for cost and stepwise logic. Accepting a value in the eighties undertreats her risk category, switching between maximal statins yields little additional lowering, and niacin adds adverse effects without outcome benefit on statin therapy."}, {"id": 493, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 59 year old man remains at 148/92 mm Hg on maximally dosed losartan, amlodipine, and chlorthalidone. Adherence is confirmed by pharmacy records, ambulatory monitoring verifies the elevation, and screening for secondary causes is unrevealing. Potassium is 4.1 mmol/L with normal renal function. What is the best next pharmacologic step?", "options": {"A": "Add hydralazine three times daily for direct arteriolar smooth muscle relaxation", "B": "Add clonidine twice daily to blunt central sympathetic outflow around the clock", "C": "Replace the chlorthalidone with furosemide to intensify urinary sodium removal further", "D": "Add doxazosin at bedtime to target early morning peripheral vascular resistance", "E": "Add low dose spironolactone while monitoring potassium and renal function closely"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Add low dose spironolactone while monitoring potassium and renal function closely): He meets the definition of true resistant hypertension after adherence, measurement, and secondary cause checks, and randomized evidence from PATHWAY 2 shows spironolactone lowers pressure more than alpha or beta blockade in exactly this situation. His potassium and kidney function permit safe use with surveillance. Hydralazine, clonidine, and doxazosin are later additions, and swapping a long acting thiazide for a short acting loop diuretic usually weakens control in preserved renal function."}, {"id": 494, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 69 year old man with diabetes and an ankle brachial index of 1.62 undergoes toe brachial index measurement to clarify whether peripheral artery disease is truly present. The vascular laboratory has now reported his toe brachial index result. Which value would confirm the diagnosis of peripheral artery disease?", "options": {"A": "A toe brachial value below 1.10, mirroring the ankle based diagnostic cutoff", "B": "A toe brachial value below 0.90, matching the standard ankle threshold exactly", "C": "A toe brachial value below 0.70, the accepted diagnostic threshold for this test", "D": "A toe pressure under 80 millimeters of mercury regardless of the calculated ratio", "E": "A toe brachial value below 0.30, the level defining any abnormal perfusion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A toe brachial value below 0.70, the accepted diagnostic threshold for this test): the toe brachial index has a lower normal range than the ankle based measurement because digital pressures run lower than ankle pressures, so a value below 0.70 is the accepted diagnostic threshold. Applying the 0.90 ankle cutoff to toe measurements would overdiagnose disease, while 0.30 describes severe ischemia associated with poor wound healing rather than the diagnostic boundary. An isolated toe pressure is interpreted alongside the ratio, not in place of it."}, {"id": 498, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 27 year old man with stable atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia receives rapid intravenous adenosine at a 6 mg dose through a proximal line with a flush. The tachycardia continues unchanged after one minute. His blood pressure remains 116/72 mm Hg and he is comfortable. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Proceed directly to synchronized electrical cardioversion with procedural sedation at this point", "B": "Switch to intravenous verapamil given as a slow bolus over two minutes", "C": "Repeat adenosine at the same 6 mg dose through the same line", "D": "Begin intravenous amiodarone loading and arrange admission for telemetry observation overnight", "E": "Give a second adenosine bolus at 12 mg with a rapid flush"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Give a second adenosine bolus at 12 mg with a rapid flush): when an initial 6 mg dose fails, guideline based practice is to escalate to 12 mg rather than repeat the same dose. Verapamil is a reasonable later option but is not preferred before completing the adenosine sequence in a stable patient. Cardioversion and amiodarone loading are unnecessary while the patient remains hemodynamically stable."}, {"id": 499, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 52 year old woman with HER2 positive breast cancer begins trastuzumab after completing anthracycline therapy. Her baseline ejection fraction is 58 percent and she has no cardiac symptoms. The oncology team asks the consulting cardiologist to define the cardiac surveillance plan during her year of trastuzumab treatment. Which of the following is the most appropriate schedule?", "options": {"A": "Repeat echocardiography approximately every three months during the trastuzumab course", "B": "Repeat echocardiography only once, at the completion of the full treatment year", "C": "Rely on annual electrocardiograms because they detect ventricular dysfunction early", "D": "Repeat surveillance imaging every two years given her entirely normal baseline ejection fraction value", "E": "Follow weekly natriuretic peptides in place of any scheduled cardiac imaging"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat echocardiography approximately every three months during the trastuzumab course): trastuzumab related ventricular dysfunction is usually asymptomatic, often reversible, and most treatable when found early, so ejection fraction is reassessed about every three months throughout therapy. A single end of treatment study or a two year interval would discover dysfunction only after months of continued exposure. Electrocardiograms do not measure ejection fraction, and natriuretic peptides alone have not replaced imaging based surveillance in this setting."}, {"id": 500, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 66 year old woman with known heart failure and an ejection fraction of 30 percent presents with atrial fibrillation, a rapid ventricular response, worsening pulmonary edema, and a blood pressure of 84/56 mm Hg. Symptoms began two hours ago. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous diltiazem bolus followed by a continuous infusion for rate control", "B": "Oral metoprolol with furosemide and reassessment after diuresis is achieved", "C": "Intravenous digoxin loading with rate reassessment over the next six hours", "D": "Urgent synchronized cardioversion after anticoagulation is initiated without delay", "E": "Intravenous amiodarone infusion alone with telemetry observation in the coronary unit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Urgent synchronized cardioversion after anticoagulation is initiated without delay): hemodynamic instability from rapid atrial fibrillation calls for immediate electrical cardioversion, and a clear onset within 48 hours does not remove the need to start anticoagulation. Diltiazem depresses contractility and can worsen hypotension with a reduced ejection fraction. Digoxin and amiodarone act too slowly to rescue a decompensating patient."}, {"id": 501, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 29 year old man presents 45 minutes after snorting cocaine with ongoing substernal chest pain. His findings are summarized in the exhibit shown. Aspirin has been given and serial troponins are pending. Which of the following is the most appropriate next pharmacologic step?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous metoprolol to control the heart rate and reduce myocardial demand", "B": "Oral labetalol, since combined receptor blockade avoids unopposed alpha stimulation", "C": "Immediate catheterization before any sedating medication clouds his neurologic examination", "D": "Intravenous benzodiazepines with nitroglycerin for blood pressure and pain control", "E": "Intramuscular haloperidol to treat agitation before addressing the chest pain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intravenous benzodiazepines with nitroglycerin for blood pressure and pain control): benzodiazepines reduce the central sympathetic drive of cocaine toxicity while nitroglycerin relieves vasoconstriction, and together they treat both hemodynamics and ischemia. Beta blockade in the acute phase, including combined alpha and beta agents, is traditionally avoided because unopposed alpha effects can worsen coronary vasoconstriction. Antipsychotics do not address ischemia and can lower the seizure threshold, and catheterization is not the first move in a patient without ST elevation whose vasospastic component may respond to medical therapy.", "image": "question_images/q_501.png"}, {"id": 502, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 48 year old woman completed three months of apixaban for a first pulmonary embolism that occurred without any identifiable provoking factor. Her physician assistant now decides about treatment duration. Bleeding risk is low. What is the most appropriate anticoagulation plan going forward for this unprovoked event?", "options": {"A": "Continue anticoagulation with periodic reassessment, favoring extended therapy given her unprovoked event", "B": "Stop the oral anticoagulation now, since three months of therapy completes treatment for every pulmonary embolism", "C": "Switch to daily aspirin, which protects as effectively as continued anticoagulation", "D": "Stop apixaban and restart it only during future long haul flights", "E": "Bridge to twice weekly enoxaparin injections for another three month block"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue anticoagulation with periodic reassessment, favoring extended therapy given her unprovoked event): recurrence after an unprovoked pulmonary embolism approaches ten percent in the first year off therapy and continues accruing, so guidelines favor extended anticoagulation when bleeding risk is low, often at a reduced apixaban dose, with the decision revisited regularly. A fixed three month course is appropriate for events with major transient provocation, which hers lacked. Aspirin lowers recurrence far less than anticoagulation, intermittent situational dosing has no evidence base, and switching to injections adds burden without advantage."}, {"id": 504, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 61 year old man hospitalized with decompensated HFrEF is not improving despite escalating intravenous diuretics. His examination is equivocal, with borderline blood pressure, uncertain volume status, and creatinine that continues to rise. The team is also beginning to discuss candidacy for advanced therapies. Which of the following is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?", "options": {"A": "Perform right heart catheterization to define filling pressures and cardiac output", "B": "Order daily serial brain natriuretic peptide levels to track the progress of his decongestion", "C": "Obtain a noncontrast chest CT to quantify the degree of pulmonary edema", "D": "Repeat transthoracic echocardiography weekly until the examination becomes clearer", "E": "Send a renal biopsy to determine the reason the creatinine keeps rising"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perform right heart catheterization to define filling pressures and cardiac output): invasive hemodynamic assessment is indicated when volume status and perfusion remain uncertain despite therapy, when renal function worsens without a clear cause, and when advanced therapy candidacy is being evaluated. Serial natriuretic peptides and repeated echocardiograms provide indirect estimates that have already failed to resolve the question. Chest CT quantifies edema without explaining hemodynamics, and renal biopsy is not the evaluation for cardiorenal deterioration in decompensated heart failure."}, {"id": 505, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 22 year old collegiate soccer player is recovering from acute idiopathic pericarditis treated with ibuprofen and colchicine, and she asks when she may return to competitive training. Her symptoms resolved within one week. Applying current recommendations for athletes, how should you structure her exercise restriction and clearance?", "options": {"A": "Allow immediate return once chest pain resolves and she tolerates light jogging", "B": "Restrict competitive sport for three months, then reassess symptoms, markers, and testing", "C": "Restrict all competitive athletic activity for twelve months even after documented full clinical recovery", "D": "Permit training when the friction rub disappears on two consecutive examinations", "E": "Limit her to noncontact drills for two weeks, then clear her fully"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Restrict competitive sport for three months, then reassess symptoms, markers, and testing): athletes are held from competitive exercise for approximately three months from onset, with return contingent on resolved symptoms and normalized CRP, ECG, and echocardiogram, because exertion during active inflammation may worsen disease. Immediate return or a two week limit is far too short for an athlete. A fixed twelve month restriction exceeds the recommendation for uncomplicated cases, and disappearance of the rub alone is an unreliable clearance endpoint."}, {"id": 506, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 66 year old man with recent pneumococcal bacteremia develops fever, hypotension, and a moderate pericardial effusion, and pericardiocentesis yields frankly cloudy fluid with a neutrophil count of 48,000 per microliter. Blood cultures remain positive. Beyond intravenous antibiotics, what management does purulent pericarditis specifically require in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Oral antibiotic step down therapy once fever resolves for twenty four hours", "B": "Corticosteroids to reduce inflammation and prevent later constrictive complications", "C": "Serial echocardiography monitoring alone, reserving any drainage procedures for later hemodynamic deterioration", "D": "Pericardial fluid cytology and repeat imaging after completing the antibiotic course", "E": "Effective pericardial drainage, by catheter or surgery, combined with intravenous antibiotics"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Effective pericardial drainage, by catheter or surgery, combined with intravenous antibiotics): purulent pericarditis behaves like an abscess, so antibiotics alone rarely sterilize the space, and definitive drainage, sometimes with intrapericardial fibrinolysis or surgical washout for loculations, is essential given mortality approaching 40 percent even when treated. Watchful waiting with serial imaging risks rapid tamponade and constriction. Early oral step down is inadequate for this infection, cytology addresses malignancy rather than infection, and corticosteroids do not substitute for source control."}, {"id": 508, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 70 year old pacemaker dependent man with an implantable cardioverter defibrillator is scheduled for shoulder surgery with monopolar electrocautery near the generator. The surgical team asks how to manage the device intraoperatively. Which of the following is the most appropriate plan for the procedure?", "options": {"A": "Tape a magnet over the generator for the duration of the operation", "B": "Reprogram shock therapies off and pacing to an asynchronous mode beforehand", "C": "Deactivate the entire device and rely on transcutaneous pacing pads if needed", "D": "Proceed with standard monitoring and make no programming changes before surgery", "E": "Postpone surgery until the generator can be repositioned away from the field"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reprogram shock therapies off and pacing to an asynchronous mode beforehand): in a pacemaker dependent patient, a magnet over a defibrillator suspends shock detection but does not change the pacing mode, so electrocautery near the generator could still inhibit pacing unless the device is reprogrammed. A magnet alone therefore leaves the real hazard unaddressed. Full deactivation with rescue pads is far less reliable than programmed pacing, making no changes risks inhibition, and repositioning the generator is unwarranted."}, {"id": 509, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 68 year old man has symptomatic severe stenosis of a bicuspid aortic valve. Computed tomography shows an ascending aortic diameter of 5.1 cm. His STS score is 2.4 percent and he has no major comorbidity. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment plan?", "options": {"A": "Surgical valve replacement with concomitant ascending aortic replacement during one operation", "B": "Transfemoral transcatheter valve implantation with ongoing imaging surveillance of the ascending aorta", "C": "Isolated surgical valve replacement leaving the dilated aorta for future follow up", "D": "Transcatheter implantation now and staged open aortic repair within two years", "E": "Medical therapy until the aortic diameter reaches the standard surgical threshold"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Surgical valve replacement with concomitant ascending aortic replacement during one operation): a bicuspid valve requiring replacement with an ascending aorta of 4.5 cm or more warrants combined surgical valve and aortic replacement at the time of surgery, even though 5.1 cm alone would not yet meet the higher 5.5 cm standalone aneurysm-repair threshold used when no valve surgery is otherwise planned. Transcatheter therapy leaves the aneurysm unaddressed, and isolated valve surgery wastes the safest moment to repair the aorta. Waiting for a threshold ignores that the criterion is already met."}, {"id": 510, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 74 year old woman with aortic stenosis attends for annual review and denies dyspnea, angina, or syncope at her usual activity level. Her surgical risk is low. Review the echocardiographic findings in the exhibit shown. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Refer for aortic valve replacement after multidisciplinary heart team review", "B": "Repeat transthoracic echocardiography in six to twelve months with clinical review", "C": "Perform a supervised exercise treadmill test to unmask latent symptoms", "D": "Begin guideline directed heart failure therapy and reassess ventricular function later", "E": "Obtain coronary angiography and defer valve decisions until symptoms clearly develop"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for aortic valve replacement after multidisciplinary heart team review): severe aortic stenosis with an ejection fraction below 50 percent is a class 1 indication for replacement even without symptoms, and the exhibit shows both severe valve hemodynamics and a fraction of 45 percent. Surveillance or heart failure medication leaves the obstructing valve untreated while the ventricle declines further. Exercise testing adds nothing once ventricular dysfunction already mandates replacement.", "image": "question_images/q_510.png"}, {"id": 512, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 62 year old man with mitral valve endocarditis from Staphylococcus aureus remains febrile with persistently positive blood cultures on day seven of appropriate intravenous therapy. Imaging shows no drainable abscess elsewhere, and repeat echocardiography shows a stable vegetation. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Extend the same antibiotic course to eight weeks with weekly surveillance cultures", "B": "Refer for valve surgery because the infection is not being controlled", "C": "Add rifampin to enhance penetration of the vegetation by primary therapy", "D": "Switch to daptomycin monotherapy and reassess the cultures in another week", "E": "Place a peripherally inserted line and continue identical therapy at home"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for valve surgery because the infection is not being controlled): persistent bacteremia beyond five to seven days of appropriate antibiotics defines uncontrolled infection, an accepted indication for surgery once other sources are excluded. Prolonging or rearranging a failing regimen leaves infected tissue that antibiotics are failing to sterilize. Rifampin is reserved for prosthetic valve staphylococcal disease, and discharge on identical therapy ignores the documented treatment failure."}, {"id": 514, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 66 year old woman with HFrEF takes valsartan 160 mg twice daily with stable blood pressure and normal potassium and renal function. Her cardiologist plans to transition her to sacubitril valsartan to improve outcomes. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to make this change in her regimen?", "options": {"A": "Wait 36 hours after the last valsartan dose before starting the new drug", "B": "Bridge with oral lisinopril for two full weeks before starting the sacubitril valsartan therapy", "C": "Taper valsartan over one month while introducing sacubitril valsartan gradually", "D": "Begin sacubitril valsartan at the next scheduled dose without any washout interval", "E": "Hold all renin angiotensin blockade for one week before making the switch"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Begin sacubitril valsartan at the next scheduled dose without any washout interval): the 36 hour washout applies only when converting from an ACE inhibitor, because angioedema risk arises from combining neprilysin inhibition with ACE inhibition. An ARB does not raise bradykinin, so the switch can occur at the next dose. Adding a washout, a lisinopril bridge, or a prolonged taper interrupts or complicates effective therapy without any safety gain, and a week without renin angiotensin blockade needlessly withdraws treatment."}, {"id": 515, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 51 year old man with native aortic valve endocarditis develops acute severe aortic regurgitation with pulmonary edema requiring noninvasive ventilation on hospital day three of appropriate antibiotics. His blood cultures have cleared. Which of the following is the most appropriate management at this point?", "options": {"A": "Complete six weeks of antibiotics before considering any operative intervention", "B": "Add an intra aortic balloon pump to stabilize him for delayed surgery", "C": "Increase diuresis and afterload reduction with surgery reserved for cardiogenic shock", "D": "Perform transcatheter aortic valve implantation to avoid operating on infected tissue", "E": "Proceed to early valve surgery during this initial hospitalization"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Proceed to early valve surgery during this initial hospitalization): valve dysfunction causing heart failure is the strongest indication for early surgery in endocarditis, and outcomes worsen when the operation is delayed for antibiotic completion. An intra aortic balloon pump is contraindicated in severe aortic regurgitation because diastolic inflation worsens the leak. Medical stabilization fails with a structurally destroyed valve, and transcatheter implantation is not performed into active infection."}, {"id": 519, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 79 year old woman with advanced dementia and metastatic ovarian cancer is admitted with a small troponin rise, no ECG changes, and resolution of chest discomfort on medical therapy. Her family emphasizes comfort focused goals. Which of the following is the most appropriate management strategy?", "options": {"A": "Immediate cardiac catheterization since any troponin elevation mandates defining the underlying coronary anatomy promptly", "B": "Early angiography within 24 hours followed by stenting of any significant lesion", "C": "Transfer to a tertiary center for surgical standby and complex intervention planning", "D": "Ischemia guided medical therapy aligned with her goals, reserving angiography for refractory symptoms", "E": "Pharmacologic stress testing tomorrow to decide whether catheterization is worthwhile"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ischemia guided medical therapy aligned with her goals, reserving angiography for refractory symptoms): an invasive strategy is chosen when its benefit outweighs risk and matches patient goals, and comfort focused care with severe comorbidity favors medical management. Reflexive catheterization or routine early angiography ignores both prognosis and stated preferences. Tertiary transfer escalates rather than aligns care, and stress testing adds little when the decision not to revascularize has effectively been made by goals of care."}, {"id": 522, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 49 year old woman with recurrent pericarditis and a contraindication to NSAIDs requires corticosteroid therapy. You want to dose prednisone in the manner that minimizes her risk of yet another recurrence while controlling symptoms. Which prescribing strategy best reflects guideline recommendations for corticosteroids in this situation?", "options": {"A": "High dose pulse methylprednisolone for three days followed by abrupt discontinuation", "B": "Alternate day high dose prednisone stopped as soon as pain improves", "C": "A single depot intramuscular injection repeated whenever symptoms recur clinically", "D": "Low to moderate dose prednisone with a very gradual taper after symptom resolution", "E": "Daily high dose prednisone continued unchanged for a full six months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Low to moderate dose prednisone with a very gradual taper after symptom resolution): guidelines advise 0.2 to 0.5 mg per kilogram per day of prednisone, maintained until symptoms resolve and CRP normalizes, followed by a slow decremental taper, because high doses and rapid withdrawal both increase recurrence. Pulse therapy with abrupt cessation and stopping as soon as pain improves invite relapse. Depot injections triggered by recurrences provide erratic exposure, and unchanged high dose therapy for six months maximizes toxicity without added benefit."}, {"id": 525, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 59 year old woman is newly diagnosed with peripheral artery disease after abnormal ankle brachial index testing performed for exertional calf pain. She currently takes no lipid lowering therapy, and her low density lipoprotein cholesterol measures 128 milligrams per deciliter. Which lipid management approach do current guidelines recommend for her?", "options": {"A": "Moderate intensity statin therapy, with escalation guided by future claudication severity", "B": "Ezetimibe monotherapy given daily, since statins provide only limited benefit in noncoronary atherosclerotic disease", "C": "Dietary modification for six months before considering any pharmacologic lipid treatment", "D": "Fenofibrate first, because triglyceride reduction drives benefit in peripheral disease", "E": "High intensity statin therapy, because peripheral artery disease is an atherosclerotic risk equivalent"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (High intensity statin therapy, because peripheral artery disease is an atherosclerotic risk equivalent): peripheral artery disease is clinical atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease, which places her in the highest risk category and calls for high intensity statin therapy regardless of baseline cholesterol. Moderate intensity dosing undertreats established vascular disease, and claudication severity does not guide statin intensity. Ezetimibe and fibrates are adjuncts rather than foundations of therapy, and delaying medication for a dietary trial leaves a high risk patient unprotected during that interval."}, {"id": 526, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 71 year old man is found to have a 4.3 centimeter infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm on a screening ultrasound. He is asymptomatic with well controlled blood pressure, and he stopped smoking after the result. According to surveillance recommendations stratified by aneurysm size, when should his next imaging study occur?", "options": {"A": "In three years, since abdominal aneurysms measuring below five centimeters generally expand very slowly over time", "B": "In approximately twelve months, the surveillance interval for aneurysms of four to five centimeters", "C": "In six months, the interval generally reserved for aneurysms above five centimeters", "D": "In one month, to confirm measurement accuracy before establishing longer intervals", "E": "No further imaging unless he develops abdominal pain or a pulsatile mass"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (In approximately twelve months, the surveillance interval for aneurysms of four to five centimeters): aneurysms measuring 4.0 to 4.9 centimeters warrant ultrasound surveillance at roughly twelve month intervals because their expansion rate and rupture risk sit between small and near threshold lesions. A three year interval applies to aneurysms of 3.0 to 3.9 centimeters, while six month imaging is used once the sac reaches 5.0 centimeters. Immediate reimaging is unnecessary for a technically adequate study, and abandoning surveillance would miss silent growth toward the repair threshold."}, {"id": 535, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 58 year old woman with symptomatic severe aortic stenosis has an STS score of 1.8 percent, no prior sternotomy, and a life expectancy exceeding twenty years. Her anatomy is suitable for either transcatheter or surgical treatment. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation from the heart team?", "options": {"A": "Transfemoral transcatheter implantation because recovery is faster than after open surgery", "B": "Balloon valvuloplasty now with definitive valve replacement deferred until age 65", "C": "Medical management until her symptoms progress enough to justify an operation", "D": "Surgical aortic valve replacement given her age and long expected survival", "E": "Enrollment in surveillance imaging until transcatheter durability data mature further"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Surgical aortic valve replacement given her age and long expected survival): the 2020 guideline recommends surgical replacement for patients younger than 65 years or with life expectancy over 20 years, because long term transcatheter valve durability in younger patients is unproven. Faster recovery does not outweigh the durability question at age 58. Valvuloplasty, watchful waiting, and surveillance all leave symptomatic severe stenosis untreated."}, {"id": 539, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 52 year old woman is evaluated in clinic for three months of exertional chest tightness. She has never had testing for coronary disease, and her presentation is summarized in the exhibit shown. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial diagnostic test?", "options": {"A": "Coronary CT angiography to define the presence and extent of coronary disease", "B": "Exercise treadmill electrocardiography, since she can achieve an adequate workload", "C": "Invasive coronary angiography, since her symptom pattern establishes obstructive disease already", "D": "Coronary artery calcium scoring alone to decide whether any symptoms need evaluation", "E": "Resting transthoracic echocardiography as the definitive assessment of her chest pain"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Coronary CT angiography to define the presence and extent of coronary disease): she has intermediate risk stable chest pain, and left bundle branch block makes exercise ST segments uninterpretable, so anatomic imaging with CT angiography is a preferred first test. Treadmill electrocardiography cannot be read through the conduction abnormality her tracing shows. Going straight to invasive angiography overtreats an untested patient, calcium scoring cannot evaluate symptoms of possible obstruction, and a resting echocardiogram does not assess the coronary arteries.", "image": "question_images/q_539.png"}, {"id": 540, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 55 year old man with intermediate high risk pulmonary embolism on heparin becomes progressively hypotensive over two hours despite monitoring. The hospital has a pulmonary embolism response team and interventional capability. His bleeding risk from recent gastrointestinal hemorrhage is judged prohibitive for systemic lysis. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Continue heparin unchanged, since anticoagulation eventually dissolves acute thrombus alone", "B": "Full dose systemic thrombolysis despite the recent gastrointestinal bleeding event", "C": "Immediate transition to warfarin, which provides stronger protection than heparin", "D": "Activate the response team for catheter directed therapy or mechanical thrombectomy", "E": "Empiric antibiotics, because sepsis better explains deterioration than clot progression"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Activate the response team for catheter directed therapy or mechanical thrombectomy): a deteriorating patient with prohibitive bleeding risk is exactly whom catheter based reperfusion serves, delivering local low dose lytic or mechanical clot removal with less hemorrhagic exposure, and pulmonary embolism response teams exist to mobilize these options rapidly. Heparin alone is failing before your eyes, and waiting on it accepts progressive shock. Full systemic lysis contradicts his stated contraindication, warfarin is slower and weaker in the acute phase rather than stronger, and nothing in the course suggests sepsis."}, {"id": 543, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 57 year old woman with tamponade undergoes successful pericardiocentesis with removal of 700 milliliters of fluid, yet her right atrial pressure remains 16 mmHg with persistent jugular distension and a Kussmaul sign afterward. Which diagnosis should this postdrainage hemodynamic picture prompt you to recognize and evaluate further?", "options": {"A": "Recurrent cardiac tamponade from immediate fluid reaccumulation within the freshly drained pericardial space", "B": "Effusive constrictive pericarditis, from a constricting visceral layer beneath the drained fluid", "C": "Iatrogenic right ventricular perforation producing an expanding new hemopericardium", "D": "Acute massive pulmonary embolism coinciding with the drainage procedure itself", "E": "Severe tricuspid regurgitation newly created by catheter injury to the valve"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Effusive constrictive pericarditis, from a constricting visceral layer beneath the drained fluid): in this syndrome the visceral pericardium is constrictive, so removing the fluid relieves compression yet right atrial pressure fails to normalize and constrictive signs such as Kussmaul physiology emerge or persist. Immediate reaccumulation and ventricular perforation would show a returning or enlarging effusion on imaging rather than a dry constrictive pattern. Pulmonary embolism and catheter induced tricuspid injury are possible procedural events, yet neither explains classic constrictive hemodynamics unmasked by drainage."}, {"id": 544, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 57 year old man with atrial fibrillation and a CHA2DS2-VASc score of 1 undergoes successful elective electrical cardioversion after three weeks of rivaroxaban. He feels well in sinus rhythm at discharge and asks when he can stop the anticoagulant. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Stop rivaroxaban at discharge now that sinus rhythm has been restored", "B": "Continue rivaroxaban for one week to cover the immediate recovery period", "C": "Continue rivaroxaban for one year before reassessing the need for therapy", "D": "Continue rivaroxaban for at least four weeks, then reassess based on risk", "E": "Switch to aspirin at discharge and continue it for four weeks"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue rivaroxaban for at least four weeks, then reassess based on risk): atrial stunning after cardioversion impairs mechanical contraction for weeks even in electrical sinus rhythm, so anticoagulation continues for a minimum of four weeks in all patients. Stopping at discharge or after one week leaves the highest risk window uncovered. Aspirin is not an adequate substitute, and a fixed one year course is longer than his risk score requires."}, {"id": 547, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 56 year old man with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has NYHA class III symptoms despite maximally tolerated beta blockade and a trial of disopyramide. His resting outflow gradient is 85 mm Hg and septal thickness is 21 millimeters. His ejection fraction is 65 percent. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in his care?", "options": {"A": "Add furosemide daily to shrink the ventricular volumes and reduce the gradient", "B": "Place an implantable defibrillator as the definitive therapy for his symptoms", "C": "Start dual chamber pacing with short atrioventricular delay as primary treatment", "D": "Arrange yearly echocardiograms and continue the current medicines unchanged", "E": "Refer to an experienced center for evaluation of septal reduction therapy"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Refer to an experienced center for evaluation of septal reduction therapy): drug refractory NYHA class III symptoms with an outflow gradient ≥50 mm Hg meet the indication for septal myectomy or alcohol septal ablation, and outcomes are strongly tied to procedural volume, so referral to a comprehensive center is specified. Continuing failed medical therapy with annual imaging leaves him severely limited. A diuretic lowers preload and typically worsens dynamic obstruction, a defibrillator treats arrhythmic risk rather than obstructive symptoms, and pacing is at best a niche option for patients who are poor candidates for septal reduction."}, {"id": 548, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 55 year old obese man has hypertension requiring four medications. His wife describes loud snoring with witnessed pauses in breathing, and he reports morning headaches with daytime sleepiness at work. His neck circumference measures 45 cm. Which evaluation is most likely to identify a treatable contributor to his resistant hypertension?", "options": {"A": "Transthoracic echocardiography to measure left ventricular wall thickness and function", "B": "Renal artery duplex imaging for atherosclerotic stenosis affecting both arteries", "C": "A 24 hour urine free cortisol collection to screen for hypercortisolism", "D": "Carotid sinus massage with rhythm monitoring to evaluate baroreflex sensitivity", "E": "Overnight polysomnography to evaluate for suspected obstructive sleep apnea"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Overnight polysomnography to evaluate for suspected obstructive sleep apnea): Loud snoring with witnessed apneas, morning headaches, daytime sleepiness, obesity, and a large neck circumference form a classic picture of obstructive sleep apnea, one of the most common treatable contributors to resistant hypertension, and sleep testing confirms it. Echocardiography documents consequences of hypertension rather than causes. Renovascular and cortisol testing lack supporting clues here, and carotid sinus massage evaluates syncope rather than resistant blood pressure elevation."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 44 year old woman with a large secundum atrial septal defect has right heart enlargement, but catheterization shows pulmonary artery systolic pressure two thirds of systemic pressure with pulmonary vascular resistance more than two thirds of systemic resistance and a net right to left shunt. What management applies here?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with device closure promptly to prevent further right ventricular dilation", "B": "Defer closure, since severe pulmonary vascular disease with reversed shunting contraindicates it", "C": "Perform surgical patch closure with concurrent tricuspid annuloplasty at the same operation", "D": "Close the defect after one month of dual antiplatelet therapy pretreatment", "E": "Create a fenestrated device closure immediately and titrate pulmonary vasodilator therapy afterward as needed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Defer closure, since severe pulmonary vascular disease with reversed shunting contraindicates it): When pulmonary pressure and resistance exceed two thirds of systemic values with a net right to left shunt, the defect functions as a relief valve and closing it can precipitate right ventricular failure. Guideline criteria for closure require a persistent left to right shunt without this degree of vascular disease. Antiplatelet pretreatment and annuloplasty do not change that prohibitive physiology."}, {"id": 557, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 24 year old man recovering from acute rheumatic fever with carditis is left with residual moderate mitral regurgitation. He asks how long he must continue monthly intramuscular benzathine penicillin G to prevent recurrence. Which of the following is the most appropriate duration of secondary prophylaxis?", "options": {"A": "Five years from the last episode or until age 21, whichever is longer", "B": "Ten years from the last episode or until age 21, whichever is longer", "C": "Twelve months of prophylaxis followed by prompt treatment of documented pharyngitis", "D": "Ten years from the last episode or until age 40, whichever is longer", "E": "Continuation until echocardiography documents complete resolution of the valve lesion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ten years from the last episode or until age 40, whichever is longer): rheumatic fever with carditis and residual valve disease requires the longest secondary prophylaxis, 10 years from the last episode or until age 40, whichever is longer, and sometimes lifelong with ongoing exposure. The shorter durations apply to carditis without residual disease or to rheumatic fever without carditis. Stopping based on echocardiographic appearance is not an accepted criterion."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 62 year old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 28 percent and New York Heart Association class II symptoms despite three months of optimized medical therapy. His infarction occurred one year ago and he has never had ventricular arrhythmias. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Implantable cardioverter defibrillator placement for primary prevention of sudden death", "B": "A wearable defibrillator vest for one year with repeat imaging afterward", "C": "An electrophysiology study first, with a device offered only if sustained tachycardia is induced", "D": "Continued medical therapy alone with echocardiographic surveillance every six months", "E": "Daily amiodarone as pharmacologic protection against sudden arrhythmic death"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Implantable cardioverter defibrillator placement for primary prevention of sudden death): ischemic cardiomyopathy with an ejection fraction of 35 percent or less and class II or III symptoms despite guideline directed therapy, beyond the early post infarction window, is a class 1 indication for a primary prevention defibrillator. A wearable vest is a bridge rather than a destination here. Induction testing is not required for this indication, surveillance alone leaves proven risk untreated, and amiodarone does not reduce sudden death in this population."}, {"id": 559, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 55 year old woman with symptomatic paroxysmal atrial fibrillation continues to have monthly episodes despite adequately dosed flecainide with a nodal blocker. She has a structurally normal heart, is anticoagulated, and wants fewer episodes. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in rhythm management?", "options": {"A": "Switch flecainide to sotalol and reassess episode frequency after three months", "B": "Add low dose amiodarone to the current flecainide based regimen", "C": "Refer for catheter ablation with pulmonary vein isolation as the next therapy", "D": "Increase flecainide to the highest available tablet strength twice daily", "E": "Discontinue antiarrhythmic therapy and accept a permanent rate control strategy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer for catheter ablation with pulmonary vein isolation as the next therapy): ablation is recommended for symptomatic paroxysmal atrial fibrillation that persists despite an adequate trial of a membrane active antiarrhythmic, and outcomes are best in structurally normal hearts. Rotating to sotalol offers modest incremental benefit. Combining amiodarone with flecainide invites proarrhythmia, dose escalation adds toxicity, and abandoning rhythm control contradicts her goals."}, {"id": 561, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 74 year old woman on sotalol develops recurrent self terminating runs of the rhythm on the strip shown, each preceded by a pause and associated with brief syncope. Her baseline corrected QT interval is markedly prolonged. Potassium is 3.4 mEq/L. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial therapy?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous amiodarone bolus followed by a continuous maintenance infusion", "B": "Oral potassium replacement alone with repeat electrolyte testing in four hours", "C": "Intravenous magnesium sulfate bolus with discontinuation of sotalol", "D": "Immediate unsynchronized defibrillation followed by an amiodarone loading dose", "E": "Intravenous lidocaine infusion with continued sotalol at a reduced dose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intravenous magnesium sulfate bolus with discontinuation of sotalol): pause dependent polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with a long QT interval is torsades de pointes, and magnesium suppresses the triggering afterdepolarizations while the offending QT prolonging drug is stopped. Amiodarone prolongs the QT further and can worsen the arrhythmia. Potassium repletion is an adjunct rather than sufficient alone, defibrillation is for sustained arrest, and continuing sotalol at a lower dose perpetuates the cause.", "image": "question_images/q_561.png"}, {"id": 562, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 34 year old woman has a patent ductus arteriosus with a continuous murmur, left atrial and left ventricular enlargement, and pulmonary artery systolic pressure less than half systemic. She is mildly dyspneic climbing stairs. What is the most appropriate recommendation for managing this ductus at this time?", "options": {"A": "Observe with serial imaging studies alone because adult ducts usually close spontaneously over time", "B": "Surgical ligation through thoracotomy as the preferred first line approach in adults", "C": "Recommend closure after right ventricular dilation appears on a follow up study", "D": "Treat with an endothelin antagonist and reassess ductal flow in six months", "E": "Proceed with transcatheter device occlusion given left heart enlargement from the shunt"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Proceed with transcatheter device occlusion given left heart enlargement from the shunt): A ductus that enlarges the left atrium and ventricle with attributable symptoms warrants closure, and percutaneous occlusion is the preferred route in adults with suitable anatomy. Spontaneous adult closure is not expected. Surgery is reserved for anatomy unsuitable for a device, and vasodilator therapy targets pulmonary vascular disease she does not have."}, {"id": 563, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 34 year old woman without risk factors reports brief, sharp left chest pains lasting seconds, unrelated to exertion, and reproducible with palpation. Her ECG is normal and she is worried about coronary disease. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Coronary CT angiography to provide definitive anatomic reassurance about her arteries", "B": "Pharmacologic nuclear perfusion imaging to quantify any silent ischemic burden", "C": "Exercise treadmill testing to establish a symptomatic and electrical baseline", "D": "No cardiac testing, with reassurance and evaluation for a musculoskeletal cause", "E": "Thirty day event monitoring to correlate her pains with any arrhythmia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No cardiac testing, with reassurance and evaluation for a musculoskeletal cause): fleeting, reproducible, nonexertional pain in a young low risk woman carries a very low pretest probability, where deferred testing is explicitly preferred because any positive result is more likely false than true. CT angiography and perfusion imaging expose her to radiation and incidental findings without changing management. Treadmill testing and ambulatory rhythm monitoring investigate questions her history has not raised."}, {"id": 568, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 60 year old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy presents with sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia at a tolerable rate. Blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg, he is comfortable, and electrolytes are normal. The team prefers pharmacologic conversion before considering electrical therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial drug?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous verapamil to interrupt reentry within the ischemic ventricular scar", "B": "Intravenous adenosine as both a diagnostic and a therapeutic maneuver", "C": "Oral mexiletine loading with telemetry observation for the next day", "D": "Intravenous digoxin to reduce the ventricular rate before chemical conversion", "E": "Intravenous procainamide with monitoring of blood pressure and QRS duration"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Intravenous procainamide with monitoring of blood pressure and QRS duration): in hemodynamically tolerated monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, procainamide achieved higher termination rates and fewer adverse events than amiodarone in comparative data and carries a guideline recommendation. Verapamil is dangerous in scar related ventricular tachycardia and is reserved for specific fascicular forms. Adenosine helps only with supraventricular mechanisms, and oral mexiletine and digoxin have no role in acute conversion."}, {"id": 573, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 72 year old man is started on long term amiodarone for recurrent atrial fibrillation after other options failed. His internist asks what surveillance the drug requires beyond clinical follow up. He has no baseline lung, liver, thyroid, or eye disease. Which of the following is the most appropriate monitoring plan?", "options": {"A": "Annual pulmonary function testing with monthly liver enzymes for the first year", "B": "Thyroid ultrasound every six months with liver imaging once a year", "C": "No routine laboratory testing as long as the patient remains asymptomatic", "D": "Baseline then periodic thyroid and liver tests, with a baseline chest radiograph", "E": "Quarterly slit lamp examinations with serial high resolution chest computed tomography"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Baseline then periodic thyroid and liver tests, with a baseline chest radiograph): standard amiodarone surveillance includes thyroid stimulating hormone and hepatic panels at baseline and roughly every six months, plus a baseline chest radiograph, with further lung testing driven by symptoms. Routine serial pulmonary function tests, thyroid ultrasounds, and computed tomography scans are not required in asymptomatic patients. Foregoing all laboratory monitoring misses the common silent thyroid and hepatic toxicities."}, {"id": 574, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 47 year old woman with newly diagnosed breast cancer is scheduled to start doxorubicin based chemotherapy in two weeks. She has hypertension controlled on lisinopril and no known cardiac disease. The oncology team asks the cardio-oncology clinic what should be done before her first treatment cycle. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Order coronary CT angiography first to exclude silent coronary disease before starting the chemotherapy", "B": "Start prophylactic carvedilol at target dose one week before the first cycle", "C": "Measure troponin monthly during treatment without any baseline cardiac imaging", "D": "Proceed with chemotherapy and image only if cardiac symptoms develop later", "E": "Obtain baseline echocardiography with strain imaging before the first anthracycline dose"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Obtain baseline echocardiography with strain imaging before the first anthracycline dose): a pretreatment assessment of ejection fraction and global longitudinal strain establishes the reference against which any later cardiotoxicity is judged and identifies patients with unrecognized dysfunction who need therapy before exposure. Waiting for symptoms misses the asymptomatic decline that early intervention can reverse. Serial troponin without a baseline imaging anchor is an incomplete surveillance plan, routine prophylactic beta blockade at target dose is not standard for average risk patients, and coronary imaging addresses a different disease process."}, {"id": 581, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 74 year old man presents 14 hours after ischemic stroke onset, outside all reperfusion windows, with blood pressure 206/112 mm Hg and stable neurologic findings. He has no aortic, cardiac, or renal emergency requiring pressure control. According to stroke guidelines, how should his blood pressure be managed during the first 24 hours?", "options": {"A": "Begin a nicardipine infusion targeting systolic 140 within the first hour", "B": "Give sublingual nifedipine to achieve a prompt but modest pressure reduction", "C": "Start an intravenous labetalol infusion targeting a mean arterial pressure reduction of near 25 percent", "D": "Withhold acute antihypertensives unless readings exceed 220/120 or another indication exists", "E": "Resume all home antihypertensives at full doses through a nasogastric tube"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold acute antihypertensives unless readings exceed 220/120 or another indication exists): After ischemic stroke without thrombolysis or thrombectomy, permissive hypertension is standard because the ischemic penumbra depends on pressure driven collateral flow, and treatment is reserved for readings above 220/120 mm Hg or a concurrent emergency such as dissection or myocardial ischemia. Infusions targeting systolic 140 or a 25 percent drop risk extending the infarct. Sublingual nifedipine causes precipitous unpredictable falls, and resuming full home regimens acutely produces the same hazard more slowly."}, {"id": 583, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 71 year old woman with stable angina remains symptomatic on metoprolol and amlodipine. Blood pressure is 96/58 mmHg and heart rate is 54 beats per minute. She becomes lightheaded when isosorbide mononitrate is attempted. Which of the following is the most appropriate additional antianginal agent?", "options": {"A": "Higher dose isosorbide mononitrate taken with extra fluids to offset lightheadedness", "B": "Diltiazem added to her current regimen for complementary rate slowing", "C": "Hydralazine three times daily as a direct arterial vasodilator for angina", "D": "Ranolazine, which relieves angina without lowering heart rate or blood pressure", "E": "Clonidine at bedtime to blunt sympathetic drive and nocturnal symptoms"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ranolazine, which relieves angina without lowering heart rate or blood pressure): with borderline hypotension and bradycardia already present, ranolazine is the antianginal that adds efficacy without hemodynamic cost. Raising the nitrate dose repeats the intolerance she just demonstrated, and diltiazem stacked on a beta blocker at a heart rate in the fifties risks symptomatic bradycardia or block. Hydralazine and clonidine are not established antianginal therapies for chronic coronary disease."}, {"id": 584, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 63 year old woman with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy develops palpitations, and ambulatory monitoring documents paroxysmal atrial fibrillation lasting several hours. Her CHA2DS2-VASc score calculates to 1, she has no prior bleeding history, and renal function is normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate decision regarding stroke prevention for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe aspirin alone because her calculated risk score remains low", "B": "Start oral anticoagulation regardless of the conventional stroke risk score result", "C": "Defer any antithrombotic therapy until the next episode has been documented", "D": "Reserve anticoagulation for episodes that persist longer than 24 hours", "E": "Order a transesophageal echocardiogram first to decide whether any anticoagulation is actually needed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Start oral anticoagulation regardless of the conventional stroke risk score result): atrial fibrillation in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy carries a substantially elevated embolic risk that conventional scoring underestimates, so guidelines recommend anticoagulation for these patients independent of the CHA2DS2-VASc value, with a direct oral anticoagulant preferred. Aspirin provides inadequate protection for this risk level. Waiting for another episode or requiring a 24 hour duration threshold leaves her exposed, and transesophageal imaging informs cardioversion timing rather than the decision to anticoagulate."}, {"id": 586, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 38 year old woman has a single office blood pressure of 136/86 mm Hg at an annual visit, with no prior readings on file and no cardiovascular risk conditions. She asks whether she now has hypertension and needs to start medication. What is the most appropriate next step in her evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Confirm the pattern with out of office readings before assigning any diagnosis", "B": "Diagnose stage 1 hypertension today and begin a low dose thiazide", "C": "Diagnose elevated blood pressure and schedule a routine review in one year", "D": "Order secondary hypertension screening given her relatively young age at detection", "E": "Start combination therapy since untreated stage values tend to progress quickly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Confirm the pattern with out of office readings before assigning any diagnosis): Hypertension should be diagnosed from an average of readings on two or more occasions, ideally supported by home or ambulatory monitoring, because a single office value cannot exclude a white coat effect. Labeling or medicating her today gets ahead of the evidence, and her value does not fit the elevated category since the diastolic is 86. Secondary cause screening is reserved for confirmed hypertension with suggestive features, not an unconfirmed first reading."}, {"id": 587, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 55 year old woman presents to a critical access hospital with 90 minutes of chest pressure and inferior ST-segment elevation. The closest PCI-capable center is a 150 minute drive away, and no helicopter is available. She has no contraindications to fibrinolysis. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Give a fibrinolytic within 30 minutes, then transfer for angiography within 24 hours", "B": "Transfer for primary PCI and defer all reperfusion therapy until arrival there", "C": "Give a fibrinolytic and admit locally, reserving angiography for recurrent ischemia only", "D": "Load dual antiplatelet therapy and anticoagulation, then reassess the ECG in one hour", "E": "Arrange ground transfer only if repeat troponin testing confirms myocardial necrosis first"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give a fibrinolytic within 30 minutes, then transfer for angiography within 24 hours): when anticipated first medical contact to device time exceeds 120 minutes, fibrinolysis within 30 minutes of arrival is preferred, followed by routine transfer for angiography within 3 to 24 hours. Transferring without treatment leaves the artery occluded for well over two hours. Admitting locally without planned angiography, waiting on serial ECGs, or requiring troponin confirmation all delay definitive care in a clear STEMI."}, {"id": 589, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 29 year old asymptomatic woman with repaired tetralogy of Fallot has severe pulmonary regurgitation. Cardiac magnetic resonance shows right ventricular end diastolic volume index of 165 mL/m² with mildly reduced right ventricular ejection fraction on two consecutive studies. What is the most appropriate next management step?", "options": {"A": "Refer for pulmonary valve replacement based on progressive right ventricular dilation", "B": "Begin an angiotensin receptor blocker to slow the right ventricular remodeling", "C": "Repeat the cardiac magnetic resonance in five years unless arrhythmias or new symptoms develop first", "D": "Offer valve replacement after symptoms appear, since earlier surgery adds little", "E": "Perform balloon valvuloplasty to reduce the regurgitant fraction across the valve"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for pulmonary valve replacement based on progressive right ventricular dilation): In asymptomatic patients with severe regurgitation, replacement is reasonable when the end diastolic volume index reaches approximately 160 mL/m² or ventricular systolic function declines, because remodeling may not reverse beyond that point. Her volumes and falling ejection fraction meet that trigger. Waiting for symptoms risks irreversible dilation, and valvuloplasty treats stenosis rather than regurgitation."}, {"id": 590, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 52 year old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy remains limited by exertional dyspnea despite maximally tolerated beta blocker therapy. Her current findings are summarized in the exhibit shown. She wishes to avoid an invasive procedure if possible. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her pharmacologic management?", "options": {"A": "Add oral losartan once daily to reduce left ventricular wall stress and slow the hypertrophy progression", "B": "Add sublingual nitroglycerin as needed for the episodes of exertional chest pressure", "C": "Stop metoprolol and substitute amlodipine for better exertional blood pressure response", "D": "Begin mavacamten with serial echocardiographic monitoring under the required safety program", "E": "Add low dose dobutamine at home through a wearable continuous infusion device"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Begin mavacamten with serial echocardiographic monitoring under the required safety program): she has drug refractory symptomatic obstruction with a gradient above 50 mm Hg and a preserved ejection fraction of at least 55 percent, which defines candidacy for a cardiac myosin inhibitor, and the drug requires enrollment in a monitoring program with serial ejection fraction checks because it can depress systolic function. Nitrates and dihydropyridine substitution both vasodilate and worsen dynamic obstruction. Losartan does not relieve obstructive symptoms, and inotropic infusion increases contractility, precisely what drives her gradient.", "image": "question_images/q_590.png"}, {"id": 591, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 63 year old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease, an estimated glomerular filtration rate of 24 mL/min, and no prior vascular events asks her nurse practitioner about cardiac prevention. Her low density lipoprotein cholesterol is 131 mg/dL. Which preventive step is most strongly supported for her?", "options": {"A": "Statin based lipid lowering, since advanced kidney disease itself confers high atherosclerotic risk", "B": "Daily full dose aspirin, because uremic platelets require stronger antiplatelet suppression", "C": "Routine invasive coronary angiography performed now, because silent coronary disease is common in kidney failure", "D": "Deferring prevention until dialysis begins, when cardiovascular risk becomes clinically relevant", "E": "Niacin therapy, chosen because it raises high density lipoprotein in renal patients"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Statin based lipid lowering, since advanced kidney disease itself confers high atherosclerotic risk): chronic kidney disease is treated as a coronary risk equivalent, and trials such as SHARP showed lipid lowering reduces atherosclerotic events in nondialysis kidney disease, so a statin is indicated without waiting for an event. Full dose aspirin adds bleeding risk without proven primary prevention benefit in this group. Angiography is not a screening tool in asymptomatic patients, and starting prevention only at dialysis forfeits the window when therapy works best."}, {"id": 592, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 58 year old man received tenecteplase for an anterior STEMI at a non-PCI hospital 90 minutes ago. He now has ongoing chest pain, and his current status is summarized in the exhibit shown. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Repeat a half dose of intravenous tenecteplase and continue close hemodynamic monitoring at the bedside", "B": "Start a nitroglycerin infusion and reassess the electrocardiogram in another hour", "C": "Add ticagrelor to strengthen platelet inhibition while continuing observation locally", "D": "Begin dobutamine for cardiogenic shock and delay transfer until pressure improves", "E": "Transfer emergently for rescue percutaneous coronary intervention at the receiving center"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Transfer emergently for rescue percutaneous coronary intervention at the receiving center): less than 50 percent ST-segment resolution at 60 to 90 minutes with ongoing pain and hypotension indicates failed fibrinolysis, which mandates immediate rescue PCI. Repeating a fibrinolytic raises intracranial bleeding risk without restoring flow reliably. Nitroglycerin titration, adding ticagrelor after lysis, or stabilizing on inotropes locally all substitute observation for the definitive treatment this patient needs.", "image": "question_images/q_592.png"}, {"id": 593, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 58 year old man with newly diagnosed HFrEF and an ejection fraction of 25 percent presents to clinic after echocardiography. He has NYHA class II symptoms, blood pressure 128/76 mm Hg, potassium 4.2 mEq/L, and eGFR 68. He is euvolemic and takes no cardiac medications. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacologic strategy?", "options": {"A": "Maximize lisinopril to its target dose before adding any other drug class", "B": "Begin carvedilol as a single agent and reassess ejection fraction in three months", "C": "Initiate low doses of ARNI, beta blocker, MRA, and SGLT2 inhibitor promptly", "D": "Start a loop diuretic alone and reassess the need for other agents later", "E": "Obtain cardiac MRI before starting medical therapy to clarify the underlying etiology"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Initiate low doses of ARNI, beta blocker, MRA, and SGLT2 inhibitor promptly): the 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA guideline supports early initiation of all four foundational classes at low doses, because each independently reduces mortality and benefits appear within weeks. Maximizing a single renin angiotensin blocker or starting a beta blocker alone delays proven therapies without added safety in a stable euvolemic patient. A loop diuretic by itself treats congestion he does not have, and imaging for etiology should proceed alongside, not instead of, disease modifying therapy."}, {"id": 595, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 54 year old woman develops constrictive physiology on echocardiography six weeks after viral pericarditis, with elevated CRP, pericardial enhancement on cardiac MRI, and moderate symptoms. Her surgeon asks whether to proceed directly to pericardiectomy. Applying the concept of transient constriction, what should you recommend as the next step?", "options": {"A": "Proceed directly with pericardiectomy now because constrictive physiology is inherently a surgical disease", "B": "A two to three month trial of antiinflammatory therapy before considering pericardiectomy", "C": "Placement of a pericardial window to relieve constriction through fluid drainage", "D": "Diuretics alone indefinitely, reserving other interventions for truly refractory symptoms", "E": "Repeat imaging in one year while withholding medical therapy meanwhile"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A two to three month trial of antiinflammatory therapy before considering pericardiectomy): recent onset constriction with active inflammation, shown by elevated CRP and pericardial enhancement on MRI, resolves with antiinflammatory treatment in a meaningful fraction of patients, so a supervised medical trial can spare her a major operation. Immediate pericardiectomy is premature when reversibility markers are present. A window drains effusions but does not treat constriction, diuretics alone palliate without addressing inflammation, and a year of untreated observation squanders the reversible phase."}, {"id": 596, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 64 year old woman with prior PCI has stopped atorvastatin, rosuvastatin, and twice weekly low dose rosuvastatin, each within weeks, for reproducible severe myalgias that resolve off therapy. LDL cholesterol is 156 mg/dL. She is willing to take nonstatin medication. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "A fourth statin rechallenge with simvastatin 80 mg to settle the question", "B": "Red yeast rice supplementation as a gentler natural statin alternative", "C": "Fish oil supplements alone, targeting inflammation rather than LDL cholesterol", "D": "Observation without lipid therapy, repeating the panel in twelve months", "E": "Ezetimibe with addition of bempedoic acid or a PCSK9 inhibitor as needed"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ezetimibe with addition of bempedoic acid or a PCSK9 inhibitor as needed): after documented intolerance to multiple statins including an intermittent low dose regimen, guideline directed care moves to nonstatin LDL lowering, and bempedoic acid has outcome data specifically in statin intolerant patients. Simvastatin 80 mg is not used at all because of myopathy risk. Red yeast rice contains unregulated statin compounds likely to reproduce her symptoms, and fish oil or observation leaves a very high risk LDL essentially untreated."}, {"id": 600, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 50 year old man has 30 minutes of crushing chest pain. His electrocardiogram shows upsloping ST depression with tall symmetric precordial T waves, and the catheterization laboratory asks whether formal ST elevation criteria are met before activation. Applying current reperfusion principles, what is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the electrocardiogram every ten minutes until diagnostic ST elevation finally appears", "B": "Start a heparin infusion and admit the patient for early morning angiography", "C": "Obtain urgent echocardiography to look for a regional wall motion abnormality first", "D": "Activate emergent catheterization now, treating the pattern as an occlusion equivalent", "E": "Give fibrinolysis only if the transfer time to intervention exceeds twelve hours"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Activate emergent catheterization now, treating the pattern as an occlusion equivalent): the de Winter pattern behaves as an occlusion equivalent of the proximal left anterior descending artery, so emergent catheterization should be activated without waiting for ST elevation. Serial tracings, heparin with next day angiography, and echocardiography first all impose delay that costs anterior myocardium. Fibrinolysis triggered by transfer time is a consideration for confirmed ST elevation infarction when intervention is remote, not a reason to defer activation here."}, {"id": 601, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 49 year old man with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and a resting outflow gradient of 55 mm Hg presents for review. For blood pressure his primary care physician previously prescribed amlodipine and isosorbide mononitrate, and he reports lightheadedness with exertion since starting them. Which of the following is the most appropriate change to his regimen?", "options": {"A": "Continue both agents but add compression stockings for the exertional lightheadedness", "B": "Discontinue the vasodilating agents because they can worsen the outflow obstruction", "C": "Add hydrochlorothiazide to improve the overall blood pressure control", "D": "Increase the amlodipine dose so the blood pressure falls more consistently", "E": "Add tamsulosin at bedtime to smooth out the daytime pressure fluctuations"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Discontinue the vasodilating agents because they can worsen the outflow obstruction): dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and nitrates reduce afterload and preload, which intensifies dynamic outflow obstruction and explains his exertional lightheadedness. Continuing or increasing these drugs perpetuates the hemodynamic insult, and compression stockings do not address a drug induced gradient. A thiazide lowers preload further, and tamsulosin adds alpha blockade with more vasodilation, both moves in the wrong direction; his hypertension should be managed with a nonvasodilating beta blocker or verapamil instead."}, {"id": 603, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 74 year old man with prior bypass surgery has limiting angina from a focal stenosis in the body of a degenerated saphenous vein graft, and the heart team selects percutaneous intervention of the graft. Which procedural adjunct is specifically recommended to reduce periprocedural myocardial infarction in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Rotational atherectomy to debulk the friable lesion before the stent placement", "B": "Routine thrombus aspiration performed before balloon dilation of the graft lesion", "C": "An embolic protection device deployed distal to the lesion during the intervention", "D": "Direct stenting with an oversized stent to trap debris against the wall", "E": "Intracoronary fibrinolysis infused through the guide catheter before wiring the lesion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An embolic protection device deployed distal to the lesion during the intervention): distal embolic protection during vein graft intervention reduces periprocedural infarction from friable atheroembolic debris and is the specifically supported adjunct. Rotational atherectomy is avoided in grafts because it liberates debris, routine aspiration lacks supporting evidence in this setting, stent oversizing raises rupture and embolization risk, and prophylactic fibrinolysis is not a recognized strategy. Protection devices are the one adjunct with trial demonstrated infarct reduction."}, {"id": 606, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 55 year old woman presents with a tender, palpable cord along her medial thigh. Duplex imaging shows 12 centimeters of superficial thrombosis within the great saphenous vein, with its upper end lying 8 centimeters below the saphenofemoral junction. There is no deep vein involvement. Which treatment approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Warm compresses and compression stockings alone, with repeat imaging in three months", "B": "Full dose anticoagulation for three months, identical to deep vein thrombosis treatment", "C": "Urgent saphenofemoral ligation to prevent proximal extension into the deep system", "D": "A ten day course of oral antibiotics with nonsteroidal analgesia as needed", "E": "Prophylactic dose fondaparinux daily for 45 days to prevent thrombotic extension"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Prophylactic dose fondaparinux daily for 45 days to prevent thrombotic extension): superficial vein thrombosis at least 5 centimeters long, sitting more than 3 centimeters from the saphenofemoral junction, is treated with prophylactic dose fondaparinux 2.5 milligrams daily for 45 days, which reduced extension and embolic events in the CALISTO trial. Symptomatic care alone leaves a meaningful risk of propagation for a thrombus of this length. Full therapeutic anticoagulation is reserved for junctional proximity or deep involvement, surgical ligation is not first line therapy, and antibiotics treat septic phlebitis rather than bland thrombosis."}, {"id": 608, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 79 year old woman has recurrent presyncope. Ambulatory monitoring captures sinus pauses lasting up to five seconds that coincide exactly with her diary entries of near fainting. She takes no rate slowing medications and her thyroid function is normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Permanent pacemaker implantation for symptomatic sinus node dysfunction", "B": "An implantable loop recorder to gather additional rhythm symptom correlation", "C": "Empiric midodrine therapy with follow up ambulatory monitoring in one month", "D": "Electrophysiology study to measure sinus node recovery time before deciding", "E": "Reassurance with counseling about hydration and avoidance of abrupt standing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Permanent pacemaker implantation for symptomatic sinus node dysfunction): documented pauses that correlate directly with symptoms establish the diagnosis and constitute a class 1 pacing indication once medication and metabolic causes are excluded. Additional monitoring would only reconfirm what is already proven. Midodrine treats orthostatic hypotension rather than sinus arrest, invasive sinus node testing adds nothing once symptom rhythm correlation exists, and reassurance leaves her at risk of injury."}, {"id": 609, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 42 year old man with no hypertension, diabetes, vascular disease, heart failure, or prior stroke has infrequent episodes of paroxysmal atrial fibrillation documented on an event monitor. Echocardiography is normal. He asks whether he needs a blood thinner. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding stroke prevention?", "options": {"A": "Aspirin 81 mg daily as a low intensity protective measure", "B": "Apixaban at the standard twice daily dose during the weeks surrounding any documented episode", "C": "Warfarin with a target international normalized ratio of 2.0 to 3.0", "D": "Left atrial appendage occlusion to avoid the need for any medication", "E": "No anticoagulant therapy, with periodic reassessment of his stroke risk factors"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (No anticoagulant therapy, with periodic reassessment of his stroke risk factors): his CHA2DS2-VASc score is 0, a level at which anticoagulation offers more bleeding risk than stroke benefit. Aspirin is not recommended for stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation at any risk level. Episode guided anticoagulation is not an established strategy, warfarin is not indicated at this risk, and appendage occlusion is reserved for patients with an indication who cannot take anticoagulants."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A resident asks how quickly a stable outpatient with newly diagnosed HFrEF should have all foundational drug classes on board. The patient is euvolemic with normal renal function and blood pressure of 126/80 mm Hg, and tolerates initial low doses well. Which of the following best describes the recommended overall timeline for initiation?", "options": {"A": "Aim to have all four classes started within approximately four weeks", "B": "Introduce one new class every three months to isolate any adverse effect", "C": "Complete titration of each class to target before starting the next one", "D": "Start all four classes only after an implantable defibrillator has been placed", "E": "Delay additional classes until natriuretic peptide levels have fully normalized"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Aim to have all four classes started within approximately four weeks): rapid sequence initiation of ARNI or ACE inhibitor, beta blocker, MRA, and SGLT2 inhibitor within about a month is achievable in stable patients and captures early mortality benefit from each class. Adding one class per quarter or titrating each to target first leaves patients undertreated for months during a high risk period. Device decisions come after at least three months of optimized therapy, and natriuretic peptides need not normalize before therapy expands."}, {"id": 615, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 26 year old woman with congenital heart disease requests preconception counseling. The clinic uses the modified World Health Organization classification to frame maternal risk. Which of the following cardiac conditions places a prospective pregnancy in class IV, where pregnancy is considered contraindicated and avoidance is advised?", "options": {"A": "A small surgically repaired secundum atrial septal defect without any residual shunt on echocardiography", "B": "Pulmonary arterial hypertension with Eisenmenger physiology from an unrepaired shunt", "C": "Mild pulmonic valve stenosis with a peak gradient of twenty five", "D": "A repaired coarctation with normal arm pressures and no residual gradient", "E": "A small patent ductus arteriosus with trivial continuous flow and normal chambers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pulmonary arterial hypertension with Eisenmenger physiology from an unrepaired shunt): Pulmonary hypertension of any cause, severe systemic ventricular dysfunction, severe symptomatic left sided obstruction, and severe unrepaired coarctation define class IV, where maternal mortality risk is extremely high and pregnancy is discouraged. Falling systemic resistance during gestation augments right to left shunting and cyanosis. The other lesions listed are low risk conditions in the lowest classes."}, {"id": 618, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 59 year old man with chronic atrial fibrillation and an ejection fraction of 32 percent is hemodynamically stable but has a resting heart rate of 122 beats per minute. He takes sacubitril valsartan and apixaban. Long term rate control is planned. Which of the following agents is the most appropriate choice?", "options": {"A": "Verapamil titrated to a resting heart rate below 80 beats per minute", "B": "Carvedilol titrated as tolerated with reassessment of rate and symptoms", "C": "Diltiazem extended release taken once daily alongside his current heart failure therapy", "D": "Dronedarone daily for combined rate and rhythm benefit in this setting", "E": "Flecainide twice daily with a nodal agent to prevent rapid conduction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Carvedilol titrated as tolerated with reassessment of rate and symptoms): beta blockers are the preferred rate controlling agents in atrial fibrillation with a reduced ejection fraction and also carry mortality benefit in heart failure. Verapamil and diltiazem are negative inotropes that are avoided with systolic dysfunction. Dronedarone is contraindicated in symptomatic heart failure, and flecainide is avoided with structural heart disease."}, {"id": 620, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 63 year old man presents with symptomatic atrial fibrillation of unknown duration. He is hemodynamically stable but very symptomatic and wants sinus rhythm restored as soon as possible. He has taken no anticoagulant. Rate control has been started. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to early cardioversion?", "options": {"A": "Cardiovert immediately after a single therapeutic dose of a direct oral anticoagulant", "B": "Start anticoagulation, perform transesophageal echocardiography, and cardiovert if no thrombus is seen", "C": "Cardiovert now and begin anticoagulation only if the procedure restores sinus rhythm", "D": "Delay all treatment until ambulatory monitoring defines the true arrhythmia duration", "E": "Perform transthoracic echocardiography first and proceed with cardioversion once the atrial appendage appears normal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Start anticoagulation, perform transesophageal echocardiography, and cardiovert if no thrombus is seen): with unknown duration, cardioversion requires either three weeks of therapeutic anticoagulation first or a transesophageal study excluding left atrial appendage thrombus, and the imaging pathway permits early conversion. A single anticoagulant dose does not exclude an existing thrombus. Anticoagulation must precede rather than follow conversion, transthoracic imaging cannot reliably visualize the appendage, and monitoring adds only delay."}, {"id": 622, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 67 year old woman with exertional chest pain has a baseline electrocardiogram showing left bundle branch block. Her physician wants a stress test but knows the baseline pattern complicates both exercise electrocardiography and some imaging modes. Which stress testing approach is most appropriate for her in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Standard exercise treadmill electrocardiography, since ST changes retain accuracy in this pattern", "B": "Exercise perfusion imaging, because exercise itself avoids septal artifacts in this setting", "C": "Dobutamine echocardiography at maximal doses to force analysis of septal motion", "D": "Ambulatory electrocardiographic monitoring for a week to capture spontaneous ischemic episodes", "E": "Vasodilator stress perfusion imaging, which avoids rate related septal perfusion artifacts"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Vasodilator stress perfusion imaging, which avoids rate related septal perfusion artifacts): vasodilator stress perfusion imaging is preferred with left bundle branch block because rapid pacing of the septum during exercise or dobutamine generates rate related septal perfusion artifacts and the septal wall motion is already abnormal. Exercise electrocardiography is uninterpretable for ischemia in this conduction pattern, and exercise perfusion imaging creates rather than avoids the septal artifact. Ambulatory monitoring is not a stress test and would not localize inducible ischemia."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 72 year old man with NSTEMI has recurrent chest pain despite aspirin, ticagrelor, heparin, and intravenous nitroglycerin. Blood pressure falls to 84/50 mmHg during pain, with new dynamic ST depression and a faint mitral regurgitation murmur. Which of the following is the most appropriate timing for invasive angiography?", "options": {"A": "Within 24 hours, consistent with an early invasive strategy for elevated risk", "B": "Within 72 hours, after first completing a full course of guideline based medical stabilization", "C": "Immediately, within two hours, given refractory ischemia with hemodynamic compromise", "D": "Only after noninvasive stress imaging confirms a significant ischemic burden", "E": "After 48 hours of glycoprotein IIb IIIa infusion cools the culprit plaque"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Immediately, within two hours, given refractory ischemia with hemodynamic compromise): ongoing pain despite maximal medical therapy, hypotension during ischemia, and new mitral regurgitation define the immediate invasive category. A 24 or 72 hour timeline is appropriate for stabilized patients, not one who is actively compromised. Stress imaging in an unstable patient is contraindicated, and prolonged antiplatelet infusions used to defer angiography delay the revascularization he needs now."}, {"id": 625, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 57 year old woman is diagnosed with nonischemic cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 28 percent after two weeks of dyspnea. She begins all four foundational drug classes and asks at her first follow up visit whether she should receive an implantable cardioverter defibrillator right away. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Reassess ejection fraction after at least three months of optimized medical therapy", "B": "Refer for defibrillator implantation now because the ejection fraction is below 30", "C": "Order an invasive electrophysiology study to decide whether a defibrillator implant is truly warranted", "D": "Prescribe a wearable defibrillator for two years before any implant decision", "E": "Tell her defibrillators are reserved for patients with prior cardiac arrest only"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reassess ejection fraction after at least three months of optimized medical therapy): primary prevention defibrillator decisions in new nonischemic cardiomyopathy require an ejection fraction that remains ≤35 percent after at least three months of guideline directed therapy, because many patients recover substantially with treatment. Implanting now may commit her to a device her improved ventricle would never have needed. Electrophysiology testing does not guide primary prevention selection in this setting, a two year wearable course far exceeds any recommended bridge, and defibrillators are clearly indicated for primary prevention, not only after arrest."}, {"id": 626, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 63 year old man with newly diagnosed peripheral artery disease reports calf claudication after two blocks that limits his daily activities. His risk factor management has already been optimized with a statin, an antiplatelet agent, and smoking cessation. Which intervention should be offered first to improve his walking distance?", "options": {"A": "Endovascular revascularization of the diseased superficial femoral artery segment to restore inline arterial flow", "B": "Pentoxifylline three times daily to improve red blood cell deformability", "C": "Home based unstructured walking advice without scheduled follow up or progression targets", "D": "A structured supervised exercise program with intermittent walking to moderate claudication", "E": "Therapeutic anticoagulation with warfarin to prevent progression of arterial obstruction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A structured supervised exercise program with intermittent walking to moderate claudication): supervised exercise therapy is first line treatment for claudication and produces walking gains that rival revascularization while carrying no procedural risk. Revascularization is reserved for lifestyle limiting symptoms that persist despite exercise and medical therapy. Pentoxifylline has minimal demonstrated efficacy, unstructured advice without supervision achieves far less than a formal program, and warfarin does not treat claudication and adds bleeding risk without benefit in stable atherosclerotic obstruction."}, {"id": 628, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 42 year old woman is recovering from a conservatively managed spontaneous coronary artery dissection of the circumflex artery. She asks what additional evaluation she needs before discharge planning is completed, since she has read about associated conditions. Which of the following is the most appropriate additional study?", "options": {"A": "Coronary bypass evaluation with surgical consultation before she leaves the hospital", "B": "Serial invasive angiography every three months to document arterial healing", "C": "Electrophysiology study to assess her risk of ventricular arrhythmia going forward", "D": "Genetic testing for familial hypercholesterolemia as the presumed underlying cause", "E": "Head to pelvis vascular imaging to screen for fibromuscular dysplasia"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Head to pelvis vascular imaging to screen for fibromuscular dysplasia): fibromuscular dysplasia is found in roughly half of patients with spontaneous coronary dissection, and one time CT or MR angiography from head to pelvis also screens for associated aneurysms. Repeated invasive angiography risks catheter induced dissection in fragile arteries, which is why healing is not routinely restudied invasively. Surgical consultation and electrophysiology study address problems she does not have, and her arteriopathy is not a lipid disorder."}, {"id": 634, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 61 year old man with prior CABG has been followed for two years with stepwise lipid therapy, currently atorvastatin 80 mg plus ezetimibe with confirmed adherence. His LDL cholesterol response is displayed in the trend shown. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in his lipid management?", "options": {"A": "Continue the current regimen, since the trajectory shows steady ongoing improvement", "B": "Stop ezetimibe, since the plateau proves it contributes nothing measurable", "C": "Add a PCSK9 inhibitor, since he remains above threshold on maximal oral therapy", "D": "Repeat the panel quarterly for another year before considering any change", "E": "Substitute high dose niacin for ezetimibe to break through the plateau"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add a PCSK9 inhibitor, since he remains above threshold on maximal oral therapy): the displayed values have plateaued in the mid 80s despite maximal statin plus ezetimibe, and a very high risk patient persistently at or above 70 mg/dL meets the indication for PCSK9 inhibition. The curve is flat, not improving, so continued observation or quarterly repetition changes nothing. Removing ezetimibe would raise the LDL, and niacin substitution adds side effects without outcome benefit.", "image": "question_images/q_634.png"}, {"id": 639, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 71 year old woman with biopsy proven giant cell arteritis relapses twice during attempts to taper prednisone below 15 milligrams daily. She has developed glucocorticoid induced hyperglycemia and vertebral osteopenia during treatment. Which additional therapy is best supported for maintaining her remission while allowing meaningful glucocorticoid dose reduction?", "options": {"A": "Weekly methotrexate, the agent with the strongest randomized evidence in this disease", "B": "Daily azathioprine, the preferred steroid sparing agent for relapsing large vessel disease", "C": "Tocilizumab, an interleukin 6 receptor antagonist with randomized evidence for steroid sparing", "D": "Intravenous cyclophosphamide infusions, the standard escalation therapy for refractory large vessel arterial inflammation", "E": "Abatacept, the first line adjunct recommended for glucocorticoid related complications"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tocilizumab, an interleukin 6 receptor antagonist with randomized evidence for steroid sparing): the GiACTA trial showed tocilizumab produced sustained glucocorticoid free remission far more often than prednisone tapers alone, making interleukin 6 blockade the best supported steroid sparing therapy for relapsing giant cell arteritis. Methotrexate has only modest and inconsistent trial support, so it is an alternative rather than the strongest option. Azathioprine and cyclophosphamide lack convincing efficacy data in this disease, and abatacept remains investigational rather than a recommended first adjunct."}, {"id": 640, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 69 year old man with drug induced QT prolongation has recurrent torsades de pointes despite two doses of intravenous magnesium and correction of potassium. Each run follows a pause during underlying sinus bradycardia at a slow rate. The offending drug has been stopped. Which of the following is the most appropriate next intervention?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous amiodarone loading to stabilize the ventricular myocardium between the recurrent torsades episodes", "B": "Intravenous metoprolol to suppress the ventricular ectopy that triggers runs", "C": "Observation alone now that the offending medication has been withdrawn", "D": "An urgent implantable defibrillator before any further medical therapy", "E": "Temporary pacing or isoproterenol to raise the heart rate and shorten repolarization"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Temporary pacing or isoproterenol to raise the heart rate and shorten repolarization): pause dependent torsades in acquired QT prolongation responds to increasing the heart rate, which shortens the QT interval and eliminates the pauses that trigger runs. Amiodarone and metoprolol each prolong repolarization or worsen bradycardia in this setting. Waiting for drug washout leaves him unprotected for hours, and a defibrillator addresses a reversible cause with a permanent device."}, {"id": 641, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 27 year old woman with a Fontan circulation is admitted with peripheral edema. The resident proposes rapid high dose intravenous diuresis to normalize her volume status quickly. Given the physiology of a total cavopulmonary connection, why does this plan require particular caution in her case?", "options": {"A": "Diuretics accumulate in these patients because hepatic congestion slows drug clearance substantially", "B": "Loop agents commonly provoke reflex tachycardia poorly tolerated after cardiac denervation", "C": "Pulmonary blood flow depends on systemic venous pressure, so preload reduction drops output", "D": "Aggressive diuresis activates renin pathways that accelerate the underlying valve disease", "E": "Rapid volume shifts precipitate protein losing enteropathy through sudden mesenteric vasoconstriction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pulmonary blood flow depends on systemic venous pressure, so preload reduction drops output): Without a subpulmonary ventricle, blood traverses the lungs passively down a gradient from the caval veins, and cardiac output is exquisitely preload dependent. Overdiuresis lowers that driving pressure and can cause hypotension and renal injury. Edema in this setting is managed with gentle, monitored diuresis rather than rapid decongestion."}, {"id": 642, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 54 year old asymptomatic woman has severe primary mitral regurgitation with an ejection fraction of 66 percent and end systolic diameter 34 mm. A comprehensive valve center reports a repair likelihood above 95 percent with expected mortality below 1 percent. Which of the following management approaches is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Early surgical repair at the experienced center as a reasonable elective strategy", "B": "Deferral of surgery until her ejection fraction falls to 60 percent", "C": "Transcatheter edge to edge repair now to spare her a sternotomy", "D": "Mechanical valve replacement to provide the most durable long term result", "E": "Annual echocardiographic imaging with surgical intervention reserved for the onset of limiting cardiac symptoms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Early surgical repair at the experienced center as a reasonable elective strategy): early repair of asymptomatic severe primary regurgitation is reasonable when a durable repair is very likely and operative mortality is very low at a primary or comprehensive valve center. Waiting for the ejection fraction to fall or symptoms to appear concedes ventricular injury the early strategy avoids. Replacement sacrifices a repairable valve, and transcatheter therapy is reserved for high surgical risk."}, {"id": 644, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 71 year old man with a severe proximal right coronary stenosis and limiting angina needs resection of a bladder tumor in approximately three weeks, a timeline urology will not extend. The heart team plans revascularization before surgery. Which of the following is the most appropriate strategy?", "options": {"A": "Drug-eluting stent now, holding both antiplatelet agents the week of surgery", "B": "Drug-eluting stent now, delaying the tumor resection to the six month mark", "C": "Balloon angioplasty without stenting, allowing surgery after roughly fourteen days", "D": "Two staged drug-eluting stents now to make the result maximally durable", "E": "Defer all coronary revascularization and treat the angina medically with nitrates through the surgery"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Balloon angioplasty without stenting, allowing surgery after roughly fourteen days): when noncardiac surgery must occur within weeks, balloon angioplasty avoids committing the patient to dual antiplatelet therapy, and operations are considered reasonable about 14 days afterward. A drug-eluting stent placed now forces a choice between interrupting dual therapy in the highest risk window and delaying a cancer operation the urologists cannot postpone. Leaving a severe, symptomatic stenosis entirely untreated on nitrates accepts avoidable perioperative ischemia."}, {"id": 647, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 66 year old man scheduled for coronary bypass surgery is found on preoperative echocardiography to have chronic severe aortic regurgitation with an ejection fraction of 59 percent and no symptoms attributable to the valve. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to the aortic valve?", "options": {"A": "Leave the valve alone and reassess it echocardiographically after bypass recovery", "B": "Replace the aortic valve concomitantly during the planned bypass operation", "C": "Plan staged transcatheter valve implantation several months after surgical revascularization", "D": "Treat with afterload reduction postoperatively and monitor ventricular dimensions annually", "E": "Perform intraoperative balloon dilation of the valve at the time of grafting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Replace the aortic valve concomitantly during the planned bypass operation): severe aortic regurgitation in a patient undergoing cardiac surgery for another indication warrants concomitant valve replacement, since a later second sternotomy carries far higher cumulative risk. Deferral, staged transcatheter treatment, and medical therapy all accept a predictable reoperation. Balloon dilation has no role in regurgitant valve disease."}, {"id": 653, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 68 year old woman is recovering after drug-eluting stenting for NSTEMI. The discharge team debates when cardiac rehabilitation should be arranged, noting she lives 40 minutes from the nearest program and feels deconditioned. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to rehabilitation referral?", "options": {"A": "Defer referral until her three month visit confirms she remains symptom free", "B": "Refer only if she develops recurrent angina or measurable functional decline", "C": "Refer before hospital discharge, offering a hybrid or home based option if needed", "D": "Substitute written walking instructions, since supervised programs add little after stenting", "E": "Order a maximal stress test first and refer only if it is abnormal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer before hospital discharge, offering a hybrid or home based option if needed): early referral before discharge is a performance measure because it sharply increases enrollment, and home based or hybrid delivery is an accepted solution to distance barriers. Waiting three months or requiring symptoms first squanders the period when participation gains are largest. Rehabilitation reduces mortality and readmission after stenting, so written instructions are not an equivalent, and an abnormal stress test is not a prerequisite for referral."}, {"id": 656, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 62 year old woman with diabetes is admitted with NSTEMI. After initial therapy she is pain free with stable vital signs, a GRACE score of 118, no dynamic ECG changes, and a modest troponin rise that is now falling. Which of the following is the most appropriate timing for angiography?", "options": {"A": "Within two hours, since diabetes places her in the immediate invasive category", "B": "No angiography this admission, with outpatient stress imaging in one month", "C": "Only if her predischarge treadmill test provokes ischemia at low workload", "D": "Immediately after a 48 hour glycoprotein inhibitor infusion completes plaque passivation", "E": "Within 25 to 72 hours during this hospitalization, before discharge planning"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Within 25 to 72 hours during this hospitalization, before discharge planning): a stabilized NSTEMI patient with intermediate risk features such as diabetes and a GRACE score below 140 fits a delayed invasive window of roughly 25 to 72 hours. The immediate category is reserved for instability or refractory ischemia she does not have. Deferring angiography to outpatient imaging or making it contingent on treadmill results understages a biomarker positive event, and prolonged glycoprotein inhibitor infusions are not a prerequisite for catheterization."}, {"id": 661, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 66 year old man with HFrEF and NYHA class II symptoms reports persistent fatigue despite optimized four pillar therapy and euvolemia. Laboratory testing shows hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL, ferritin 78 ng/mL, and transferrin saturation 14 percent. His renal function is stable. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for these findings?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe oral ferrous sulfate tablets three times daily continuing over the next six consecutive months", "B": "Give intravenous ferric carboxymaltose to improve symptoms and functional capacity", "C": "Start an erythropoiesis stimulating agent to raise the hemoglobin concentration", "D": "Transfuse one unit of packed red blood cells at the next clinic visit", "E": "Recheck iron studies in six months and treat only if the hemoglobin falls"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give intravenous ferric carboxymaltose to improve symptoms and functional capacity): his ferritin below 100 ng/mL defines iron deficiency in heart failure even without anemia, and intravenous iron improves symptoms, quality of life, and exercise capacity in this population. Oral iron failed to replete iron stores or improve exercise capacity in the IRONOUT-HF trial, largely because absorption is impaired in heart failure. Erythropoiesis stimulating agents showed harm without benefit in this setting, transfusion is not indicated at this hemoglobin, and deferring treatment ignores a symptomatic, treatable deficiency."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 68 year old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy, ejection fraction 32 percent, and severe secondary mitral regurgitation remains class III despite months of maximally tolerated guideline directed therapy and cardiac resynchronization. End systolic diameter is 58 mm and anatomy suits a clip. What is the appropriate next escalation for his regurgitation?", "options": {"A": "Surgical mitral valve replacement, since mechanical prosthetic valves outlast any transcatheter leaflet approximation device", "B": "Further uptitration attempts for another year before revisiting the valve question", "C": "Isolated surgical annuloplasty, because ring downsizing reliably eliminates secondary regurgitation", "D": "Transcatheter edge to edge repair, which reduced hospitalizations and mortality in comparable trial patients", "E": "Left ventricular assist device implantation as the primary treatment of the valve lesion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Transcatheter edge to edge repair, which reduced hospitalizations and mortality in comparable trial patients): a patient still severely regurgitant and symptomatic on optimized medical and resynchronization therapy, with suitable anatomy, ejection fraction in the 20 to 50 percent range, and a ventricle not yet massively dilated, mirrors the trial population in which the device improved survival. Surgical replacement for secondary regurgitation has not shown a mortality benefit and carries operative risk. Another year of titration prolongs proven futility, isolated annuloplasty has high recurrence in secondary disease, and assist devices are reserved for refractory pump failure."}, {"id": 666, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 61 year old man on hydrochlorothiazide has his second gout flare this year; serum urate is 8.9 mg/dL. His blood pressure is 138/86 mm Hg, slightly above goal, and he takes no other antihypertensive medications. Which single medication change best addresses both his gout risk and his blood pressure?", "options": {"A": "Continue hydrochlorothiazide and add daily colchicine for long term flare prophylaxis", "B": "Switch to chlorthalidone for smoother blood pressure coverage across the day", "C": "Add allopurinol and keep the current antihypertensive regimen in place", "D": "Switch to furosemide, which lowers urate more effectively than the thiazides", "E": "Switch to losartan, which lowers pressure and promotes urinary urate excretion"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Switch to losartan, which lowers pressure and promotes urinary urate excretion): Losartan uniquely inhibits the URAT1 transporter, producing a modest uricosuric effect while providing first line blood pressure control, so one change removes the urate raising thiazide and treats both problems. Keeping the offending drug and layering colchicine or allopurinol manages consequences while preserving the cause and adds nothing for his above goal pressure. Chlorthalidone raises urate at least as much as hydrochlorothiazide, and loop diuretics also impair urate excretion rather than improving it."}, {"id": 668, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 71 year old woman with severe aortic stenosis, valve area 0.9 cm², reports a quiet lifestyle and denies symptoms, though her family notes she has gradually stopped climbing stairs. Ejection fraction is 61 percent. What is the most appropriate way to resolve whether she is truly asymptomatic?", "options": {"A": "Accept her own verbal account and repeat surveillance transthoracic echocardiography in twelve months as routine practice", "B": "Proceed directly to valve replacement, since family observations outweigh patient reports", "C": "Order coronary computed tomography, since silent ischemia likely explains the activity change", "D": "Perform supervised treadmill exercise testing, watching for symptoms, blood pressure fall, or limited capacity", "E": "Start a loop diuretic empirically and reassess her exercise tolerance in clinic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Perform supervised treadmill exercise testing, watching for symptoms, blood pressure fall, or limited capacity): patients with severe stenosis often shrink their activity to hide symptoms, and supervised exercise testing safely unmasks limitation, with provoked symptoms or a blunted blood pressure response constituting an indication for replacement. Accepting her account with distant follow up risks missing a now symptomatic lesion. Operating on family testimony alone skips an available objective test, coronary imaging answers a different question, and empiric diuretics neither test nor treat the stenosis."}, {"id": 670, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 57 year old man one year after myocardial infarction has a lipoprotein(a) of 190 nmol/L and LDL cholesterol of 88 mg/dL on maximally tolerated statin plus ezetimibe. He asks what can be done about the lipoprotein(a) itself. Which management approach best reflects the current evidence?", "options": {"A": "Intensify LDL lowering with a PCSK9 inhibitor, since targeted lipoprotein(a) drugs remain investigational", "B": "Begin niacin at high doses, which improves outcomes by lowering his lipoprotein(a)", "C": "Start weekly extracorporeal lipoprotein apheresis sessions as the standard first line option for this laboratory level", "D": "Repeat the lipoprotein(a) level every three months and treat once it doubles", "E": "Add fenofibrate, which reduces lipoprotein(a) enough to normalize his residual risk"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intensify LDL lowering with a PCSK9 inhibitor, since targeted lipoprotein(a) drugs remain investigational): no lipoprotein(a) specific therapy is yet approved, so management intensifies proven risk reduction, and PCSK9 inhibitors both lower his above threshold LDL cholesterol further and modestly reduce lipoprotein(a). Niacin lowers the level without improving outcomes, apheresis is reserved for exceptional progressive disease rather than first use, serial remeasurement adds nothing because levels are genetically stable, and fenofibrate has no meaningful effect. Targeted antisense and small interfering RNA agents remain in trials."}, {"id": 675, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 67 year old man with cardiac index 1.7 L/min/m2 and estimated glomerular filtration rate 22 mL/min needs inotropic support. The fellow proposes standard dose milrinone for its vasodilating benefit, while the attending prefers dobutamine. Which consideration most strongly favors the attending's choice in this specific patient?", "options": {"A": "Dobutamine produces stronger renal artery dilation, directly improving his filtration rate substantially", "B": "Milrinone loses most inotropic efficacy below a filtration rate of thirty", "C": "Dobutamine costs less, and economic stewardship should decide between equivalent inotropic agents", "D": "Milrinone requires central access, whereas dobutamine runs safely through peripheral catheters indefinitely", "E": "Dobutamine clears independently of the kidneys, avoiding the accumulation milrinone shows in renal failure"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Dobutamine clears independently of the kidneys, avoiding the accumulation milrinone shows in renal failure): dobutamine is metabolized rapidly by tissue and hepatic pathways with a very short half life, while milrinone depends on renal elimination and accumulates in advanced kidney disease, producing hypotension and arrhythmia unless doses are cut substantially. Milrinone remains pharmacologically active in renal failure, which is exactly the problem. Renal dose vasodilation, cost, and access route are not the decisive physiologic issues here."}, {"id": 677, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 22 year old man with persistent preexcitation on every electrocardiogram is asymptomatic. During a treadmill test, the delta wave disappears abruptly at a moderate heart rate in a single beat, and conduction becomes normal thereafter. What does this exercise response indicate about his accessory pathway?", "options": {"A": "The pathway conducts retrogradely only and needs no further evaluation or follow up", "B": "Sudden loss of preexcitation at higher rates suggests a longer anterograde refractory period", "C": "The abrupt change reflects catecholamine enhancement of pathway conduction and higher risk", "D": "Exercise testing poorly reflects pathway behavior and should be replaced with genetic screening", "E": "Resting preexcitation alone already establishes a high risk pathway needing prompt ablation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sudden loss of preexcitation at higher rates suggests a longer anterograde refractory period): abrupt single beat disappearance of the delta wave during exercise shows the pathway blocks as the sinus rate climbs, implying it could not conduct atrial fibrillation dangerously fast. This is a reassuring, lower risk finding. It does not mean conduction is retrograde only, since the resting tracing proves anterograde conduction, and resting preexcitation by itself does not define risk without such functional testing."}, {"id": 678, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 68 year old woman with exertional dyspnea undergoes pharmacologic perfusion imaging. It shows a large reversible anterior and anteroseptal defect involving about 22 percent of the myocardium, transient ischemic dilation of the left ventricle, and stress induced ejection fraction drop. How should these findings direct her management?", "options": {"A": "Continue medical therapy alone since the defect is reversible rather than fixed", "B": "Repeat perfusion imaging in six months to assess for interval progression", "C": "Begin heart failure therapy for a primary cardiomyopathy rather than ischemia", "D": "Arrange coronary computed tomography instead to noninvasively confirm the perfusion findings", "E": "Proceed to angiography since the pattern suggests extensive jeopardized myocardium"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Proceed to angiography since the pattern suggests extensive jeopardized myocardium): A large reversible defect exceeding ten percent of the myocardium, cavity dilation with stress, and a falling ejection fraction collectively indicate high risk anatomy such as proximal left anterior descending or multivessel disease, where revascularization decisions require invasive definition. Medical therapy alone and surveillance imaging in six months accept substantial jeopardized muscle without defining the anatomy behind it. A primary cardiomyopathy does not produce stress inducible reversible perfusion defects of this kind, and adding coronary computed tomography merely restates the question the catheterization will answer definitively."}, {"id": 684, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 72 year old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy presents with a regular wide complex tachycardia. The twelve lead electrocardiogram shows entirely negative QRS complexes across all precordial leads with no RS pattern in any chest lead. Hemodynamics are borderline. Which interpretation of this precordial pattern is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Negative precordial concordance strongly favors ventricular tachycardia arising from the anteroapical region", "B": "Absent precordial RS complexes indicate antidromic conduction occurring over a right sided atrioventricular accessory pathway", "C": "Uniform negativity reflects severe hyperkalemia widening the QRS in a sinus rhythm", "D": "This pattern is typical of left bundle branch aberrancy during atrial tachycardia", "E": "Concordance has no diagnostic value unless capture or fusion beats are also present"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Negative precordial concordance strongly favors ventricular tachycardia arising from the anteroapical region): when every chest lead is negative, activation begins at the apex and spreads away from the precordium, a pattern that conduction over the normal His Purkinje system essentially never produces. Left bundle aberrancy still yields transitional RS complexes in the mid precordium. Antidromic preexcitation can mimic ventricular tachycardia but classically produces positive rather than negative concordance, and hyperkalemia widens complexes without abolishing every RS pattern."}, {"id": 685, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 60 year old man has forty minutes of severe chest pressure. His ECG shows 2 mm upsloping ST depression at the J point in V1 through V6 rising into tall, symmetric, prominent T waves, without ST elevation in any lead. The physician plans routine chest pain observation. What should happen instead?", "options": {"A": "Activate the laboratory because this pattern signifies proximal left anterior descending occlusion", "B": "Continue routine observation since current activation criteria require frank ST elevation in two contiguous leads", "C": "Treat for hyperkalemia given the tall T waves across the precordium", "D": "Obtain serial tracings every thirty minutes until frank elevation finally appears", "E": "Start a nitroglycerin infusion and reassess the tracing within several hours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Activate the laboratory because this pattern signifies proximal left anterior descending occlusion): Upsloping J point depression flowing into tall symmetric precordial T waves is the de Winter pattern, an ST elevation equivalent representing acute proximal left anterior descending occlusion that requires immediate reperfusion despite absent classic elevation. Insisting on contiguous elevation before acting applies the wrong criterion to a recognized equivalent. Hyperkalemia produces narrow peaked T waves with other conduction changes rather than this anginal presentation, and both serial waiting and a nitroglycerin infusion with delayed reassessment sacrifice anterior myocardium to a technicality."}, {"id": 686, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 22 year old rower has wall thickness of 14 mm, a dilated left ventricular cavity of 60 mm, normal diastolic indices, and a family history without sudden death. Genetic testing is uninformative. The distinction between athletic remodeling and cardiomyopathy remains uncertain. Which next step most directly resolves this gray zone?", "options": {"A": "Immediate sport disqualification while annual imaging tracks the hypertrophy over several years", "B": "Beta blocker trial, since regression on therapy would confirm pathological hypertrophy convincingly", "C": "Electrophysiology study, since inducible arrhythmia separates athletic hearts from cardiomyopathic ones", "D": "A supervised detraining period with repeat imaging to see whether wall thickness regresses", "E": "Coronary computed tomography, since myocardial bridging distinguishes these two conditions reliably"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A supervised detraining period with repeat imaging to see whether wall thickness regresses): physiologic hypertrophy from training reverses measurably after roughly three months of reduced load, while hypertrophic cardiomyopathy does not, and his dilated cavity with normal diastolic function already leans toward athletic remodeling. Disqualification with surveillance answers nothing for years. Beta blockers do not regress pathological hypertrophy diagnostically, and neither electrophysiology testing nor coronary anatomy addresses the remodeling question."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 65 year old man on chronic amiodarone develops thyrotoxicosis with atrial fibrillation. Thyroid ultrasound shows a multinodular goiter, and color Doppler demonstrates markedly increased glandular vascularity. His electrophysiologist asks which amiodarone induced thyrotoxicosis type this represents and what treatment follows. What is the best conclusion?", "options": {"A": "Type 2 disease, because nodular glands cannot synthesize excess hormone under iodine load", "B": "Mixed disease requiring immediate thyroidectomy, since imaging cannot inform therapy choices", "C": "Type 2 disease, so glucocorticoids alone should quiet the destructive inflammation", "D": "Type 1 disease, so thionamide therapy directed at hormone synthesis is the mainstay", "E": "Neither type, because amiodarone thyrotoxicosis requires a normal baseline gland"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Type 1 disease, so thionamide therapy directed at hormone synthesis is the mainstay): increased vascularity on Doppler in an abnormal nodular gland marks iodine driven hormone overproduction, the type 1 mechanism, which responds to methimazole, sometimes with perchlorate, rather than to glucocorticoids alone. Type 2 disease shows absent or reduced vascularity in a typically normal gland, the reverse of these findings. An abnormal baseline gland is precisely what predisposes to type 1, and surgery is a rescue option rather than the reflex answer when imaging clearly points one way."}, {"id": 688, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "During the index angiogram of a 48 year old woman with infarction and no stenosis above 40 percent, the operator wonders whether anything more should happen before she leaves the table. Which addition to the index procedure most improves the diagnostic yield in this scenario of infarction with nonobstructive arteries?", "options": {"A": "Left ventriculography repeated in three projections for wall motion analysis", "B": "Immediate placement of a stent in the least smooth arterial segment", "C": "Intravascular imaging to look for plaque disruption or a subtle dissection", "D": "Aortography to evaluate the arch and exclude an aortic source", "E": "Right heart catheterization with oximetry sampling at each cardiac level"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intravascular imaging to look for plaque disruption or a subtle dissection): Ultrasound or optical coherence tomography at the index procedure frequently reveals plaque rupture, erosion, or spontaneous dissection that the angiographic lumen silhouette misses, and these findings change antithrombotic and interventional decisions. Repeated ventriculography adds contrast without clarifying the coronary mechanism. Stenting an angiographically unremarkable segment treats nothing identified, aortography addresses a diagnosis the presentation does not suggest, and oximetry sampling screens for shunts rather than coronary pathology."}, {"id": 689, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 64 year old man with chronic coronary disease has an LDL cholesterol of 58 mg/dL on statin therapy yet a persistently elevated high sensitivity C reactive protein of 4.1 mg/L on repeated measurement. He asks what the inflammation result means for further treatment. Which response is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "The elevated marker reflects assay interference and should not influence his therapy", "B": "His statin should be stopped, since it has failed to control the inflammation", "C": "Residual inflammatory risk requires canakinumab, the specifically approved therapeutic agent for this particular indication", "D": "Residual inflammatory risk persists despite lipid control, and low dose colchicine merits consideration", "E": "The result matters for rheumatologic screening but not for future coronary events"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Residual inflammatory risk persists despite lipid control, and low dose colchicine merits consideration): an elevated high sensitivity C reactive protein despite well controlled LDL cholesterol identifies residual inflammatory risk, exactly the population in which low dose colchicine reduced events in randomized trials. A reproducibly elevated result is not assay interference, and the statin is achieving its lipid target and should continue. Canakinumab was not approved for coronary prevention, and the marker predicts coronary events rather than being a purely rheumatologic signal."}, {"id": 690, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 58 year old man four months after mechanical mitral replacement presents with ten days of worsening dyspnea. His INR has been 1.6 to 1.9 for a month. Echo shows raised gradients and restricted disc motion, and gated computed tomography shows a mobile hypoattenuating mass on the valve. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Prosthetic valve thrombosis, given early timing, subtherapeutic anticoagulation, and a low density mobile mass", "B": "Fibrous pannus tissue ingrowth beneath the prosthesis, given the fixed disc excursion and short postoperative interval", "C": "Structural degeneration of the prosthesis with early mechanical component fatigue and fracture", "D": "Culture negative endocarditis producing a sterile fibrinous vegetation on the sewing ring", "E": "Patient prosthesis mismatch unmasked by interval weight gain and higher cardiac output"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Prosthetic valve thrombosis, given early timing, subtherapeutic anticoagulation, and a low density mobile mass): thrombosis characteristically appears early after implantation during lapses in anticoagulation, and on computed tomography thrombus is hypoattenuating and often mobile, exactly this picture. Pannus develops over years, appears dense and fixed, and occurs despite therapeutic INR. Mechanical prostheses rarely fail structurally at four months, endocarditis is not suggested without fever or bacteremia, and mismatch produces stable high gradients from the day of surgery rather than a subacute decline."}, {"id": 693, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 79 year old man with an estimated glomerular filtration rate of 24 mL/min is started on a standard milrinone infusion for low output heart failure. Six hours later he is hypotensive with frequent ventricular ectopy. Which mechanism most likely explains this deterioration and should have shaped dosing?", "options": {"A": "Renal clearance of milrinone is reduced, so accumulation caused vasodilation and arrhythmia", "B": "Milrinone converts to an active hepatic metabolite that concentrates in renal impairment", "C": "Uremia blocks phosphodiesterase inhibition, so higher doses were needed to reach effect", "D": "Milrinone stimulates renin release, producing rebound hypertension followed by reflex hypotension later", "E": "Potassium wasting from milrinone caused the ectopy independent of any dosing choice"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Renal clearance of milrinone is reduced, so accumulation caused vasodilation and arrhythmia): milrinone is eliminated largely unchanged by the kidney, and unadjusted infusions in advanced renal impairment produce climbing plasma levels, with hypotension from vasodilation and ventricular ectopy as characteristic toxicity. There is no dominant active hepatic metabolite, and uremia does not neutralize phosphodiesterase inhibition. The drug does not act by renin stimulation or direct potassium wasting."}, {"id": 697, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 79 year old man with known severe aortic stenosis presents with pulmonary edema, blood pressure 92/60 mm Hg, and rising creatinine. He stabilizes partially with careful diuresis but cannot leave the hospital. The heart team confirms suitable anatomy. What is the most appropriate definitive plan during this admission?", "options": {"A": "Discharge on optimized diuretics with outpatient valve evaluation over the following month", "B": "Begin an angiotensin receptor neprilysin inhibitor and reassess the valve after remodeling", "C": "Insert an intra aortic balloon pump and continue medical stabilization for two weeks", "D": "Proceed with expedited transcatheter valve replacement before discharge from this hospitalization", "E": "Perform elective surgical aortic valve replacement after a prolonged period of nutritional and renal optimization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proceed with expedited transcatheter valve replacement before discharge from this hospitalization): decompensated severe stenosis carries a high early mortality, medical therapy cannot relieve a fixed obstruction, and an anatomically suitable patient benefits from urgent relief of afterload mismatch during the same admission. Discharge for elective workup invites recurrent decompensation and renal injury. Neurohormonal agents and balloon counterpulsation do not open the valve, and a long optimization period before surgery lets the treatable driver of his instability persist."}, {"id": 698, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 34 year old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and maximal wall thickness of 22 mm collapsed without warning while walking to his car, waking within seconds. His electrocardiogram is unchanged and an event monitor shows no arrhythmia over two weeks. How should this syncope influence his risk stratification?", "options": {"A": "It is broadly reassuring, because two monitored weeks without documented arrhythmia effectively exclude an arrhythmic mechanism", "B": "It carries weight as unexplained syncope, strengthening consideration of a defibrillator despite negative monitoring", "C": "It should prompt tilt table testing, since vasovagal physiology is likelier at his age", "D": "It matters little compared with wall thickness, which alone determines defibrillator decisions here", "E": "It warrants an implantable loop recorder before any risk discussion can reasonably occur"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It carries weight as unexplained syncope, strengthening consideration of a defibrillator despite negative monitoring): abrupt collapse without prodrome in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy counts as unexplained syncope, a major risk marker, and brief negative monitoring cannot exclude an arrhythmia that occurs sporadically. Tilt testing and loop recorders may add data but do not neutralize the event's prognostic meaning in guideline models. Wall thickness is one input among several, not a substitute for the syncope history."}, {"id": 699, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 19 year old woman with a Fontan circulation presents after coughing up firm, branching, rubbery material that resembles a cast of her airways. She has had several similar episodes with transient hypoxemia. Connecting this striking symptom to her circulation, which complication does this presentation represent?", "options": {"A": "Necrotizing bacterial pneumonia producing sloughed membranes within the bronchial tree", "B": "Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis with thick mucoid impaction filling the proximal conducting airways", "C": "Plastic bronchitis from lymphatic leakage forming proteinaceous casts inside the airways", "D": "Aspirated foreign material molded into the shape of the bronchial branches", "E": "Tracheobronchial amyloid deposits fragmenting intermittently into the central airways"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Plastic bronchitis from lymphatic leakage forming proteinaceous casts inside the airways): Elevated systemic venous pressure after Fontan palliation congests thoracic lymphatics, which can decompress into the bronchial tree and form rubbery branching casts that patients expectorate. It is a serious complication that warrants hemodynamic assessment of the Fontan pathway and consideration of lymphatic intervention. Infection, allergy, and aspiration do not produce recurrent branching casts in this vascular context."}, {"id": 701, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 27 year old woman at eight weeks gestation is taking lisinopril for chronic hypertension. You recommend changing therapy today, and she asks what specifically could happen to the baby if she remained on her current medication through the second and third trimesters. Which risk correctly answers her question?", "options": {"A": "Neonatal hyperglycemia from pancreatic beta cell suppression late in gestation", "B": "Fetal macrosomia with shoulder dystocia from placental growth factor excess", "C": "Neural tube defects from folate antagonism during early fetal organogenesis", "D": "Fetal bradycardia and neonatal hypoglycemia appearing most prominently in the hours after the delivery", "E": "Fetal kidney injury with oligohydramnios, growth restriction, and skull ossification defects"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Fetal kidney injury with oligohydramnios, growth restriction, and skull ossification defects): Fetal urine production depends on angiotensin mediated glomerular pressure, so continued renin angiotensin blockade in the second and third trimesters causes fetal renal dysgenesis and anuria, the resulting oligohydramnios with limb contractures and pulmonary hypoplasia, growth restriction, and hypocalvaria. This fetopathy is the reason the drug is replaced the moment pregnancy is recognized. Folate antagonism describes drugs like methotrexate and some anticonvulsants, macrosomia belongs to poorly controlled diabetes, and neonatal bradycardia with hypoglycemia is the atenolol pattern."}, {"id": 703, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 47 year old man followed for congenitally corrected transposition presents with new fatigue and a resting heart rate of 38 beats per minute. His electrocardiogram shows regular P waves dissociated from a slower narrow complex escape rhythm. How should this rhythm change be interpreted within his congenital condition?", "options": {"A": "An anticipated progression to complete heart block from his fragile displaced conduction axis", "B": "A reversible nodal blocking medication effect that should resolve once the offending pharmacologic agents are held", "C": "Athletic conditioning producing benign vagally mediated slowing of sinus rates", "D": "An acute inferior infarction transiently suppressing the atrioventricular nodal tissue", "E": "Hyperkalemia induced conduction slowing requiring urgent potassium lowering measures"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An anticipated progression to complete heart block from his fragile displaced conduction axis): Congenitally corrected transposition carries an abnormally located atrioventricular node and an elongated penetrating bundle that degenerate at roughly two percent per year, so atrioventricular dissociation here represents the natural history of the lesion. He needs evaluation for pacing with attention to his systemic right ventricle. Athletic slowing and reversible metabolic causes would not produce dissociated P waves with an escape rhythm in this context."}, {"id": 704, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "An 84 year old woman with symptomatic severe aortic stenosis is planned for transcatheter valve replacement. Angiography shows one discrete 85 percent proximal left anterior descending stenosis with normal flow elsewhere, and she reports angina with exertion. Which revascularization approach best fits her overall treatment plan?", "options": {"A": "Percutaneous intervention on the proximal lesion, coordinated with the planned transcatheter valve procedure", "B": "Conversion to open surgery so the artery can be bypassed during valve replacement", "C": "Medical antianginal therapy alone, because revascularization adds no value around valve procedures", "D": "Invasive fractional flow reserve physiologic testing performed after the new valve is implanted and symptoms reassessed", "E": "Deferral of the valve procedure until six months after coronary stenting is completed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Percutaneous intervention on the proximal lesion, coordinated with the planned transcatheter valve procedure): in a transcatheter candidate, a discrete proximal severe stenosis in a major vessel with angina is reasonably stented, either before or around the valve procedure, preserving the less invasive overall strategy. Converting to open surgery discards the rationale for choosing transcatheter therapy in an 84 year old. Leaving a critical proximal lesion untreated risks ischemia during rapid pacing and hypotension, and a six month delay leaves severe symptomatic stenosis exposed."}, {"id": 705, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "An 81 year old woman with heart failure on digoxin presents with nausea, fatigue, and new frequent premature ventricular beats after a week of gastroenteritis. Her furosemide was recently doubled and she started clarithromycin. The laboratory results shown are from this visit. Which management reasoning is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Give supplemental potassium alone, since correcting electrolytes reverses digoxin cardiotoxicity when present", "B": "Continue digoxin at half dose, since levels marginally above range rarely cause symptoms", "C": "Treat this as digoxin toxicity precipitated by renal decline and interaction, holding the drug", "D": "Attribute the symptoms to viral gastroenteritis and reassess digoxin at routine follow up", "E": "Administer calcium and insulin, prioritizing membrane stabilization for potassium above five"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat this as digoxin toxicity precipitated by renal decline and interaction, holding the drug): volume depletion raised her creatinine, clarithromycin inhibits digoxin clearance, and the resulting level with gastrointestinal symptoms, ectopy, and hyperkalemia is a coherent toxicity syndrome that warrants stopping digoxin and assessing for antibody fragment therapy. Her potassium elevation reflects sodium potassium pump inhibition, so it signals severity rather than a deficit to replace. Dismissing the picture as gastroenteritis or merely halving the dose leaves the toxic exposure in place.", "image": "question_images/q_705.png"}, {"id": 709, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 72 year old woman with acute ischemic stroke is an alteplase candidate at 100 minutes from symptom onset. Blood pressure is 192/104 mm Hg despite calm surroundings and an empty bladder. The stroke team is otherwise ready to treat her. What should happen next regarding thrombolysis and blood pressure?", "options": {"A": "Administer alteplase now and treat the pressure should it rise further", "B": "Lower the pressure below 185/110 with intravenous agents, then give alteplase", "C": "Defer thrombolysis today and begin aspirin with permissive blood pressure management", "D": "Reduce pressure below 140/90 first to minimize reperfusion hemorrhage risk", "E": "Proceed with mechanical thrombectomy instead, since pressure precludes lytic use"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower the pressure below 185/110 with intravenous agents, then give alteplase): Her pressure exceeds the 185/110 mm Hg lytic threshold, but titratable intravenous labetalol or nicardipine usually achieves the target within minutes, preserving her place in the treatment window; after the drug is given, pressure is then held below 180/105 for 24 hours. Injecting alteplase at the current value raises intracranial hemorrhage risk, while abandoning lysis for aspirin forfeits proven benefit over a correctable number. Driving pressure below 140/90 starves penumbral collaterals, and thrombectomy candidacy is anatomic rather than a workaround for a treatable pressure."}, {"id": 712, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 69 year old man with chronic coronary disease and an estimated glomerular filtration rate of 22 mL/min asks about adding colchicine after reading about its cardiovascular benefit. He already takes a statin, aspirin, and an ACE inhibitor. Which consideration most appropriately guides the colchicine decision for him?", "options": {"A": "Colchicine requires dose doubling in kidney disease to reach effective plasma levels", "B": "His kidney disease increases inflammatory risk, making the colchicine benefit proportionally larger", "C": "Severe renal impairment increases toxicity risk, so colchicine should generally be avoided", "D": "Colchicine can substitute for his statin if muscle symptoms ever develop later", "E": "The cardiovascular trials enrolled mainly dialysis patients, supporting use at this filtration rate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Severe renal impairment increases toxicity risk, so colchicine should generally be avoided): colchicine is cleared renally, accumulates when the filtration rate falls below roughly 30 mL/min, and can cause myopathy, cytopenias, and neuropathy, so it should generally be avoided at his level of kidney function. The pivotal trials excluded severe renal impairment rather than enrolling dialysis patients, and kidney disease amplifies toxicity rather than benefit. Dose doubling would worsen accumulation, and colchicine has no role as a statin substitute."}, {"id": 713, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A pulmonary artery catheter in a hypotensive 61 year old woman shows pulmonary capillary wedge pressure 28 mm Hg, cardiac index 1.6 L/min/m2, and systemic vascular resistance 1900 dynes. She is cold and oliguric after a large anterior infarction. Which shock physiology do these numbers most clearly define?", "options": {"A": "Distributive shock, since vasodilation typically follows large infarctions through inflammatory cytokine release", "B": "Hypovolemic shock, because oliguria in this setting usually reflects inadequate circulating volume", "C": "Obstructive shock, given that high wedge pressure suggests impaired ventricular filling mechanics", "D": "Cardiogenic shock, given elevated filling pressure, depressed index, and intense compensatory vasoconstriction", "E": "Mixed shock, because resistance this high generally indicates a second distributive process"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cardiogenic shock, given elevated filling pressure, depressed index, and intense compensatory vasoconstriction): the triad of high wedge, low index, and high resistance is the classic pump failure signature after anterior infarction. Distributive physiology would show low resistance, and hypovolemia would show a low wedge rather than 28 mm Hg. Obstructive shock impairs filling with a low or discordant wedge pattern, which is absent here."}, {"id": 714, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 63 year old man with an assist device presents to a community clinic with two days of purulent drainage and tenderness at his driveline exit site, temperature 38.2, and heart rate 104. The covering clinician considers bedside incision and drainage with oral antibiotics. What is the appropriate management framework?", "options": {"A": "Blood cultures, imaging for tract extension, and urgent coordination with his implanting center", "B": "Bedside incision and drainage now, since source control outweighs specialist coordination concerns", "C": "Oral antibiotics targeting skin flora with routine wound checks in one week", "D": "Topical antisepsis and dressing changes alone, escalating just if fever persists further", "E": "Immediate device explant referral, since driveline infections cannot be controlled without removal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Blood cultures, imaging for tract extension, and urgent coordination with his implanting center): driveline infections can track along the tunnel toward the pump pocket, and systemic signs mean bacteremia must be excluded while the device team directs culture guided intravenous therapy and any surgical decisions. Blind incision risks violating the tract and seeding deeper planes. Oral or topical management undertreats a device related infection with fever, and many driveline infections are controlled without explant."}, {"id": 715, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 44 year old woman has months of fatigue and pounding sensations. The rhythm strip shown reveals a pattern in which every sinus beat is followed by a premature wide complex beat. A monitor shows this pattern for much of the day, and her ejection fraction is 44 percent. Which management consideration is most important?", "options": {"A": "Reassurance alone, because isolated premature beats do not affect ventricular function", "B": "Immediate defibrillator implantation because her ejection fraction is below 50 percent", "C": "Coronary angiography, since ischemia is the usual cause in young women", "D": "A high ectopic burden may be depressing her ventricle, warranting suppression or ablation", "E": "Therapeutic anticoagulation, since frequent ventricular ectopy carries the same embolic risk as atrial fibrillation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A high ectopic burden may be depressing her ventricle, warranting suppression or ablation): the strip shows ventricular bigeminy, and when premature ventricular complexes occupy a large fraction of the day, typically above 10 to 15 percent of beats, they can cause a reversible cardiomyopathy, so suppression with medication or catheter ablation is indicated alongside repeat imaging. Reassurance ignores her reduced ejection fraction. A defibrillator is not indicated for this potentially reversible process, ischemia is an uncommon driver at her age, and frequent ectopy does not confer fibrillation level embolic risk.", "image": "question_images/q_715.png"}, {"id": 716, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 72 year old diabetic man with typical angina has a vasodilator perfusion study reported as normal, with transient ischemic dilation noted and a modest global flow reduction on quantitative analysis. His symptoms continue to worsen. Which explanation for the reassuring perfusion images deserves the most concern here?", "options": {"A": "Balanced ischemia from left main or three vessel disease can produce uniform perfusion", "B": "The tracer dose was likely insufficient, and the study should simply be repeated", "C": "His diabetes causes neuropathy that eliminates angina, making the imaging findings irrelevant", "D": "Transient ischemic dilation is a normal variant seen with vasodilator stress protocols", "E": "Worsening symptoms despite normal images establish a noncardiac cause of his pain"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Balanced ischemia from left main or three vessel disease can produce uniform perfusion): left main or severe three vessel disease can reduce flow globally, so relative perfusion images look uniform while transient ischemic dilation and reduced global flow are the warning signs, a combination that warrants anatomic evaluation. This is not a tracer dosing problem, and dilation with vasodilator stress is not a normal variant. Diabetic neuropathy blunting symptoms cannot explain his worsening angina, and normal relative images do not establish a noncardiac cause."}, {"id": 720, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 49 year old woman with a mechanical mitral valve has obstructive thrombosis with New York Heart Association class II symptoms and a small thrombus under 0.8 cm² on imaging. The surgical team estimates high reoperative risk given two prior sternotomies. Which treatment strategy is most reasonable for her now?", "options": {"A": "Immediate redo surgery, since operative risk matters less than mechanical valve function", "B": "Slow infusion, low dose fibrinolytic therapy with serial echocardiographic monitoring of the gradient", "C": "Increased warfarin dosing targeting a higher INR with echocardiographic reassessment of the thrombus in three months", "D": "Dual antiplatelet therapy added to current warfarin to dissolve the platelet rich clot", "E": "Transcatheter valve in valve implantation across the thrombosed mechanical prosthesis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Slow infusion, low dose fibrinolytic therapy with serial echocardiographic monitoring of the gradient): for prosthetic thrombosis with a smaller thrombus burden and milder symptoms, slow infusion fibrinolysis achieves high success with acceptable embolic risk, and it is especially attractive when reoperation carries high risk. Emergency surgery is favored for large obstructive thrombi or severe compromise in acceptable candidates. Anticoagulation intensification alone acts too slowly for an obstructing clot, antiplatelet agents do not lyse fibrin, and implanting within a thrombosed mechanical valve is not feasible."}, {"id": 721, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 59 year old woman with confirmed chronic constrictive pericarditis after remote radiation asks why her escalating diuretics no longer control ascites and leg swelling, and whether stronger medication combinations could substitute for the operation her cardiologist keeps recommending. Which framing of definitive management is most accurate for her?", "options": {"A": "Transcatheter pericardial ballooning offers durable relief comparable to surgery with less risk", "B": "Combination diuretic regimens control most chronic constriction indefinitely when dosed to weight", "C": "Anti inflammatory therapy with colchicine reverses established fibrous pericardial constriction in many treated patients", "D": "Diuretics palliate the congestion, but pericardiectomy is the definitive treatment for chronic constriction", "E": "Heart transplantation is the preferred operation once constriction produces refractory ascites"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diuretics palliate the congestion, but pericardiectomy is the definitive treatment for chronic constriction): a rigid fibrotic or calcified pericardium is a mechanical problem no diuretic regimen can remove, and escalating doses eventually fail while the underlying restraint progresses. Colchicine helps transient inflammatory constriction, not established fibrosis, and no balloon technique strips the pericardium. Transplantation replaces a healthy myocardium unnecessarily, although radiation disease does raise surgical complexity worth discussing candidly."}, {"id": 723, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 66 year old woman on combination ipilimumab and nivolumab reports mild diplopia, proximal weakness, and dyspnea. Her creatine kinase is markedly elevated. The resident focuses on a neurologic workup alone. Which reasoning should broaden the immediate evaluation before she leaves the clinic today?", "options": {"A": "Diplopia with weakness suggests stroke, so brain imaging supersedes any cardiac testing", "B": "Checkpoint myositis overlaps with myocarditis and myasthenia, so troponin and electrocardiography are mandatory", "C": "Creatine kinase elevation implies statin toxicity, so stopping the statin resolves the syndrome", "D": "Combination checkpoint immunotherapy rarely causes clinically significant cardiotoxicity, so reassurance and routine follow up suffice", "E": "Fatigue and dyspnea suggest anemia, so transfusion thresholds should be evaluated first"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Checkpoint myositis overlaps with myocarditis and myasthenia, so troponin and electrocardiography are mandatory): immune related myositis travels with myocarditis and a myasthenia like syndrome in a recognized triad, and combination checkpoint therapy raises that risk severalfold, so ocular weakness plus elevated muscle enzymes demands same day cardiac screening. Missing concurrent myocarditis risks conduction collapse. Stroke does not elevate creatine kinase this way, and statin toxicity or anemia cannot unify the neuromuscular and respiratory picture."}, {"id": 724, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A woman with moderate mitral stenosis tolerated early pregnancy well but develops orthopnea and a resting heart rate of 110 at 28 weeks. Her valve area is unchanged from before conception. Which physiologic explanation best accounts for her deterioration despite a stable valve orifice at this gestational stage?", "options": {"A": "Progesterone directly stiffens the rheumatic leaflets, functionally narrowing the orifice further", "B": "The gravid uterus elevates the diaphragm, mechanically compressing the left atrium", "C": "Placental shunting steals systemic flow, lowering the gradient the atrium can generate", "D": "Expanded blood volume and faster heart rate raise atrial pressure across the fixed orifice", "E": "Physiologic anemia thickens blood viscosity, increasing resistance across the stenotic valve"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Expanded blood volume and faster heart rate raise atrial pressure across the fixed orifice): pregnancy raises blood volume by roughly half and accelerates heart rate, and since mitral flow occurs only in diastole, tachycardia leaves less time to push a larger volume through a fixed narrow orifice, so atrial pressure climbs steeply. Hormones do not acutely stiffen leaflets. Diaphragm elevation does not obstruct atrial inflow, placental flow increases rather than steals cardiac output demand, and anemia lowers viscosity instead of raising it."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 68 year old woman on lisinopril and chlorthalidone presents after two days of vomiting and diarrhea with lightheadedness on standing. Supine blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg with pulse 84; at three minutes standing it is 92/58 mm Hg with pulse 118. Which management best addresses the mechanism of her symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Start midodrine before ambulation until her standing symptoms resolve fully", "B": "Begin daily oral fludrocortisone therapy to expand her plasma volume gradually over the coming weeks", "C": "Add compression stockings and abdominal binders as durable first line therapy", "D": "Give volume repletion and hold the diuretic and other hypotensive medications temporarily", "E": "Order autonomic reflex testing for suspected neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give volume repletion and hold the diuretic and other hypotensive medications temporarily): Her orthostatic fall comes with a compensatory pulse rise of 34 beats per minute, showing an intact baroreflex responding to true hypovolemia from gastrointestinal losses compounded by a diuretic and an angiotensin blocker. Restoring volume and pausing those drugs treats the actual mechanism. Midodrine and fludrocortisone target chronic autonomic failure rather than acute depletion, compression garments are supportive rather than corrective here, and autonomic testing is unnecessary when tachycardia already proves the reflex works."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 30 year old man with a known unrepaired ventricular septal defect reports that childhood physicians described a very loud murmur, yet his current murmur is barely audible. He now has exertional cyanosis and clubbing. What best explains the striking change in his murmur over the years?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary vascular resistance rose until ventricular pressures equalized and shunt velocity fell", "B": "The septal defect underwent spontaneous partial closure by redundant accessory tricuspid valve leaflet tissue", "C": "Progressive obesity increased the distance between the heart and the stethoscope", "D": "Left ventricular systolic failure lowered the driving pressure across the defect", "E": "An aortic cusp prolapsed into the defect and redirected the jet posteriorly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pulmonary vascular resistance rose until ventricular pressures equalized and shunt velocity fell): Cyanosis and clubbing signal shunt reversal, meaning irreversible pulmonary vascular disease has raised right ventricular pressure to systemic levels. With little pressure difference remaining, flow across the defect is slow and quiet, so the murmur fades as the disease worsens. Spontaneous closure or cusp prolapse could soften the murmur but would not produce cyanosis."}, {"id": 728, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 55 year old woman presents four hours into an anterior infarction. En route to the laboratory she becomes pulseless with a narrow complex rhythm on the monitor, and is resuscitated after two minutes of compressions. Bedside echocardiography shows a new moderate circumferential pericardial effusion. What has most likely occurred?", "options": {"A": "Free wall rupture with blood entering the pericardium causing electromechanical dissociation", "B": "Massive acute pulmonary embolism producing sudden right ventricular pressure overload and obstructed output", "C": "Stent thrombosis with reinfarction of the anterior wall territory", "D": "Tension pneumothorax from the brief period of chest compressions", "E": "Acute severe mitral regurgitation from an ischemic papillary muscle"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Free wall rupture with blood entering the pericardium causing electromechanical dissociation): Sudden pulseless electrical activity during an evolving transmural anterior infarction with a new pericardial effusion is the classic signature of free wall rupture, where organized electrical activity persists while tamponade abolishes output, and survival depends on immediate pericardiocentesis with emergency surgery. Pulmonary embolism produces right ventricular strain rather than a new effusion. Stent thrombosis cannot be the mechanism when no stent has yet been placed, compressions of two minutes rarely cause tension pneumothorax and none was seen, and papillary muscle rupture floods the lungs rather than the pericardial space."}, {"id": 729, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 58 year old asymptomatic man with well controlled type 2 diabetes, normal renal function, and a normal resting ECG requests annual stress testing because his brother had bypass surgery at seventy. He exercises regularly without limitation. What does current evidence support for coronary screening in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Annual myocardial perfusion imaging since diabetes is considered a coronary artery disease equivalent", "B": "Immediate angiography given the family history of surgical revascularization", "C": "Annual treadmill testing alternating with echocardiography every other year", "D": "Optimize preventive risk factor therapy rather than routine screening stress imaging", "E": "Continuous ambulatory monitoring to capture any silent ischemic episodes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Optimize preventive risk factor therapy rather than routine screening stress imaging): Randomized screening of asymptomatic diabetic patients with stress imaging did not reduce cardiac events compared with contemporary preventive therapy, so statins, blood pressure control, and glycemic management carry the actual benefit. Annual perfusion imaging and alternating treadmill studies generate radiation, cost, and downstream procedures without demonstrated event reduction in this population. Angiography for a family history of late onset disease in an asymptomatic exerciser has no supporting indication, and ambulatory monitoring for silent ischemia has never been shown to improve outcomes as a screening tool."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "The patient with papillary muscle rupture is in pulmonary edema with a systolic pressure of 82 mm Hg. The surgical team needs ninety minutes to open a room. Which stabilization strategy best supports him during this interval while respecting the underlying valve physiology and his marginal pressure?", "options": {"A": "High dose norepinephrine alone to raise the systemic pressure before surgery", "B": "Afterload reduction with balloon pump counterpulsation as a bridge to emergency repair", "C": "Aggressive intravenous crystalloid volume loading to improve forward output through the regurgitant valve", "D": "Intravenous beta blockade to lengthen diastole and reduce the regurgitant volume", "E": "Delay surgery several weeks so the necrotic tissue can fibrose and hold sutures"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Afterload reduction with balloon pump counterpulsation as a bridge to emergency repair): Counterpulsation lowers the resistance the left ventricle ejects against, shifting flow forward and away from the regurgitant path while augmenting coronary perfusion, which is exactly what acute mitral regurgitation with hypotension needs. Pure vasoconstriction with norepinephrine raises afterload and drives more of each beat backward into the lungs. Volume loading worsens the pulmonary edema, beta blockade removes the compensatory tachycardia that maintains output, and deliberate delay for tissue fibrosis carries a prohibitive early mortality."}, {"id": 733, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 61 year old man with chest pressure develops recurrent runs of the arrhythmia on the rhythm strip shown, with beat to beat variation in QRS shape and axis. Between runs his corrected QT interval measures 420 milliseconds. Which management priority follows most directly from these findings?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous magnesium sulfate and temporary overdrive pacing, since pause dependent torsades is the operative mechanism", "B": "Genetic testing for congenital long QT syndrome before any invasive evaluation", "C": "Urgent coronary angiography, because a normal QT points toward ischemia driven polymorphic tachycardia", "D": "Oral quinidine to treat presumed Brugada syndrome with recurrent arrhythmic storms", "E": "Reassurance and outpatient monitoring because the runs terminate on their own"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Urgent coronary angiography, because a normal QT points toward ischemia driven polymorphic tachycardia): polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with a normal baseline QT interval, particularly with chest pain, is most often caused by acute ischemia, and revascularization is the definitive therapy. Torsades de pointes requires a prolonged QT between runs, which he does not have, so magnesium and pacing are not the priority. Congenital long QT testing and empiric quinidine do not fit this presentation, and self terminating runs in ischemia still herald ventricular fibrillation.", "image": "question_images/q_733.png"}, {"id": 735, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 73 year old man has moderate aortic stenosis with valve area 1.2 cm² and moderate aortic regurgitation. He reports progressive exertional dyspnea, and his mean gradient is 38 mm Hg with peak velocity 3.9 m/s. Neither lesion alone is severe. How should the hemodynamic burden of this valve be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "As two moderate lesions each requiring only surveillance until one becomes independently severe", "B": "As predominant aortic regurgitation, since combined valve lesions weight the assessment toward the volume loading component", "C": "As measurement artifact, because stenosis and regurgitation cannot coexist at these values", "D": "As a low gradient state requiring dobutamine testing before any conclusion is reached", "E": "As a combined lesion whose total load approaches severe disease, warranting intervention given his symptoms"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (As a combined lesion whose total load approaches severe disease, warranting intervention given his symptoms): mixed moderate stenosis and moderate regurgitation impose simultaneous pressure and volume overload, and the elevated transvalvular velocity reflects that summed burden, so a symptomatic patient with these values is managed like severe disease. Treating each lesion in isolation misses their additive physiology. The lesions coexist commonly in calcific and rheumatic disease, and dobutamine protocols are designed for low flow, low gradient states rather than a gradient already near severe."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "An echocardiogram of a 55 year old man with a moderate effusion reports an inferior vena cava of 24 millimeters that fails to collapse with sniffing. Blood pressure is normal. How should you interpret this isolated finding of caval plethora when judging whether he has cardiac tamponade?", "options": {"A": "It confirms tamponade by itself and mandates drainage within the hour", "B": "It excludes tamponade physiology, because a distended cava indicates unrestricted systemic venous return", "C": "It indicates that the effusion is transudative rather than exudative fluid", "D": "It is a sensitive but nonspecific sign, reflecting raised right atrial pressure broadly", "E": "It localizes the effusion posteriorly where the cava enters the atrium"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is a sensitive but nonspecific sign, reflecting raised right atrial pressure broadly): a dilated noncollapsing cava is present in nearly all significant tamponade, so its absence argues strongly against the diagnosis, yet it also occurs with right heart failure, tricuspid disease, pulmonary hypertension, and volume overload, so alone it cannot confirm compression. It therefore neither mandates immediate drainage nor excludes tamponade in the opposite direction. Caval size says nothing about fluid chemistry, and it does not localize where an effusion sits."}, {"id": 748, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "Two patients with identical borderline risk estimates both have coronary artery calcium scores of zero. One is a 56 year old nonsmoker; the other is a 56 year old current smoker with prediabetes. Their physician treats the two results differently. Which reasoning best supports making that distinction between them?", "options": {"A": "Calcium scoring is invalid in smokers because tar deposits mimic coronary calcification", "B": "A zero score in a smoker mandates immediate coronary computed tomography angiography", "C": "The nonsmoker should still receive a statin because calcium scores fluctuate daily", "D": "A zero score is less reassuring in smokers and diabetes, where noncalcified plaque is likelier", "E": "Radiation exposure from the calcium scan is measurably higher in current smokers, changing the risk calculus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A zero score is less reassuring in smokers and diabetes, where noncalcified plaque is likelier): calcium scanning detects only calcified plaque, and smokers and patients with dysglycemia carry a higher burden of noncalcified disease, so a zero score is less reassuring and the statin discussion may continue despite it. Tar does not mimic calcification on computed tomography, and an adverse risk factor profile does not compel angiography by computed tomography. Scores do not fluctuate daily, and radiation dose differences play no role in interpreting the result."}, {"id": 749, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 46 year old man presents with chest pain and ST elevation, and the catheterization laboratory is placed on standby. His troponin is mildly elevated and the ECG shows changes in most leads. Which electrocardiographic reasoning most strongly supports deferring emergency angiography in favor of treating pericarditis?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse concave elevation with PR depression across territories, sparing the reciprocal leads", "B": "A troponin elevation measuring below five times the normal limit reliably excludes an acute coronary occlusion", "C": "ST elevation greater in lead III than lead II indicates pericardial inflammation", "D": "Chest pain that improves with sublingual nitroglycerin establishes a nonischemic cause", "E": "A normal echocardiogram at presentation rules out infarction as the explanation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Diffuse concave elevation with PR depression across territories, sparing the reciprocal leads): involvement across multiple coronary territories with concave morphology, PR segment depression, and absence of reciprocal depression outside aVR points strongly away from a single occluded vessel, while mild troponin elevation is compatible with myopericarditis. Troponin thresholds cannot exclude occlusion. Greater elevation in lead III than lead II actually suggests right coronary infarction, nitroglycerin response is diagnostically unreliable, and a normal echocardiogram does not exclude early infarction."}, {"id": 753, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 56 year old man with longstanding rheumatoid arthritis and a ten year atherosclerotic risk score of 6.5 percent asks whether he needs a statin. His physician assistant weighs how the arthritis should influence that estimate. What is the most appropriate reasoning for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Standard calculators fully capture inflammatory disease, so his score stands as calculated", "B": "Rheumatoid arthritis lowers his vascular risk because chronic weekly methotrexate therapy suppresses coronary plaque formation reliably", "C": "Chronic inflammatory disease acts as a risk enhancer, favoring earlier statin initiation despite his borderline score", "D": "Statins are relatively contraindicated with methotrexate, so lifestyle measures must substitute entirely", "E": "His joint disease matters cardiovascularly only if rheumatoid nodules involve the myocardium"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chronic inflammatory disease acts as a risk enhancer, favoring earlier statin initiation despite his borderline score): rheumatoid arthritis carries excess atherosclerotic risk on the order of one and a half fold, driven by systemic inflammation that standard calculators do not capture, so prevention guidelines list it among the risk enhancing conditions that tip borderline and intermediate patients toward statin therapy. Methotrexate may modestly reduce events but does not neutralize the disease's vascular effect. Statins combine safely with methotrexate, and myocardial nodules are a rarity irrelevant to routine risk assessment."}, {"id": 755, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "In the emergency department, a 64 year old man with known ejection fraction of 25 percent has a blood pressure of 118/96 mm Hg, mottled knees, cool forearms, and a creatinine nearly double his baseline. The triage nurse proposes routine floor admission. What is the most appropriate disposition decision?", "options": {"A": "Routine floor admission with oral diuretics, since systolic pressure remains comfortably above ninety", "B": "Escalate to a monitored unit for perfusion focused evaluation despite the reassuring pressure", "C": "Discharge with close follow up, because stable pressure excludes shock in chronic cardiomyopathy", "D": "Floor admission after a saline bolus to correct the creatinine before reassessing him", "E": "Observation unit placement pending a repeat creatinine in twelve hours before deciding"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Escalate to a monitored unit for perfusion focused evaluation despite the reassuring pressure): mottling, cool limbs, a narrow pulse pressure of 22, and doubling creatinine identify hypoperfusion that a systolic number conceals. Floor placement or observation delays lactate measurement, hemodynamic assessment, and possible inotropic support. A saline bolus treats the creatinine as prerenal from volume loss, which would deepen congestion in a failing ventricle."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 41 year old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has three concerning features under review: an apical aneurysm on magnetic resonance imaging, repetitive nonsustained ventricular tachycardia on monitoring, and a brother who died suddenly at 38. His calculated five year risk score is intermediate. How should the clinician weigh this information?", "options": {"A": "Follow the calculated score strictly, since risk models outperform individual clinical judgment consistently", "B": "Defer to patient preference against implantation, since intermediate scores make devices largely elective", "C": "These major markers support offering a defibrillator even though the score is intermediate", "D": "Repeat monitoring annually until the score itself crosses the high risk threshold", "E": "Treat with amiodarone first, reserving device discussion for breakthrough arrhythmia on therapy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (These major markers support offering a defibrillator even though the score is intermediate): apical aneurysm, repetitive nonsustained ventricular tachycardia, and first degree family sudden death are each guideline recognized markers that justify primary prevention implantation independent of a numeric score, and their combination strengthens the case. Risk calculators inform but do not override marker based judgment in American guidance. Amiodarone is not an accepted substitute for defibrillation in high risk hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."}, {"id": 757, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 66 year old woman presents nine years after mechanical aortic replacement with slowly progressive dyspnea over eighteen months. Her INR log shows consistent therapeutic values. Imaging reveals a small, dense, immobile circumferential ridge beneath the valve with elevated gradients. Which process best explains these findings, and why does timing matter?", "options": {"A": "Acute thrombosis, because mechanical valves accumulate thrombus fastest late after surgery", "B": "Sewing ring dehiscence, because chronic disc motion loosens annular sutures over a decade", "C": "Recurrent subclinical endocarditis, because indolent low virulence organisms slowly build fibrous tissue beneath mechanical valves", "D": "Pannus ingrowth, because fibrous tissue proliferates gradually over years despite adequate anticoagulation", "E": "Leaflet calcification, because mechanical discs mineralize along their hinge points with age"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pannus ingrowth, because fibrous tissue proliferates gradually over years despite adequate anticoagulation): pannus is a slow fibroblastic overgrowth from the periannular tissue that presents late, appears dense and immobile on imaging, and is unrelated to anticoagulation quality, matching every element here. Thrombosis favors early timing, anticoagulation gaps, and a mobile low density mass. Dehiscence produces paravalvular regurgitation rather than a subvalvular ridge, indolent infection would show vegetations or clinical stigmata, and pyrolytic carbon discs do not calcify."}, {"id": 761, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 73 year old pacemaker dependent woman reports dizzy spells when lifting groceries. Event monitoring during arm exercise shows long pauses with no pacing artifacts at all, and interrogation reveals high frequency noise on the ventricular channel during isometric arm maneuvers. Which mechanism best explains her symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Failure to capture caused by a chronically elevated ventricular pacing threshold", "B": "Normal rate hysteresis allowing the ventricular rate to fall by design", "C": "Myopotential or lead noise oversensing inhibiting pacemaker output during arm activity", "D": "Pacemaker mediated tachycardia initiated by retrograde atrial activation after ventricular beats", "E": "Magnet mode reversion producing asynchronous pacing at a fixed programmed rate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Myopotential or lead noise oversensing inhibiting pacemaker output during arm activity): absent pacing artifacts during pauses mean the device is withholding output because it senses signals it interprets as intrinsic beats, and reproducible high frequency noise with isometric exertion is classic for myopotential oversensing or a failing lead conductor. Failure to capture would still show delivered spikes. Hysteresis produces modest programmed slowing rather than symptomatic pauses, and both pacemaker mediated tachycardia and magnet reversion increase rather than abolish pacing."}, {"id": 763, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 59 year old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy reports worsening fatigue and new confusion. Blood pressure is 92/78 mm Hg, heart rate 112, and her hands are cold. Her family notes she has nearly stopped urinating since yesterday morning. Which bedside finding most strongly suggests a critically low cardiac output?", "options": {"A": "Proportional pulse pressure under one quarter of systolic value together with cool extremities", "B": "Sinus tachycardia at this rate, which commonly accompanies decompensated chronic systolic failure", "C": "Confusion, because cerebral symptoms track diuretic dosing more closely than organ perfusion", "D": "Jugular venous distention, which quantifies forward output when measured at forty five degrees", "E": "Cold hands considered alone, since peripheral temperature depends chiefly on ambient room conditions"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Proportional pulse pressure under one quarter of systolic value together with cool extremities): a pulse pressure of 14 over a systolic of 92 is roughly 15 percent, a validated marker of a severely reduced cardiac index. Tachycardia and jugular distention reflect sympathetic stress and filling pressure rather than forward flow. Cold hands and confusion support the picture, but they gain diagnostic weight when integrated with the narrow proportional pulse pressure and oliguria."}, {"id": 764, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 17 year old soccer player collapses during a sprint, waking within a minute. Resting electrocardiogram, echocardiogram, and corrected QT interval are normal. His sister drowned unexpectedly at age 15. During graded exercise testing he develops ventricular ectopy of alternating QRS axis at higher workloads. Which diagnosis best fits?", "options": {"A": "Vasovagal syncope provoked by exertional heat stress and dehydration during play", "B": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy missed by echocardiography and requiring cardiac magnetic resonance", "C": "Long QT syndrome type 1 with concealed resting repolarization abnormality", "D": "Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with characteristic bidirectional ectopy on exertion", "E": "Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy presenting with exercise induced ectopy of left bundle branch morphology"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with characteristic bidirectional ectopy on exertion): exertional syncope, a family history of youthful drowning, a completely normal resting evaluation, and exercise provoked ventricular ectopy with beat to beat axis alternation form the classic picture of this ryanodine receptor disease. Vasovagal collapse during peak sprinting, rather than after stopping, would be atypical. A normal QT argues against long QT syndrome, and both hypertrophic and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy usually show structural or electrocardiographic clues."}, {"id": 765, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 78 year old man with low flow, low gradient aortic stenosis and ejection fraction 25 percent undergoes dobutamine echocardiography. Stroke volume rises only 10 percent, and the gradient and valve area barely change, leaving severity unresolved. Which test most directly settles whether the valve itself is severely stenotic?", "options": {"A": "Noncontrast gated computed tomography quantifying aortic valve calcium against sex specific thresholds", "B": "Exercise treadmill testing with blood pressure monitoring to unmask latent valve symptoms", "C": "Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging focused on late gadolinium enhancement of the septum", "D": "Transesophageal echocardiography with direct planimetry of the valve orifice performed during a second dobutamine infusion", "E": "Right heart catheterization measuring pulmonary pressures during graded supine bicycle work"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Noncontrast gated computed tomography quantifying aortic valve calcium against sex specific thresholds): when contractile reserve is absent, flow cannot be augmented, so severity is judged from the calcium burden itself, with roughly 2000 Agatston units in men and 1200 in women indicating likely severe disease. Exercise testing is unsafe and uninformative in a symptomatic patient with ejection fraction 25 percent. Late gadolinium imaging characterizes fibrosis rather than orifice severity, and repeating flow dependent measurements by any route repeats the same limitation."}, {"id": 771, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 52 year old woman with acute severe aortic regurgitation from a torn leaflet is hypotensive with pulmonary edema while awaiting emergency surgery. A resident proposes intra aortic balloon counterpulsation for support during transfer. Why must this proposal be rejected in favor of alternative stabilization for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Balloon insertion delays transfer longer than any hemodynamic benefit could reasonably justify", "B": "Counterpulsation raises heart rate reflexively, which shortens systole and worsens forward output", "C": "Femoral access would conflict with the cannulation sites required by the surgical team", "D": "The device lowers coronary perfusion pressure during diastole, provoking myocardial ischemia in this particular hemodynamic setting", "E": "Diastolic balloon inflation raises aortic diastolic pressure, driving more blood backward across the incompetent valve"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Diastolic balloon inflation raises aortic diastolic pressure, driving more blood backward across the incompetent valve): counterpulsation augments diastolic aortic pressure, which is precisely the force propelling regurgitant flow, so the device magnifies the lesion it is meant to offset. Insertion time and access logistics are secondary practical matters rather than the physiologic contraindication. The device typically lowers heart rate demand rather than raising it, and it normally improves coronary diastolic perfusion, which is why it helps in mitral regurgitation but not here."}, {"id": 774, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "Six months after myocarditis, a 27 year old amateur triathlete returns seeking clearance to resume competition. Her ejection fraction has normalized and she feels well. Her nurse practitioner wonders what evaluation should precede clearance beyond the resting echocardiogram already obtained during this follow up visit. Which reassessment package is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Resting electrocardiography alone, since a normal tracing establishes electrical healing after myocarditis", "B": "Genetic testing for inherited cardiomyopathy variants, which determines return to play eligibility after an inflammatory injury", "C": "Coronary angiography, since post inflammatory coronary involvement drives most exercise related events", "D": "Repeat troponin monthly for a year, with clearance after twelve normal values", "E": "Ambulatory rhythm monitoring, exercise testing, and biomarkers, with imaging for residual inflammation or scar"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ambulatory rhythm monitoring, exercise testing, and biomarkers, with imaging for residual inflammation or scar): consensus guidance conditions return to competition on the whole package, absence of arrhythmia at rest and under exertional load, normalized injury markers, and recovered function, with magnetic resonance informing about residual scar that predicts arrhythmic events. A resting tracing or serial troponins alone sample too little of that risk. Genetic panels and angiography answer questions this clinical situation is not asking."}, {"id": 776, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "During effective decongestion of a 69 year old woman, creatinine rises from 1.3 to 1.6 mg/dL and the covering intern discontinues her sacubitril valsartan and spironolactone overnight, reasoning that kidney protection comes first. On rounds the attending disagrees. Which reasoning best supports restarting the neurohormonal therapy?", "options": {"A": "These agents are renally cleared, so kidney injury raises their levels into protective ranges", "B": "Stopping them was correct, but potassium binders should have been started simultaneously instead", "C": "Modest creatinine shifts during decongestion rarely justify withdrawing therapies that lengthen survival", "D": "Neurohormonal blockers improve the creatinine directly within days, offsetting any diuresis related rise in this measurement", "E": "The agents should be restarted at double dose to compensate for missed benefit"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Modest creatinine shifts during decongestion rarely justify withdrawing therapies that lengthen survival): small expected creatinine changes reflect hemodynamic adaptation and hemoconcentration, while reflexive discontinuation of disease modifying therapy is associated with worse long term outcomes and often never gets corrected after discharge. These drugs do not become protective at higher levels, and they do not acutely improve creatinine. Doubling doses to compensate has no basis, and potassium binders address a different problem than the one at hand."}, {"id": 777, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 74 year old man with ejection fraction 30 percent has aortic valve area 0.9 cm2 but a mean gradient of only 24 mm Hg. The heart failure clinic must decide whether his valve is truly severe before considering replacement. Which investigation directly answers that specific question?", "options": {"A": "Transesophageal echocardiography to reexamine leaflet anatomy under sedation with better imaging windows", "B": "Right heart catheterization to document wedge pressure and estimate his pulmonary resistance", "C": "Exercise treadmill testing to reproduce symptoms and correlate them with valve area", "D": "Low dose dobutamine echocardiography to test whether the gradient rises as flow increases", "E": "Repeat resting echocardiography in six months to observe the gradient's natural trajectory"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Low dose dobutamine echocardiography to test whether the gradient rises as flow increases): a weak ventricle may fail to generate a high gradient across a truly severe valve, and augmenting flow separates true severe stenosis, where the gradient rises while area stays small, from pseudosevere disease, where the area opens up. Anatomy review and wedge measurement do not resolve flow dependence. Waiting or treadmill testing in suspected severe stenosis with dysfunction delays a decision the stress study can make now."}, {"id": 779, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 34 year old man with Brugada syndrome and no prior events calls with a fever of 39.2 degrees Celsius from influenza. He feels otherwise well and lives an hour from the hospital. Beyond antiviral care, what is the most appropriate arrhythmia specific advice for him right now?", "options": {"A": "Fever is protective in Brugada syndrome, so antipyretics should be withheld today", "B": "Begin a beta blocker immediately because adrenergic surges during illness provoke events", "C": "Take a single dose of flecainide to suppress ventricular ectopy during the fever", "D": "Treat the fever aggressively with antipyretics and seek monitoring if it persists", "E": "Schedule an elective electrophysiology study once the influenza has fully resolved"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the fever aggressively with antipyretics and seek monitoring if it persists): fever accentuates the sodium channel dysfunction of Brugada syndrome and is a well documented trigger of ventricular fibrillation, so prompt antipyretic therapy and a low threshold for monitored observation are the core recommendations. Flecainide is a sodium channel blocker that can unmask or worsen the Brugada pattern and is used diagnostically, not therapeutically. Beta blockers do not protect in this disease, and an electrophysiology study does not address the immediate febrile risk."}, {"id": 781, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "Two days into diuresis, a 67 year old man becomes hypotensive at 84/60 mm Hg with new somnolence, urine output under 200 mL per shift, muddy brown casts on urine microscopy, and creatinine rising faster than before. Which feature set most reliably distinguishes true kidney injury from benign hemoconcentration here?", "options": {"A": "The absolute creatinine value, since injury begins reliably above two milligrams per deciliter", "B": "The blood urea to creatinine ratio, which separates these two states in most patients", "C": "The weight trend alone, because falling weight excludes tubular injury during active diuresis", "D": "The administered furosemide dose, since kidney injury occurs mainly above conventional ceiling doses of loop diuretics", "E": "Hypotension with oliguria and granular casts, showing hypoperfusion and tubular damage together"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Hypotension with oliguria and granular casts, showing hypoperfusion and tubular damage together): hemoconcentration occurs in patients who are clinically improving, whereas falling pressure, somnolence, near anuria, and muddy brown casts demonstrate acute tubular injury from hypoperfusion. No single creatinine cutoff separates the two states, and the urea to creatinine ratio is confounded by diuretics themselves. Weight loss and diuretic dose describe therapy intensity, not the kidney's condition."}, {"id": 782, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 45 year old man whose ejection fraction normalized on therapy insists on stopping everything at once, saying he researched the relapse statistics and accepts them. He asks his nurse practitioner to document the refusal and discharge him from clinic. What is the most constructive response consistent with shared decision making?", "options": {"A": "Discharge as requested, since documented informed refusal ends the clinician's role here", "B": "Refuse further prescriptions until he commits to lifelong therapy without further negotiation", "C": "Recommend stopping the diuretic and beta blocker together first as a compromise", "D": "Counsel him that relapse occurring after medication withdrawal is typically irreversible once the deterioration has occurred", "E": "Negotiate staged withdrawal, if he insists, with scheduled imaging, biomarkers, and rapid reinstitution criteria"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Negotiate staged withdrawal, if he insists, with scheduled imaging, biomarkers, and rapid reinstitution criteria): when a fully informed patient declines maintenance therapy, the safest achievable plan mirrors trial methodology, removing one agent at a time with surveillance echocardiography, natriuretic peptides, and predefined triggers to restart. Abandoning him or issuing ultimatums forfeits the chance to catch early relapse. Recovered ventricles that relapse frequently improve again with prompt reinstitution, so claiming universal irreversibility misrepresents the evidence."}, {"id": 784, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 45 year old man without known heart disease has a regular monomorphic wide complex tachycardia with a stable blood pressure. The team considers adenosine both diagnostically and therapeutically. Before giving it, which feature of the tachycardia would make adenosine clearly contraindicated in this specific setting?", "options": {"A": "A ventricular rate that remains constant despite carotid sinus massage attempts", "B": "A QRS duration longer than 140 milliseconds in the precordial leads", "C": "An irregularly irregular rhythm suggesting atrial fibrillation conducting over an accessory pathway", "D": "A history of seasonal allergic rhinitis treated with an oral antihistamine", "E": "A history of prior catheter ablation for typical atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia several years earlier"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An irregularly irregular rhythm suggesting atrial fibrillation conducting over an accessory pathway): adenosine is reasonable for a regular monomorphic wide complex tachycardia, but in preexcited atrial fibrillation blocking the atrioventricular node removes competing conduction and can accelerate pathway conduction into ventricular fibrillation. A constant rate during vagal maneuvers and a modestly prolonged QRS carry no such hazard. Allergic rhinitis and a remote nodal reentry ablation do not alter adenosine safety."}, {"id": 785, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 61 year old man with acute aortic dissection is started on intravenous antihypertensive therapy. A trainee asks why a beta blocker must be established before adding a vasodilator such as nitroprusside or nicardipine in this condition. Which explanation correctly states the physiologic rationale for that treatment sequence?", "options": {"A": "Vasodilators are ineffective at lowering systolic pressure until heart rate falls", "B": "Vasodilators alone trigger reflex tachycardia that raises aortic wall shear stress", "C": "Beta blockade prevents the cyanide toxicity associated with prolonged nitroprusside infusion", "D": "Beta blockade increases stroke volume, offsetting the venodilation the vasodilators produce", "E": "Combined therapy shortens intensive care stay by reducing total drug requirements"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Vasodilators alone trigger reflex tachycardia that raises aortic wall shear stress): Dropping arterial pressure with a pure vasodilator activates the baroreflex, and the resulting tachycardia and increased contractility steepen the rate of pressure rise with each ejection, the very force that propagates a dissection flap. Establishing beta blockade first blunts that reflex so pressure can then be lowered without increasing shear. Vasodilators do lower systolic pressure regardless of rate, cyanide accumulation relates to nitroprusside dose and duration rather than beta blockade, and stroke volume augmentation is neither true nor desirable here."}, {"id": 787, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A cardiology fellow asks why papillary muscle rupture complicates inferior infarction far more often than anterior infarction, even though anterior infarcts are usually larger. Which anatomic explanation correctly accounts for the vulnerability of the papillary muscle most often involved in post infarction rupture of the mitral apparatus?", "options": {"A": "The anterolateral papillary muscle sits nearer the ventricular apex, where wall stress concentrates following myocardial infarction", "B": "Inferior infarcts produce more transmural necrosis than anterior infarcts of comparable size", "C": "The chordae of the anterolateral muscle are thinner and tear before the muscle belly fails", "D": "The posteromedial muscle usually depends on a single arterial supply from the posterior descending artery", "E": "Right ventricular branches perfuse the posteromedial muscle and occlude earlier in inferior infarction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The posteromedial muscle usually depends on a single arterial supply from the posterior descending artery): the posteromedial papillary muscle typically has solitary perfusion from the posterior descending artery, so an inferior occlusion can infarct it completely, whereas the anterolateral muscle enjoys dual supply from diagonal and circumflex branches. Apex proximity and wall stress are not the operative factors. Inferior infarcts are not intrinsically more transmural, chordal caliber does not determine rupture site, and right ventricular branches are not the muscle's principal source."}, {"id": 789, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 78 year old woman with atrial fibrillation presents with a cold, painful right leg first noticed eighteen hours ago. Her examination findings are summarized in the exhibit shown. Applying the standard viability classification for acute limb ischemia to these documented findings, which management approach is most appropriate for her?", "options": {"A": "Emergent surgical embolectomy with fasciotomy to restore flow and decompress compartments", "B": "Catheter directed thrombolysis with staged bedside reassessment of motor and sensory function every four hours", "C": "Amputation planning, because restoring flow to a nonviable limb risks lethal reperfusion injury", "D": "Therapeutic anticoagulation alone with limb elevation and serial Doppler reassessment overnight", "E": "Intravenous vasodilator infusion to recruit collateral circulation before definitive imaging"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Amputation planning, because restoring flow to a nonviable limb risks lethal reperfusion injury): fixed mottling, rigid calf muscles, complete anesthesia and paralysis, and absent arterial plus venous Doppler signals define Rutherford category III, an irreversibly ischemic limb. Revascularizing dead muscle releases potassium, myoglobin, and acid, producing hyperkalemia, renal failure, and cardiovascular collapse, so embolectomy and thrombolysis are withheld and amputation is planned once she is stabilized. Anticoagulation alone and vasodilators address neither the nonviable tissue nor the systemic threat it poses.", "image": "question_images/q_789.png"}, {"id": 791, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 70 year old man with stage 3 kidney disease takes lisinopril and spironolactone for hypertension with reduced ejection fraction, and recently began daily ibuprofen for knee arthritis. Routine laboratory work now shows potassium 6.0 mmol/L without electrocardiographic changes. Which initial management best addresses the cause of this laboratory abnormality?", "options": {"A": "Stop lisinopril and continue the spironolactone, ibuprofen, and remaining medications", "B": "Admit for emergent dialysis given the degree of the potassium elevation", "C": "Add daily patiromer so his current medications can each be continued", "D": "Discontinue the ibuprofen, hold the spironolactone, and recheck potassium promptly", "E": "Restrict dietary potassium and repeat the panel at the next annual visit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discontinue the ibuprofen, hold the spironolactone, and recheck potassium promptly): The newest and most dispensable contributor is the nonsteroidal drug, which suppresses renin and renal perfusion, and pausing the aldosterone antagonist removes the second potassium retaining hit while the level is rechecked quickly. Lisinopril carries mortality benefit for his reduced ejection fraction, so it is the drug to preserve rather than the first to sacrifice. Dialysis is not indicated at 6.0 mmol/L without electrocardiographic change, binders paper over a reversible drug interaction, and waiting a year invites a lethal recurrence."}, {"id": 793, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 38 year old woman treated at age 24 with mediastinal radiation for Hodgkin lymphoma presents with exertional dyspnea. Her physician assistant considers the spectrum of late radiation heart disease before ordering tests. Which framing best captures why her evaluation must be broader than a standard echocardiogram?", "options": {"A": "Radiation injury affects pericardium, valves, coronaries, conduction tissue, and myocardium, often decades later", "B": "Late radiation effects remain confined to the pericardium, making pericardial constriction the sole diagnosis requiring exclusion", "C": "Cardiac injury from radiation appears within the first year or not at all", "D": "Modern dosimetry has eliminated late cardiac effects, so her symptoms must be pulmonary", "E": "Her age at treatment makes coronary involvement improbable compared with valvular scarring"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Radiation injury affects pericardium, valves, coronaries, conduction tissue, and myocardium, often decades later): mediastinal radiation produces a panoramic late cardiotoxicity including constrictive pericarditis, ostial and proximal coronary stenoses, aortic and mitral thickening, conduction disease, and restrictive myopathy, typically surfacing ten to thirty years after exposure, so her workup should screen several territories rather than one. Limiting concern to the pericardium or to the first post treatment year misses most of the disease burden. Younger age at irradiation raises rather than lowers long term coronary risk, and modern techniques reduce but do not abolish it."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 42 year old woman with confirmed vasospastic angina controlled on diltiazem asks whether she can resume sumatriptan, which reliably aborts her migraines. She has no obstructive coronary disease. Which response reflects the safest reasoning about this medication request in a patient with documented coronary spasm?", "options": {"A": "Sumatriptan is acceptable because migraine and coronary spasm involve unrelated receptor systems", "B": "Triptans cause vasoconstriction and can provoke coronary spasm, so alternatives are preferred", "C": "Sumatriptan is safe if she takes it two hours or more after diltiazem", "D": "The risk applies to injectable formulations, while the oral tablets remain acceptable", "E": "Her diltiazem provides sufficient protection to neutralize any triptan induced coronary spasm"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Triptans cause vasoconstriction and can provoke coronary spasm, so alternatives are preferred): triptans are serotonin receptor agonists that constrict coronary arteries and are contraindicated in vasospastic angina because they can trigger spasm and infarction. The receptor systems are related rather than independent, spacing doses around diltiazem does not remove the mechanism, the warning covers oral as well as injectable forms, and background calcium channel blockade cannot be assumed protective. Nonvasoconstrictive migraine options such as gepants are preferable for her."}, {"id": 797, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 54 year old man with dilated cardiomyopathy is cold and wet with cardiac index 1.8 L/min/m2, wedge pressure 26 mm Hg, systemic vascular resistance 2100 dynes, and mean arterial pressure 78 mm Hg. The team wants to improve output without inotropes if possible. Which strategy fits these hemodynamics best?", "options": {"A": "Normal saline challenge, since forward output usually rises with additional preload here", "B": "Vasopressin infusion, since raising resistance further supports coronary perfusion during low output", "C": "Careful intravenous vasodilator therapy to unload the ventricle while pressure remains adequate", "D": "Immediate intraaortic balloon placement, since pharmacologic afterload reduction is contraindicated here", "E": "Beta blocker uptitration now, since slowing the rate lengthens diastolic filling time"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Careful intravenous vasodilator therapy to unload the ventricle while pressure remains adequate): with intense vasoconstriction, high filling pressure, and a preserved mean arterial pressure, afterload reduction with an agent such as nitroprusside lets the failing ventricle eject more at lower wall stress, often raising output without inotropic exposure. Volume would worsen a wedge of 26, and vasopressin raises the resistance that is already strangling output. Mechanical support is premature, and acute beta blocker uptitration depresses contractility in decompensation."}, {"id": 798, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 40 year old woman with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension had a negative acute vasoreactivity test, yet her outside clinician started amlodipine as pulmonary therapy, reasoning that calcium channel blockers help some patients with her diagnosis. Why is this reasoning flawed for her specifically?", "options": {"A": "Amlodipine fails in this circumstance only because dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers require twice daily dosing schedules", "B": "Calcium channel blockers work in this disease regardless of testing, so the choice is sound", "C": "Without a positive vasoreactivity response, calcium channel blockade offers no benefit and risks hemodynamic compromise", "D": "The drug class is appropriate, but verapamil is the required first line agent", "E": "Her test should simply be repeated until a positive result eventually appears"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Without a positive vasoreactivity response, calcium channel blockade offers no benefit and risks hemodynamic compromise): the benefit of calcium channel blockers is confined to the small minority with an acute positive response, and in nonresponders these agents lower systemic pressure and contractility without dilating the remodeled pulmonary bed, which can precipitate right heart decompensation, so she needs approved pulmonary arterial hypertension therapy instead. The class does not work irrespective of testing. Verapamil is actually avoided for its negative inotropy, dosing frequency is irrelevant to the failure, and repeating a negative test until it turns positive misunderstands what the test measures."}, {"id": 802, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 52 year old woman presents with a definite non ST elevation infarction confirmed by a clear troponin rise and fall. Coronary angiography shows smooth arteries without any stenosis above 30 percent. Echocardiography shows mildly reduced function without a pericardial effusion. What is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?", "options": {"A": "Discharge with reassurance since the coronary arteries are essentially normal", "B": "Repeat coronary angiography in one month to reassess the arteries", "C": "Begin empiric heart failure therapy without any further diagnostic testing", "D": "Obtain cardiac magnetic resonance imaging to distinguish true infarction from myocarditis", "E": "Perform endomyocardial biopsy promptly as the immediate first tissue based diagnostic investigation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Obtain cardiac magnetic resonance imaging to distinguish true infarction from myocarditis): Infarction with nonobstructive coronary arteries is a working diagnosis rather than a final one, and magnetic resonance identifies the actual cause in most patients by separating subendocardial infarction from myocarditis and stress cardiomyopathy. Reassurance and discharge abandon the search while the mechanism, and therefore the therapy, remains unknown. Repeating the same angiogram adds little new information, empiric heart failure treatment skips the diagnosis that should direct it, and biopsy is reserved for suspected specific myocardial diseases after noninvasive imaging."}, {"id": 806, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 36 year old woman's blood pressure has risen from 118/74 to 148/94 mm Hg over three months. She recently began daily naproxen for back pain, uses a pseudoephedrine decongestant most mornings, and continues a combined oral contraceptive. Her examination and basic laboratory studies are normal. What is the best initial management?", "options": {"A": "Withdraw or substitute the pressor medications and remeasure before antihypertensive therapy", "B": "Start amlodipine now since three separate agents cannot realistically be stopped", "C": "Order aldosterone and renin testing before making any medication adjustments", "D": "Begin lisinopril while continuing the current analgesic and contraceptive regimen", "E": "Refer for twenty four hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring while all current medications continue unchanged"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withdraw or substitute the pressor medications and remeasure before antihypertensive therapy): She is taking three recognized pressor exposures at once, a nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drug promoting sodium retention, a sympathomimetic decongestant, and estrogen containing contraception, and a new rise coinciding with them makes drug induced hypertension the leading explanation. Removing or replacing the culprits and remeasuring may resolve the problem without lifelong therapy. Starting an antihypertensive against ongoing pressor drugs treats the wrong lever, secondary hormonal testing is premature before this reversible cause is addressed, and monitoring alone lets an identified exposure continue."}, {"id": 809, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 76 year old woman on warfarin for atrial fibrillation receives a drug eluting stent. Her time in therapeutic range has been 48 percent over the past year and she lives ninety minutes from the nearest laboratory for monitoring. Which anticoagulation adjustment most reduces her bleeding hazard going forward?", "options": {"A": "Continue warfarin but tighten the target range around 2.5 exactly", "B": "Transition to a direct oral anticoagulant to lower the bleeding hazard", "C": "Stop anticoagulation entirely while she completes her antiplatelet course", "D": "Add low dose aspirin permanently to stabilize her fluctuating anticoagulation", "E": "Reduce the warfarin dose by half during the antiplatelet treatment period"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Transition to a direct oral anticoagulant to lower the bleeding hazard): Every major trial of anticoagulated patients undergoing stenting showed less bleeding with direct agents than with warfarin based regimens, and her poor time in therapeutic range plus monitoring barriers make warfarin specifically unsuitable. Tightening the target range does not fix the control problem her circumstances create. Stopping anticoagulation exposes her to stroke that antiplatelet agents cannot prevent, permanent aspirin addition increases bleeding rather than stabilizing anything, and arbitrary dose halving of warfarin produces subtherapeutic levels without protecting her."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 42 year old woman asks whether the preeclampsia she had at 31 weeks in her only pregnancy still matters a decade later, since her blood pressure today is normal. How should her cardiology nurse practitioner frame the long term significance of that obstetric history?", "options": {"A": "Once postpartum pressures normalize, prior preeclampsia carries no residual cardiovascular meaning", "B": "The history matters mainly for kidney stones, which preeclampsia strongly predicts", "C": "Hypertensive pregnancy disorders roughly double lifetime cardiovascular risk, so she merits ongoing risk factor surveillance", "D": "Risk applies exclusively to future pregnancies, so a woman past childbearing needs nothing further", "E": "Her residual risk remains confined to venous thromboembolism, making arterial cardiovascular screening clinically unnecessary going forward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hypertensive pregnancy disorders roughly double lifetime cardiovascular risk, so she merits ongoing risk factor surveillance): preeclampsia is now recognized as a sex specific risk enhancer that approximately doubles later hypertension, ischemic heart disease, stroke, and heart failure, so guidelines advise eliciting this history and maintaining regular blood pressure, lipid, and glucose assessment for decades. Normal pressures today lower but do not erase that trajectory. The excess risk is arterial as well as thrombotic, extends far beyond future pregnancies, and has no established link to kidney stones."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A trainee asks why a colleague declined to close a large atrial septal defect in a cyanotic patient whose pulmonary vascular resistance approached systemic levels, arguing that the defect is the underlying disease. Which explanation best conveys why closure would be harmful in this hemodynamic situation?", "options": {"A": "The defect vents a failing right heart, and closure removes that decompression pathway", "B": "Device placement mechanically irritates the septum and commonly triggers refractory arrhythmias", "C": "Closure abruptly raises left ventricular preload beyond what the ventricle tolerates", "D": "Prosthetic material seeds infection readily in patients with chronically low saturations", "E": "Residual leaks around devices reopen frequently in patients with elevated pressures"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The defect vents a failing right heart, and closure removes that decompression pathway): With pulmonary resistance near systemic levels, the right ventricle depends on the shunt to unload itself, accepting cyanosis as the price of maintaining cardiac output. Sealing the defect forces the entire venous return through a diseased pulmonary bed and can precipitate acute right heart failure. Arrhythmia, infection, and device leak concerns are secondary to this fundamental physiologic objection."}, {"id": 815, "domain": "normal_cardiovascular_anatomy_and_physiology", "question": "A 21 year old endurance cyclist is referred for resting heart rate 44, sinus arrhythmia, early repolarization, and isolated QRS voltage meeting hypertrophy criteria. He is asymptomatic with normal examination and family history. Which management conclusion best reflects the physiology of the trained athlete's heart?", "options": {"A": "Order an electrophysiology study because bradycardia below 50 requires investigation", "B": "Begin a beta blocker to protect against bradycardia related pauses overnight", "C": "Restrict him from cycling until his resting heart rate normalizes", "D": "Refer for permanent pacemaker evaluation given the combination of electrocardiographic findings described here", "E": "Reassurance, since isolated voltage criteria and early repolarization are training related findings"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Reassurance, since isolated voltage criteria and early repolarization are training related findings): endurance conditioning raises vagal tone and produces sinus bradycardia, sinus arrhythmia, early repolarization, and increased QRS voltage from physiologic chamber enlargement, all recognized normal variants in an asymptomatic athlete with a benign history. An electrophysiology study and pacemaker referral pathologize expected adaptation. Beta blockade would deepen the bradycardia it claims to treat, and restricting sport until the heart rate rises misreads a healthy training effect as disease."}, {"id": 816, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 57 year old man with typical exertional angina undergoes treadmill testing. At four minutes he develops 2 mm ST elevation in leads without prior Q waves, and his systolic pressure falls from 142 to 108 mm Hg with continued exercise. The test is stopped. What should happen next?", "options": {"A": "Schedule outpatient stress imaging within several weeks to better localize the ischemic myocardial territory", "B": "Intensify antianginal medications and repeat the treadmill in three months", "C": "Refer promptly for coronary angiography because these responses indicate severe proximal disease", "D": "Reassure him because ST elevation during exercise reflects early repolarization physiology", "E": "Order computed tomography calcium scoring to refine his overall risk estimate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer promptly for coronary angiography because these responses indicate severe proximal disease): Exercise induced ST elevation in leads without infarct Q waves signals transmural ischemia from a critical proximal stenosis or spasm, and a falling blood pressure with continuing workload means the ischemic ventricle cannot sustain output, together among the highest risk treadmill findings. Deferring to outpatient imaging or a medication trial with repeat testing leaves anatomy this dangerous undefined. Attributing exertional ST elevation with hypotension to benign repolarization misreads a provoked change, and calcium scoring quantifies plaque burden rather than the flow limiting lesion already demonstrated."}, {"id": 817, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "An assist device patient reports recurrent brief dizzy spells with device alarms showing transient low flow, each episode following diuretic dose increases during a hot week. His clinic Doppler pressure is 72 mm Hg and mucous membranes look dry. Which mechanism best explains these events and the initial response?", "options": {"A": "Pump thrombosis intermittently obstructing flow, requiring immediate thrombolysis in the clinic setting", "B": "Driveline fracture causing electrical dropout, requiring urgent operative driveline repair this week", "C": "Suction events from an underfilled ventricle, addressed with volume and device team speed adjustment", "D": "Aortic insufficiency recirculating output, explaining alarms that fluid administration would clearly worsen", "E": "Arrhythmic collapse from ventricular tachycardia, requiring empiric amiodarone before any rhythm documentation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Suction events from an underfilled ventricle, addressed with volume and device team speed adjustment): overdiuresis and heat losses shrink preload until the inflow cannula transiently pulls against the ventricular wall, producing low flow alarms and dizziness, so cautious volume repletion, diuretic reduction, and coordinated speed reduction are the response. Pump thrombosis produces power rises and hemolysis rather than positional brief alarms. A fractured driveline shows electrical faults, insufficiency causes chronic underperfusion, and treating undocumented arrhythmia is premature."}, {"id": 820, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 74 year old woman reports two weeks of temporal headache and jaw fatigue with chewing, and this morning she experienced ten minutes of transient monocular vision loss. Her laboratory results are shown. Considering the diagnosis these findings support, what is the most appropriate immediate management step for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Schedule a temporal artery biopsy within one week and treat based on the histology results obtained", "B": "Order magnetic resonance angiography of the head before starting any therapy", "C": "Give high dose intravenous methylprednisolone now, before biopsy, to protect her vision", "D": "Start aspirin and a moderate oral prednisone taper pending ophthalmology consultation", "E": "Begin tocilizumab monotherapy to avoid glucocorticoid exposure in an older patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give high dose intravenous methylprednisolone now, before biopsy, to protect her vision): cranial symptoms with markedly elevated inflammatory markers, anemia, and thrombocytosis indicate giant cell arteritis, and transient monocular vision loss signals impending permanent blindness, so high dose intravenous methylprednisolone is given immediately without waiting for biopsy. Delaying treatment for histology or vascular imaging risks irreversible visual loss, since biopsy findings persist for about two weeks after therapy begins. A moderate oral taper is inadequate for ocular involvement, and tocilizumab supports remission maintenance rather than emergency vision rescue.", "image": "question_images/q_820.png"}, {"id": 823, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 76 year old man with atrial fibrillation and a CHA2DS2 VASc score of 6 suffered a lobar intracerebral hemorrhage six weeks ago, attributed on imaging to probable cerebral amyloid angiopathy. His ischemic stroke risk remains high. Which strategy best manages his competing risks going forward?", "options": {"A": "Resume full dose anticoagulation immediately because six weeks of healing is sufficient", "B": "Start dual antiplatelet therapy as a safer equivalent to oral anticoagulation", "C": "Resume warfarin therapy with a lower target international normalized ratio range to limit bleeding risk", "D": "Evaluate for percutaneous left atrial appendage occlusion given high recurrent hemorrhage risk", "E": "Defer all stroke prevention decisions until another hemorrhage occurs or five years pass"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Evaluate for percutaneous left atrial appendage occlusion given high recurrent hemorrhage risk): lobar hemorrhage from probable amyloid angiopathy carries one of the highest recurrence rates of any bleeding source, making long term anticoagulation especially hazardous, and appendage occlusion offers stroke protection without chronic anticoagulant exposure. Early resumption of full dose therapy invites rebleeding in this substrate. Dual antiplatelet therapy still raises intracranial bleeding risk while protecting poorly against cardioembolism, and a lowered warfarin target sacrifices efficacy without proven safety."}, {"id": 824, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 46 year old woman has dyspnea and an echocardiogram with a tricuspid regurgitant velocity of 3.1 m/s, a mildly dilated right atrium, and normal ventricular function, an intermediate probability result. Her primary clinician wants to start sildenafil empirically rather than investigate further. What is the strongest objection to this plan?", "options": {"A": "Sildenafil requires renal dose adjustment that outpatient settings cannot reliably manage", "B": "Her velocity is high enough that therapy should wait for symptoms to worsen", "C": "Intermediate probability echocardiographic results resolve spontaneously over time in the large majority of these patients", "D": "Empiric therapy without hemodynamic confirmation may treat the wrong disease, since other causes dominate", "E": "The objection is purely financial, since generic sildenafil is otherwise appropriate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Empiric therapy without hemodynamic confirmation may treat the wrong disease, since other causes dominate): most pulmonary hypertension is group 2 or group 3, where phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibition lacks benefit and can worsen congestion or gas exchange, so an intermediate probability echo should trigger workup for left heart disease, lung disease, and chronic thromboembolism, with right heart catheterization before any targeted therapy. Waiting for deterioration is not the alternative, since the correct move is investigation now. Renal dosing and cost are minor issues beside prescribing a vasodilator for an unconfirmed diagnosis."}, {"id": 825, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 31 year old woman has recurrent episodes of very rapid irregular palpitations. During an episode, the emergency physician documents an irregular tachycardia with QRS complexes that change width from beat to beat and shortest RR intervals under 250 milliseconds. Which underlying diagnosis best explains this combination of findings?", "options": {"A": "Atrial fibrillation with rate related left bundle branch block aberrancy throughout", "B": "Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia triggered by recurrent early afterdepolarizations during adrenergic stress", "C": "Multifocal atrial tachycardia conducting variably through a diseased atrioventricular node", "D": "Atrial flutter with alternating two to one and four to one conduction", "E": "Atrial fibrillation conducting over an accessory pathway with varying degrees of preexcitation"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Atrial fibrillation conducting over an accessory pathway with varying degrees of preexcitation): the irregular rhythm identifies fibrillation, the beat to beat change in QRS width reflects shifting fusion between pathway and nodal conduction, and extremely short RR intervals show the pathway has a short refractory period. Fixed aberrancy would produce a uniform wide morphology rather than variable width. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia rarely presents as recurrent self terminating palpitations over years, and flutter with alternating block produces a patterned rather than chaotic irregularity."}, {"id": 829, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 31 year old woman with a mechanical mitral valve well controlled on warfarin 4 mg daily is newly pregnant at six weeks. After counseling on fetal and maternal risks, she asks which strategy carries the lowest overall risk of valve thrombosis while remaining acceptable for the fetus. What should she be told?", "options": {"A": "Continuing low dose warfarin is a reasonable option, since embryopathy risk is dose dependent", "B": "Switching to a direct oral anticoagulant offers equivalent valve protection with simpler monitoring", "C": "Fixed dose subcutaneous heparin twice daily protects the valve as reliably as warfarin", "D": "Aspirin alone during the first trimester bridges safely until warfarin resumes later", "E": "Stopping anticoagulation until twelve weeks is acceptable because pregnancy inhibits clotting factors"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continuing low dose warfarin is a reasonable option, since embryopathy risk is dose dependent): warfarin is the most effective agent against mechanical valve thrombosis, and at daily doses of 5 mg or less the fetal embryopathy risk is low enough that continuation is a guideline endorsed option after shared decision making. Direct oral anticoagulants are contraindicated with mechanical valves. Fixed dose unmonitored heparin has an unacceptable thrombosis record, aspirin alone provides no adequate protection, and pregnancy is prothrombotic rather than protective."}, {"id": 831, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 55 year old asymptomatic man has severe mitral regurgitation from a flail posterior leaflet, ejection fraction 63 percent, left ventricular end systolic diameter 38 mm, sinus rhythm, and normal pulmonary pressures. A primary valve center quotes repair likelihood above 95 percent with mortality under 1 percent. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Early surgical repair now, since durable repair at negligible risk preempts irreversible ventricular injury", "B": "Annual surveillance echocardiography until symptoms develop, because clinical outcomes after delayed repair are identical", "C": "Transcatheter edge to edge therapy, because it defers sternotomy while controlling regurgitation", "D": "Beta blocker therapy to slow remodeling, with surgery reserved for ejection fraction decline", "E": "Exercise stress testing every five years to detect latent symptoms before intervening"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Early surgical repair now, since durable repair at negligible risk preempts irreversible ventricular injury): with a flail leaflet, severe regurgitation, and repair essentially assured at a primary valve center, early operation is reasonable even before symptoms or triggers appear, because waiting risks atrial fibrillation, pulmonary hypertension, and occult ventricular decline. Watchful waiting is acceptable only with vigilant surveillance and loses ground if follow up lapses. Edge to edge devices are less durable than surgical repair in a good operative candidate, medical therapy does not treat primary regurgitation, and five year intervals are far too sparse."}, {"id": 832, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 70 year old man with acute decompensated heart failure and baseline creatinine 1.4 mg/dL receives intravenous diuresis. On day three, creatinine is 1.8 mg/dL while he has lost five kilograms, breathes comfortably, and his jugular venous pressure has fallen. The team debates halting diuretics. What is the soundest interpretation?", "options": {"A": "The creatinine rise signals renal injury from overdiuresis, so fluids should be given back", "B": "Modest creatinine rises during effective decongestion often reflect hemoconcentration and need not stop diuresis", "C": "Dialysis planning should begin now because cardiorenal physiology has become irreversible", "D": "Diuretic therapy must stop until the creatinine concentration returns fully to baseline before resuming decongestive treatment", "E": "The kidney findings prove the dyspnea was pulmonary in origin from the start"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Modest creatinine rises during effective decongestion often reflect hemoconcentration and need not stop diuresis): in decompensated heart failure, relieving venous congestion improves the physiology that injures the kidney, and trial analyses show small creatinine bumps during successful decongestion do not predict worse outcomes, so therapy should continue toward euvolemia guided by examination. Reflexively giving fluid back recongests the patient and undoes the gains. Nothing here suggests irreversible renal failure requiring dialysis, and improving jugular pressure with weight loss confirms the cardiac origin of his symptoms."}, {"id": 835, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 70 year old woman has severe symptomatic aortic stenosis and moderate secondary mitral regurgitation with a structurally normal mitral valve, ejection fraction 45 percent, and no mitral annular calcification. The heart team debates a double valve procedure. Which reasoning should guide the approach to her mitral valve?", "options": {"A": "Replace both valves during a single operation, since a second operation carries prohibitive combined operative mortality", "B": "Repair the mitral valve first, because regurgitation contributes more to symptoms than stenosis", "C": "Perform transcatheter mitral repair before any aortic procedure to test symptomatic response", "D": "Decline treating either valve until the ejection fraction recovers above fifty percent", "E": "Treat the aortic stenosis alone first, expecting secondary mitral regurgitation to lessen once afterload falls"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Treat the aortic stenosis alone first, expecting secondary mitral regurgitation to lessen once afterload falls): relieving aortic obstruction lowers ventricular systolic pressure and remodeling stress, and moderate secondary mitral regurgitation with a structurally normal valve frequently improves afterward, so staged reassessment avoids an unnecessary second prosthesis. Prophylactic double valve surgery adds operative risk for a lesion that may resolve. The stenotic valve is the primary symptomatic driver, mitral first sequencing leaves the dominant lesion untreated, and waiting for ejection fraction recovery inverts cause and effect."}, {"id": 839, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 31 year old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy faints near the end of a recreational soccer match. Resting echocardiography shows a left ventricular outflow gradient of 20 mm Hg, and his clinician calls the syncope unrelated because the gradient seems trivial. Which physiologic flaw undermines that reassurance most directly?", "options": {"A": "Outflow obstruction is dynamic, so exertion can provoke severe gradients a resting study misses", "B": "Resting gradients above fifteen already define severe obstruction requiring septal reduction therapy", "C": "Syncope in this disease arises chiefly from arrhythmia, making gradient measurements uninformative", "D": "Echocardiographic gradients underestimate catheter gradients, so the true resting value was severe", "E": "Exertional syncope reflects dehydration in athletes, so electrolyte replacement resolves the concern"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Outflow obstruction is dynamic, so exertion can provoke severe gradients a resting study misses): obstruction in this disease intensifies with catecholamines, reduced preload, and vasodilation, so a trivial resting gradient can exceed 50 mm Hg during exercise, and provocative or exercise echocardiography is required before dismissing exertional syncope. Arrhythmia also matters, but the claim that gradient measurements are uninformative is exactly backwards for exertional events. Neither a fixed severity threshold of fifteen nor systematic echocardiographic underestimation reflects real measurement behavior."}, {"id": 845, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 59 year old man received a drug eluting stent in the left anterior descending artery two weeks ago. He stopped ticagrelor five days ago because of cost and now presents with an abrupt anterior ST elevation infarction. Which mechanism most likely explains this presentation and its timing?", "options": {"A": "Acute thrombosis of the stent precipitated by premature antiplatelet therapy discontinuation", "B": "Gradual neointimal tissue proliferation progressively narrowing the stent lumen over the past two weeks", "C": "Progression of atherosclerosis in a separate segment of the same artery", "D": "Coronary vasospasm at the stent edges triggered by medication withdrawal stress", "E": "Embolization of atrial thrombus into the previously treated coronary artery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Acute thrombosis of the stent precipitated by premature antiplatelet therapy discontinuation): An abrupt ST elevation event within weeks of stenting, immediately after stopping a P2Y12 inhibitor, is stent thrombosis until proven otherwise, because the struts remain unendothelialized and fully thrombogenic this early. Neointimal proliferation takes months and presents as gradual exertional symptoms rather than sudden occlusion. De novo disease progression and edge vasospasm rarely produce infarction on this compressed timeline, and atrial embolization requires an arrhythmic substrate this vignette never establishes."}, {"id": 849, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 48 year old man has 40 minutes of severe chest pain with diaphoresis. The exhibit shown summarizes his electrocardiogram and current symptoms. The triage algorithm has classified him as a non ST elevation case scheduled for delayed angiography. Which vessel status does the exhibit most strongly indicate instead?", "options": {"A": "Subtotal circumflex occlusion producing an isolated posterior wall injury current pattern", "B": "Diffuse subendocardial ischemia from demand imbalance during a hypertensive urgency episode", "C": "Pericardial inflammation producing widespread repolarization changes without any vessel occlusion", "D": "A patent left anterior descending artery with severe downstream microvascular obstruction", "E": "Acute occlusion of the proximal left anterior descending artery requiring immediate reperfusion"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Acute occlusion of the proximal left anterior descending artery requiring immediate reperfusion): upsloping precordial ST depression with tall symmetric T waves and ongoing pain is the de Winter pattern, indicating acute proximal left anterior descending occlusion that needs immediate reperfusion rather than delayed angiography. Posterior injury from the circumflex shows horizontal depression in V1 to V3 with dominant R waves, demand related subendocardial ischemia lacks the hyperacute T morphology, and pericarditis causes diffuse elevation. A patent artery with microvascular obstruction is a post reperfusion phenomenon.", "image": "question_images/q_849.png"}, {"id": 850, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 20 year old woman presents with abrupt palpitations at 210 beats per minute that terminate with vagal maneuvers. Baseline electrocardiography shows a short PR interval with delta waves, and echocardiography reveals apical displacement of the septal tricuspid leaflet. How should the relationship between her findings be understood?", "options": {"A": "The valve displacement stretches the sinus node and produces inappropriate tachycardia", "B": "Two unrelated conditions coincide, warranting separate evaluations and independent treatment plans", "C": "Chronic tricuspid regurgitation caused atrial dilation that now sustains atrial flutter", "D": "The delta waves represent a normal variant frequently seen in young athletes", "E": "Her congenital valve malformation predisposes to accessory pathways causing reentrant tachycardia"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Her congenital valve malformation predisposes to accessory pathways causing reentrant tachycardia): Apical displacement of the septal tricuspid leaflet defines Ebstein anomaly, and the malformed atrioventricular junction leaves muscular connections that function as accessory pathways. Preexcitation with paroxysmal reentrant tachycardia is therefore an expected part of the same disease rather than coincidence. Sinus node stretch and normal variant explanations ignore this established association."}, {"id": 854, "domain": "systemic_disorders_affecting_the_circulatory_system", "question": "A 32 year old woman with no cardiac history presents with exertional presyncope and is found to have Mobitz II second degree atrioventricular block. Chest imaging shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. Her physician assistant considers the unifying diagnosis. Why does this combination demand urgent evaluation rather than simple pacemaker referral?", "options": {"A": "Conduction disease in the young is usually congenital, so family screening takes priority over imaging", "B": "Hilar adenopathy indicates underlying lymphoma in most cases, making immediate oncology referral the appropriate next step", "C": "Cardiac sarcoidosis may underlie the block, and it carries ventricular arrhythmia risk that alters device choice", "D": "The block will typically resolve with corticosteroids, so no device evaluation is ever pursued", "E": "Young patients tolerate high grade block well, so outpatient follow up in three months suffices"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cardiac sarcoidosis may underlie the block, and it carries ventricular arrhythmia risk that alters device choice): unexplained high grade atrioventricular block in an adult under sixty with hilar adenopathy should prompt evaluation for sarcoidosis with advanced imaging such as cardiac magnetic resonance or positron emission tomography, because granulomatous myocardium also generates ventricular tachycardia and often justifies a defibrillator rather than a simple pacemaker. Immunosuppression may help but does not reliably abolish the arrhythmic substrate, so device deferral on steroids alone is unsafe. Assuming congenital disease or lymphoma skips the cardiac workup this pairing requires, and high grade block with presyncope is never a three month outpatient problem."}, {"id": 855, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 78 year old woman with sepsis and poor oral intake has a moderate pericardial effusion, right atrial collapse on echocardiography, and a measured right atrial pressure of only 6 mmHg. A colleague argues tamponade is impossible at such a low filling pressure. Which physiologic rebuttal is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Right atrial pressure measurements are unreliable whenever a pericardial effusion is present", "B": "Sepsis induced vasodilation independently produces chamber collapse without pericardial involvement", "C": "Collapse at low pressure implies the transducer was zeroed incorrectly beforehand", "D": "Tamponade requires intrapericardial pressure above 15 mmHg independent of volume status", "E": "Reduced intravascular volume lets modest pericardial pressure equal diastolic filling pressures"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Reduced intravascular volume lets modest pericardial pressure equal diastolic filling pressures): tamponade physiology depends on equalization between intrapericardial and cardiac diastolic pressures, not on any absolute threshold, so a hypovolemic patient with low filling pressures can be compressed by an effusion generating only mild intrapericardial pressure, the entity called low pressure tamponade. No fixed 15 mmHg requirement exists. Effusions do not invalidate atrial pressure measurement, vasodilation does not cause anatomic chamber collapse, and blaming transducer zeroing dismisses a coherent physiologic picture."}, {"id": 857, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "Two months after drug eluting stent placement, a 61 year old woman reports frequent forearm bruising and occasional gum bleeding when brushing, without melena, hematuria, or hemoglobin change. She asks whether to stop one of her antiplatelet medications. What is the most appropriate response to this concern?", "options": {"A": "Stop aspirin immediately since bruising signals dangerous bleeding ahead", "B": "Stop the P2Y12 inhibitor and continue aspirin alone from now on", "C": "Halve the doses of both agents to reduce the bruising tendency", "D": "Reassure her, review interacting medications, and continue both antiplatelet agents", "E": "Admit her to the hospital for observation with serial hemoglobin measurements over several inpatient days"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reassure her, review interacting medications, and continue both antiplatelet agents): Bruising and minor gum bleeding are nuisance events without hemodynamic or hemoglobin significance, and interrupting therapy two months after stenting for them exposes her to thrombosis risk that far exceeds their harm. Stopping either agent this early converts trivial bleeding into a meaningful ischemic gamble. Dose halving has no evidence base and undermines both drugs, and hospital admission for stable nuisance symptoms consumes resources without changing anything."}, {"id": 858, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 68 year old man with chronic angina reads that ranolazine improved his symptoms and asks whether it also protects him from heart attack and death, wondering aloud whether other preventive drugs could be stopped. Which response most accurately characterizes the outcome evidence for ranolazine in coronary disease?", "options": {"A": "Ranolazine reduces angina frequency but has not reduced death or myocardial infarction", "B": "Ranolazine lowered mortality in large trials, so his statin can be reconsidered", "C": "Ranolazine prevents infarction specifically in diabetic patients through its metabolic effects", "D": "Ranolazine matches beta blockers for event reduction when dosed at the maximum", "E": "Outcome data are lacking because ranolazine has been studied purely in animals"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ranolazine reduces angina frequency but has not reduced death or myocardial infarction): in the large randomized experience ranolazine lessened angina and improved exercise tolerance but did not reduce death or myocardial infarction, so preventive therapy continues unchanged. No mortality benefit exists to justify statin withdrawal, the diabetic subgroup showed improved glycemic markers rather than fewer infarctions, and event reduction comparable to beta blockade has not been demonstrated. Extensive human outcome trials exist, so missing animal data is not the explanation."}, {"id": 862, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "An 81 year old man with a dual chamber pacemaker for complete heart block presents with recurrent presyncope. Telemetry shows pacing artifacts that arrive on time but are intermittently followed by no ventricular depolarization. Interrogation shows a rising ventricular threshold over recent weeks. What is the most likely explanation?", "options": {"A": "Oversensing of skeletal muscle potentials inhibiting delivery of scheduled pacing stimuli", "B": "Battery depletion causing the generator to stop delivering any pacing output", "C": "Crosstalk between atrial and ventricular channels causing safety pacing at short intervals", "D": "Failure to capture from an elevated threshold at the lead myocardium interface", "E": "Pacemaker mediated tachycardia sustained by retrograde conduction through the atrioventricular node"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Failure to capture from an elevated threshold at the lead myocardium interface): pacing spikes delivered on schedule without a following QRS define failure to capture, and a progressively rising threshold points to problems at the lead tissue interface such as microdislodgment, fibrosis, or exit block. Oversensing inhibits output, so no spike would appear at all. Battery depletion and crosstalk alter timing or output delivery rather than producing on time spikes that fail, and pacemaker mediated tachycardia produces a paced tachycardia rather than dropped beats."}, {"id": 865, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 79 year old man with atrial fibrillation and a CHA2DS2 VASc score of 5 suffered a diverticular bleed on apixaban four months ago, treated endoscopically without recurrence. His family fears resuming any anticoagulant. Which recommendation best reflects the balance of stroke and bleeding risk?", "options": {"A": "Substitute daily aspirin, which protects against stroke with far less bleeding risk", "B": "Resume warfarin specifically, since vitamin K antagonists cause the least gastrointestinal bleeding", "C": "Resume a direct oral anticoagulant now, with left atrial appendage occlusion an alternative", "D": "Withhold all antithrombotic therapy and screen annually with carotid ultrasound imaging", "E": "Continue a proton pump inhibitor alone as sufficient protection against cardioembolic stroke"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Resume a direct oral anticoagulant now, with left atrial appendage occlusion an alternative): after a treated, nonrecurrent gastrointestinal bleed, restarting anticoagulation improves survival in high stroke risk patients, and appendage occlusion is a guideline supported option when long term anticoagulation is truly unacceptable. Aspirin provides little stroke protection in atrial fibrillation while still causing bleeding. Warfarin does not bleed less than the direct agents in the gut overall, and acid suppression alone offers no cardioembolic protection."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "During emergency angiography for infarction with cardiogenic shock, a 69 year old woman has a thrombotic proximal right coronary occlusion plus chronic appearing 80 percent stenoses in the left anterior descending and circumflex arteries. Regarding how much revascularization to perform during this index procedure, what does trial evidence support?", "options": {"A": "Complete multivessel intervention immediately because shock demands maximal restoration of flow", "B": "Fibrinolysis into the nonculprit vessels after the culprit artery is opened", "C": "Emergency bypass surgery instead of any percutaneous approach to this anatomy", "D": "Treat the culprit artery alone and stage decisions about the other stenoses", "E": "Abort intervention entirely and stabilize on mechanical support before any revascularization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the culprit artery alone and stage decisions about the other stenoses): In shock, immediate multivessel intervention increased the combined risk of death and renal replacement therapy compared with culprit only treatment, so the additional stenoses are addressed later if indicated. Immediate complete revascularization sounds physiologically appealing but was the strategy that performed worse in the randomized comparison. Intracoronary fibrinolysis of nonculprit vessels has no supporting role, emergency surgery adds unacceptable delay for a thrombotic culprit that is percutaneously accessible, and deferring all reperfusion abandons the one intervention proven to improve shock survival."}, {"id": 869, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 24 year old man presents with palpitations and lightheadedness. The rhythm strip shown reveals an irregularly irregular wide complex tachycardia with beat to beat variation in QRS width, and some intervals between beats are strikingly short. Which treatment would be most dangerous for the rhythm shown?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous diltiazem given as a bolus followed by a continuous maintenance infusion", "B": "Intravenous procainamide infused slowly while monitoring blood pressure and QRS duration", "C": "Synchronized electrical cardioversion performed after appropriate procedural sedation is administered", "D": "Intravenous ibutilide with continuous monitoring of the corrected QT interval afterward", "E": "Urgent referral for catheter ablation of the accessory pathway after stabilization"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous diltiazem given as a bolus followed by a continuous maintenance infusion): the strip shows preexcited atrial fibrillation, where fibrillatory impulses reach the ventricles over an accessory pathway, and blocking the atrioventricular node removes the decremental conduction that competes with the pathway. Nodal blockers can therefore accelerate the ventricular response and provoke ventricular fibrillation. Procainamide and ibutilide slow pathway conduction directly, synchronized cardioversion is appropriate whenever compromise develops, and ablation is the definitive cure.", "image": "question_images/q_869.png"}, {"id": 870, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 68 year old man with symptomatic severe aortic stenosis has angiography showing severe three vessel coronary disease including a complex left main lesion. His surgical risk score is low, and he is robust without frailty. How should the valve and coronary problems be sequenced or combined in his care?", "options": {"A": "Stage percutaneous coronary intervention first, then transcatheter valve replacement several weeks later", "B": "Perform transcatheter replacement first and revascularize afterward only if angina persists", "C": "Refer for surgical valve replacement with concomitant coronary bypass in one operation", "D": "Treat medically until ejection fraction declines, then reconsider a combined intervention", "E": "Offer bypass surgery alone now and reassess the stenotic valve within a year"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer for surgical valve replacement with concomitant coronary bypass in one operation): a low risk surgical candidate with severe symptomatic stenosis and complex multivessel disease including the left main is best served by one definitive operation addressing both lesions. Staged percutaneous strategies leave complex left main anatomy treated less durably than bypass in a patient who tolerates surgery well. Deferring either the valve or the revascularization leaves a symptomatic, prognostically important lesion untreated while risk accumulates."}, {"id": 872, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 79 year old man labeled with hypertensive heart failure with preserved ejection fraction has a history that includes bilateral carpal tunnel release, lumbar spinal stenosis surgery, and repeated lightheadedness forcing withdrawal of low dose lisinopril and metoprolol. Which unifying reconsideration should this constellation trigger for the evaluating clinician?", "options": {"A": "Occupational overuse syndrome coinciding with degenerative spine disease and drug sensitivity", "B": "Transthyretin amyloidosis, whose tissue deposits explain the orthopedic history and the drug intolerance", "C": "Diabetic neuropathy complex, since compressive neuropathies cluster with autonomic drug intolerance", "D": "Frailty of aging, since polypharmacy intolerance and orthopedic wear accumulate past seventy five", "E": "Hypothyroidism, which links entrapment neuropathy, spinal symptoms, and medication sensitivity mechanistically"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Transthyretin amyloidosis, whose tissue deposits explain the orthopedic history and the drug intolerance): amyloid infiltrates the flexor retinaculum and ligamentum flavum years before the heart declares itself, so bilateral carpal tunnel surgery plus spinal stenosis in an older man with preserved ejection fraction failure is a recognized red flag cluster, and the stiff low stroke volume ventricle tolerates vasodilators and rate slowing poorly. Attributing the pattern to aging, overuse, diabetes, or thyroid disease scatters clues a single infiltrative diagnosis unites. Recognizing the cluster matters because confirming transthyretin disease opens stabilizer therapy that plain antihypertensive management never provides."}, {"id": 873, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 49 year old woman with severe pulmonary arterial hypertension develops hypoxemic respiratory failure, and the intensive care team prepares for endotracheal intubation. The attending insists on specific precautions beyond a standard rapid sequence approach. Why is intubation singularly hazardous in severe pulmonary hypertension, and what follows from that?", "options": {"A": "Upper airway anatomy is distorted by advanced pulmonary vascular disease, making direct laryngoscopy technically considerably harder", "B": "The hazard is aspiration alone, so cricoid pressure removes the excess risk entirely", "C": "Induction vasodilation and positive pressure can collapse right ventricular output, so hemodynamic preparation is essential", "D": "Oxygen toxicity develops within minutes in these patients once ventilation begins", "E": "These patients need deeper anesthesia than usual, which mainly prolongs the procedure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Induction vasodilation and positive pressure can collapse right ventricular output, so hemodynamic preparation is essential): induction agents drop systemic vascular tone and right coronary perfusion while positive pressure ventilation raises pulmonary vascular resistance and reduces venous return, a combination that can produce irretrievable circulatory collapse in a preload dependent, afterload sensitive right ventricle, so vasopressors running before induction, cautious agent selection, and the most experienced operator are standard. Peri-intubation arrest in this population is a hemodynamic event rather than an airway anatomy problem. Aspiration, oxygen toxicity, and anesthetic depth are minor considerations beside the circulatory physiology."}, {"id": 875, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 79 year old man with a recent minor stroke and 80 percent ipsilateral internal carotid stenosis is anatomically suitable for either carotid endarterectomy or transfemoral carotid stenting. His operative risk is acceptable. Considering trial evidence on how age modifies periprocedural stroke risk, which revascularization approach is preferred for him?", "options": {"A": "Transfemoral stenting, because avoiding general anesthesia benefits patients of advanced age", "B": "Either procedure equally, since outcomes converge in patients beyond age 75", "C": "Transfemoral carotid stenting with proximal embolic protection deployed to minimize catheter manipulation of the aortic arch", "D": "Carotid endarterectomy, because periprocedural stroke with transfemoral stenting rises sharply with age", "E": "Medical therapy alone, since revascularization offers little at his advanced age"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Carotid endarterectomy, because periprocedural stroke with transfemoral stenting rises sharply with age): pooled trial analyses show the periprocedural stroke risk of transfemoral stenting climbs steeply beyond about age 70, driven by aortic arch atherosclerosis and tortuosity encountered during catheter delivery, while endarterectomy outcomes change little with age. Anesthesia considerations do not outweigh that stroke difference, and outcomes diverge rather than converge in older patients. Proximal protection does not neutralize the diseased arch itself, and withholding revascularization from a fit symptomatic patient with severe stenosis forfeits proven stroke prevention."}, {"id": 882, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 54 year old woman treated with mediastinal radiation for lymphoma at age 26 now has severe symptomatic aortic stenosis with heavy calcification of the aortomitral curtain, a calcified ascending aorta, and dense mediastinal fibrosis. Her surgical risk score is intermediate. Which treatment consideration should most influence the heart team?", "options": {"A": "Surgical valve replacement remains preferable because radiation damaged valves respond poorly to balloon expandable prostheses", "B": "Medical therapy is appropriate because radiation disease progresses more slowly than calcific disease", "C": "Combined surgical replacement of both aortic and mitral valves prevents any future reoperation", "D": "Transcatheter replacement is favored because the irradiated mediastinum and calcified aorta amplify surgical risk", "E": "Valve intervention should wait until the mitral valve also reaches severe dysfunction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Transcatheter replacement is favored because the irradiated mediastinum and calcified aorta amplify surgical risk): radiation heart disease creates fibrotic tissue planes, poor wound healing, and a porcelain aorta that standard risk scores do not capture, so transcatheter therapy is generally preferred in these patients. Radiation valves expand acceptably with current devices. The disease is progressive rather than indolent, prophylactic double valve surgery in a hostile chest compounds the danger, and deferring treatment of severe symptomatic stenosis awaiting mitral progression abandons the indication at hand."}, {"id": 883, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 67 year old man with chronic lung disease reports fluttering and fatigue. The rhythm strip shown demonstrates continuous regular sawtooth atrial activity with QRS complexes arriving in an irregular pattern as the conduction ratio changes. Which statement best guides his subsequent stroke prevention and rhythm management?", "options": {"A": "Irregular conduction makes this atrial fibrillation, which carries higher embolic risk than flutter", "B": "This is atrial flutter with variable block, warranting the same anticoagulation rules as fibrillation", "C": "Sawtooth activity with an irregular response indicates multifocal atrial tachycardia from lung disease", "D": "Anticoagulation is unnecessary because organized flutter waves do not form appendage thrombus", "E": "Catheter ablation offers little benefit once the conduction ratio has become variable"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (This is atrial flutter with variable block, warranting the same anticoagulation rules as fibrillation): regular sawtooth flutter waves with a changing conduction ratio produce an irregular ventricular response while the atrial rhythm itself remains organized, and guidelines assign flutter the same thromboembolic risk scoring and anticoagulation thresholds as atrial fibrillation. The irregular response does not convert the diagnosis to fibrillation, and multifocal atrial tachycardia shows discrete P waves of varying morphology rather than sawtooth activity. Organized flutter still promotes appendage thrombus, and cavotricuspid isthmus ablation remains highly effective for typical flutter.", "image": "question_images/q_883.png"}, {"id": 884, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "Two patients have severe functional tricuspid regurgitation. One has longstanding atrial fibrillation, a hugely dilated annulus, and normal pulmonary pressures with an unremarkable right ventricle. The other has pulmonary hypertension with a pressure of 68 mm Hg and leaflet tethering over a dilated ventricle. Why does this distinction change treatment?", "options": {"A": "The atrial functional form responds poorly to annuloplasty rings, so surgical leaflet augmentation is mandatory there", "B": "The atrial form may improve with rhythm control or annular repair, unlike pressure driven tethering", "C": "The ventricular form is benign because the tethered leaflets still coapt during inspiration", "D": "Both forms share identical physiology, so the distinction only affects imaging protocols", "E": "The atrial form requires transplantation referral because atrial myopathy is untreatable"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The atrial form may improve with rhythm control or annular repair, unlike pressure driven tethering): atrial functional regurgitation is an annulus problem on a relatively normal ventricle, so restoring sinus rhythm or downsizing the annulus works, whereas ventricular functional regurgitation reflects tethering from pressure overload, and repairing the valve without addressing pulmonary hypertension yields recurrence and right ventricular afterload mismatch. Annuloplasty actually suits the atrial form well. Tethered leaflets do not coapt reliably, the physiologies differ fundamentally, and atrial disease is often modifiable."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 34 year old woman five weeks postpartum presents with infarction. Angiography shows a long smooth tapering narrowing of the distal left anterior descending artery with brisk flow and no visible plaque, consistent with spontaneous dissection. Her pain is resolving and she is hemodynamically stable. What is the preferred management?", "options": {"A": "Conservative management with monitoring because most such dissections heal spontaneously", "B": "Immediate coronary stenting across the entire narrowed segment to restore a normal angiographic silhouette", "C": "Emergency bypass grafting to the distal vessel before propagation occurs", "D": "Full dose fibrinolysis to dissolve any thrombus within the false lumen", "E": "Glycoprotein inhibitor infusion to prevent platelet accumulation within the dissection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Conservative management with monitoring because most such dissections heal spontaneously): With preserved flow, resolving symptoms, and stability, spontaneous coronary artery dissection is managed conservatively since the vast majority of affected segments heal within weeks, and intervention in fragile dissected tissue often extends the injury. Stenting risks propagating the intramural hematoma and frequently fails in this friable substrate, and bypass is reserved for left main involvement or ongoing ischemia. Fibrinolysis can expand the hematoma and convert a contained dissection into occlusion, and glycoprotein inhibitors add bleeding without addressing the wall pathology."}, {"id": 887, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 77 year old man on warfarin and digoxin for atrial fibrillation is started on amiodarone after an episode of ventricular tachycardia. Two weeks later he reports nausea, visual disturbance, and easy bruising, with an international normalized ratio of 4.8. What pharmacologic mechanism best explains this deterioration?", "options": {"A": "Digoxin induces hepatic enzymes that accelerate the metabolism of warfarin", "B": "Amiodarone inhibits clearance of both warfarin and digoxin, raising each drug level", "C": "Warfarin displaces amiodarone from tissue stores, producing acute amiodarone toxicity", "D": "Amiodarone accelerates warfarin metabolism, so the elevated ratio reflects laboratory error", "E": "The three drugs compete for renal secretion, lowering all their concentrations"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Amiodarone inhibits clearance of both warfarin and digoxin, raising each drug level): amiodarone inhibits the cytochrome enzymes that metabolize warfarin and the P glycoprotein transporter that clears digoxin, so both drugs accumulate, producing the elevated international normalized ratio along with the nausea and visual symptoms of digoxin excess. Empiric dose reductions of roughly a third to a half with close monitoring are standard when amiodarone is added. Digoxin does not induce hepatic enzymes, displacement from tissue stores is not the operative interaction, and shared renal competition lowering every level is not a real mechanism."}, {"id": 888, "domain": "pulmonary_circulation_disorders", "question": "A 74 year old woman with obesity, hypertension, and atrial fibrillation is referred as possible pulmonary arterial hypertension after an outside echocardiogram. Right heart catheterization results appear in the exhibit shown. The referring clinician proposes starting an endothelin receptor antagonist. What does the hemodynamic data indicate instead?", "options": {"A": "This is postcapillary hypertension from left heart disease, so managing that disease replaces vasodilator plans", "B": "The hemodynamic values confirm precapillary pulmonary vascular disease, supporting immediate endothelin receptor antagonist initiation", "C": "The numbers indicate chronic thromboembolic obstruction requiring urgent operability review", "D": "The study is uninterpretable because atrial fibrillation invalidates wedge pressure tracings", "E": "The data suggest exercise induced disease needing invasive exercise testing before therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (This is postcapillary hypertension from left heart disease, so managing that disease replaces vasodilator plans): a wedge pressure of 22 mmHg with pulmonary vascular resistance below 2 Wood units defines isolated postcapillary pulmonary hypertension, the expected signature of heart failure with preserved ejection fraction in an obese hypertensive patient with atrial fibrillation, and pulmonary arterial hypertension drugs offer no benefit and possible harm here. Precapillary disease requires a wedge of 15 mmHg or less with resistance above 2 Wood units, the opposite of these values. Nothing in the data suggests thromboembolic obstruction, wedge tracings remain interpretable in atrial fibrillation with careful averaging, and resting hemodynamics already explain her condition.", "image": "question_images/q_888.png"}, {"id": 889, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 52 year old woman treated for dilated cardiomyopathy has improved from an ejection fraction of 22 percent to 54 percent on full guideline therapy over two years. Feeling cured, she asks to stop her four heart failure medications. What does the best available trial evidence say about that plan?", "options": {"A": "Medications can stop safely once ejection fraction exceeds fifty percent for a year", "B": "Withdrawal in recovered cardiomyopathy caused relapse in roughly forty percent within months", "C": "Half doses maintain remission as effectively as full doses in recovered patients", "D": "Stopping is safe when the natriuretic peptide level stays within normal limits for six consecutive months", "E": "Discontinuation risk applies mainly to ischemic disease, not to dilated cardiomyopathy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withdrawal in recovered cardiomyopathy caused relapse in roughly forty percent within months): in the randomized withdrawal trial of recovered dilated cardiomyopathy, staged discontinuation led to relapse in about four of ten patients within six months, while those continuing therapy remained stable, establishing that this is remission maintained by treatment rather than cure. No ejection fraction duration or peptide threshold has been shown to make stopping safe. Dose reduction strategies and an ischemic only risk pattern are not supported by the trial."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 58 year old man on hemodialysis who missed two sessions presents with weakness and vague chest heaviness. The ECG shows a widened QRS, peaked T waves, and ST elevation in V1 and V2, prompting a request for laboratory activation. Based on the laboratory results shown, what should happen first?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with emergency angiography because the elevation involves two contiguous leads", "B": "Give intravenous calcium and correct the potassium before considering any angiography", "C": "Administer fibrinolytic therapy while arranging transfer to a percutaneous intervention center", "D": "Start dual antiplatelet therapy and heparin pending a repeat troponin measurement", "E": "Obtain an urgent echocardiogram to look for a regional wall motion abnormality"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give intravenous calcium and correct the potassium before considering any angiography): Severe hyperkalemia produces QRS widening, peaked T waves, and a right precordial injury pattern that resolves once the membrane is stabilized and potassium is lowered. Emergency angiography and fibrinolysis treat a coronary occlusion the normal troponin and metabolic picture argue against, and fibrinolysis adds major hemorrhage risk. Antiplatelet loading with heparin and waiting on echocardiography both delay the electrolyte correction that is the actual emergency here.", "image": "question_images/q_890.png"}, {"id": 892, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 59 year old woman stopped atorvastatin for muscle aching that fully resolved during a four week washout. Symptoms returned within ten days of restarting the same dose. Her ten year risk is high after a prior stent, and she is motivated to stay on lipid therapy. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Declare statin intolerance final and start no lipid lowering therapy", "B": "Try an alternative statin at low intensity with an every other day schedule", "C": "Restart the identical atorvastatin dose a third consecutive time to formally confirm causality", "D": "Rely solely on dietary modification given the reproducible muscle symptoms", "E": "Begin a fibrate as the primary replacement for statin therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Try an alternative statin at low intensity with an every other day schedule): Reproducible symptoms on one statin do not predict intolerance to all of them, and most affected patients tolerate a different agent at reduced intensity or intermittent dosing, often a long half life statin given nondaily, preserving meaningful risk reduction. Declaring final intolerance or retreating to diet alone abandons the drug class with the strongest secondary prevention evidence. A third identical rechallenge repeats an experiment that has already answered its question twice, and a fibrate lowers triglycerides without providing statin level protection against recurrent coronary events."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 66 year old woman with ejection fraction 20 percent and pneumonia has cardiac index 1.8 L/min/m2, wedge pressure 24 mm Hg, and systemic vascular resistance 900 dynes despite hypotension. Vasoconstriction seems inappropriately absent for this degree of low output. What is the most accurate hemodynamic characterization?", "options": {"A": "Mixed cardiogenic and vasodilatory shock, since resistance is inappropriately low for this output", "B": "Pure cardiogenic shock, because the elevated wedge pressure defines the physiology by itself", "C": "Pure septic shock, because pneumonia sufficiently explains hypotension when infection and hypotension coexist", "D": "Hypovolemic shock, since pneumonia commonly causes insensible losses that reduce preload substantially", "E": "Compensated heart failure, because an index near two supports adequate resting perfusion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mixed cardiogenic and vasodilatory shock, since resistance is inappropriately low for this output): a failing ventricle should trigger intense vasoconstriction, so a resistance of 900 with an index of 1.8 reveals a concurrent distributive insult from infection. An elevated wedge alone does not exclude a second process, and pneumonia alone cannot explain the low index with high filling pressure. Recognizing both components matters because vasopressors and inotropes address different halves of this physiology."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 71 year old woman with atrial fibrillation, a CHA2DS2 VASc score of 4, and end stage kidney disease on hemodialysis asks about stroke prevention. Her nephrologist worries about bleeding, and trial data in dialysis are limited. Which statement most accurately frames the anticoagulation decision for her?", "options": {"A": "Dabigatran is preferred because dialysis clears it efficiently after any bleeding event", "B": "Standard dose rivaroxaban is proven superior to warfarin in dialysis dependent patients", "C": "Anticoagulation is contraindicated once the estimated filtration rate falls below fifteen", "D": "Warfarin clearly improves survival in dialysis patients and should be started routinely", "E": "Evidence is limited, so shared decision making weighs apixaban or warfarin individually"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Evidence is limited, so shared decision making weighs apixaban or warfarin individually): randomized outcome data for anticoagulation in dialysis patients are sparse, and available studies have not shown a clear winner, so guidelines endorse individualized decisions with apixaban and warfarin as the usual candidates. Dabigatran is predominantly renally cleared and is avoided in dialysis, and rivaroxaban lacks proven superiority in this population. Neither an absolute filtration threshold contraindicating therapy nor a routine warfarin survival benefit is established."}, {"id": 900, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 74 year old man with exertional dyspnea has aortic valve area 0.8 cm², mean gradient 24 mm Hg, and ejection fraction 30 percent. Low dose dobutamine echocardiography raises stroke volume by 25 percent and mean gradient to 44 mm Hg, while valve area remains 0.9 cm². What do these findings establish?", "options": {"A": "Pseudo severe stenosis in which the valve opens adequately once forward flow improves", "B": "Absent contractile reserve, favoring continued medical therapy with repeat imaging in one year", "C": "A primary cardiomyopathy with incidental valve thickening explaining the reduced ejection fraction", "D": "True severe stenosis with contractile reserve, supporting referral for valve replacement", "E": "Indeterminate severity that requires invasive catheterization before the valve can be classified"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (True severe stenosis with contractile reserve, supporting referral for valve replacement): a stroke volume rise of at least 20 percent proves contractile reserve, and a gradient climbing above 40 mm Hg while area stays near 1.0 cm² or below confirms a fixed stenotic orifice. Pseudo severe disease would instead show the calculated area enlarging well beyond 1.0 cm² as flow normalizes. A primary cardiomyopathy would augment flow without a proportional gradient rise, and the study is already diagnostic, so catheterization adds little."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 28 year old woman has blood pressure of 168/104 mm Hg despite two medications. She has no family history of hypertension, normal potassium and creatinine, and a soft bruit in the right upper abdominal quadrant. Which next study is most likely to identify the underlying cause of her hypertension?", "options": {"A": "Plasma free metanephrines drawn supine after thirty minutes of quiet rest", "B": "Computed tomographic angiography of the renal arteries for fibromuscular dysplasia", "C": "Transthoracic echocardiography with suprasternal views of the full aortic arch", "D": "Aldosterone and renin sampling after careful correction of dietary sodium", "E": "Overnight polysomnography to evaluate nocturnal hypoxemia as a sustained pressor mechanism in this patient"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Computed tomographic angiography of the renal arteries for fibromuscular dysplasia): Severe early onset hypertension in a young woman without family history, combined with an abdominal bruit lateralizing toward one kidney, points strongly to renovascular disease, and fibromuscular dysplasia is the dominant cause in this demographic; angiography visualizes its typical beaded mid vessel lesions and can direct curative angioplasty. Metanephrines fit episodic adrenergic spells she does not have. Normal potassium weakens an aldosteronism lead, coarctation would show arm to leg differences, and she reports no sleep symptoms."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A previously healthy 29 year old woman deteriorates over four days from mild dyspnea to cardiogenic shock with recurrent ventricular tachycardia requiring repeated cardioversion. Lymphocytic myocarditis was presumed, but she worsens on supportive care. Beyond escalating mechanical support, which diagnostic and therapeutic pairing is most urgent right now?", "options": {"A": "Endomyocardial biopsy paired with readiness to begin immunosuppression and transplant center involvement", "B": "Cardiac magnetic resonance paired with colchicine, deferring invasive testing until she stabilizes hemodynamically", "C": "Serologic viral panel paired with intravenous immunoglobulin, which reverses most fulminant presentations", "D": "Coronary angiography paired with dual antiplatelet loading for presumed plaque rupture events", "E": "Genetic panel testing paired with beta blockade, targeting an inherited arrhythmia syndrome"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Endomyocardial biopsy paired with readiness to begin immunosuppression and transplant center involvement): fulminant deterioration with recurrent ventricular arrhythmia despite support raises giant cell or necrotizing eosinophilic myocarditis, diagnoses only tissue can establish and that demand immediate immunosuppression and advanced therapy planning. Magnetic resonance is impractical and insufficiently specific in an unstable patient. Viral serologies and immunoglobulin lack proven benefit here, angiography addresses a less likely mechanism, and genetic testing does not treat this emergency."}, {"id": 903, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "Six weeks after stenting for infarction, a 74 year old man on aspirin and ticagrelor develops melena with a hemoglobin of 8.4 g/dL and stable vital signs. The admitting team proposes stopping both antiplatelet agents indefinitely. Which management better reconciles his gastrointestinal bleeding with his fresh stent?", "options": {"A": "Stop both agents until a colonoscopy can be scheduled next month", "B": "Continue both agents unchanged and transfuse to a hemoglobin above ten", "C": "Continue aspirin, hold the P2Y12 inhibitor briefly, and arrange early endoscopy", "D": "Reverse the platelet inhibition with a transfusion before any endoscopic evaluation", "E": "Switch both agents to therapeutic enoxaparin during the bleeding evaluation period"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue aspirin, hold the P2Y12 inhibitor briefly, and arrange early endoscopy): With a six week old stent, stopping all platelet inhibition invites thrombosis with high mortality, so the standard compromise keeps aspirin, adds proton pump therapy, controls the source endoscopically, and resumes the second agent promptly. Stopping everything for a month is the highest risk choice on the table. Continuing both agents unchanged while transfusing to an arbitrary threshold ignores an active source, platelet transfusion in a stable patient promotes stent thrombosis, and enoxaparin substitution provides anticoagulation rather than the platelet inhibition the stent requires."}, {"id": 904, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "Four days after an untreated anterior infarction, a 68 year old man becomes hypotensive with a new harsh holosystolic murmur at the left sternal border accompanied by a palpable thrill. Right heart catheterization shows an oxygen saturation rise from 62 percent in the right atrium to 81 percent in the right ventricle. What is the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture with giant V waves", "B": "Right ventricular infarction with elevated right sided filling pressures throughout", "C": "Ventricular septal rupture producing a left to right shunt with oxygen step up", "D": "Subacute free wall rupture with contained pericardial hematoma and tamponade physiology", "E": "Severe tricuspid regurgitation from ischemic distortion of the subvalvular apparatus"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ventricular septal rupture producing a left to right shunt with oxygen step up): A harsh sternal border murmur with a thrill plus a saturation increase of nearly twenty points between right atrium and right ventricle is diagnostic of septal rupture, since oxygenated left ventricular blood is entering the right ventricle directly. Papillary muscle rupture produces a soft murmur without a thrill and shows giant V waves rather than an oxygen step up. Right ventricular infarction and tricuspid regurgitation change pressures and wave forms but never raise right ventricular oxygen content, and contained rupture presents with tamponade signs instead."}, {"id": 907, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 71 year old woman with hypertension has computed tomography for musculoskeletal back discomfort that has since resolved. The scan reveals a focal 12 millimeter contrast outpouching through an atherosclerotic plaque in the mid descending aorta. She is currently pain free with normal hemodynamics and no periaortic hematoma. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Emergency open replacement of the involved descending thoracic aortic segment", "B": "Immediate thoracic endovascular repair, since these lesions typically rupture within days", "C": "Intravenous heparin infusion to prevent thrombus propagation within the ulcer crater", "D": "Percutaneous aspiration of the outpouching under computed tomography image guidance", "E": "Blood pressure optimization with scheduled surveillance imaging of the ulcerated segment"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Blood pressure optimization with scheduled surveillance imaging of the ulcerated segment): an incidentally discovered, asymptomatic penetrating aortic ulcer in the descending aorta without periaortic hematoma or hemodynamic compromise is managed with blood pressure control and surveillance imaging, since intervention is reserved for pain, growth, or signs of contained leak. Emergency surgery and immediate endovascular grafting overtreat a stable lesion, and most such ulcers do not rupture within days. Anticoagulation offers no protection against transmural progression, and percutaneous aspiration of an aortic wall lesion is not an existing therapy."}, {"id": 908, "domain": "normal_cardiovascular_anatomy_and_physiology", "question": "A 63 year old woman with an acute inferior infarction develops second degree atrioventricular block with a narrow escape rhythm at 48 beats per minute, and she stabilizes with atropine alone. Which anatomic reasoning best explains why her conduction disturbance appeared with this particular infarct territory?", "options": {"A": "The right coronary artery usually supplies the atrioventricular nodal artery", "B": "Inferior infarcts stretch the mitral annulus, mechanically compressing the His bundle", "C": "The circumflex artery routinely perfuses the node and is the occluded vessel", "D": "Inferior ischemia preferentially injures the left bundle branch fascicles first", "E": "The sinus node fails first in inferior infarction, unmasking junctional disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The right coronary artery usually supplies the atrioventricular nodal artery): in the roughly 85 percent of people with right dominant circulation the atrioventricular nodal artery arises near the crux from the right coronary artery, so inferior infarction commonly produces nodal level block that is narrow complex, atropine responsive, and usually transient, exactly her picture. Mechanical annular compression of the His bundle is not a described mechanism. The circumflex supplies the node only in a minority, fascicular injury is a feature of anterior septal infarction, and her problem is atrioventricular rather than sinus nodal."}, {"id": 909, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "The same patient undergoes posterior lead recording. Leads V7 through V9 show 0.7 mm of ST elevation with small Q waves, while his pain continues at rest despite sublingual nitroglycerin. The nearest catheterization laboratory is in the same hospital. Which action best reflects guideline based reperfusion reasoning?", "options": {"A": "Defer activation because posterior elevation must reach one full millimeter first", "B": "Load antiplatelet agents and reassess the posterior leads in four hours", "C": "Activate the laboratory now since 0.5 mm in posterior leads is diagnostic", "D": "Arrange echocardiography first to confirm a posterior wall motion abnormality exists", "E": "Start a nitroglycerin infusion and proceed to angiography the next morning"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Activate the laboratory now since 0.5 mm in posterior leads is diagnostic): Posterior leads sit farther from the myocardium, so the diagnostic threshold is 0.5 mm rather than the 1 mm used elsewhere, and this patient exceeds it with ongoing pain. Waiting for a full millimeter or repeating leads in four hours applies the wrong cutoff while myocardium is lost. Echocardiographic confirmation and an overnight nitroglycerin infusion each convert a true ST elevation equivalent into a delayed reperfusion, which is precisely the error posterior leads exist to prevent."}, {"id": 910, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 64 year old man with native aortic valve endocarditis is reassessed on day six of appropriate therapy, with findings summarized in the exhibit shown. He has no neurologic deficits, and coronary anatomy is acceptable. Integrating the culture, conduction, and imaging data, what is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Extend the same intravenous regimen to eight weeks with weekly surveillance cultures", "B": "Add gentamicin for synergy and repeat transthoracic imaging in one week", "C": "Place a permanent pacemaker now, then continue antibiotics to completion", "D": "Switch to an oral regimen once he has been afebrile for two days", "E": "Proceed to early aortic valve surgery for uncontrolled paravalvular infection"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Proceed to early aortic valve surgery for uncontrolled paravalvular infection): persistent staphylococcal bacteremia on day six, a PR interval lengthening from 160 to 240 milliseconds, and new echolucent root thickening together indicate an aortic root abscess, which antibiotics cannot sterilize and which requires operative debridement. Prolonging or intensifying the same medical approach leaves the abscess advancing toward complete heart block and root destruction. Pacing addresses only a downstream consequence, and oral step down therapy has no role while infection is uncontrolled.", "image": "question_images/q_910.png"}, {"id": 911, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 38 year old man hospitalized with influenza and a temperature of 39.5 degrees Celsius has the rhythm strip shown, demonstrating coved ST elevation with T wave inversion that was absent on tracings from last year. He has never had syncope and has no family history of sudden death. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Aggressive antipyretics with continuous monitoring until the fever and pattern resolve", "B": "Immediate implantation of a permanent cardioverter defibrillator before he is discharged from the hospital", "C": "An oral sodium channel blocker challenge to confirm the diagnosis while febrile", "D": "Lifelong empiric quinidine started now because the pattern predicts imminent arrest", "E": "Discharge home with reassurance that fever related changes carry no significance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Aggressive antipyretics with continuous monitoring until the fever and pattern resolve): a fever induced type 1 Brugada pattern in an asymptomatic patient warrants vigorous temperature control and monitored observation while the pattern persists, since fever increases arrhythmic risk but an asymptomatic, fever unmasked pattern does not by itself justify a defibrillator. A drug challenge adds risk and is illogical when the diagnostic pattern is already present. Quinidine is reserved for arrhythmic events or storms, and discharge without monitoring ignores a real short term risk.", "image": "question_images/q_911.png"}, {"id": 914, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "An 81 year old woman with diabetes, three vessel disease, frailty, moderate cognitive impairment, and a predicted operative mortality above 8 percent has limiting angina despite two antianginal drugs. Her family asks about the trial showing surgery is better in diabetes. Which reasoning best guides her revascularization decision?", "options": {"A": "Trial based surgical preference yields to her prohibitive operative risk, making percutaneous treatment reasonable", "B": "The diabetes trial evidence extends to her age group, so surgery remains recommended", "C": "Neither revascularization approach is permissible once cognitive impairment has been documented", "D": "Angina in the elderly reflects deconditioning, so revascularization offers little benefit", "E": "Her frailty argues for surgical revascularization, since one definitive operation avoids repeated percutaneous procedures later"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Trial based surgical preference yields to her prohibitive operative risk, making percutaneous treatment reasonable): the surgical advantage in diabetic multivessel disease was demonstrated in patients fit for surgery, so a predicted operative mortality above 8 percent with frailty and cognitive impairment shifts the decision toward percutaneous treatment of her limiting symptoms. Trial evidence does not simply extend to prohibitive risk patients, and cognitive impairment does not bar revascularization. Attributing elderly angina to deconditioning dismisses real ischemia, and frailty argues against rather than for sternotomy."}, {"id": 918, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 58 year old man had successful stenting of the right coronary artery for infarction two days ago. Overnight he develops recurrent chest pressure similar to his presenting pain, with new inferior ST changes. Considering the biomarker trend shown, what is the most appropriate immediate management decision?", "options": {"A": "Observe on telemetry since troponin fluctuations are expected after intervention", "B": "Increase the statin intensity and add a long acting nitrate overnight", "C": "Return to the laboratory for emergency angiography to evaluate for stent thrombosis", "D": "Order a pharmacologic stress test for the morning to formally document any residual inducible ischemia", "E": "Give fibrinolysis at the bedside since the artery was recently opened"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Return to the laboratory for emergency angiography to evaluate for stent thrombosis): A troponin that fell appropriately after the index event and then re rose sharply alongside recurrent pain and new ST changes in the treated territory indicates reinfarction, and early stent thrombosis is the presumed cause until angiography proves otherwise. Passive observation and antianginal adjustment leave a probably occluded stent closed while myocardium dies. A morning stress test is contraindicated during an evolving acute event, and fibrinolysis is inferior to immediate repeat intervention when the laboratory is available in the same building.", "image": "question_images/q_918.png"}, {"id": 922, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 77 year old man carries a diagnosis of hypertensive heart failure with preserved ejection fraction. His echocardiogram shows wall thickness of 17 mm, yet his electrocardiogram shows unexpectedly small QRS complexes and he stopped lisinopril for lightheadedness. Which reasoning step should this discordant picture prompt first?", "options": {"A": "Pursue cardiac amyloidosis, since low voltage despite thick walls suggests infiltration, not hypertrophy", "B": "Increase antihypertensives, since uncontrolled afterload remains the likeliest driver of thick walls", "C": "Reassure him, since voltage criteria on electrocardiograms correlate poorly with measured thickness", "D": "Order coronary angiography, since ischemia commonly produces this specific structural and electrical mismatch", "E": "Repeat the echocardiogram in one year to track hypertensive remodeling before acting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pursue cardiac amyloidosis, since low voltage despite thick walls suggests infiltration, not hypertrophy): true hypertrophy from hypertension adds myocyte mass and usually raises electrocardiographic voltage, so thick walls with small complexes imply that something other than muscle fills the wall. Intolerance of standard vasoactive drugs is another classic amyloid clue. Escalating antihypertensives, reassurance, ischemic evaluation, or surveillance imaging would each delay a diagnosis with specific disease modifying therapy."}, {"id": 924, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "During dobutamine testing for low flow low gradient aortic stenosis, a 76 year old woman's stroke volume barely rises, leaving the severity question unanswered because no contractile reserve emerged. The team still needs an assessment independent of flow before referring her onward. Which measurement provides that assessment most directly?", "options": {"A": "Natriuretic peptide level, since values above a threshold can confirm severity independent of flow", "B": "Aortic valve calcium scoring by computed tomography, which grades severity anatomically without flow", "C": "Global longitudinal strain, which distinguishes valvular from myocardial disease in this setting", "D": "Invasive gradient measurement, since catheter gradients are inherently independent of transvalvular flow", "E": "Diastolic function grading, which correlates with stenosis severity when reserve is absent"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aortic valve calcium scoring by computed tomography, which grades severity anatomically without flow): calcium burden reflects the structural disease itself, with sex specific thresholds that identify severe stenosis even when a weak ventricle cannot augment flow. Catheter gradients remain just as flow dependent as echocardiographic ones. Peptides, strain, and diastolic grading describe myocardial consequences and prognosis rather than answering whether the valve orifice itself is severely diseased."}, {"id": 929, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 68 year old man with prior inferior myocardial infarction presents with palpitations and lightheadedness. The monitor shows a regular wide complex tachycardia. You note intermittent cannon A waves in the neck and variable intensity of the first heart sound. What do these physical findings most strongly indicate?", "options": {"A": "Orthodromic atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia using a concealed accessory pathway with beat by beat retrograde atrial capture", "B": "Ventricular tachycardia with atrioventricular dissociation between independent atrial and ventricular activity", "C": "Supraventricular tachycardia with rate related aberrancy and consistent one to one conduction", "D": "Atrial flutter conducting with a fixed ratio through a diseased His Purkinje system", "E": "Sinus tachycardia with a preexisting bundle branch block and prominent jugular pulsations"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ventricular tachycardia with atrioventricular dissociation between independent atrial and ventricular activity): cannon A waves and a varying first heart sound occur when the atria intermittently contract against closed atrioventricular valves, which happens only when atrial and ventricular rhythms are dissociated. In a patient with prior infarction, this finding makes ventricular tachycardia the overwhelmingly likely diagnosis. Orthodromic reentry and supraventricular tachycardia with aberrancy both require linked atrial and ventricular activity, so they cannot produce these variable findings."}, {"id": 932, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 54 year old man with symptomatic persistent atrial fibrillation, mild left ventricular hypertrophy, and no coronary disease asks about long term antiarrhythmic therapy. Amiodarone would likely work, but he may need decades of treatment. Which consideration should most influence the choice against starting amiodarone first?", "options": {"A": "Cumulative organ toxicity over decades argues for ablation or safer agents first", "B": "Amiodarone is ineffective for persistent atrial fibrillation in hypertrophied ventricles", "C": "Amiodarone requires intravenous loading in the hospital before any oral use", "D": "Outpatient thyroid and liver monitoring rarely detects toxicity before it becomes irreversible", "E": "Rate control is proven superior to any rhythm control strategy in this group"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cumulative organ toxicity over decades argues for ablation or safer agents first): amiodarone is often the most effective drug, but pulmonary, thyroid, hepatic, ocular, and neurologic toxicity accumulate with dose and duration, so guidelines position it behind catheter ablation and better tolerated agents in a relatively young patient facing decades of exposure. It remains effective in hypertrophy and needs no hospital intravenous load for elective use. Structured monitoring does detect most toxicity early, and rhythm control is a reasonable, often preferred strategy in symptomatic patients."}, {"id": 934, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "On hospital day five of diuresis for decompensated heart failure, a 73 year old woman feels markedly better with nearly resolved edema. The laboratory results shown were drawn this morning, and admission values were notably lower for the first two analytes. What do these results most likely indicate?", "options": {"A": "Occult gastrointestinal fluid losses demanding urgent upper endoscopy before any additional intravenous diuretic dosing", "B": "Effective decongestion with intravascular volume contraction, supporting a transition toward oral dosing", "C": "Polycythemia vera unmasked by admission, requiring hematology referral and phlebotomy planning now", "D": "Laboratory error from a hemolyzed specimen, requiring redraw before any clinical decision", "E": "Dangerous overdiuresis requiring intravenous saline to restore circulating volume this morning"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Effective decongestion with intravascular volume contraction, supporting a transition toward oral dosing): rising hemoglobin, hematocrit, and albumin during diuresis reflect concentration of the intravascular space as excess plasma water is removed, a pattern associated with better outcomes when symptoms improve in parallel. A myeloproliferative disorder does not appear over five days. Saline would reverse the achieved decongestion, and nothing suggests hemolysis or gastrointestinal losses in an improving patient.", "image": "question_images/q_934.png"}, {"id": 937, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "An 84 year old woman with atrial fibrillation weighs 62 kg with a creatinine of 1.1 mg/dL. Her physician prescribed apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily out of general bleeding concern, though she meets only the age criterion for dose reduction. How should this prescription be assessed?", "options": {"A": "She qualifies for full dose apixaban, and underdosing raises stroke risk without clear benefit", "B": "The reduced dose is correct because advanced age alone mandates apixaban dose reduction in atrial fibrillation", "C": "Apixaban should be replaced with aspirin at her age to limit bleeding", "D": "Any dose is acceptable because apixaban trials showed no dose response relationship", "E": "Dabigatran at full dose is preferred because kidney function does not affect it"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She qualifies for full dose apixaban, and underdosing raises stroke risk without clear benefit): apixaban is reduced to 2.5 mg twice daily only when at least two of three criteria are met, age ≥80 years, weight ≤60 kg, and creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL, and she meets just the age criterion. Registry data show off label underdosing increases thromboembolic events without reliably reducing bleeding. Aspirin is not an anticoagulant substitute, and dabigatran is the most renally dependent of the direct agents."}, {"id": 941, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 47 year old asymptomatic man with chronic severe aortic regurgitation is followed annually. This year his ejection fraction is 53 percent and left ventricular end systolic diameter measures 52 mm, both worse than before. He runs recreationally without limitation and questions why surgery is suddenly on the table. What justifies operating now?", "options": {"A": "Aortic regurgitation this severe carries a yearly rupture risk that surgery removes", "B": "His running distance will decline within months, and surgery works best before deconditioning", "C": "His ejection fraction and end systolic dimension both cross thresholds that signal contractile decline", "D": "Valve surgery in regurgitation is timed principally by patient age rather than ventricular measurements", "E": "Progressive annular dilation will soon make the valve technically irreparable by any approach"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (His ejection fraction and end systolic dimension both cross thresholds that signal contractile decline): in chronic severe regurgitation the volume loaded ventricle flatters its own ejection fraction, so values drifting to 55 percent or below, or an end systolic dimension beyond 50 mm, mark genuine systolic impairment and constitute surgical indications despite absent symptoms. Aortic rupture is a concern of aneurysm disease rather than regurgitation itself. Predictions about his running are speculative, age is not the timing metric, and repairability is not the driving concern."}, {"id": 942, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 48 year old untreated man with diabetes has office blood pressures of 118/76 and 121/78 mm Hg on separate days. Because of his risk profile you request two weeks of duplicate home readings with a validated upper arm device. Given the trend shown, what is the most appropriate conclusion and plan?", "options": {"A": "White coat effect is present, so continue annual office based screening", "B": "Home device error explains the discrepancy, so recalibrate and repeat next year", "C": "The readings confirm normotension, so reinforce lifestyle measures and reassess annually", "D": "Elevated blood pressure category applies, so lifestyle change alone is sufficient", "E": "Masked hypertension is present, so begin antihypertensive therapy guided by home values"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Masked hypertension is present, so begin antihypertensive therapy guided by home values): The trend shown demonstrates home systolic averages persistently in the 134 to 142 range while his office values remain normal, the defining pattern of masked hypertension, which carries cardiovascular risk comparable to sustained hypertension and is especially consequential in diabetes. Treatment decisions and titration should then follow the out of office numbers. A white coat effect is the reverse discrepancy, device error is unlikely with a validated monitor and duplicate technique, and calling this normotension ignores the entire home record.", "image": "question_images/q_942.png"}, {"id": 943, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 61 year old man with a chronic total occlusion, controlled angina on two medications, and preserved ejection fraction seeks a second opinion after being offered elective occlusion intervention to make him live longer. Which summary of the randomized evidence for this procedure is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Randomized trials show reduced mortality when occlusions are opened within one year", "B": "The trials show fewer myocardial infarctions but no meaningful change in angina", "C": "The trials were stopped early because intervention arms showed unexpected excess mortality", "D": "Evidence supports the intervention primarily to prevent future ventricular arrhythmic events", "E": "Trials show symptom improvement in selected patients but no demonstrated survival benefit"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Trials show symptom improvement in selected patients but no demonstrated survival benefit): randomized trials of chronic total occlusion intervention show improvement in symptoms and quality of life in selected symptomatic patients but no reduction in death or infarction. Mortality claims exceed the data in either direction, since the trials were limited by enrollment and crossover rather than stopped for harm, and no consistent infarct prevention emerged. Arrhythmia prevention has never been an established indication, so consent should present the procedure as symptom therapy."}, {"id": 945, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 66 year old man is found at a routine visit to have blood pressure 204/112 mm Hg after running out of his medications two weeks ago. He feels well, and examination, creatinine, troponin, electrocardiogram, and chest radiograph show no acute target organ injury of any kind. What is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Admit for a nicardipine infusion targeting a normal pressure within six hours", "B": "Give sublingual immediate release nifedipine and observe for two hours in clinic", "C": "Arrange emergency department transfer for arterial line monitoring and serial testing", "D": "Give intravenous labetalol boluses in clinic until the pressure falls below 160", "E": "Restart oral therapy and arrange follow up within days, avoiding rapid reduction"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Restart oral therapy and arrange follow up within days, avoiding rapid reduction): Severe elevation without acute target organ injury is hypertensive urgency, and the evidence based response is reinstituting or intensifying oral therapy with reassessment over days, since aggressive acute lowering in asymptomatic patients causes ischemic events without improving outcomes. His negative examination, chemistries, troponin, and imaging exclude emergency. Infusions, arterial lines, and clinic intravenous boluses answer a diagnosis he does not have, and sublingual immediate release nifedipine produces precipitous falls that have caused strokes and infarcts."}, {"id": 946, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 76 year old man on stable digoxin for years develops confusion, bradycardia at 38 beats per minute with hypotension, and a digoxin level of 4.2 ng/mL after starting amiodarone and developing kidney injury. The admitting team debates therapy sequencing. Which intervention takes priority for this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Digoxin specific antibody fragments, since hemodynamically significant bradyarrhythmia defines an indication", "B": "Urgent hemodialysis, the most efficient route for removing tissue bound digoxin", "C": "Transvenous pacing first, since electrical capture must precede any antidote administration", "D": "Activated charcoal administration, since gut decontamination remains effective even days after chronic digoxin accumulation", "E": "Intravenous magnesium, which reverses digoxin receptor binding at the sodium potassium pump"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Digoxin specific antibody fragments, since hemodynamically significant bradyarrhythmia defines an indication): symptomatic bradycardia with hypotension from digoxin toxicity is a core indication for antibody therapy, which binds free drug within minutes and pulls tissue digoxin back into plasma. Digoxin's large distribution volume makes dialysis ineffective, and charcoal helps mainly after acute recent ingestion. Pacing may bridge but does not treat the poisoning, and magnesium does not displace digoxin from its receptor."}, {"id": 948, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 66 year old woman is referred for resistant hypertension at 162/94 mm Hg on four prescribed agents. Pharmacy records show refill gaps of several weeks for three of the drugs, and she admits splitting pills to save money. What is the most appropriate next step in her evaluation and management?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with aldosterone and renin testing while continuing the current regimen", "B": "Order renal artery imaging promptly since four drug failure suggests underlying renovascular disease", "C": "Address adherence barriers and simplify the regimen before secondary cause testing", "D": "Add a fifth agent from a class not yet represented in therapy", "E": "Refer for renal denervation given the failure of a four drug regimen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Address adherence barriers and simplify the regimen before secondary cause testing): Apparent resistance must be separated from true resistance, and her refill gaps and pill splitting show the prescribed regimen is not actually being taken, most often because of cost and complexity, so fixing affordability and consolidating to combination pills comes first. Secondary cause testing and renovascular imaging performed now would evaluate a treatment failure that has not genuinely occurred. Adding a fifth drug deepens the cost burden driving the problem, and device therapy has no place before adherence is secured."}, {"id": 951, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 47 year old woman with two weeks of new heart failure develops sustained ventricular tachycardia and then a new complete heart block despite guideline therapy, with ejection fraction falling from 45 to 25 percent within days. The team debates whether endomyocardial biopsy would change management. What is the strongest argument for proceeding?", "options": {"A": "Biopsy grades congestion severity, guiding diuretic intensity better than examination findings can", "B": "This presentation suggests giant cell myocarditis, where biopsy directed immunosuppression changes survival", "C": "Biopsy reliably identifies the specific causative virus, permitting targeted antiviral therapy during this same admission", "D": "All new cardiomyopathies merit biopsy, since tissue remains the diagnostic gold standard", "E": "A negative biopsy would justify stopping guideline medications and monitoring off therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (This presentation suggests giant cell myocarditis, where biopsy directed immunosuppression changes survival): fulminant deterioration with ventricular tachycardia and new conduction block is exactly the phenotype in which giant cell disease clusters, and its diagnosis mandates combination immunosuppression and early transplant evaluation, so tissue directly changes therapy. Routine biopsy of ordinary new cardiomyopathy is not recommended. Viral identification rarely alters treatment, biopsy does not grade volume status, and a negative result would not justify stopping guideline medications."}, {"id": 953, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 71 year old woman with end stage cardiomyopathy chooses home milrinone for comfort after declining advanced therapies. Her nurse practitioner drafts the care plan and pauses over her active implanted defibrillator and her stated wish to die peacefully at home. Which element most needs explicit discussion before discharge?", "options": {"A": "Addressing defibrillator shock deactivation, since palliative inotropes raise arrhythmia likelihood near death", "B": "Scheduling monthly echocardiograms to track ejection fraction response to the milrinone infusion", "C": "Arranging serial right heart catheterizations to confirm the milrinone infusion maintains an adequate resting cardiac index", "D": "Documenting a plan to titrate milrinone upward in response to weight gain", "E": "Securing prior authorization for a backup dobutamine infusion if milrinone fails"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Addressing defibrillator shock deactivation, since palliative inotropes raise arrhythmia likelihood near death): milrinone increases ventricular arrhythmia frequency, and an active device in a comfort focused patient can deliver repeated painful shocks during dying, directly defeating her stated goals, so deactivation options belong in the discharge conversation. Surveillance echocardiography and catheterization serve disease modification, not comfort. Weight triggered titration and backup infusions add burden without advancing the palliative intent of this plan."}, {"id": 954, "domain": "systemic_hypertension_and_hypotension", "question": "A 62 year old man developed lip and tongue swelling eight months after starting benazepril, requiring overnight observation. He has albuminuric chronic kidney disease and blood pressure above goal. The swelling has fully resolved and he feels well. Which long term antihypertensive plan is most appropriate for this patient now?", "options": {"A": "Resume benazepril at half dose with an epinephrine autoinjector prescription", "B": "Avoid both angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors and thiazide diuretics going forward", "C": "Use lisinopril instead, since angioedema risk differs between the individual agents", "D": "Rely on clonidine plus hydralazine to avoid renin angiotensin pathway drugs", "E": "Stop the class permanently and consider an angiotensin receptor blocker cautiously"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Stop the class permanently and consider an angiotensin receptor blocker cautiously): Angioedema on an angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor is bradykinin mediated and recurs unpredictably with any agent in the class, so every member is avoided for life, including at reduced doses. His albuminuric kidney disease still benefits from renin angiotensin blockade, and receptor blockers, which do not raise bradykinin, carry a low crossover risk that most references consider acceptable with counseling once swelling has resolved. Thiazides were never implicated, and a clonidine plus hydralazine strategy forfeits kidney protection without a safety requirement to do so."}, {"id": 956, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 62 year old man who underwent coronary bypass surgery eight years ago now has progressive edema, ascites, and elevated jugular pressure that rises with inspiration. His ejection fraction is normal and natriuretic peptide is only mildly elevated. Why does distinguishing constriction from other preserved ejection fraction phenotypes matter so much here?", "options": {"A": "Constriction requires lifelong anticoagulation, unlike other preserved ejection fraction heart failure phenotypes", "B": "Constriction progresses faster than restriction, so hospice discussions should begin at diagnosis", "C": "Constriction is potentially curable with pericardiectomy, unlike most other causes of this syndrome", "D": "Constriction responds uniquely well to sacubitril valsartan, which other phenotypes tolerate poorly", "E": "Constriction contraindicates diuretics, which remain first line for the alternative diagnoses considered"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Constriction is potentially curable with pericardiectomy, unlike most other causes of this syndrome): prior cardiac surgery, Kussmaul physiology, right sided congestion with preserved ejection fraction, and a modest natriuretic peptide form a classic constriction profile, and surgical removal of the pericardium can fully reverse the syndrome. Neuromodulating drugs do not treat a mechanical shell, and anticoagulation is not disease specific. Diuretics remain useful for symptom control in constriction while surgery is planned."}, {"id": 957, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 76 year old woman has aortic valve area 0.9 cm², mean gradient 26 mm Hg, ejection fraction 63 percent, indexed stroke volume 31 mL/m², and blood pressure 172/95 mm Hg in clinic. Before labeling this paradoxical low flow, low gradient severe stenosis, what should the clinician address first?", "options": {"A": "Order dobutamine stress echocardiography to test whether the gradient rises with flow", "B": "Schedule transcatheter replacement promptly because preserved ejection fraction hides severe disease", "C": "Begin high intensity statin therapy to slow further calcific progression of the leaflets", "D": "Refer for balloon valvuloplasty to clarify whether symptoms improve when the orifice enlarges", "E": "Control the hypertension and verify measurements, then repeat echocardiography under normal loading"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Control the hypertension and verify measurements, then repeat echocardiography under normal loading): uncontrolled afterload depresses transvalvular flow and gradient, and outflow tract measurement error commonly produces a falsely small calculated area, so both must be excluded before this pattern is accepted. Dobutamine testing is designed for low ejection fraction, not a preserved one with uncontrolled pressure. Proceeding to replacement or valvuloplasty risks treating a valve that reclassifies as moderate, and statins have not been shown to slow calcific stenosis."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 62 year old woman receives six defibrillator shocks in thirty minutes while fully alert with a normal monitored rhythm between shocks. Her device interrogation is not immediately available, and telemetry during a shock shows no preceding tachycardia. What is the most appropriate immediate bedside intervention for her?", "options": {"A": "Place a magnet over the generator to suspend tachyarrhythmia detection while monitored", "B": "Administer intravenous amiodarone to suppress the ventricular arrhythmia causing the shocks", "C": "Sedate and intubate her before any attempt to modify device behavior", "D": "Send her urgently to radiology for a chest radiograph before touching the device", "E": "Deactivate pacing entirely through an emergency reprogramming of the pulse generator"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Place a magnet over the generator to suspend tachyarrhythmia detection while monitored): shocks delivered without any preceding tachycardia in an alert patient are inappropriate, most often from lead noise or oversensing, and a magnet immediately disables further shocks while leaving bradycardia pacing intact. Amiodarone treats an arrhythmia that is not occurring. Intubation is unnecessary for this purpose, a radiograph can wait until the shocks are stopped, and pacing should remain active rather than be deactivated."}, {"id": 963, "domain": "congenital_heart_disease", "question": "A 25 year old man with a Fontan circulation develops progressive peripheral edema, ascites, and chronic diarrhea. Laboratory studies show serum albumin of 2.0 g/dL, low immunoglobulin levels, and lymphopenia, with normal urinary protein excretion. Which diagnosis best integrates this constellation of findings with his underlying physiology?", "options": {"A": "Nephrotic syndrome from chronic venous congestion affecting the glomerular basement membrane", "B": "Cirrhotic ascites with hepatorenal physiology from longstanding hepatic venous congestion", "C": "Celiac disease unmasked by mesenteric venous hypertension and mucosal edema", "D": "Chronic malnutrition from intestinal edema limiting adequate caloric and protein intake", "E": "Protein losing enteropathy driven by elevated systemic venous and lymphatic pressures"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Protein losing enteropathy driven by elevated systemic venous and lymphatic pressures): Chronically elevated caval pressure in the Fontan circuit congests intestinal lymphatics, which leak protein rich lymph into the gut and produce hypoalbuminemia with low immunoglobulins and lymphopenia. Normal urinary protein excludes a renal source, and stool alpha 1 antitrypsin confirms the enteric loss. Cirrhosis and celiac disease do not explain the combined immunoglobulin and lymphocyte depletion."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 60 year old woman with angina and nonobstructive coronary arteries is told by a colleague that her normal angiogram means an excellent prognosis and no need for preventive therapy. Her cardiologist disagrees and cites outcome studies of this population. Which statement best reflects what those data actually show?", "options": {"A": "Event rates in this population match those of people without any symptoms", "B": "Prognosis in this condition tracks with underlying anxiety burden rather than with traditional cardiovascular factors", "C": "These patients have elevated rates of events and hospitalization compared with asymptomatic peers", "D": "Outcomes worsen when preventive medications are prescribed to this patient group", "E": "The risk is confined to patients whose angiograms show mild irregularities"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (These patients have elevated rates of events and hospitalization compared with asymptomatic peers): outcome studies show that patients with ischemia and nonobstructive coronary arteries experience more infarction, hospitalization, and repeat testing than asymptomatic people, so the syndrome is not benign and preventive therapy is justified. Event rates do not match asymptomatic populations, and the prognosis is not an anxiety phenomenon. Preventive medication improves rather than worsens outcomes, and elevated risk is not confined to visibly irregular angiograms."}, {"id": 968, "domain": "pericardial_disease", "question": "A 35 year old woman with recurrent pericarditis has relapsed every time prednisone is tapered below 10 mg despite maximal colchicine and NSAID therapy for over a year, and she now has steroid related weight gain and osteopenia. Which management change most directly addresses both her recurrences and her steroid toxicity?", "options": {"A": "Increase prednisone permanently to a dose that fully suppresses her symptoms", "B": "Replace colchicine with weekly oral methotrexate while continuing her current prednisone dose unchanged", "C": "Schedule pericardiectomy now, since medical options are exhausted after one year", "D": "Stop current therapy abruptly to reset her inflammatory cycle before retreating", "E": "Initiate rilonacept to induce remission and enable a corticosteroid withdrawal taper"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Initiate rilonacept to induce remission and enable a corticosteroid withdrawal taper): she has colchicine resistant, corticosteroid dependent recurrent pericarditis, the exact population in which interleukin 1 blockade has demonstrated sustained remission and successful steroid discontinuation in randomized trial data. Permanently escalating prednisone worsens the toxicity that is already a problem. Methotrexate has weaker evidence and does not require abandoning colchicine, abrupt cessation of all therapy predictably provokes relapse, and pericardiectomy for recurrence is a last resort after biologic therapy fails."}, {"id": 974, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 72 year old man with hypertension, diabetes, and prior stroke presents with atrial fibrillation that began 36 hours ago by a clear history. He is comfortable and hemodynamically stable, taking no anticoagulant. He requests same day cardioversion. Which approach best reflects current guideline thinking?", "options": {"A": "Cardiovert now without any preprocedural imaging because symptom onset within 48 hours removes the embolic concern", "B": "Cardiovert now and prescribe four weeks of aspirin afterward for protection", "C": "Choose permanent rate control because his stroke history makes cardioversion inappropriate", "D": "Anticoagulate and either delay cardioversion three weeks or proceed after transesophageal imaging", "E": "Give a single dose of apixaban one hour before an immediate cardioversion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anticoagulate and either delay cardioversion three weeks or proceed after transesophageal imaging): in patients at elevated stroke risk, thrombus can form well before 48 hours, and current guidance no longer treats early onset as a license to cardiovert an inadequately anticoagulated high risk patient without imaging. A transesophageal study excluding appendage thrombus permits prompt cardioversion with anticoagulation continued afterward. Aspirin gives inadequate protection around cardioversion, one preprocedure dose does not secure the vulnerable weeks that follow, and his stroke history argues for caution rather than abandoning rhythm control."}, {"id": 976, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 64 year old woman with a prior anterior infarction and an ejection fraction of 30 percent presents with a regular wide complex tachycardia at a stable pressure. A colleague argues the rhythm looks like her old left bundle branch block. How should this history shape your working diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Her baseline conduction system disease makes aberrantly conducted supraventricular tachycardia the most probable underlying mechanism", "B": "Mechanism cannot be inferred until an electrophysiology study maps the circuit directly", "C": "A stable blood pressure during the rhythm argues strongly against a ventricular origin", "D": "Similar morphology to a prior tracing reliably excludes scar related ventricular tachycardia", "E": "Prior infarction makes ventricular tachycardia the presumed diagnosis until definitively proven otherwise"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Prior infarction makes ventricular tachycardia the presumed diagnosis until definitively proven otherwise): in patients with a healed myocardial scar, roughly nine of ten regular wide complex tachycardias are ventricular in origin, so structural heart disease dominates every morphology argument. Hemodynamic stability reflects rate and ventricular function rather than mechanism, and scar circuits can produce complexes resembling a baseline bundle branch block. Deferring the working diagnosis to an electrophysiology study leaves a dangerous rhythm untreated in the meantime."}, {"id": 977, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 73 year old woman with known chronic left bundle branch block presents with one hour of severe substernal pressure and diaphoresis. Her ECG shows 2 mm of ST elevation in V5 and V6, concordant with the positive QRS complexes in those leads. The team hesitates because bundle branch block obscures interpretation. What is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the serial troponin results since bundle branch block invalidates meaningful ST segment analysis", "B": "Obtain an echocardiogram first to look for a wall motion abnormality", "C": "Proceed with emergency reperfusion because concordant elevation indicates acute occlusion", "D": "Compare with old tracings and act only if the block is new", "E": "Give maximal medical therapy overnight and reassess the tracing at daybreak"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Proceed with emergency reperfusion because concordant elevation indicates acute occlusion): Bundle branch block normally forces ST segments opposite the QRS, so elevation concordant with a positive complex is profoundly abnormal, carries the highest validated score for infarction within the block, and justifies immediate reperfusion in a patient with an ischemic presentation. Waiting for biomarkers or morning reassessment discards a diagnosis the tracing has already made while myocardium is lost. Echocardiography adds delay for confirmation that concordance has already provided, and whether the block is old or new matters far less than the concordant injury current itself."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 45 year old man with infective endocarditis develops abrupt hypotension, tachycardia to 128, and severe pulmonary edema. Auscultation reveals only a soft, short early diastolic sound and no wide pulse pressure. Echocardiography confirms severe aortic regurgitation. Why does his examination appear so unimpressive despite catastrophic regurgitation?", "options": {"A": "The regurgitant jet is directed posteriorly, away from the stethoscope on the sternum", "B": "Aortic and ventricular diastolic pressures equalize rapidly, shortening the murmur and preventing pulse pressure widening", "C": "Marked compensatory tachycardia amplifies the systolic sounds and thereby conceals accompanying diastolic events from the examiner", "D": "The unconditioned left ventricle contracts weakly, lowering the systolic component of pressure", "E": "Pulmonary edema transmits airway sounds that overwhelm faint cardiac murmurs at the base"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Aortic and ventricular diastolic pressures equalize rapidly, shortening the murmur and preventing pulse pressure widening): a noncompliant, unprepared ventricle fills abruptly, so the aorta to ventricle gradient collapses early in diastole, truncating the murmur and blocking the classic wide pulse pressure of chronic disease. Jet direction does not explain the missing peripheral signs. Tachycardia is a consequence of low stroke volume rather than an acoustic mask, systolic function is typically hyperdynamic acutely, and lung sounds do not abolish hemodynamic findings."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 48 year old man with aortic valve endocarditis suffered a large intracerebral hemorrhage six days ago. He now has a mobile 12 mm vegetation but is hemodynamically stable on antibiotics without heart failure or abscess. The surgical team asks how the hemorrhage should shape operative timing. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "Operate within forty eight hours, because vegetation size overrides every neurologic consideration", "B": "Proceed once the platelet counts fully normalize, since coagulation laboratory status alone determines the rebleeding risk", "C": "Delay surgery about four weeks when feasible, since bypass anticoagulation threatens catastrophic rebleeding", "D": "Cancel surgical consideration permanently and treat with lifelong suppressive oral antibiotics", "E": "Perform the operation without systemic heparin by using minimal extracorporeal circulation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delay surgery about four weeks when feasible, since bypass anticoagulation threatens catastrophic rebleeding): recent major intracranial hemorrhage is the scenario where guidance favors postponing cardiopulmonary bypass, since full heparinization into a fresh hemorrhage bed risks devastating expansion, and his stability permits waiting. Vegetation size alone does not trump that danger in a stable patient. Platelet normalization does not neutralize the ruptured vascular bed, lifelong suppression abandons a curable lesion, and bypass cannot be run safely without systemic anticoagulation."}, {"id": 988, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 61 year old man with a mechanical mitral valve on warfarin develops Staphylococcus aureus prosthetic endocarditis and suffers a moderate embolic ischemic stroke without hemorrhage on imaging. The team must decide what to do with his anticoagulation now. Which approach best balances valve thrombosis risk against neurologic risk?", "options": {"A": "Continue warfarin unchanged at therapeutic intensity, because mechanical valves demand uninterrupted therapeutic anticoagulation", "B": "Pause all anticoagulation temporarily, then resume cautiously once hemorrhagic transformation risk declines", "C": "Switch to full dose low molecular weight heparin, which crosses the injured blood brain barrier less", "D": "Add aspirin to warfarin to reduce further platelet mediated embolic events", "E": "Reverse anticoagulation with vitamin K and prothrombin complex before repeat imaging"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pause all anticoagulation temporarily, then resume cautiously once hemorrhagic transformation risk declines): infected emboli create friable, hemorrhage prone infarcts, and in staphylococcal prosthetic endocarditis with recent stroke the early hemorrhagic conversion risk generally outweighs the short term thrombosis risk of a mechanical valve, so a brief pause with early reimaging is favored. Continuing unchanged ignores that danger. Swapping anticoagulants does not remove the bleeding substrate, adding aspirin raises hemorrhage risk further, and active reversal is reserved for actual intracranial bleeding."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "arrhythmias", "question": "A 70 year old man with a dual chamber pacemaker reports sudden regular palpitations that start after premature beats and stop abruptly. Interrogation shows episodes of ventricular pacing at the upper tracking rate with atrial sensed events following each paced beat. Which programming change most directly prevents these episodes?", "options": {"A": "Increasing the programmed ventricular pacing output amplitude to twice the measured capture threshold value", "B": "Extending the postventricular atrial refractory period so retrograde P waves are ignored", "C": "Lowering the base pacing rate so the atria receive fewer paced impulses", "D": "Switching the device to an asynchronous pacing mode for all daily activity", "E": "Shortening the programmed atrioventricular delay to encourage more ventricular tracking"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Extending the postventricular atrial refractory period so retrograde P waves are ignored): pacing at the upper tracking rate with an atrial sensed event after every paced beat describes pacemaker mediated tachycardia, an endless loop in which retrograde conduction is sensed and tracked. Lengthening the postventricular atrial refractory period makes the device blind to those retrograde signals, breaking the loop. Output and base rate changes do not affect tracking behavior, and asynchronous modes eliminate sensing altogether at the cost of competitive pacing."}, {"id": 993, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 60 year old man with acute severe aortic regurgitation is tachycardic at 118 beats per minute with borderline blood pressure while awaiting urgent surgery. A colleague suggests intravenous metoprolol to control the rate. What physiologic reasoning makes isolated beta blockade hazardous in this specific situation?", "options": {"A": "Tachycardia limits regurgitant time each beat, so slowing the rate enlarges backward flow", "B": "Beta blockade dilates the aortic root acutely, which enlarges the effective regurgitant orifice", "C": "Metoprolol raises systemic vascular resistance sharply, redirecting flow toward the coronary circulation", "D": "Negative inotropy converts hyperdynamic contraction into stunning within a few circulation times", "E": "Rate control masks the fever curve that guides antibiotic decisions in valve disruption"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tachycardia limits regurgitant time each beat, so slowing the rate enlarges backward flow): the rapid rate is a compensation that trims each diastolic interval during which blood leaks backward, so blunting it prolongs regurgitation and can precipitate collapse. Beta blockers do not acutely dilate the root or meaningfully raise systemic resistance in this way. Reduced contractility is an additional concern but the decisive mechanism is diastolic prolongation, and fever monitoring is irrelevant to the hemodynamic danger."}, {"id": 994, "domain": "heart_failure_and_cardiomyopathy", "question": "A 61 year old man on pembrolizumab for melanoma develops fatigue, a troponin of twelve times the reference limit, and new conduction delay on his electrocardiogram three weeks after his second dose. The oncology clinic asks how urgently this must be handled. What is the appropriate immediate response?", "options": {"A": "Continue pembrolizumab with weekly troponin surveillance, since low grade myocarditis often self resolves", "B": "Arrange outpatient magnetic resonance imaging within two weeks and decide on results", "C": "Start oral low dose prednisone at home with cardiology follow up next month", "D": "Admit now, stop the checkpoint inhibitor, and begin high dose intravenous corticosteroids promptly", "E": "Observe on telemetry for one day, treating just if arrhythmia actually appears"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Admit now, stop the checkpoint inhibitor, and begin high dose intravenous corticosteroids promptly): checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis carries mortality approaching half when fulminant, deteriorates unpredictably, and conduction disease plus a markedly elevated troponin marks exactly the high risk group, so drug cessation and immediate immunosuppression cannot wait. Continued dosing or delayed imaging risks abrupt heart block or ventricular arrhythmia. Low dose home steroids and observation without treatment both underdose a potentially lethal inflammatory process."}, {"id": 996, "domain": "coronary_artery_disease", "question": "A 61 year old woman with longstanding hypertension develops exertional dyspnea. Her ECG shows very deep S waves in V1 through V3 with 2 mm ST elevation in those same leads, plus asymmetric ST depression with T inversion in V5 and V6. Emergency activation is proposed. What is the best initial interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Anterior infarction requiring immediate reperfusion because elevation exceeds two millimeters", "B": "Discordant repolarization changes from ventricular hypertrophy warranting serial troponins and comparison tracings", "C": "Posterior infarction because the lateral leads show depression with T inversion", "D": "Acute pericarditis given the combination of elevation and depression in different territories", "E": "A sodium channelopathy pattern requiring urgent electrophysiology consultation before any catheterization laboratory activation proceeds"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Discordant repolarization changes from ventricular hypertrophy warranting serial troponins and comparison tracings): Hypertrophy produces secondary ST elevation discordant to deep anterior S waves and a lateral strain pattern, which together mimic infarction. Anterior infarction is not established by millimeter criteria alone when voltage is extreme, and posterior infarction produces depression in the right precordial leads rather than the lateral ones. Pericarditis causes diffuse concave elevation with PR changes, and a channelopathy pattern shows coved right precordial elevation rather than strain morphology."}, {"id": 997, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 61 year old man has duplex confirmed thrombosis of the great saphenous vein extending to within 2 centimeters of the saphenofemoral junction. There is no deep venous involvement. His clinician asks whether prophylactic dose fondaparinux would be sufficient treatment. Which management approach is most appropriate for a thrombus at this location?", "options": {"A": "Prophylactic dose fondaparinux given subcutaneously for 45 days, the standard for superficial thrombosis of this length", "B": "Nonsteroidal therapy with compression, since superficial clots seldom cause embolic events", "C": "Therapeutic anticoagulation, managing it like deep vein thrombosis given junctional proximity", "D": "Surgical high ligation of the junction followed by aspirin for six weeks", "E": "Serial duplex imaging weekly, with treatment added if the thrombus enters deep veins"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Therapeutic anticoagulation, managing it like deep vein thrombosis given junctional proximity): a saphenous thrombus within 3 centimeters of the saphenofemoral junction behaves like proximal deep vein thrombosis because propagation across the junction is common, so full therapeutic anticoagulation is recommended rather than prophylactic dosing. The 45 day prophylactic fondaparinux regimen applies to superficial thrombosis sitting farther from the junction. Symptomatic care and watchful imaging both gamble on a short distance the clot can cross quickly, and surgical ligation with aspirin is inferior to anticoagulation for preventing embolic complications."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "vascular_diseases", "question": "A 31 year old woman with Takayasu arteritis has active inflammation, elevated acute phase reactants, and new arm claudication from a 70 percent subclavian stenosis. Her rheumatologist and a vascular surgeon are discussing whether to revascularize now. Which approach best reflects the recommended sequencing of therapy in this disease?", "options": {"A": "Immediate subclavian stenting, since mechanical relief prevents progression of the arteritis", "B": "Surgical bypass performed now, delaying glucocorticoid therapy until postoperative healing is well established", "C": "Combined stenting and high dose glucocorticoids on the same day for efficiency", "D": "Observation without immunosuppression, since arm claudication rarely threatens the limb", "E": "Glucocorticoid therapy first, deferring revascularization until active inflammation is controlled"}, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Glucocorticoid therapy first, deferring revascularization until active inflammation is controlled): revascularization performed during active Takayasu arteritis fails at high rates from restenosis, anastomotic complications, and progression of inflamed vessel walls, so glucocorticoids come first and intervention is deferred until disease activity is controlled, since arm claudication is not limb threatening. Stenting does not treat the underlying arteritis, and operating before immunosuppression sews grafts into actively inflamed tissue. Withholding immunosuppression altogether allows ongoing arterial damage well beyond the symptomatic subclavian segment."}, {"id": 999, "domain": "valvular_disease", "question": "A 58 year old man with flushing, diarrhea, and hepatic metastases from a small bowel neuroendocrine tumor develops progressive right heart failure. Echocardiography shows thickened, retracted, immobile tricuspid leaflets fixed in a semi open position with severe regurgitation, while the mitral and aortic valves appear normal. Why are the left sided valves spared?", "options": {"A": "Left sided valves resist serotonin because their leaflets lack the responsive receptor entirely", "B": "The lungs inactivate circulating vasoactive substances before blood reaches the left heart", "C": "Higher left sided pressures wash mediators off the leaflets before fibrosis begins", "D": "Hepatic metastases release mediators only into the portal circulation, which bypasses systemic veins", "E": "The pericardium selectively shields left sided structures from circulating tumor products"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The lungs inactivate circulating vasoactive substances before blood reaches the left heart): serotonin and related mediators from hepatic metastases drain into the systemic venous return, bathe the right sided valves, and are then largely degraded by pulmonary monoamine oxidase, so the left heart sees little active hormone unless a patent foramen ovale or pulmonary metastases exist. Left sided leaflets do carry responsive receptors and become involved when exposure occurs. Pressure washing is not the protective mechanism, hepatic venous drainage enters the systemic veins directly, and the pericardium filters nothing."}];
